{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"I’ll read only the two specified files and assess each judgement against the evidence shown in its own request."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md && cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/ruler-v1.md && cat docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json'","aggregated_output":"# 判断的尺子，第 1 版（D3 立场金标）\n\n写于 2026-11-02。Alex 的话：\"你已经改了好几次尺子了，到底什么是对的，以及你怎么 freeze。\"\n\n## 0. 一句话\n\n一道题判得对不对，拿它和**一位资深对冲基金分析师读完同样 14 天材料后的看法**比：他认为市场原来预期什么、材料推出来的结果是多少、\n相对预期是高是低；系统最后一次判断落的那一档，有没有把这个结果包进去。分析师不是我，不是写代码的人，不看系统的输出；他的看法写成文件、算出\nsha256、记进决定，之后一个字不改。\n\n## 1. 什么是\"对\"\n\n- **单位**：一道金标题 × 一个时点（14 天窗口末，2026-07-14 收盘后）。23 道题：22 道种子（`poc/seeds/nvda-2026-07-01-v2.json` v1–v22，每次运行里\n  编号固定）加金标 must_open 里种子没覆盖的 1 道（俄亥俄租约担保是/否，2026-07-01 的 must_open）。其余 27 条 must_open 都是这 23 道的同一赌注\n  （信用支持金额 ×10、Kyber 出货 ×11、云厂商资本开支 ×5、内存价格 ×1、中国放行 H200 ×2），映射写在 `questions-v1.json` 里，Alex 签。\n- **材料**：签过的抽取标准对这 14 天的读法，即金标 golden-v5（dev，118 篇）的 309 条事项（核心 86、辅助 151、可选 72），带日期、标题、\n  来源层、说话人、原文和意思。不用系统的账本（账本随每臂变，尺子不能随臂变），不用原始全文（那是重做抽取）。这和抽取的尺子是同一把：标准第 5 节\n  说的\"分析员会记的\"就是这些。\n- **分析师写什么**（每道题一条，`reader-brief.md` 是他拿到的全部说明，里面只有标准和 PRD 06 的原话，没有系统的提示词）：\n  1. `reference`：到 2026-07-14 为止市场预期这道题的答案是什么（一致预期、公司指引、既定计划或截止日），出自哪几条事项；材料里没有就写\n     \"没有，参照 = 现状\"。\n  2. `implied_outcome`：材料推出来的结果——数字题给一个数或一个区间，事件题给是/否和日期——怎么推的，出自哪几条。\n  3. `direction`：结果相对参照：above / in_line / below；事件题 yes / no。\n  4. `confidence`：high / medium / low（材料把这件事定到什么程度）。\n  5. `decisive_items`：撑起这个看法的 2–6 条事项编号；`against`：最强的反面一条。\n  6. `no_view`：材料里没有任何关于这道题的东西时为 true；这道题不进分数，记\"没练到\"。\n  7. `reasoning`：三到六句。只用材料，不用材料外的知识，每个说法都要有编号。\n- **分析师是谁**：两位互相独立、都没写过这套代码也没看过系统输出的模型读者（A：Codex；B：一个干净上下文的 Claude 读者），各自从同一个包\n  写一遍。都标明\"模型写的\"。**Alex 定（2026-11-02，D262）：金标 = 读者 B 的 23 条答案，原样；读者 A 的答案留作对照记录**，两者的差别\n  （区间或是/否不同 3/23：q09、q12、q23；方向标签不同 12/20）就是这把尺子自身噪声的记录（`reconcile-v1.md`）。\n- **判\"对\"的方法**（`score_judgement.py`，全部是代码）：取一次运行里每道题在 2026-07-14 及之前最后一次判断。数字题：金标的区间\n  [low, high] 和系统所选那一档的 [lo, hi) 有没有交集，有 = 一致；事件题：是/否相同 = 一致。没判过的题单独数（那是证据没到，D2 的事，不算\n  判断错）。另外打印两条零假设：**查表基线**（把金标写的参照数落进哪一档，就选那一档）和**永远中性基线**；判断要在这两条线之上才算有判断力。\n  读者之间的分歧率一起打印，那是尺子自身的噪声。\n- **第二层，读不是数**：分数之外，对一个样本的判断，由没写代码的读者看系统写的理由（从 judging-v6 起还有 reference 和 implied_outcome）\n  是不是从所示证据得出。这是 AGENTS §7.2 的\"分数是线索、trace 是测量\"。\n\n## 2. 怎么 freeze\n\n原则先于机制（D261，Alex 2026-11-02：\"我们做完的 review 过的尺子金标不能再动了\"）：这一页、读者说明、题单和金标在 Alex 审过签字之后一个字不改；之前只在建的过程中改，签字前不拿它打任何分。下面是把这句话变成代码和文件的做法。\n\n1. 读者只看 `packet-v1.json` 和 `reader-brief.md`，看不到任何运行、账本、提示词；读者的原始答案 `reader-A.json`、`reader-B.json` 原样保存，不改。\n2. 我不写金标。我做的只有：拼包（代码）、按 direction 机械对齐两位读者、把分歧按材料写成对照记录（引编号）；分歧由材料定，定不了的列给 Alex。\n3. 金标文件 `gold-v1.json` 带 `ruler_version: 1`、两位读者的 sha256、每道题的来源（A/B 一致，或裁定及理由）；文件的 sha256 写进\n   `gold-v1.sha256` 和决定 D261。Alex 签的是：23 道题的清单和映射、裁定过的题。签之前每个分数都标\"金标第 1 版，未签\"。\n4. `score_judgement.py` 开头读金标的 sha256 和 `ruler_version`，和自己写死的对不上就拒绝打分，打印两边的 sha；每份分数都印金标版本。\n5. 冻住后不改文件。发现错了，写进 `corrections-v1.json` 一条带日期的记录：哪道题、改前、改后、为什么；打分器不悄悄应用它，只在分数旁印\n   \"有 N 条更正未并入\"。更正积累到要并入时，出第 2 版：新文件、新 sha、决定里记，旧分数作废并说明。\n6. 这一页的定义本身改了（单位、材料、字段、判对的方法），就是第 2 版尺子：金标必须重写，打分器拒绝版本不符的金标。改定义只能由记录在 Alex\n   原话上的决定触发（AGENTS §7.1）。\n7. 一把尺子：判断这一步不再有别的评分标准。我昨天定的判断规则 D260 是系统这边的规则，不是尺子；读者的说明里没有它。\n\n**冻结记录**：2026-11-02，`gold-v1.json` sha256 `636c056d1e4252fa759660803067e6ccd67a81067b012905e918aa9dec4005f2`，写在 `gold-v1.sha256` 和 `score_judgement.py` 里；`corrections-v1.json` 为空。当天下午提过的两条改法（读者分叉时打三档分；五五开的题不计分）Alex 没采纳：一题一个区间，每题都算。\n\n这把尺子作为整体（抽取标准 + 题目 + 判断）写在 AGENTS §7.1 \"The frozen ruler\"，每个文件的 sha256 由 `tests/test_frozen_rulers.py` 守着：任何一个动了，整套测试变红。\n\n## 2′. 这把尺子沿用了什么，新的只有哪一层\n\n沿用、不重做：抽取标准页（第 3 版签字 D243 + D250 的四条界线）和 golden-v5 是这把尺子的**材料**，按 sha 引用（`packet-v1.json` 记了金标的 sha）；golden-v5 的题目部分（28 条 must_open，题目标准第 3 版）映射进 23 道题；种子 v2（D253）给题的身份和档的边界；PRD 06 的判断规则和 AGENTS §8 的分析师原话是读者说明里的全部规则；桌面尺子 README 的 D1、D2 不动。2026-10-27 的步骤 3/4 金标（`research/2026-10-27-stage34-gold/`，24 例，量的是重复 / 替换 / 并入的决定，不是档位）里写的分析师标准 A（谁先说的，复述只加一个出处不加事实）、B（期间和定义要对上，季度不是全年）和这份说明一致，两者量的是不同步骤；那份金标是在旧账本上写的，助手自己写的，当时就标了不是独立金标。新的只有一层：判断这一步的档位从来没有金标，这是第一份，还有打分器。\n\n## 3. 这把尺子不量什么\n\n该开的题开了没有（D1）、证据挂得对不对（D2）、14 天里每天的路径、依赖和主题推力。它只量：窗口末，系统的档位有没有包住分析师认为的结果。\n\n## 4. 已知的弱点，写明\n\n- 读者是模型，不是人。金标是一位读者（B）的看法，Alex 选的；另一位（A）留作对照，两者在 3/23 道题上区间不交，这是尺子的噪声下限；Alex 的裁定是人的那一票。\n- 材料外的知识：事后结果（FY2027 年报、2026 年底 Rubin 出不出货）都在模型知识截止之后，无法作弊；但模型可能用它记得的\"常识\"补材料，说明里\n  禁止并要求每句引编号，对照时查。\n- 参照：材料里不一定有每道题的一致预期（内存价格、CoWoS 产能），那时参照是现状，direction 的意义弱一点，分数仍按区间交集算。\n- 种子的档不是围着一致预期写的（总收入题一致预期 $392B 落在中性档 350–400 的上沿）：所以分数不比\"看涨/看跌\"的标签，只比区间有没有包住。\n\n## 5. 文件\n\n`judgement-gold/`：`ruler-v1.md`（本页）、`reader-brief.md`、`build_packet.py` → `packet-v1.json` + `questions-v1.json`、\n`run_reader_codex.py` → `reader-A.json`、读者 B 的答案 `reader-B.json`、`reconcile.py` → `gold-v1.json` + `reconcile-v1.md`、\n`gold-v1.sha256`、`corrections-v1.json`、`score_judgement.py`。\n[\n {\n  \"item\": 1,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v13 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Nvidia's CPO switch shipment volume from Q4 2026 through calendar 2027 (GF Securities: 15,000 units in Q4 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, 30,000 by mid-2027, 100,000 for all of 2027), as reflected in Nvidia's networking revenue disclosures and suppliers' CPO/NPO laser shipments.\\\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027, as shown by Nvidia’s shipment disclosures. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-04 and before reaching 100,000 units, Nvidia formally abandons CPO switch shipments for calendar 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-04 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 4 items]\\n- e1068 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities said Nvidia is scaling out co-packaged optics (CPO) faster than expected and forecast Nvidia's CPO switch shipments at 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, rising to 30,000 by mid-2027 and potentially 100,000 units for all of 2027. The figures are the analyst's estimate, not an Nvidia disclosure; the article links them to supplier Lumentum, whose laser chips and photonic components go into optical systems.\\n    Quote: GF Securities says Nvidia (NVDA) is scaling out co-packaged optics, or CPO, faster than expected, creating another potential growth driver for Lumentum. … The forecast is striking. GF Securities expects Nvidia's CPO switch shipments to reach 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, rising to 16,000 in early 2027 and 30,000 by mid-2027. For all of 2027, shipments could reach 100,000 units.\\n- e1069 [fact; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum reported fiscal Q4 revenue of $1.01 billion, more than doubling from a year earlier and its eighth consecutive quarter of year-over-year growth, with Components revenue up 103% to $649.4 million and Systems revenue up 123% to $356.9 million; gross margin rose to 50% and operating margin to 36.6%, net income was $326.3 million, adjusted EPS was $3.23 (over three times the year-ago quarter), free cash flow was $196.2 million, and cash and short-term investments totaled $2.74 billion at period end. Lumentum supplies laser chips and photonic components used in optical systems.\\n    Quote: In Q4, the company generated $1.01 billion in revenue, more than doubling from a year ago and extending its streak of year-over-year growth to eight straight quarters. … The increase was widespread throughout the company. Components' revenue grew 103% to $649.4 million, while Systems' revenue increased 123% to $356.9 million. Profitability also improved, as the gross margin climbed to 50% and the \\n- e1070 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum management guided fiscal Q1 2027 revenue to roughly $1.225–1.275 billion, an increase of more than $150 million sequentially, with adjusted earnings per share of $4.05–$4.35 and expected gains in profitability, saying it expects the current trend to persist through fiscal 2027.\\n    Quote: Management anticipates the trend to persist through fiscal 2027 as well. Lumentum is projecting an increase of more than $150 million in sales for the first quarter, along with gains in profitability, as revenue for the period is expected to be in the neighborhood of $1.225 billion to $1.275 billion and adjusted earnings per share are projected to range between $4.05 and $4.35.\\n- e1071 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities forecast that Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028, and that its near-packaged optics (NPO) laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028, as Nvidia's shift to co-packaged and near-packaged optics drives optical component demand. These are the analyst's estimates of a supplier's revenue, not Nvidia figures.\\n    Quote: GF Securities sees Lumentum benefiting directly from that shift. The firm forecasts Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028. … Near-packaged optics, or NPO, could become an even larger opportunity. GF Securities estimates Lumentum's NPO laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The supplied market-facing reference is GF Securities’ forecast of 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in 2027, exactly the rung 1 boundary. Because this is a forecast for a product with no shipment volume shown in hand, it is discounted: the supplier evidence supports optical demand but does not establish Nvidia CPO switch unit volume or convert to 100,000 Nvidia switches. The most likely outcome is therefore below 100,000, while no evidence shows a formal abandonment or other decision against the event as a whole; rung 3 is the best forecast.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is GF Securities' estimate of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027, which sits at rung 1. The evidence supports an optical-demand ramp, but the 100,000 figure remains an analyst estimate rather than Nvidia shipment volume: Lumentum's revenue and guidance cover broader business, while its projected CPO and NPO laser revenues are not converted into Nvidia switch units. Discounting the unshipped product target, the most likely outcome remains below 100,000, but there is no evidence of formal abandonment or another decision against the event as a whole; therefore rung 3 is the forecast.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e1068\",\n    \"e1069\",\n    \"e1070\",\n    \"e1071\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1068\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1069\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1070\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1071\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"No change in rung or key supporting facts: the 100,000-unit figure remains a third-party forecast, and supplier growth and optical-revenue estimates still do not establish Nvidia's shipment volume or a decision to abandon CPO.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v10\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v9\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"GF Securities' forecast of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027 is the market-facing reference; 100,000 is the rung 1 boundary.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies calendar-2027 shipments below 100,000, but does not support a defensible more precise unit estimate: Lumentum's projected CPO/NPO laser revenues span products and are not attributable or convertible to Nvidia switch units. No evidence establishes formal abandonment, so the outcome remains open.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 2,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v10 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"US data-center capacity additions in gigawatts for calendar 2026, versus the pace of 'somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year' that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang described, and against how far labour availability constrains that pace.\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?\\nLadder: rung 1 below 10 GW: below 10 GW / rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW: from 10 GW to below 15 GW / rung 3 from 15 GW to below 20 GW: from 15 GW to below 20 GW (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 GW to below 25 GW: from 20 GW to below 25 GW / rung 5 25 GW or more: 25 GW or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 13 items]\\n- e678 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of Nvidia ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, speaking on a panel at the White House, said the US is adding \\\"somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year\\\" of AI data-center infrastructure and that the work extends beyond data centers to power generation, construction, power cooling and pipe fitting. This is a management estimate of the annual pace of US AI-infrastructure additions, not a reported outturn.\\n    Quote: Huang explained that the US is adding \\\"somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year\\\" of the infrastructure and said the work reaches well beyond the data centers themselves. … \\\"Don't forget you got to build a power generation plants,\\\" the AI hardware giant's chief executive said, adding: \\\"You've got construction, you've got power cooling, you've got pipe fitters.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e675; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e679 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (Brian Sozzi, cited); original source Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] The Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST), an initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google, estimated in a new report that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled-trades job openings annually through 2035, and that current training programmes produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed. The figures are the organisation's estimates of a labour-supply constraint on construction and infrastructure work, including data-center build-out.\\n    Quote: As Yahoo Finance's Brian Sozzi detailed, the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) — an initiative launched by BlackRock ( BLK ), Carhartt, Ford ( F ), and Google ( GOOG ) — estimated that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled trades job openings annually through 2035. … Current training programs produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed, according to a new AAST report.\\n- e680 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft (joining AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft are joining the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades this week, the workforce initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google that targets the skilled-trades occupations used in building and operating infrastructure such as data centers.\\n    Quote: Nvidia, Meta ( META ), and Microsoft ( MSFT ) are joining AAST this week. … AAST defines skilled trade jobs as the 124 occupations — including carpenters, electricians, mechanics of various kinds, plumbers, and pipe fitters — that \\\"build, install, operate, maintain, repair, fabricate, or extract the physical materials, products, equipment, systems, and infrastructure the U.S. economy relies on.\\\"\\n- e40 [party_statement; SB Energy (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source SB Energy ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] SB Energy said it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI, where NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider.\\n    Quote: SB Energy says it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI.\\n- e263 [fact; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source OpenAI (agreement with Advanced Micro Devices) ; from gnews:dad3a4651f31a14be8d86d8c 2026-09-25] OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts with AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026; the agreement also gives OpenAI a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\\n    Quote: OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts using AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026. In addition, the deal also provides OpenAI with a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\\n- e330 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon, part of what the article describes as spreading its compute dollars across suppliers.\\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\\n- e331 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\\n- e675 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of NVIDIA 2026-09-29; from gnews:a58c5a2a6302b9af5cfd79c3 2026-09-30] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking at an America.Gov event on 29 September 2026 alongside Elon Musk, estimated that the United States could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI data-center infrastructure annually — an estimate he framed as a rough calculation ('let's just estimate') — and said that building out those data centers would probably create about one million jobs. The article notes that his million-job figure refers to infrastructure-related employment, not net job creation after accounting for potential AI displacement elsewhere.\\n    Quote: He estimated that the U.S. could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI infrastructure annually, requiring substantial investment in electricity generation, cooling systems, networking and construction. … \\\"And so we're building this infrastructure out and, call it across all of the United States, somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year. Let's just estimate, that creates just, probably about\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e678; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e727 [party_statement; Bloomberg, citing Elon Musk's post on X; original source xAI (Elon Musk) ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] xAI CEO Elon Musk said in a post on X, reported by Bloomberg, that the Colossus 2 cluster in the Memphis area already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s—about 550,000 Nvidia chips—and could more than double to top 1.2 million by end-2026, with another 220,000 GB300s due the following week, 220,000 expected in November and a further 220,000 possible in late December \\\"if we get lucky.\\\" xAI had previously said it planned to equip the Memphis facility with 1 million GPUs by 2026.\\n    Quote: Colossus 2, the Memphis area cluster built by xAI, may more than double its current Nvidia chip count by the end of the year, Bloomberg reported, citing a post Musk made on X.\\nHere is how the numbers stack up, according to Musk:\\nColossus 2 already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s.\\nAnother 220,000 GB300s were set to go live the following week.\\nA further 220,000 are expected in Nov\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n- e1085 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Amazon.com Inc. ; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Amazon.com signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy to secure electricity from Maryland's Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant, buying 690 megawatts of the 1,790-megawatt facility; the agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable power for growing AI operations.\\n    Quote: Amazon.com Inc. signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy Corp. to secure electricity from Maryland’s Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant. Under the deal, Amazon will purchase 690 megawatts from the 1,790-megawatt facility, Maryland’s only nuclear power plant. The agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable electricity for gro\\n- e1104 [third_party_view; Benzinga, citing Reuters; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley estimates that U.S. data-center developers face a 34% net power shortfall through 2028, equivalent to 32 gigawatts, even after accounting for workarounds such as fuel cells and power generated directly at data-center sites, according to Reuters.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley estimates U.S. data-center developers face a 34% net power shortfall through 2028, equivalent to 32 gigawatts, even after accounting for workarounds such as fuel cells and power generated directly at data-center sites, according to Reuters.\\n- e1105 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Jensen Huang (Nvidia CEO) ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said AI developers now need to \\\"secure the land, power, and shell\\\" before filling data centers with computing equipment, and that the process can take two to three years.\\n    Quote: Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has described the bottleneck in similar terms, saying AI developers now need to \\\" secure the land, power , and shell\\\" before filling data centers with computing equipment. He said that process can take two to three years.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough estimate of 10–20 GW annually: that range spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW), rung 3 (15–<20 GW), and reaches rung 4 at 20 GW; its 15 GW midpoint is in rung 3. I forecast about 14 GW, below that midpoint. The skilled-trades shortfall and stated scarcity of land, power, and building space weigh against the upper half of the range. The announced projects and deployments provide some counterweight, but their capacity is not shown to be fully new US capacity added during calendar 2026: some are multi-period or unscheduled, and some lack a stated US location. The evidence therefore does not imply 15 GW or more.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-year lead time for securing land, power, and a shell weigh on 2026 delivery. Signed or planned capacity and xAI’s announced expansion support additions, but do not establish that those amounts will be new U.S. capacity completed in calendar 2026; several are unscheduled, span other periods or suppliers, or lack a stated U.S. location. The evidence supports roughly 12–14 GW, not enough to cross the 15 GW boundary.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e675\",\n    \"e679\",\n    \"e40\",\n    \"e263\",\n    \"e330\",\n    \"e331\",\n    \"e727\",\n    \"e732\",\n    \"e1085\",\n    \"e1104\",\n    \"e1105\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e678\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e679\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e680\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e40\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e263\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e330\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e331\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e675\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e727\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e732\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1085\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1104\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1105\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 2, but the implied estimate moves from about 14 GW to roughly 12–14 GW as the new quantified power-shortfall estimate and the additional labour and project lead-time evidence weigh against delivery. The repeated Huang range is not a new fact; the announced capacity and power arrangements provide counterweight but do not establish calendar-2026 U.S. additions at their full stated amounts.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"Huang’s rough estimate of 10–20 GW per year is the stated reference. It spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW) and rung 3 (15–<20 GW), with the 20 GW endpoint at rung 4; its 15 GW midpoint is rung 3.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About 12–14 GW of U.S. additions in calendar 2026, with a central estimate near 13 GW. This is a qualitative forecast from Huang’s annual pace, tempered by the quantified labour and power constraints and partly offset by announced projects; the project figures are discounted where timing, U.S. location, or delivery in 2026 is not established.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 3,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v3 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \\\"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\\\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: By January 31, 2027, U.S. authorities authorize exports of Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow Nvidia’s shipments into China. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 23, 2026, the U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security formally prohibits Blackwell exports to China through January 31, 2027, or Chinese authorities formally prohibit Nvidia’s Blackwell shipments into China through that date. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 84 items]\\n[Not shown: 29 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e80 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source The Trump administration (U.S. government) ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China, per the article; the H200 is described as an older-generation product behind Nvidia's current Blackwell chips.\\n    Quote: The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e365, e425, e565, e745, e851, e879, e942; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e81 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Chinese authorities ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Chinese authorities have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips into China, according to the article, despite U.S. permission for those shipments to approved customers.\\n    Quote: Chinese authorities, however, have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e566; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e82 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Nvidia has not factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, according to the article — meaning any resumption of China shipments would be incremental to its stated outlook.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia hasn't factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, the stock is still up 21% year to date, powered by the AI boom here in the US.\\n- e86 [fact; barchart.com; original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO) 2026-09-23; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang is scheduled to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, 2026, along with other technology leaders, as the U.S. and China discuss AI, trade, tariffs and advanced semiconductors. The article says the dinner itself does not guarantee policy changes but could provide a backdrop for signals on NVIDIA's access to the Chinese market and export restrictions.\\n    Quote: Nvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e302; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e88 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points. The forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\\n    Quote: Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e57; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e111 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion, a guidance the article says excludes China Data Center compute revenue, cited as geopolitical uncertainty.\\n    Quote: Management guided Q3 to $108 billion. … Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e57; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e148 [fact; CNBC; original source U.S. government (Washington) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] The article reports that Washington has for years restricted China's access to Nvidia's most advanced AI chips and to the equipment used to make them, on the bet that limiting computing power would keep Chinese AI a generation behind; the restriction is described as in force as of the 23 September 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Washington has for years restricted China's access to Nvidia's most advanced AI chips and the equipment to make them, betting that starving computing power would keep Chinese AI a generation behind.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e428, e918; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e149 [party_statement; CNBC; original source U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] During the September 2026 Trump–Xi state visit, the article said neither side appeared ready to put chip export controls on the table; Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer met Vice Premier He Lifeng in New York over the weekend and set up a formal AI channel for incident warnings, and Greer said explicitly that export controls on advanced chips and chipmaking equipment were not on the agenda.\\n    Quote: As Trump hosts Xi for a multi-day state visit, neither side appears ready to put chip export controls on the table. … Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer met Vice Premier He Lifeng in New York over the weekend and set up a formal AI channel for incident warnings. But Greer said explicitly that export controls on advanced chips and chipmaking equipment were\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e380, e399, e404, e442; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e150 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Huawei ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] Huawei said at its annual Huawei Connect conference, the week before the 23 September 2026 article, that its Ascent 960DT AI chip will ship in the first quarter of 2027, three quarters ahead of its original schedule.\\n    Quote: At its annual Huawei Connect conference last week, Huawei said its Ascent 960DT will ship in the first quarter of 2027 , three quarters ahead of its original schedule.\\n- e151 [fact; CNBC; original source Alibaba (chip unit) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] At the Apsara Conference in Hangzhou on the Tuesday before the 23 September 2026 article, Alibaba's chip unit unveiled the Zhenwu V900 AI chip, which it says triples the performance of its predecessor and will go on sale in early 2027.\\n    Quote: At the Apsara Conference in Hangzhou on Tuesday, Alibaba's chip unit unveiled the Zhenwu V900 chip , which triples the performance of its predecessor and will go on sale in early 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e53; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e152 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source CNBC ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] The article reports that Huawei's latest SuperPoD computing architecture links far fewer processors than originally planned, and that each Huawei chip still delivers roughly half the compute of Nvidia's chips.\\n    Quote: But the approach has limits. Huawei's latest SuperPoD computing architecture links far fewer processors than originally planned, and each chip still delivers roughly half the compute of Nvidia's.\\n- e153 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Huawei (Chairman Eric Xu) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] Huawei Chairman Eric Xu acknowledged that Huawei may not be able to make enough AI chips to meet Chinese domestic demand, and that Chinese AI developers may not be advanced enough to experience rogue safety risks, as reported in the 23 September 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Chairman Eric Xu acknowledged that Huawei may not be able to make enough chips to meet domestic demand, and that Chinese AI developers may not be advanced enough to experience rogue safety risks.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e440, e749; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e154 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source Chris Miller (Tufts University) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] Chris Miller, a Tufts University professor and author of 'Chip War', said Chinese firms remain substantially short of the chips needed to train and deploy advanced models and rely heavily on smuggling chips or gaining access to data centers outside China, concluding that China is still highly dependent on the U.S. for advanced chips.\\n    Quote: Chinese firms remain substantially short of the chips needed to train and deploy advanced models, and rely heavily on smuggling chips or gaining access to data centers outside China, Miller said. \\\"China is still highly dependent on the U.S. for advanced chips.\\\"\\n- e155 [rumour; CNBC; original source DeepSeek ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] DeepSeek, the AI startup whose low-cost model rattled global markets the previous year, reportedly lined up the largest known cluster of Huawei chips built to date, according to the article.\\n    Quote: DeepSeek, the startup whose low-cost model rattled global markets last year, reportedly lined up the largest known cluster of Huawei chips built to date.\\n- e226 [fact; tradingview.com; original source  ; from gnews:6599781487f187538641b19e 2026-09-24] According to tradingview.com, export curbs have limited China's access to NVIDIA chips, shrinking China's share of NVIDIA's data center sales to a minimal level.\\n    Quote: Export curbs have limited China’s access, shrinking China’s share of NVDA’s data center sales to a minimal level.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e443; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e302 [fact; Business Chief (Alec McDonnell); original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO), with Donald Trump and Xi Jinping ; from gnews:d6b6359e9b16cc349069ac0c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang is reported to be joining US President Donald Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping at a state dinner on Thursday, an occasion at which sales of chips to China are on the agenda — a diplomatic-level step bearing on whether NVIDIA's China data-center business can resume.\\n    Quote: Jensen joins Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping at Thursday's state dinner, with chip sales to China on the table\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e86; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e308 [fact; UNN; original source U.S. authorities (the export ban is not attributed to a named agency in the item) ; from gnews:d1b9fc5258b883721374fb1e 2026-09-25] The article states that Nvidia's and AMD's most advanced artificial intelligence chips are banned from being exported to China, while noting China remains a key market and manufacturing hub for Apple and Tesla. The item names no agency, date or scope for the ban beyond 'most advanced' chips.\\n    Quote: Nvidia and AMD's most advanced artificial intelligence chips are banned from being exported to China, although the country remains a key market and manufacturing hub for Cook's Apple and Musk's Tesla.\\n- e365 [fact; ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News); original source U.S. government (Trump administration) ; from gnews:a883c7d707bfef9843efbc2a 2026-09-26] As reported on 26 September 2026, U.S. export restrictions currently bar Nvidia's most powerful (current-generation) AI chips from being exported to China, while the prior year (2025) President Donald Trump permitted Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers subject to a 25% tariff, according to ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News).\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s most powerful chips are currently prohibited from export to China due to Washington’s restrictions. At the same time, last year Trump allowed Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers with a 25% tariff.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e377 [rumour; Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance); original source The Information (unnamed-source report on China's MIIT and Alibaba/ByteDance) 2026-09-27; from gnews:b4ef3fc37047e9a76439ecdb 2026-09-27] The Information reported on Sunday 27 September 2026, citing two people familiar with the matter, that China may allow Alibaba and ByteDance to buy a new NVIDIA chip, and that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) asked the two firms how many RTX PRO 5500 cards they want and why. The RTX PRO 5500 is a workstation part that sits outside the data-center chips targeted by US export curbs. Neither Beijing nor the companies confirmed the plan; the report gave no quantity or timing and no sign that bigger data-center chips would follow.\\n    Quote: China may allow Alibaba and ByteDance to buy a new Nvidia chip, The Information reported on Sunday. … China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT), which oversees the tech sector, asked the two firms how many RTX PRO 5500 cards they want and why. That is according to The Information, citing two people familiar with the matter. … Because it is a workstation part, it sits outside t\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e382, e384, e397, e400, e403, e418, e419, e423, e427, e430, e519; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e382 [rumour; Reuters (published by CNBC); original source The Information 2026-09-27; from gnews:c24441966fa661191bb1a601 2026-09-27] The Information reported on Sunday, citing two unnamed people familiar with the matter, that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked companies including ByteDance and Alibaba to report their plans to purchase Nvidia's RTX PRO 5500 chips — a chip built for high-end professional computers — and told some Chinese companies that the government intends to approve the purchases. Reuters said it could not immediately verify the report, and Alibaba and ByteDance did not immediately respond to requests for comment.\\n    Quote: The Chinese government has signaled it could allow some domestic companies like ByteDance and Alibaba to buy Nvidia's chip built for high-end professional computers, The Information reported on Sunday, citing two people familiar with the matter. … China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked companies including ByteDance and Alibaba to report their plans to purchase Nvidi\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e383 [party_statement; Nvidia spokesperson, reported by Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c24441966fa661191bb1a601 2026-09-27] Asked by Reuters to comment on the report, an Nvidia spokesperson said U.S. firms continue to be restricted by a combination of outdated U.S. export controls — which the spokesperson said cover gaming products released nearly half a decade ago — and China's own limits on U.S. imports.\\n    Quote: \\\"US firms continue to be restricted by a combination of outdated US export controls, which cover gaming products released nearly half a decade ago, and China's own limits on US imports,\\\" an Nvidia spokesperson said when asked to comment.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e424; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e398 [fact; investing.com; original source Nvidia (product release); specifications per TechPowerUp ; from gnews:68448e3de272bc46d0ede0fa 2026-09-27] Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU released this month with 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores and a starting price reported above $6,000 according to TechPowerUp; the chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of successive rounds of U.S. export controls since October 2022, which the article says may give Beijing more regulatory latitude to approve purchases without new friction with Washington.\\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU released this month, featuring 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores, with a starting price reported above $6,000, according to TechPowerUp. The chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of successive rounds of U.S. export controls since October 2022, potentially giving Beiji\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e433; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e401 [fact; Bloomberg; original source US administration ; from gnews:95fdd0c465e5c78a407a379b 2026-09-27] The US administration recently lifted export restrictions on some Nvidia chips, but the products covered tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold into China only in small quantities, Bloomberg reported.\\n    Quote: The US administration recently lifted restrictions on some Nvidia chips, though the products tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold in small quantities.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e421, e429; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e426 [fact; The Next Web, reporting in August 2026; original source Chinese authorities (unnamed) ; from gnews:905fc2415065c07f926596d0 2026-09-27] ByteDance and Tencent were told to keep the first shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips in Hong Kong rather than bring them into mainland China, The Next Web reported in August 2026.\\n    Quote: However, ByteDance and Tencent were told to keep the first shipments in Hong Kong , TNW reported in August, rather than bring them into mainland China.\\n- e428 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source US government (Washington) ; from gnews:246875816e58773740fb09d8 2026-09-27] The US government bars Nvidia's most powerful accelerators from China on national-security grounds, a restriction the article describes as still in force and distinguishes from the RTX Pro 5500, which is built on older-generation processes and is intended mainly for graphics and simulations rather than being a top-of-the-line accelerator such as the GB300 series.\\n    Quote: Washington bars Nvidia's most powerful accelerators from China, concerned they would propel the country's AI efforts and aid the military of a geopolitical ally. … Unlike Nvidia's top-of-the-line accelerators such as the GB300 series, it's built on older-generation processes and is designed to provide workstation computation at scale.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e148; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e435 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Jeffrey Kessler, under secretary of commerce for industry and security ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] In July, under secretary of commerce for industry and security Jeffrey Kessler told the House Foreign Affairs Committee that roughly $10 billion in approved export licences had yielded \\\"a very small quantity of chips,\\\" which he called trivial; the article frames the H200 clearance as the largest reopening of chip sales to China in years that still produced almost nothing.\\n    Quote: The H200 clearance was the largest reopening of chip sales to China in years, and it still produced almost nothing. In July, the under secretary of commerce for industry and security, Jeffrey Kessler, told the House Foreign Affairs Committee that roughly $10 billion in approved licences had yielded \\\"a very small quantity of chips,\\\" which he called trivial.\\n- e436 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source  ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chinese buyers had ordered more than 2 million Nvidia H200 chips for 2026 against Nvidia inventory of about 700,000 units, as reported by the article.\\n    Quote: Chinese buyers had ordered more than 2 million H200s for 2026 against Nvidia inventory of about 700,000 units.\\n- e437 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line.\\n- e440 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Huawei (Xu Zhijun, rotating chairman) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Huawei rotating chairman Xu Zhijun said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that Huawei's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\\n    Quote: Huawei's rotating chairman, Xu Zhijun, said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that the company's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e443 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e226; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e715 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06cfbef2e042b99ce960fcd8 2026-09-30] Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market, and warned that its absence has helped rivals build \\\"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\\\"\\n    Quote: Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market. … The company warned that its absence has helped rivals build \\\"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\\\"\\n- e745 [fact; theregister.com; original source US Commerce Department / Trump administration ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] US export policy toward Nvidia's China sales: the US Commerce Department imposed new licensing requirements on Nvidia's and AMD's China-spec accelerators in April 2025, reversed course that summer and later reached an arrangement that gave the US government a percentage of certain China-bound chip sales; by late 2025 the Trump administration loosened restrictions further and announced that Nvidia would be allowed to sell more potent H200-series parts to approved Chinese customers for the first time.\\n    Quote: After imposing new licensing requirements on Nvidia's and AMD's China-spec accelerators in April 2025, the US Commerce Department reversed course that summer, later reaching an arrangement to cut the US government in on a percentage of certain China-bound chip sales. By late last year, the Trump administration loosened restrictions further, announcing that Nvidia would be allowed to sell its more \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e746 [party_statement; theregister.com (citing Nvidia's Q2 earnings call); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, executives said the company had begun shipping a small volume of H200 accelerators to China but that these shipments amounted to less than 1 percent of its data-center revenues.\\n    Quote: During Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, executives revealed that while the company had begun shipments of a small volume of H200 accelerators to the region, they amounted to less than 1 percent of its datacenter revenues.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e747 [fact; theregister.com; original source Chinese government officials ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] The item reports that the earlier-in-2026 blockade of Nvidia H20 accelerators for China set off alarm bells in Beijing and that Chinese government officials began pressuring data-center operators to transition away from foreign accelerators toward homegrown alternatives, even after Washington allowed Nvidia to resume sales.\\n    Quote: While Uncle Sam had given Jensen Huang its blessing to resume sales, the initial blockade of H20s earlier in the year had set off alarm bells in Beijing. Government officials there began pressuring bit barn operators to transition away from foreign accelerators in favor of homegrown alternatives.\\n- e763 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%; the outlook excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, which Zacks said suggests current expectations do not depend on a China recovery.\\n    Quote: The near-term outlook also remains strong. NVIDIA expects fiscal third-quarter revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. This outlook excludes Data Center compute revenues from China, suggesting current expectations do not depend on a recovery in that market.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e57; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e780 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's guidance already excludes China data center compute revenue, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Guidance already excludes China data center compute revenue.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e378; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e784 [fact; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese state-backed financing entity, funded AI infrastructure developer Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of more than 700 servers, and at least one transaction explicitly funded 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers powered by Nvidia's high-performance B300 Blackwell chips, according to Bloomberg News citing filings with the People's Bank of China. The item notes the documented batch of Blackwell-equipped servers is relatively small but highlights gaps in Western export restrictions.\\n    Quote: The financing, provided by Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., supported the purchase of more than 700 servers by AI infrastructure developer Glory View Technology Co. … Filings with the People's Bank of China cited by Bloomberg reveal that at least one transaction explicitly funded 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers powered by Nvidia's high-performance B300 Blackwell chips. … While the documented batch of\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e957; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e785 [party_statement; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Nvidia told Bloomberg it is investigating the matter with its equipment manufacturing partners, after a Bloomberg report that a Chinese state-backed lessor financed purchases of servers powered by Nvidia B300 Blackwell chips; Asus reiterated its commitment to international export control regulations.\\n    Quote: Nvidia told Bloomberg that it is investigating the matter with its equipment manufacturing partners, while Asus reiterated its commitment to international export control regulations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e959; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e786 [fact; Investing.com (citing Bloomberg News and Chinese regulatory filings); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Semi-Tech Leasing Group, originally established with backing from China's national semiconductor fund and now predominantly controlled by municipal government entities in Shenzhen and Beijing, has deployed more than 11 billion yuan ($1.6 billion) into compute infrastructure, positioning itself as a core financing engine for national AI priorities.\\n    Quote: Semi-Tech Leasing, originally established with backing from China's national semiconductor fund, is now predominantly controlled by municipal government entities in Shenzhen and Beijing. … The lessor has deployed more than 11 billion yuan ($1.6 billion) into compute infrastructure, positioning itself as a core financing engine for national AI priorities.\\n- e787 [fact; Investing.com (citing Bloomberg News and Chinese regulatory filings); original source Glory View Technology Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Glory View Technology secured over 3 billion yuan ($450 million) under sale-and-leaseback arrangements initiated in mid-2025 to expand its data-center capacity, with the majority of the procured hardware slated for deployment at a major China Mobile Ltd. computing hub in Ningxia, a key node in China's state-backed data-center network.\\n    Quote: Under a series of sale-and-leaseback arrangements initiated in mid-2025, Glory View secured over 3 billion yuan ($450 million) to expand its data center capacity. … The majority of the procured hardware was slated for deployment at a major China Mobile Ltd. computing hub in Ningxia, a key node in China's state-backed data center network.\\n- e788 [fact; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] After initial scrutiny, Semi-Tech Leasing resubmitted altered filings to Chinese credit registries that removed specific hardware descriptions, supplier details and server model designations, which the report says reflects efforts by domestic market participants to obscure the supply chains feeding China's AI infrastructure buildout.\\n    Quote: Following initial scrutiny, Semi-Tech Leasing resubmitted altered filings to Chinese credit registries that removed specific hardware descriptions, supplier details, and server model designations. … The selective redactions reflect ongoing efforts by domestic market participants to obscure the supply chains feeding China's rapid AI infrastructure buildout.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e958; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e802 [third_party_view; Fortune (Sasha Rogelberg); original source Epoch AI ; from gnews:d45e5daf2a6f66edffc0d92a 2026-10-01] Epoch AI estimated that, by the end of 2025, smuggling accounted for between 290,000 and 1.6 million Nvidia H100-equivalent chips, about one-third of China’s total compute stock; Epoch AI did not attribute the smuggled chips to cargo theft, according to Fortune.\\n    Quote: By the end of 2025, smuggling was responsible for between 290,000 and 1.6 million Nvidia H100–equivalent chips , making up about one-third of China’s total compute stock, according to research institute Epoch AI, though it did not attribute these smuggled chips to cargo theft.\\n- e803 [fact; Fortune (Sasha Rogelberg); original source Trump administration ; from gnews:d45e5daf2a6f66edffc0d92a 2026-10-01] Fortune reports that the Trump administration is taking action to ban exports of domestic AI technology, and that organized criminals are smuggling such products into sanctioned countries including China and Russia for black-market resale.\\n    Quote: As the Trump administration takes action to ban exports of domestic AI technology, organized criminals see an opportunity to smuggle these products into sanctioned countries like China and Russia and sell them on the black market.\\n- e850 [fact; Business Insider (article by Truman Dickerson); original source US federal prosecutors (indictment and court records) ; from gnews:ffd4a1b5211f4a70bf6ad9d1 2026-10-02] US federal prosecutors charged Yiu Kong Lui, 38, of San Gabriel, California, with violating export controls, smuggling and money laundering for helping to move more than $300 million worth of Nvidia semiconductor chips into China; he was arrested on Thursday and is to be arraigned. According to a 22-page indictment, the operation ran from 2023 until August, with Lui and co-conspirators using shell companies to route chips first to Malaysia or Singapore and then into China; a company he owned brokered the deals and was paid over $180 million by Malaysian shipping companies, and Lui lied to US chip manufacturers that the chips would not end up in China.\\n    Quote: A California man is facing federal charges for helping smuggle over $300 million worth of Nvidia semiconductor chips into China, prosecutors said this week. … Yiu Kong Lui, 38, of San Gabriel, Cali., was arrested on Thursday and is set to be arraigned in federal court and charged with violating export controls, smuggling, and money laundering, court records show. … In a 22-page indictment, prosecu\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e876, e884, e886, e887, e888, e919; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e851 [fact; Business Insider (article by Truman Dickerson); original source US government (export-licensing requirement); President Donald Trump (2025 decision allowing Nvidia chip sales to China) ; from gnews:ffd4a1b5211f4a70bf6ad9d1 2026-10-02] US policy in force as reported: exporting advanced semiconductor chips to China without a licence is illegal, and President Donald Trump drew criticism last year for allowing Nvidia to sell its chips to China, a departure from the previous Biden administration policy that effectively blocked all exports of advanced chips to China.\\n    Quote: It is illegal to export advanced semiconductor chips into China without a license, as the US competes with the East Asian giant in the AI race. … President Donald Trump drew criticism last year for allowing Nvidia to sell its chips to China , a departure from the previous US policy under President Joe Biden, which effectively blocked all exports of advanced chips to China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e876 [fact; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source US federal prosecutors/authorities 2026-10-01; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] US prosecutors charged, and federal authorities arrested, Greg Lui for smuggling servers containing $300 million worth of AI chips to China in violation of US export laws, Bloomberg reported; the arrest occurred the day before the 2 October 2026 article.\\n    Quote: US prosecutors just charged a man with smuggling NVIDIA chips to China, and government officials are saying the company isn't doing all it could to stop the illegal activities, Bloomberg reported. … Earlier this year, officials released a surveillance video showing someone using a red hair dryer to peel serial number stickers off NVIDIA server boxes in an effort to sneak AI chips to China. And yes\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e877 [rumour; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (citing unnamed sources) ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] Bloomberg reported that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers capable of housing 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors, and US officials now believe China has already smuggled that many chips and more, according to Bloomberg's sources.\\n    Quote: At the same time, Bloomberg reported that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers that could house 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors. US officials now think that China has already smuggled that many chips and more, according to Bloomberg 's sources.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e940; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e878 [rumour; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source US prosecutors (allegations in the Super Micro case) ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] In the Super Micro case, prosecutors allege that collaborators, including co-founder Yih-Shyan 'Wally' Liaw, sold approximately $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to Bangkok-based OBON, ostensibly to build AI infrastructure in Thailand, but the servers were shipped directly to end-users in China; stickers from genuine NVIDIA products were moved onto dummy servers to fool compliance officers.\\n    Quote: An example of such an operation involved Super Micro and the famous red hair dryer. Accused collaborators from that wholesaler (including Super Micro co-founder Yih-Shyan \\\"Wally\\\" Liaw) allegedly found buyers in China eager to purchase NVIDIA's latest AI tech. To get around export controls, the company sold approximately $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to a Bangkok-based company called OBON, which\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e925, e941; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e879 [fact; Steve Dent (Engadget); original source NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang / Trump administration ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has lobbied US officials to relax export curbs on China and other Asian nations and persuaded the Trump administration last year (2025) to allow sales of NVIDIA's previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA's CEO Jensen Huang has lobbied US officials to relax export curbs to China and other Asian nations, and convince the Trump administration last year to allow sales of its previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e880 [third_party_view; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source Daniel Remler and unnamed US government officials ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] Former State Department AI policy head Daniel Remler and unnamed US government officials questioned gaps in NVIDIA's due diligence on China-bound shipments, saying Southeast Asia received more chips than it could use and that NVIDIA should have noticed OBON lacked the 100 megawatts of data-center capacity to host the servers it bought.\\n    Quote: However, government officials and industry critics have highlighted gaps in the company's due diligence and questioned how it failed to notice red flags. … Officials say that NVIDIA should have noticed that OBON didn't have the physical capacity (100 megawatts of data center space) to host the number of servers it purchased. … \\\"I've never felt like we got a straight answer as to how it's possible \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e939; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e884 [fact; Etiido Uko, Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. Department of Justice 2026-10-01; from gnews:4cffee893f7dfe0993352552 2026-10-02] The U.S. Department of Justice announced on October 1, 2026 that Greg Lui (also Yiu Kong Lui), owner of California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., was arrested and charged with smuggling more than $300 million worth of restricted Nvidia chips inside high-end servers to China from 2023 to 2024, allegedly routing shipments first through Malaysia and Singapore — jurisdictions where the restrictions did not apply — and using false paperwork misrepresenting the end users and destinations so no export licence was required, with buyers including the Chinese government. The DOJ charged him with conspiracy to violate the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration Regulations, outbound smuggling, and conspiracy to commit money laundering; he was arraigned on October 1 and faces up to 20 years in prison if convicted on all counts. The FBI, the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security and the Defense Criminal Investigative Service are jointly investigating. The article frames this as the latest in a series of prosecutions for illegal export of restricted AI accelerators to China, where it says demand for Nvidia GPUs remains very high.\\n    Quote: A California man has been arrested for smuggling over $300 million worth of restricted Nvidia chips to China in violation of U.S. export controls. According to a U.S. Department of Justice (DoJ) press release on October 1, the defendant — Greg Lui, also known as Yiu Kong Lui — and unidentified co-conspirators smuggled the chips in high-end servers from 2023 to 2024. He allegedly first exported the\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e885 [fact; Etiido Uko, Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:4cffee893f7dfe0993352552 2026-10-02] The article reports, in an item dated 2 October 2026, that nine people were indicted in Taiwan in the previous month (September 2026) over the illegal export of Nvidia B300 GPUs to China.\\n    Quote: Last month, nine people were indicted in Taiwan over the illegal export of Nvidia B300 GPUs to China .\\n- e886 [fact; theregister.com; original source US federal prosecutors (indictment) and arresting authorities ; from gnews:a163858fac4dd8592ca6170a 2026-10-02] Greg Lui, 38, a California business owner, was arrested on Thursday (the article was published Friday 2 October 2026) after being charged with allegedly smuggling Nvidia hardware to China without the required US export licenses; he is charged with one count each of Export Control Reform Act / Export Administration Regulations violations, outbound smuggling and money laundering, which together carry a maximum prison sentence of 50 years.\\n    Quote: A California business owner was arrested on Thursday after being charged with allegedly smuggling Nvidia hardware to China without the proper export licenses. … Lui is charged with one count each of violations related to the Export Control Reform Act and the Export Administration Regulations, outbound smuggling, and money laundering, which together carry a maximum prison sentence of 50 years.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e887 [fact; theregister.com; original source US federal prosecutors (court documents) ; from gnews:a163858fac4dd8592ca6170a 2026-10-02] Prosecutors allege that from about October 2023 until at least 12 August 2026 Greg Lui used his company Earthmade Computer Inc to order servers containing Nvidia A100, H100, PNY GeForce RTX 4090 and GeForce RTX 5090 GPUs in the US and route them through Malaysia and Singapore to China, brokering sales of almost $300 million worth of Nvidia kit; they claim he received more than $176 million in payments from two Malaysian transshipment companies, and court documents tie a shipment of 92 servers ordered by Earthmade from San Francisco International Airport to Kuala Lumpur, with the onward re-shipment consignee in Hong Kong listed as a Chinese customer.\\n    Quote: Greg Lui, 38, allegedly tried to export around $300 million worth of Nvidia kit - typically used for artificial intelligence (AI) development - via US-based freight businesses and Malaysian transshipment companies. … Prosecutors allege that Lui used his company, Earthmade Computer Inc, to order the products in the US, which consisted of servers containing high-powered Nvidia components, including \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e888 [fact; qz.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors 2026-10-01; from gnews:018f6db02c8c3495a8cc108b 2026-10-02] Greg Lui, 38, owner of City of Industry, California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., was arrested on Thursday on a three-count federal indictment charging him with smuggling more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers containing Nvidia AI chips to China, the U.S. Justice Department said. The counts are conspiracy to violate the Export Control Reform Act, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering; the DOJ said conviction on all three could bring up to 20 years on each of the two conspiracy charges and up to 10 years on the smuggling count.\\n    Quote: Greg Lui, the owner of a San Gabriel Valley-based technology company, was arrested Thursday on a three-count federal indictment charging him with smuggling more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers containing Nvidia $NVDA artificial intelligence chips to China. … Lui, 38, of San Gabriel, California, owns Earthmade Computer Inc., based in City of Industry, California. He fa\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e889 [fact; qz.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (indictment allegations) ; from gnews:018f6db02c8c3495a8cc108b 2026-10-02] U.S. prosecutors alleged that Greg Lui and unnamed co-conspirators used Earthmade between 2023 and 2024 to buy export-controlled servers containing Nvidia GPUs and route them to China without the required Commerce Department licences, submitting false documentation to U.S. manufacturers about the equipment's destination and shipping via Malaysia and Singapore before re-exporting to China; Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based shipment companies between January and October 2024 as part of the scheme. In one example detailed in the indictment, Lui submitted a purchase order for 27 servers containing Nvidia H100 GPUs at a cost of approximately $7.6 million; the servers went from Los Angeles to Kuala Lumpur and a co-conspirator emailed a Malaysian government official confirming they were transshipped to a buyer in China, per the Justice Department.\\n    Quote: Prosecutors said Lui and unnamed co-conspirators used Earthmade between 2023 and 2024 to buy export-controlled servers containing Nvidia GPUs and route them to China without the required U.S. Commerce Department licenses. To circumvent export controls, Lui and his co-conspirators submitted false documentation to U.S. manufacturers misrepresenting where the equipment would end up, then shipped the \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e901, e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e901 [fact; yahoo.com; original source US federal prosecutors (Justice Department) 2026-09-29; from gnews:a79a082545d70337856c230a 2026-10-02] US federal prosecutors arrested and charged Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, of San Gabriel, California, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc., on three criminal counts alleging he smuggled more than $300 million of export-controlled US-manufactured AI servers containing GPUs to China via Malaysia and Singapore using falsified end-user and destination paperwork. The indictment, returned by a federal grand jury on September 29, 2026, says the operation ran from 2023 through 2024 and alleges Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based companies tied to the shipments between January and October 2024 (an allegation, not a judicial finding), and that the servers were shipped without the U.S. Commerce Department export licences required.\\n    Quote: More than $300 million in export-controlled servers containing U.S.-manufactured GPUs allegedly left the United States for China, routed through Malaysia and Singapore on falsified paperwork. Federal prosecutors arrested Greg Lui , 38, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, of San Gabriel, California, on three criminal counts that carry up to 20 years in federal prison if convicted. … Lui owns Earthmade Comp\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e889; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e902 [fact; yahoo.com; original source Taiwanese prosecutors ; from gnews:a79a082545d70337856c230a 2026-10-02] In August 2026, Taiwanese prosecutors charged nine people in a separate alleged smuggling operation, including an Nvidia Taiwan employee and two Super Micro employees; the case involved 130 servers allegedly routed through Indonesia, Japan and Hong Kong toward Chinese customers, of which 74 were reportedly delivered.\\n    Quote: In August 2026, Taiwanese prosecutors charged nine people in a separate alleged smuggling operation, including an Nvidia Taiwan employee and two Super Micro employees. … That case involved 130 servers allegedly routed through Indonesia, Japan, and Hong Kong toward Chinese customers, with 74 reportedly delivered.\\n- e918 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source US government (export-control regime), as reported by Fool.com ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] Fool.com's 2 October 2026 article states that NVIDIA faces U.S. export controls that limit sales of its high-end chips in China, describing the restriction as a standing obstacle to its China business; the article names no specific chip, approval or date.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA faces significant geopolitical challenges, specifically U.S. export controls limiting its high-end chip sales in China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e148; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e919 [fact; TTNews (ttnews.com); original source U.S. federal prosecutors / Department of Justice, FBI and Bureau of Industry and Security 2026-10-01; from gnews:7a4e01c68a1faa3ffcac0d7c 2026-10-02] U.S. federal prosecutors arrested Greg Lui, owner of privately held Earthmade Computer, on 1 October 2026 and charged him with smuggling computer servers containing $300 million worth of Nvidia artificial intelligence chips to China in violation of U.S. export controls. Prosecutors alleged that Lui and unidentified co-conspirators worked from 2023 to 2024 to ship servers with restricted Nvidia chips to buyers in China without the required U.S. licenses, exporting them first to Malaysia and Singapore — jurisdictions that do not require licences for purchases of advanced AI chips from U.S. companies — and providing false documentation to U.S. manufacturers that misrepresented the final destination; they said the alleged conspiracy continued until at least mid-August. In one purchase order, prosecutors said, Lui obtained 27 servers from an unidentified U.S. manufacturer containing Nvidia H100 GPUs for about $7.6 million. Lui faces one count each of conspiracy to violate U.S. export controls, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering, and could face more than 20 years in prison if convicted; prosecutors sought detention without bail, citing flight risk. The charges were announced jointly by federal prosecutors, the FBI and the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security.\\n    Quote: Federal prosecutors arrested a California man Oct. 1 on charges of smuggling computer servers containing $300 million worth of Nvidia Corp. artificial intelligence chips to China in violation of U.S. export controls. … Authorities said that Greg Lui, the owner of privately held Earthmade Computer, worked with other unidentified co-conspirators from 2023 to 2024 to ship servers containing the restr\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e920 [fact; TTNews (ttnews.com); original source U.S. authorities (federal prosecutors); Nvidia for its own statement ; from gnews:7a4e01c68a1faa3ffcac0d7c 2026-10-02] The article reports that in March, U.S. authorities charged a co-founder of Super Micro Computer with illegally diverting billions of dollars in Nvidia chips to China; the co-founder pleaded not guilty. The item cites this as part of a series of federal prosecutions of individuals accused of violating export controls meant to keep advanced U.S. chips out of China, and reports Nvidia as saying its work with law enforcement has led to prosecutions.\\n    Quote: The charges against Lui mark the latest in a series of federal prosecutions of individuals accused of violating export controls aimed at keeping advanced U.S.-made chips out of Chinese hands over national security concerns. In March, authorities charged a co-founder of Super Micro Computer with illegally diverting billions of dollars in Nvidia chips to China. He pleaded not guilty. … “This case sh\\n- e925 [fact; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. authorities; three Supermicro employees including co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw ; from gnews:e15aee588a6b29a561565353 2026-10-02] U.S. authorities arrested three Supermicro employees, including co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw, who are suspected of illicitly shipping $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware to China, according to the article, which cites this as the major arrest so far in a recent U.S. crackdown on GPU smuggling. The smuggling conduct and the $2.5 billion figure are described as suspected rather than established.\\n    Quote: Even though GPUs that have the processing power of RTX 4090s or higher were first banned for export to China in November 2023, U.S. authorities only started seriously clamping down on smuggling operations recently. One major arrest, so far, is that of three Supermicro employees, including its co-founder, Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw, who are suspected of illicitly shipping $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia h\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e878; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e935 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (Central District of California) 2026-09-29; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] Federal prosecutors in the Central District of California indicted Greg Lui, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. of the City of Industry, California, charging him with smuggling more than $300 million of restricted computer hardware to China — high-end servers containing U.S.-manufactured Nvidia chips, including the Nvidia A100 and H100 — allegedly by declaring Malaysia and Singapore as destinations (which do not require a U.S. export licence) and then freight-forwarding the servers on to China. The indictment cites bank records and emails, including a purchase order to a U.S. manufacturer for 27 servers with Nvidia H100 chips for about $7.6 million, and says Earthmade received more than $176 million in 2024 from two Malaysia-based shipment companies as part of the scheme. Lui was arrested on Thursday and faces one count of conspiracy to violate export controls, one count of outbound smuggling and one count of conspiracy to commit money laundering; the conduct described is alleged, not proven.\\n    Quote: The owner of a San Gabriel Valley technology company was arrested Thursday after federal prosecutors charged him with smuggling more than $300 million of restricted computer hardware to China. … Greg Lui, the owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. in the City of Industry, allegedly sold high-end computer servers containing U.S.-manufactured Nvidia chips to the Chinese government, while claiming they wer\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e889; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e936 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source President Trump / U.S. government ; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] President Trump announced late last year (2025) that he authorized Nvidia to sell its H200 chip in China in exchange for the U.S. government taking 25% of the revenue, although exports remain heavily restricted, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Late last year, Trump announced that he authorized Nvidia to sell its H200 chip in China in exchange for taking 25% of the revenue, although exports remain heavily restricted.\\n- e937 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source U.S. government (export-control actions); Nvidia product response ; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] After the U.S. placed chip-export controls on China in 2022, Nvidia designed less-powerful versions of its chips to access the Chinese market while complying with the rules, but those reduced-capability models were later restricted too, according to the article.\\n    Quote: After the U.S. placed chip-export controls on China in 2022, Nvidia designed less-powerful versions to access the market while complying with the rules. But those reduced-capability models were later restricted too.\\n- e938 [fact; Bloomberg (reported by northeasttimes.com/Maya Brooks); original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors 2026-10-01; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] The U.S. Justice Department announced on Oct. 1, 2026 the arrest of Greg Lui, whom federal prosecutors charged with illegally shipping NVIDIA AI chips to China; prosecutors accuse him of smuggling servers holding $300 million worth of AI chips in violation of U.S. export laws.\\n    Quote: Federal prosecutors have charged a man with illegally shipping NVIDIA artificial intelligence chips to China, Bloomberg reported on Oct. 2. … The Justice Department announced the arrest of Greg Lui on Oct. 1. He is accused of smuggling servers holding $300 million worth of AI chips to China in violation of US export laws.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e966, e967, e996, e1004, e1084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e939 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (unnamed U.S. government officials) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] Unnamed U.S. government officials told Bloomberg in an Oct. 1 feature that NVIDIA is not doing enough to stop illegal exports of its AI chips to China, specifically saying it should have noticed that OBON lacked the 100 megawatts of data-center space needed to host the servers it supposedly bought, since Thailand has only a few hundred megawatts of AI data-center capacity running; the article says NVIDIA has never been accused of helping smugglers and that NVIDIA says it has followed the rules.\\n    Quote: Government officials told Bloomberg in a feature published Oct. 1 that NVIDIA is not doing enough to stop the trade. NVIDIA has never been accused of helping smugglers. It says it has followed the rules. … Officials told Bloomberg that NVIDIA should have noticed OBON lacked the 100 megawatts of data center space needed to host that many servers. Thailand has only a few hundred megawatts of AI data\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e880; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e940 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (unnamed sources / U.S. officials) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] Bloomberg reported, citing sources, that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers capable of housing 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors, and that U.S. officials now believe China has smuggled at least that many chips.\\n    Quote: Bloomberg also reported that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers able to house 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors. Its sources said US officials now believe China has smuggled at least that many chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e877; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e941 [rumour; Bloomberg (reported by northeasttimes.com/Maya Brooks); original source U.S. federal prosecutors (allegation against Yih-Shyan Liaw and collaborators) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] In one criminal case, prosecutors allege that Yih-Shyan Liaw, a co-founder of Super Micro, and collaborators sold about $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to Bangkok-based OBON, supposedly for AI infrastructure in Thailand, but the servers went directly to end users in China; the conspirators allegedly moved stickers from genuine NVIDIA products to dummy servers to evade compliance.\\n    Quote: The accused collaborators in one case include Yih-Shyan Liaw, a co-founder of Super Micro. They allegedly sold about $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to OBON, a Bangkok-based company that was supposedly building AI infrastructure in Thailand. … Instead, the servers went straight to end users in China. To get past compliance officers, the conspirators allegedly peeled stickers from genuine NVIDIA p\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e878; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e942 [fact; northeasttimes.com (Maya Brooks); original source Trump administration ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] The Trump administration last year agreed to allow sales of NVIDIA's previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Last year, the Trump administration agreed to allow sales of NVIDIA's previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e957 [fact; Bloomberg (article published by qz.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. (regulatory filings) ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese financing company controlled by local government entities, provided capital for Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of restricted Nvidia AI chips, per Beijing regulatory filings reviewed by Bloomberg. The filings show Semi-Tech Leasing funded more than 700 servers totaling more than 3 billion yuan ($440 million) in financing, with one contract covering 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers equipped with Nvidia's B300 chips (part of the Blackwell family); U.S. regulations prohibit selling B300 chips to China without an explicit license. Most of the servers, including the 32 Blackwell units, were listed as deployed inside a China Mobile Ltd. data center facility in Zhongwei, Ningxia, an officially designated national computing cluster zone.\\n    Quote: Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese financing company controlled by local government entities, provided capital for the purchase of restricted Nvidia $NVDA Corp. AI chips, according to regulatory filings reviewed by Bloomberg . … The documents, filed with Beijing regulators, show Semi-Tech Leasing funded purchases by Glory View Technology Co. of more than 700 servers totaling more than 3 billio\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e784; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e958 [fact; Bloomberg (article published by qz.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Semi-Tech Leasing later replaced its regulatory submissions with redacted copies, removing server model designations, specification details that had enabled identification of the Blackwell units, supplier names and installation locations; the process was completed by the end of June. Bloomberg had obtained and reviewed the unredacted filings earlier, in May and June, before the identifying details were removed.\\n    Quote: Semi-Tech Leasing later replaced its regulatory submissions with redacted copies, having removed the server model designations, specification details that had enabled identification of the Blackwell units, supplier names, and installation locations — a process completed by the end of June. Bloomberg had obtained and reviewed the unredacted filings earlier, in May and June, before the identifying d\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e788; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e959 [party_statement; qz.com (citing Nvidia's statement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Nvidia said it is \\\"looking into this report and will work with our OEM customer to investigate\\\" the financing and deployment in China of servers equipped with its B300 (Blackwell) chips, which U.S. rules bar selling to China without an explicit license.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said it is \\\"looking into this report and will work with our OEM customer to investigate.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e785; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e966 [fact; scmp.com; original source US Department of Justice 2026-10-01; from gnews:f261a85410fee1ff259dee8e 2026-10-02] The US Department of Justice announced that Greg Lui (also known as Yiu Kong Lui), 38, of San Gabriel, California, was arrested on Thursday 1 October 2026 and charged with conspiracy to violate US export controls, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering in connection with an alleged scheme to send restricted AI computer servers to China.\\n    Quote: Amid intensifying competition in the AI technology race, US authorities have arrested a California technology company owner accused of using Malaysia and Singapore as transit points to circumvent US export controls and send more than US$300 million worth of restricted computer servers to China. … Greg Lui, 38, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, of San Gabriel, was arrested on Thursday and charged with co\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e938; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e967 [rumour; scmp.com; original source US prosecutors (Department of Justice); John A. Eisenberg, assistant attorney general for national security ; from gnews:f261a85410fee1ff259dee8e 2026-10-02] US prosecutors allege that California technology-company owner Greg Lui routed restricted AI computer servers to China through Malaysia and Singapore, using false documents and transshipment schemes to conceal China as the true destination, and that more than US$300 million worth of restricted servers were sent to China in circumvention of US export controls; Assistant Attorney General for National Security John A. Eisenberg said the indictment details these alleged evasion efforts. The allegations are unconfirmed charges, not established findings.\\n    Quote: Prosecutors say California tech firm owner routed restricted AI servers through Malaysia and Singapore to evade US export controls … Amid intensifying competition in the AI technology race, US authorities have arrested a California technology company owner accused of using Malaysia and Singapore as transit points to circumvent US export controls and send more than US$300 million worth of restricte\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e938; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e996 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source US Department of Justice, Office of Public Affairs 2026-10-01; from gnews:9ed0bd3a9ad55db91108d32a 2026-10-03] The US Department of Justice announced on 1 October 2026 the arrest of Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, owner of City of Industry, California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., following a three-count indictment alleging a scheme to route more than $300 million of servers containing NVIDIA A100, H100, GeForce RTX 4090 and RTX 5090 GPUs to China. Prosecutors allege Lui and co-conspirators purchased restricted servers from American manufacturers using paperwork that falsely named permissible overseas customers as final recipients, and shipped them through Malaysia and Singapore before redirecting them to China without required US export licenses; the alleged conspiracy ran from around October 2023 until at least 12 August 2026. The charges (conspiracy to violate export controls, outbound smuggling, money-laundering conspiracy) carry separate maximum prison terms of up to 20, 10 and 20 years if convicted.\\n    Quote: A California company owner faces federal charges over an alleged scheme to route more than $300 million in NVIDIA GPU servers to China using false paperwork and shipments through Malaysia and Singapore. The indictment names A100, H100, RTX 4090, and RTX 5090 products; the charges carry separate maximum prison terms of up to 20, 10, and 20 years. … Greg Lui, 38, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, owns Ear\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e938; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1004 [fact; beinsure.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (Central District of California) 2026-09-29; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] A federal grand jury returned an indictment on Sept. 29, 2026, and Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. in City of Industry, California, was arrested Oct. 1, 2026, on federal charges alleging he helped smuggle more than $300 million of export-controlled computer servers — containing Nvidia A100 and H100 GPUs — to China in 2023–2024 without Commerce Department licences, presenting shipments as destined for Malaysia and Singapore. Prosecutors cited a January 2024 order for 27 servers worth about $7.6 million shipped from Los Angeles to Kuala Lumpur; a 92-server shipment routed through Singapore and Malaysia to Hong Kong and a company in Hangzhou; and a 100-server shipment of Nvidia H100 GPUs worth more than $22 million. They also allege Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based shipping companies between January and October 2024. The charges remain allegations.\\n    Quote: Greg Lui, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. in California’s City of Industry, has been arrested on federal charges alleging he helped smuggle more than $300 mn of export-controlled computer servers to China. … The 38-year-old San Gabriel resident, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, faces three counts: conspiracy to violate the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration Regulations, outbound smugg\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e938; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1005 [party_statement; beinsure.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] Nvidia said reported diversion of its products represents less than 0.5% of its products and argued unauthorized shipments account for a small share of China's available computing capacity; the company said it screens customers and has stopped shipments when final buyers could not be established. Nvidia also disputed suggestions that unusual order sizes or expansion plans are enough to establish wrongdoing, saying growing technology businesses in permitted markets regularly buy equipment before expanding data-center capacity.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said reported diversion represents less than 0.5% of its products and argued that unauthorized shipments account for a small share of China’s available computing capacity. … The company screens customers and has stopped shipments when final buyers couldn’t be established. … Nvidia has disputed suggestions that unusual order sizes or expansion plans are enough to establish wrongdoing. The co\\n- e1006 [third_party_view; beinsure.com; original source  ; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] U.S. officials and other investigators have examined whether manufacturers and suppliers should conduct deeper checks when large orders are sent to facilities in countries neighbouring China, according to the article; recent cases have included allegations involving data centres or buyers in Taiwan, Thailand, Malaysia and Singapore, and in some instances authorities later found inconsistencies between the stated destination, the size of the purchasing facility and the subsequent movement of the servers.\\n    Quote: U.S. officials and other investigators have nevertheless examined whether manufacturers and suppliers should conduct deeper checks when large orders are sent to facilities in countries neighboring China. … Recent cases have included allegations involving data centers or buyers in Taiwan, Thailand, Malaysia and Singapore. In some instances, authorities later found inconsistencies between the stated\\n- e1066 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA management guided to $108 billion of next-quarter revenue, plus or minus 2%, excluding China data-center compute sales, the article reports.\\n    Quote: The bullish case has direct support: management guided to $108 billion of next-quarter revenue, plus or minus 2%, excluding China data-center compute sales.\\n- e1084 [rumour; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source US authorities (federal prosecutors/investigators) ; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Authorities arrested Greg Lui, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, 38, of California, alleging he helped route more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers to China without required licenses; the servers reportedly contained GPUs made by Nvidia. Lui faces federal charges involving alleged violations of the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration Regulations, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering.\\n    Quote: Authorities arrested Greg Lui , also known as Yiu Kong Lui , alleging that the 38-year-old California resident helped route more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers to China without required licenses. The servers reportedly contained GPUs made by Nvidia Corp . Lui faces federal charges involving alleged violations of the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e938; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1139 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA's Hopper 200 (H200) products shipped to customers in China were less than 1% of its data center revenue in fiscal Q2 2027.\\n    Quote: The Hopper 200 products NVIDIA shipped to customers in China were less than 1% of its data center revenue in fiscal Q2 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e746; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1142 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said its revenue forecast includes no data center chip revenue from China.\\n    Quote: Management said its forecast includes no data center chip revenue from China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e378; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1156 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source U.S. government (export restrictions); characterization by The Motley Fool ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] The article states that Nvidia faces significant geopolitical risk from U.S. export restrictions on AI-capable semiconductors to China, which it says remain in place and create revenue volatility and inventory risk for the company. No new regulatory step, approval, licence or date is reported.\\n    Quote: Nvidia faces significant geopolitical risks, particularly from U.S. export restrictions on AI-capable semiconductors to China, which create revenue volatility and inventory risk.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is Nvidia’s Q3 FY2027 guidance of $108 billion, which excludes China Data Center compute revenue; that reference sits at rung 3, though it is a near-term baseline rather than a direct forecast through January 31, 2027. The evidence keeps the likely outcome at rung 3: U.S. restrictions on current-generation chips are reported as continuing, and Chinese restrictions on even U.S.-permitted H200 shipments show that U.S. permission alone would not satisfy rung 1. Reports of B300-equipped servers deployed in China do not establish authorized exports: the reporting says an explicit U.S. license is required, and Nvidia said it was investigating. The smuggling investigations and allegations further distinguish illicit arrival from shipments authorized by both\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell exports and no Chinese allowance for those shipments by January 31, 2027: current-generation chips remain restricted, and Chinese authorities have restricted even U.S.-permitted H200 shipments. Reports of B300 servers in China concern deployments for which U.S. regulations require an explicit license, not evidence that both governments have authorized shipments. No supplied evidence establishes a post-September 23, 2026 formal prohibition that stands through the due date, so the evidence does not cross into rung 2.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e88\",\n    \"e149\",\n    \"e80\",\n    \"e81\",\n    \"e148\",\n    \"e365\",\n    \"e428\",\n    \"e426\",\n    \"e437\",\n    \"e715\",\n    \"e745\",\n    \"e784\",\n    \"e785\",\n    \"e957\",\n    \"e959\",\n    \"e763\",\n    \"e383\",\n    \"e1156\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e80\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e81\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e82\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e86\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e88\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e111\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e148\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e149\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e150\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e151\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e152\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e153\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e154\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e155\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e226\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e302\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e308\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e365\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e377\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e382\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e383\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e398\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e401\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e426\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e428\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e435\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e436\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e437\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e440\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e443\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e715\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e745\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e746\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e747\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e763\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e780\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e784\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e785\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e786\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e787\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e788\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e802\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e803\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e850\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e851\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e876\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e877\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e878\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e879\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e880\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e884\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e885\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e886\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e887\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e888\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e889\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e901\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e902\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e918\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e919\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e920\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e925\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e935\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e936\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e937\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e938\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e939\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e940\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e941\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e942\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e957\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e958\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e959\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e966\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e967\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e996\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1004\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1005\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1006\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1066\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1084\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1139\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1142\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1156\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and its key supporting picture are unchanged: no evidence establishes dual official authorization, while the Blackwell deployment reports are explicitly not proof of authorized shipments and restrictions remain in force. The additional reporting and repeated accounts do not change that conclusion.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance of $108 billion, excluding China Data Center compute revenue. That reference sits at rung 3, Still open; it is a near-term revenue assumption, not a formal ruling on Blackwell authorization by January 31, 2027.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"By January 31, 2027, the most likely outcome is that Blackwell exports have not received verified U.S. authorization together with Chinese permission for Nvidia shipments. Existing restrictions and Chinese limits on H200 shipments support that forecast, but the evidence does not show a qualifying post-September 23 formal prohibition, leaving the outcome at rung 3.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 4,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v11 (semiconductor-cycle, theme, focus \\\"The change in average DRAM/HBM contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, per TrendForce/DRAMeXchange data.\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?\\nLadder: rung 1 below 0 percent: below 0 percent / rung 2 from 0 percent to below 10 percent: from 0 percent to below 10 percent / rung 3 from 10 percent to below 20 percent: from 10 percent to below 20 percent (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 percent to below 30 percent: from 20 percent to below 30 percent / rung 5 30 percent or more: 30 percent or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 61 items]\\n- e881 [party_statement; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr), quoting an unnamed Nvidia spokesperson; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] An Nvidia spokesperson told Gizmodo that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, and said Nvidia has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model to focus production on the Shield TV Pro.\\n    Quote: “The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry. We’ve also stopped producing the standard Shield TV model as we focus on production for Shield TV Pro.”\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e883 [fact; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr); original source Nvidia add-in-card (AIC) makers, per Gizmodo's reported market prices ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] Nvidia's RTX 50-series GPUs sold by all major add-in-card makers now cost hundreds or even thousands of dollars more than at launch, the article reports; a GeForce RTX 5090 that launched at $2,000 in 2025 now sells for well over $7,000, or close to $9,000 from some makers.\\n    Quote: The company’s RTX 50-series GPUs across all major AIC (add-in card) makers now cost hundreds or even thousands of dollars more than they did at launch. A GeForce RTX 5090, which launched for $2,000 in 2025, now costs well over $7,000 or close to $9,000 by some makers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e353; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e238 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's gross margin is guided to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lowered because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly helped by price increases the company has already executed.\\n    Quote: Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e288; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e293 [party_statement; Zacks; original source Micron Technology 2026-06-24; from gnews:90ebbd46135613dc0cb8830f 2026-09-25] Micron guided fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue to around $50 billion and a gross margin of approximately 86%, citing strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\\n    Quote: Looking ahead, Micron expects revenues to rise to around $50 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026. The company also projects a healthy gross margin of approximately 86%, supported by strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\\n- e355 [fact; Engadget (Husain Parvez); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d9c36b37c17bf3bafb47e561 2026-09-26] NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition desktop GPU is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched. Engadget (Husain Parvez) reports that NVIDIA has not disclosed why it raised the price, and that the increase comes amid sharply rising memory costs and broader GPU price hikes. The article frames the card as a professional/workstation product aimed at AI developers, engineers and 3D artists rather than gamers.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched. … NVIDIA has not disclosed why it raised the price, although the increase comes amid sharply rising memory costs and broader GPU price hikes.\\n- e368 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron is actively expanding its memory-chip production footprint to supply clients with more memory chips, and the article reports that this production capacity will not be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\\n    Quote: While Micron could sit around and enjoy the elevated prices, it's actively expanding its production footprint to provide clients with more memory chips. That production capacity won't be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\\n- e369 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron does not expect the \\\"tightness\\\" in the memory-chip market to alleviate until 2028, per The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury; memory chips are described as a component NVIDIA's and Broadcom's computing units cannot run without.\\n    Quote: Micron also doesn't expect the \\\"tightness\\\" in the memory chip space to alleviate until 2028\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e987; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e533 [third_party_view; Jacqueline Thomas (IGN); original source IGN 2026-09-28; from gnews:240dc4dbbafffa84b05b213d 2026-09-28] IGN reported, in its own account accompanying a story about stolen Nvidia-branded trailers, that RAM prices have continued to climb since the start of 2026 and that Nvidia graphics cards specifically have had massive price jumps during the year. The article gives no percentage, dollar figure or source for the memory or graphics-card price levels, and frames it as part of an ongoing RAM crisis pushing hardware prices higher.\\n    Quote: These thefts probably aren't helping with the cost of hardware. RAM prices have continued to climb since the start of 2026, and Nvidia graphics cards specifically have had massive price jumps this year. … It's no secret that GPUs are worth their weight in gold right now, with the ongoing RAM crisis shooting prices through the stratosphere.\\n- e793 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:5f9ed396852a9b7ad63bc5f3 2026-10-01] Michael Burry noted that older Nvidia chip architectures such as the A100 and H100 have seen stabilised or rising rental rates in recent months, but attributed the trend to acute memory and power supply-chain bottlenecks rather than the silicon's intrinsic durability, writing that 'a chip earning more than it costs is a sign of scarcity due to memory and power shortages, not GPU die shortages and not durability.'\\n    Quote: While older chip architectures like the A100 and H100 have seen stabilized or rising rental rates in recent months, Burry points out that this trend stems from acute supply-chain bottlenecks rather than the silicon's intrinsic durability. … \\\"A chip earning more than it costs is a sign of scarcity due to memory and power shortages, not GPU die shortages and not durability,\\\" Burry writes.\\n- e841 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:0f88bce495310b4588d29700 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced that the Shield TV Pro streaming device will be priced at $299.99, a $100 increase from its original $199.99, and that it has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model, focusing production on the Pro variant instead. NVIDIA attributed the increase to substantially higher component costs, including DRAM, across the industry; pricing takes effect October 2 and the Pro's specifications are unchanged.\\n    Quote: Today, NVIDIA is announcing that the Shield TV Pro will now be priced at $299.99, a $100 US price bump versus its original $199.99 price. NVIDIA says that the cost of components, including DRAM, has increased substantially across the industry, and that is why the Shield device will now feature a higher price. … Additionally, NVIDIA has also announced that it will cease the production of the standa\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e881, e882, e921, e922, e943, e946, e969, e1021, e1023, e1026, e1132, e1133; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e842 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] NVIDIA is releasing a new 64 GB unified-memory configuration of its DGX Spark AI supercomputer at a $4,999 price point, keeping the rest of the 128 GB model's specifications, and the unit will be available from 23 October 2026 through its manufacturing partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI. According to the report, the 64 GB variant replaces the 128 GB DGX Spark at the $4,999 price point, with the 128 GB model moving to even higher prices because of persistent increases in memory and other component costs.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is launching a new version of its DGX Spark AI Supercomputer which packs half the memory, but is priced the same as the 128 GB model. … Due to persistent hikes across memory and other essential components, NVIDIA is unable to sustain those prices, and while the original 128 GB model will see even higher prices, NVIDIA is releasing a new variant of the DGX Spark that replaces the 128 GB mode\\n- e843 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] The 128 GB NVIDIA DGX Spark, which launched at $3,999 and was listed at $4,699 and then $4,999 in the weeks before the report, is now selling for more than $6,000, and wccftech reports the 128 GB model will rise further because persistent increases in memory and other essential components mean NVIDIA cannot sustain the earlier prices.\\n    Quote: Although the Spark launched at $3999, it went through a range of price bumps, with the most significant being the $4699 and $4999 prices, which were listed a few weeks back. … Due to persistent hikes across memory and other essential components, NVIDIA is unable to sustain those prices, and while the original 128 GB model will see even higher prices, NVIDIA is releasing a new variant of the DGX Sp\\n- e853 [party_statement; Insider Monkey (published via Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA Corporation (management) ; from gnews:a10cd465475cc83dc7c5e184 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\\n    Quote: While management's gross margin outlook remains elevated at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, maintaining these historic profitability levels requires navigating escalating high-bandwidth memory costs and intensifying research and development expenditures for next-generation systems.\\n- e896 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia introduced a cut-down DGX Spark with half the memory and storage of the existing model — a 64 GB GB10-based system versus the 128 GB version — to be sold exclusively through hardware partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI, expected to retail at around $4,999. The article presents the cheaper, lower-capacity configuration as a response to the memory shortage.\\n    Quote: In an effort to provide a more affordable AI system, Nvidia is introducing a cut down version of the DGX Spark with half the memory and storage. The GB10-based systems will be offered exclusively through hardware partners including Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP, and MSI, and are expected to retail for around $4,999. … While the 64 GB version’s lower MSRP should make the systems more accessible am\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e899, e903, e985; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e897 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia raised the price of its 128 GB DGX Spark to $6,950, an increase of nearly 75 percent from this time last year; the article attributes the increase to skyrocketing memory prices. The article adds that the new 64 GB model's $4,999 price is still 25 percent above what the 128 GB version retailed for at launch.\\n    Quote: The less-powerful SKUs are necessary after Nvidia jacked the price of its 128 GB DGX Spark on Friday to $6,950 — an increase of nearly 75 percent from this time last year. As you might have already guessed, skyrocketing memory prices are to blame for the massive price adjustment. … That makes the 64 GB model considerably less than Nvidia’s top-spec DGX Spark and GB10 systems given the ongoing memo\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1025, e1189; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e843 (); both are in force: The 128 GB NVIDIA DGX Spark, which launched at $3,999 and was listed at $4,699 and then $4,999 in the weeks before the report, is now selling for more than $6,000, and wccftech reports the 128 GB model will rise further because persistent increases in memory and other essential components mean NVIDIA cannot sustain the earlier prices.\\n- e900 [third_party_view; Jeffrey Kampman, Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware 2026-10-02; from gnews:d77fe74e308e447bc64c8895 2026-10-02] Tom's Hardware reported that skyrocketing RAM prices make a chip permanently paired with too much costly LPDDR5X a barrier to entry rather than an asset, that 128GB GB10 systems, when in stock, currently cost roughly $7,000 to $9,000 each — far above even Nvidia's adjusted MSRP — and that with memory and storage prices ever-shifting, the new 64GB GB10 systems' $4,999 starting price may not hold for long.\\n    Quote: And skyrocketing RAM prices mean that a chip that’s permanently paired with too much costly LPDDR5X is more of a barrier to entry than an asset. … Given the ever-shifting prices of memory and storage right now, they might not stay there for long. Assuming you can find a 128GB GB10 system in stock, you can expect to pay roughly $7000 to $9000 for one right now, far above even Nvidia's adjusted MSRP\\n- e903 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system, halving the unified memory of the original 128GB system, to be sold through Acer, ASUS, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI from 23 October 2026 at prices starting at $4,999. That starting price is $1,000 (25%) above the original 128GB Founders Edition's $3,999 MSRP and $300 above NVIDIA's current $4,699 Founders Edition price for the 128GB model, though the 64GB configuration is not directly equivalent because NVIDIA says it will be sold exclusively through manufacturer partners who set their own configurations and regional pricing.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has announced a new 64GB version of DGX Spark , cutting the unified memory capacity of the original system in half. The new configuration will be available from Acer, ASUS, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI starting October 23 , with prices starting at $4,999 . … NVIDIA’s 128GB Founders Edition previously carried a $3,999 MSRP , meaning the new 64GB systems start $1,000 higher, or 25% above the or\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e896; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e904 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA raised the MSRP of its 128GB DGX Spark Founders Edition to $4,699 in February 2026, citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA raised the 128GB Founders Edition MSRP to $4,699 in February 2026 , citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\\n- e917 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] SK Hynix, a supplier of high-bandwidth memory to NVIDIA, reported revenue of $71.5 billion for the fiscal year ended December 31, 2025, year-over-year growth of 46.8%, net income of $31.6 billion and a 44.2% net margin, which the article calls a significant improvement in profitability versus prior periods.\\n    Quote: In the fiscal year ended Dec. 31, 2025, revenue reached $71.5 billion, representing year-over-year growth of 46.8%. Net income for the period was $31.6 billion, yielding a net margin of 44.2%. This represents a significant improvement in profitability compared with prior periods.\\n- e921 [fact; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (citing WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the remaining Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429, before tax) — a $100 increase — according to the article, which cites WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement.\\n    Quote: One of our favorite streaming gadgets, the standard Nvidia Shield TV , is no more. … According to WCCFTech , the Pro is increasing in price by 50% from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429). … The list price is now $299 (about £225 / AU$429 before tax)\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e922 [party_statement; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (quoting Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] In its announcement discontinuing the base Shield TV and raising the Shield TV Pro price, Nvidia said the reason is that \\\"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\\\" — a company statement attributing the change to industry-wide component and memory cost inflation.\\n    Quote: The reason, and the reason for discontinuing the base model, is AI: As Nvidia says in its announcement, \\\"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\\\".\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e923 [third_party_view; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar; original source TechRadar (Carrie Marshall) ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] TechRadar's Carrie Marshall wrote that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \\\"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\\\" — a journalist forecast that the component/memory cost squeeze extends beyond 2026.\\n    Quote: This is likely to be the last Shield TV Pro model for some time, because all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least, and Nvidia can already reach gamers on multiple platforms with its GeForce Now streaming service, which is available on all the major desktop and mobile platforms as well as smart TVs from the likes of LG and Samsung .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e944; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e931 [fact; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron Technology reported quarterly earnings per share of $33.42, beating Wall Street's already high expectations by 5.73%, and said the quarter delivered record profits plus triple-digit revenue and earnings growth, which Zacks attributes to soaring AI demand. Zacks reported the results on the Wednesday before its 2 October 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Wednesday, Micron , the AI memory leader, crushed Wall Street's earnings expectations on every metric. Once again, the quarter delivered record profits and triple-digit revenue and earnings growth. … Micron reported earnings per share of $33.42, beating Wall Street's already lofty expectations by 5.73%.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1083, e1163; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e932 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra (Micron CEO), reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron's guidance for its fiscal 2027 first quarter did not disappoint, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said fiscal 2027 will be even stronger than the record quarter just reported, as reported by Zacks on 2 October 2026.\\n    Quote: In addition to the blockbuster quarter, Micron's Q1 2027 guidance did not disappoint, with CEO Sanjay Mehrotra stating that fiscal 2027 will be even stronger.\\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\\n    Quote: According to management, Micron has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements, covering more than 35% of expected revenue through 2030. Customer financial commitments have soared to $32 billion from $22 billion last quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\\n    Quote: Last night, Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains, saying: … \\\"We do not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1027; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e108 (); both are in force: The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\\n- e943 [fact; tech.yahoo.com (citing WCCFTech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429 before tax), a $100 increase. The article attributes both moves to AI-driven component costs, quoting Nvidia's announcement: \\\"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\\\".\\n    Quote: The Shield TV Pro will stick around, but is now $100 more expensive … One of our favorite streaming gadgets, the standard Nvidia Shield TV , is no more. … According to WCCFTech , the Pro is increasing in price by 50% from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429). The reason, and the reason for discontinuing the base model, is AI: As Nvidia says in its announcement, \\\"the cost of components, including mem\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e944 [third_party_view; tech.yahoo.com; original source tech.yahoo.com ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] The tech.yahoo.com article's assessment is that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \\\"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\\\"; it cites Nvidia's GeForce Now streaming service, available on major desktop, mobile and smart-TV platforms including LG and Samsung TVs, as an alternative route to gamers. The forecast carries no figure or named source.\\n    Quote: This is likely to be the last Shield TV Pro model for some time, because all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least, and Nvidia can already reach gamers on multiple platforms with its GeForce Now streaming service, which is available on all the major desktop and mobile platforms as well as smart TVs from the likes of LG and Samsung .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e923; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e946 [fact; Craig Donaldson, Pocket-lint (citing Wccftech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bdf9837d06e742f9f03a27a8 2026-10-02] Nvidia announced it is discontinuing its standard Shield TV and, effective October 2, 2026, raising the price of the Shield TV Pro by $100, from $200 to $299, attributing the increase to rising component costs amid an AI-driven global memory shortage.\\n    Quote: In a recent statement, Nvidia confirmed that it's discontinuing its standard Shield TV while simultaneously raising the price of the Shield TV Pro by $100, taking the price from $200 to $300 (via Wccftech ). … Nvidia says the main reason for the price increase is the rising cost of components, driven by the ongoing global memory shortage caused by AI demand, which Nvidia has played a part in.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e968 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the price of its 2019 SHIELD TV Pro media streamer by 50% to $299.99, up from its $199.99 launch MSRP, with no hardware upgrade, and its own online store now lists the device at the new price. NVIDIA attributed the increase to rising industry-wide component costs, specifically citing memory as one of the affected components.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has bumped the price of its 2019 SHIELD TV Pro media streamer by 50%, now retailing for $299.99, up from its launch MSRP of $199. … NVIDIA's own online store now lists the device for $299.99, confirming the new price. … NVIDIA has attributed the price increase to rising industry-wide component costs, specifically citing memory as one of the affected components.\\n- e970 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source Roku and Google ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] Roku raised prices on several of its streaming devices and Google raised the price of the Google TV Streamer from $99.99 to $149.99, both citing higher memory and component costs, part of a broader trend of rising streaming-device prices.\\n    Quote: The price increase also aligns with a broader trend of rising streaming-device costs. Roku has raised prices on several devices, citing higher memory and component costs, and Google recently raised the Google TV Streamer from $99.99 to $149.99 for the same reason.\\n- e987 [party_statement; tweaktown.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:947ae81b89f5813da44a3364 2026-10-03] Memory supplier Micron said it has no line of sight to when memory supply and demand will return to balance and expects the memory crunch to persist through 2027 and 2028.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Micron has said it has no line of sight to when memory supply and demand will return to balance, and expects the crunch to persist through 2027 and 2028 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e369; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e997 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the US retail price of its Shield TV Pro streaming device from $199.99 to $299.99, effective October 2, 2026, roughly 50% above the $199 launch price of the same Tegra X1+ hardware introduced in October 2019; the company attributed the increase to rising component costs, including memory.\\n    Quote: Starting October 2, SHIELD Pro will be priced at $299. The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry. … The change took effect on October 2, with the Shield TV Pro moving from $199.99 to $299.99 at retail. … The change took effect on October 2, with the Shield TV Pro moving from $199.99 to $299.99 at retail.\\nThe price change also leaves the Pro as the re\\n- e998 [party_statement; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA stated that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, giving that as its reason for raising the Shield TV Pro's price; the claim is the company's characterisation of industry-wide input-cost inflation and carries no figure.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA attributed the increase to rising component costs, including memory, which the company says have increased substantially across the industry. … The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry.\\n- e1003 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source fool.com ; from gnews:284a01485767b622f994fe39 2026-10-03] The article states that the memory chip space is in a serious supply crunch and that chip prices are skyrocketing as a result, which it gives as the reason Micron is currently producing stronger growth than Taiwan Semiconductor.\\n    Quote: Right now, Micron is producing stronger growth than Taiwan Semiconductor. That's because the memory chip space is in a serious supply crunch, and chip prices are skyrocketing as a result.\\n- e1012 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source finance.biggo.com 2026-10-03; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] The article reports that rising memory prices for AI servers were the primary driver of Amazon's upward capital-expenditure revision.\\n    Quote: The primary driver of the upward revision is rising memory prices for AI servers.\\n- e1019 [fact; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Nvidia and partners including Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI will, from 23 October 2026, deliver a new entry-level version of the DGX Spark mini-PC that keeps the GB10 Blackwell GPU but halves RAM and SSD: 64 GByte of LPDDR5X memory at US$5,000, versus the smallest 128-GByte version that reached the market a few weeks earlier for around US$4,000. The 128-GByte DGX Spark model now costs US$6,000, 50 percent more than a year ago. The item notes Nvidia does not this time name the tight memory market as the reason, though the author says it is still obvious, and that individual manufacturers will likely set their own prices, with US$5,000 a guideline.\\n    Quote: Starting October 23, Nvidia and its partners like Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP, and MSI will deliver a new version of the DGX Spark series of mini-PCs. It still uses the GB10 chip with the Blackwell architecture for its GPU and is designed for running AI models locally. The catch: Compared to the model introduced about a year ago, the RAM and SSD are halved, but the price is higher. … The first \\n- e1020 [third_party_view; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia (product and memory supply); price observation by heise.de ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Heise reports that Nvidia's fastest RTX card, the RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell with 96 GByte, is constantly increasing in price and is currently no longer available for less than 17,000 Euros; the article notes that GDDR7 memory for RTX cards, like LPDDR5 for the DGX Spark, was apparently not covered by the long-term supplies Nvidia said at the beginning of 2026 it had secured.\\n    Quote: However, LPDDR5, like GDDR7 for RTX cards, was apparently not included. Even the fastest RTX card, the RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell with 96 GByte , is constantly increasing in price and is currently no longer available for less than a staggering 17,000 Euros .\\n- e1022 [party_statement; An unnamed Nvidia spokesperson, quoted by Ars Technica (reported by Yahoo Tech); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d823b7f949cb0596bcd02e4b 2026-10-03] An NVIDIA spokesperson told Ars Technica that from October 2 the SHIELD Pro would be priced at $299, and stated that the cost of components, including memory, \\\"has increased substantially across the industry\\\" — a company claim about industry-wide input cost inflation used to explain the device's price increase.\\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson told Ars Technica , \\\"Starting October 2, SHIELD Pro will be priced at $299. The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry.\\\"\\n- e1024 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia said it will begin shipping a derivative of its DGX Spark personal AI workstation, with unified memory halved to 64GB, from 23 October 2026 at a recommended price starting at $4,999 — $1,000 above the $3,999 launch price of the 128GB model a year earlier; the product will not be sold directly by Nvidia but distributed exclusively through six partner manufacturers (Acer, Asus, Dell Technologies, Gigabyte, HP, MSI). Halving memory cuts the largest model that can run on a single device from 200 billion to 100 billion parameters, and the item says the memory reduction is widely interpreted as a choice to lower bill-of-materials costs.\\n    Quote: Nvidia will begin shipping a derivative model of its personal AI workstation, the DGX Spark, with unified memory cut in half to 64GB starting October 23. The recommended price starts at $4,999 (approximately 6.7 million won). That's a full $1,000 (approximately 1.3 million won) more than the $3,999 (approximately 5.4 million won) launch price of the 128GB model a year ago. … The new product will n\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1188; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1025 [fact; The Register (cited by finance.biggo.com); original source Nvidia 2026-10-02; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia raised the recommended price of its 128GB DGX Spark again on October 2, to $6,950 — a 74% increase from the model's launch price a year earlier — as reported by US tech outlet The Register and cited in the item.\\n    Quote: According to US tech outlet The Register, Nvidia raised the recommended price of the 128GB model again on October 2, to $6,950. That's a 74% increase from the launch price a year ago, and the 64GB model's $4,999 price point is still 25% higher than the 128GB model's original launch price.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e897; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e904 (); both are in force: NVIDIA raised the MSRP of its 128GB DGX Spark Founders Edition to $4,699 in February 2026, citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\\n- e1026 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced it would raise the price of its SHIELD TV Pro streaming set-top box from $199.99 to $299.99 — a $100 increase — with no hardware specification changes, attributed solely to rising component costs including DRAM; Nvidia also said it will discontinue the standard SHIELD TV and focus on the Pro model.\\n    Quote: On the same day, Nvidia announced it would raise the price of its SHIELD TV Pro streaming set-top box from $199.99 to $299.99 — a $100 (approximately 130,000 won) increase. The adjustment comes with no hardware specification changes, attributed solely to rising component costs including DRAM. Nvidia also said it will discontinue the standard SHIELD TV and focus on the Pro model.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \\\"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\\\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\\n    Quote: Major memory suppliers including Micron Technology have warned that as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained. These suppliers project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing prospects for early normalization. … Micron CEO San\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e934; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1028 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported that its data center segment gross margin reached 90% in its most recent fiscal fourth quarter, more than doubling from 41% a year earlier — a result the item ties to the memory shortage.\\n    Quote: Micron's data center segment gross margin reached 90% in its most recent fiscal fourth quarter, more than doubling from 41% a year earlier.\\n- e1030 [fact; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported fiscal Q4 results that exceeded earnings expectations, with a significant increase in data center revenues, and warned of a continuing memory chip shortage.\\n    Quote: Micron Technology MU exceeded Q4 earnings expectations with a significant increase in data center revenues, while warning of a continuing memory chip shortage.\\n- e1031 [party_statement; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology said it expects its growth to continue despite industry challenges affecting memory chip supplies, following its strong fiscal Q4 earnings.\\n    Quote: Following strong earnings, Micron Technology (MU) expects growth to continue despite industry challenges affecting memory chip supplies.\\n- e1074 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia (CFO) ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia's CFO flagged 'extreme pricing conditions in memory' as a margin headwind.\\n    Quote: Nvidia also disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement, while its CFO flagged \\\"extreme pricing conditions in memory\\\" as a margin headwind.\\n- e1083 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Micron Technology 2026-09-30; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Micron Technology reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 results after the Wednesday closing bell, posting revenue of $54.23 billion, above Benzinga Pro's analyst estimate of $50.75 billion, and adjusted earnings of $33.42 per share, above the $31.45-per-share estimate.\\n    Quote: Micron Technology reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 results after Wednesday’s closing bell, posting revenue of $54.23 billion. Revenue surpassed Benzinga Pro’s analyst estimate of $50.75 billion. The semiconductor company also reported adjusted earnings of $33.42 per share, exceeding the $31.45-per-share estimate.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e931; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e293 (2026-06-24); both are in force: Micron guided fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue to around $50 billion and a gross margin of approximately 86%, citing strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\\n- e1107 [third_party_view; Benzinga; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley said that delayed data-center deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley said delayed deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\\n- e1108 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed.\\n    Quote: Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed\\n- e1123 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said it expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in the fourth quarter of fiscal 2027 because memory costs are rising.\\n    Quote: Margins are expected to bottom at 71% to 72% in the fourth quarter because memory costs are rising.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e853 (); both are in force: NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\\n- e1132 [fact; The Verge (citing Ars Technica); original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:f674aa00a3b8657dbc4bb737 2026-10-05] NVIDIA raised the price of its Shield TV Pro streaming box by $100, from $199.99 to $299.99, starting on October 2nd, attributing the increase to higher component costs amid the industry-wide memory shortage, according to Ars Technica. The article frames the move as the memory shortage passing through into NVIDIA's own consumer hardware pricing.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s Shield TV streaming boxes are the latest victims of the memory shortage that has seen prices increase dramatically for everything from digital cameras to game consoles . When the Shield TV Pro launched nearly seven years ago with an upgraded Tegra X1+ processor, 3GB of RAM, and 16GB of storage, it sold for $199.99. But starting on October 2nd Nvidia increased its price by $100 to $299.99 \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1150 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source Micron Technology (with unnamed forecasters) ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] MarketBeat reported that current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron Technology indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\\n    Quote: Current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1190; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1151 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com (article); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:4cf776a64aef8ef7dde73e49 2026-10-05] Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in June 2026 that DRAM and NAND industry demand continues to significantly exceed industry supply, and that he expects tight memory-market conditions to persist beyond calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in June that \\\"DRAM and NAND industry demand continues to significantly exceed industry supply\\\" and that he expects \\\"tight conditions to persist beyond calendar 2027.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e1150 (); both are in force: MarketBeat reported that current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron Technology indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\\n- e1163 [fact; Zacks Equity Research (Zacks Analyst Blog); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:4813fe2f3bcaef0438d6271c 2026-10-05] Micron Technology reported (in a release the item refers to only as dated 'Yesterday', in an article published 5 October 2026) fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue that surged 379.3% year over year, beating the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 6.33%, with EPS of $33.42 topping the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 5.73%; Zacks attributed the results to strong demand for memory products tied to AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Yesterday, the company reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue surge of 379.3% year over year, beating the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 6.33%, while EPS of $33.42 topped the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 5.73%. The results reflect strong demand for memory products tied to AI infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e931; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1169 [fact; fool.com (Bram Berkowitz); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:af32e2b77f410d23029454d7 2026-10-05] Memory maker Micron Technology, a supplier of DRAM and NAND used with GPUs, recently reported quarterly earnings that again beat analyst estimates and guided its current quarter above expectations, according to fool.com, in a year the article describes as supply-constrained for memory.\\n    Quote: Micron recently reported earnings that once again blew past analyst estimates, and it also provided guidance for the current quarter, which came in higher than expected.\\n- e1173 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA guided gross margin down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027 to 74% expected in fiscal Q3 2027, and said it expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027, blaming memory prices that rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA does not expect its gross margin to hold. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and management expects 74% for Q3. Management then expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in Q4, and it blamed the price of memory, which rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1188 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia is launching a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system at a starting price of $4,999 when it goes on sale on 23 October 2026 — $1,000 more than the original 128GB model's $3,999 launch price, even though it offers half as much memory. The lower-memory unit keeps the same GB10 Grace Blackwell Superchip and AI software stack and supports models of up to 100 billion parameters, versus up to 200 billion for the 128GB system.\\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA ) is launching a lower-memory version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system at a higher price than the original model cost at launch, underscoring how persistent memory shortages are pushing up the cost of AI hardware. … The new 64GB DGX Spark will start at $4,999 when it goes on sale October 23. … That is $1,000 more than the original 128GB models $3,999 launch price , despite offering\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1024; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1189 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source  ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] The original 128GB DGX Spark now costs about $6,950, roughly 75% above its debut price, and partner versions have reportedly been selling for $5,999 to $9,000 depending on storage and configuration, according to the report cited by the article (which uses 'reportedly').\\n    Quote: The pricing contrast becomes even more striking when looking at the original model today. … According to the report, the 128GB DGX Spark now costs about $6,950 , roughly 75% above its debut price . Partner versions have reportedly been selling anywhere from $5,999 to $9,000 , depending on storage and configuration.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e897; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1190 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Memory supplier Micron has warned that memory shortages could worsen through 2027 and 2028, with no clear timeline for supply and demand to return to balance, as cited by the article.\\n    Quote: Micron has warned that memory shortages could worsen through 2027 and 2028 , with no clear timeline for supply and demand to return to balance.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1150; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1191 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia has attributed the rising prices of its systems to industry-wide memory supply constraints, which the article says may not ease soon.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has already blamed rising prices on industry-wide memory supply constraints, and those pressures may not ease soon.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no market consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The most likely outcome remains 30% or more: Micron’s latest results exceeded its prior revenue guide, customer commitments rose, and management says the DRAM imbalance persists with no line of sight to balance and demand exceeding supply in 2027 and 2028. Those facts support sustained contract-price strength, while capacity additions described as not coming online until mid-2027 or later offer little relief within calendar 2026. They do not translate directly into an average contract-price percentage, so the 30%+ forecast is a directional inference, not a reported or precisely calculable figure; devi\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 5,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028, while more than 75% of its 2027 output is committed. Micron's strong results and explicit reports of extreme memory pricing reinforce the direction. Together these make a 30%+ annual average contract-price increase the most likely rung, though the evidence does not provide a direct contract-price series or a precise percentage. Consumer-product price rises and memory-cost comments are supporting context, not measurements of DRAM/HBM contract prices; the deployment-delay risk is a counterweight.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e934\",\n    \"e1027\",\n    \"e1108\",\n    \"e933\",\n    \"e368\",\n    \"e369\",\n    \"e1074\",\n    \"e1173\",\n    \"e293\",\n    \"e1107\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e881\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e883\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e238\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e293\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e355\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e368\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e369\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e533\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e793\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e841\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e842\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e843\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e853\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e896\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e897\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e900\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e903\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e904\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e917\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e921\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e922\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e923\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e931\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e932\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e933\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e934\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e943\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e944\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e946\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e968\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e970\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e987\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e997\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e998\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1003\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1012\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1019\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1020\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1022\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1024\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1025\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1026\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1027\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1028\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1030\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1031\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1074\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1083\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1107\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1108\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1123\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1132\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1150\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1151\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1163\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1169\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1173\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1188\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1189\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1190\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1191\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5, but the supporting facts are now more specific: the evidence adds Micron's stated 2027–2028 demand-supply outlook, its quantified customer commitments and committed 2027 output, and further reported pricing pressure. These update the supporting facts and figures without supplying a direct full-year contract-price figure.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"No market consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price figure is supplied. The opening no-reference position is rung 3: 10% to below 20%.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"At least 30% (rung 5); the evidence supports a 30%+ forecast through persistent, multi-year DRAM supply tightness and reported extreme memory pricing, but does not permit a reliable point estimate for the calendar-year average contract-price change.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 5,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 5,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 15,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 15,000,000,000 USD to below 30,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 30,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 50,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 33 items]\\n[Not shown: 12 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e60, e274; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\\n- e39 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA had already invested $1.5 billion in SB Energy and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the developer's IPO.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) has already invested $1.5 billion and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the IPO.\\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61, e143; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e93 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing for AI cloud provider Nscale, the IPO candidate whose filing the article discusses; the article notes this gives NVIDIA direct capital exposure in addition to its role as hardware supplier.\\n    Quote: Yet NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing, while Microsoft Corporation (NASDAQ: MSFT ) is expected to become one of Nscale's most important customers. … Nvidia can still benefit from Nscale's expansion by selling the picks and shovels, although its $1 billion financing commitment gives it direct capital exposure as well.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136, e141; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e135 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); Jensen Huang quoted; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers, and CEO Jensen Huang acknowledged the scale of that support while saying the company does not see it as 'circular financing'.\\n    Quote: The louder concern is circular financing. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers. CEO Jensen Huang addressed the skepticism directly: \\\"We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\\n- e328 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia is in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering, according to Reuters, and an unnamed source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion; the article describes it as a possible second $10 billion check.\\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) might be gearing up to write Anthropic a second $10 billion check.\\nThe chipmaker's in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering (IPO) , Reuters said this month, a deal that could deepen its ties to a major customer. A source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion.\\n- e329 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic is reportedly seeking to raise as much as $100 billion in an IPO that might value it at roughly $2 trillion, and Reuters has since said the listing may slip past the U.S. midterm elections in November; the article says the plans may change and neither company has confirmed the talks.\\n    Quote: The artificial intelligence (AI) company behind the Claude models is reportedly looking to raise as much as $100 billion in a listing that might value it at roughly $2 trillion. Reuters has since said that the IPO may slip past the U.S. midterm elections in November. Of course, the plans might still change, and neither company has confirmed the talks.\\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e372 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] CoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, which the article describes as another large financing round for an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity and as evidence that GPU expansion is translating into balance-sheet pressure for the AI cloud operator.\\n    Quote: CoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, adding another large financing round to an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity. CoreWeave’s latest convertible financing showed how quickly GPU expansion is translating into balance-sheet pressure.\\n- e373 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nebius (as reported by cryptorank.io) ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] The article reports that Nebius has also turned to billions in financing and that the broader AI infrastructure boom is increasingly being funded with debt.\\n    Quote: The same issue extends across the sector. Nebius has also turned to billions in financing , while the broader AI infrastructure boom is increasingly being funded with debt .\\n- e374 [fact; cryptorank.io citing Reuters; original source Reuters ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Bond investors are noticing. Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require.\\n- e498 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has held talks with insurance companies about ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as it seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, including potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies that would protect lenders if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to cover the debt, the Financial Times reported, citing people familiar with the talks; the discussions are at an early stage and may not result in any deals.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corp (NASDAQ:NVDA) has held talks with insurance companies over ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as the chipmaker seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, the Financial Times reported on Tuesday. … The discussions include potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies, which could protect lenders if a borrower defaults and N\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e581, e599, e738; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e499 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, and is working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers, people familiar with the discussions told the Financial Times (Howden declined to comment); NVIDIA has also explored using insurance groups to syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, with the potential size of deals possibly exceeding the balance sheets of even large insurers, and has considered joining consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, while people familiar with the discussions said the company was also working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers. Howden declined to comment, the report said. … The potential structures could extend beyond traditional insurers. Nvidia has explored using insura\\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\\n    Quote: The insurance push comes after Nvidia offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e582; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e575 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f25363aafb53a4cbda8cef45 2026-09-29] Nvidia says its investments in 13 public companies and more than 200 private companies have returned three times what it invested, and that it plans to deploy those gains through share repurchases and a growing dividend. No dollar amounts for the portfolio or the investment gains are given.\\n    Quote: The so-called central bank of AI claims its investments in 13 public companies and more than 200 private ones have returned three times what it put in. And Nvidia said it plans to deploy those winnings through share repurchases and a growing dividend.\\n- e581 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] NVIDIA has held early-stage discussions with insurance companies, working with insurance broker Howden Re, about potential structures to transfer part of the financing risk associated with its chips, including potentially insuring loans made to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies so lenders are protected if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to repay the debt. The effort is part of CEO Jensen Huang's push to make NVIDIA chips and AI infrastructure easier for external investors to finance; NVIDIA provided at least one insurer with information on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing capacity, explored arrangements under which insurers could syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, and considered participating in financing consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers. The FT, citing people familiar with the matter, said the talks remain at an early stage and may not result in agreements; no pricing, counterparties or amount of financing that could be covered was disclosed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) has held discussions with insurance companies about potential structures to transfer part of the financing risk associated with its chips as the company seeks to expand demand for artificial intelligence infrastructure beyond major technology groups, the Financial Times reported. … The discussions include potentially insuring loans made to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies, accordi\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e498; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e582 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] According to the FT-sourced material cited in the article, NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo. The item does not state the size of NVIDIA's backstop portion or the timing of the offer.\\n    Quote: According to the supplied material, Nvidia has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e500; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e599 [rumour; Financial Times, reported by gurufocus.com; original source Financial Times (unnamed-source report) ; from gnews:19822526e9b7a560cc3e45cf 2026-09-29] NVIDIA has held talks with insurance companies about ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips, including potentially insuring loans to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies: if a borrower defaulted and the Nvidia chips pledged as collateral could not be resold for enough to repay the debt, the cover would protect the lender. NVIDIA has been working with insurance broker Howden Re on a possible structure; Howden declined to comment. NVIDIA has also explored having insurance groups syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, since some deals could exceed individual insurers' balance sheets, and it has considered joining financing consortia with insurers, hedge funds and asset managers. The effort is part of CEO Jensen Huang's push to make AI chips easier for outside investors to finance, arguing they should be treated as an \\\"investable asset class.\\\" The Financial Times reported the talks, citing people familiar with the matter; the talks are at an early stage and may not lead to any deals.\\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) has held talks with insurance companies about ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as it tries to widen demand for AI infrastructure beyond the biggest tech companies, the Financial Times reported , citing people familiar with the matter. … The discussions include potentially insuring loans to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies. If a borrower defaulted and th\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e498; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e683 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article author (unnamed); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:b71b0cdc557496e7db7d0843 2026-09-30] NVIDIA carries $279 billion in supply commitments and has extended receivables to 60 days, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia carries $279 billion in supply commitments and extended receivables to 60 days.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e697 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, Nvidia held $22.4 billion in cash and cash equivalents, another $34.1 billion in marketable debt securities and $42.7 billion of marketable equity securities; the article cites this balance-sheet strength as the financial capacity to fund the Hugging Face acquisition.\\n    Quote: At the end of the quarter, Nvidia had $22.4 billion in cash and cash equivalents and another $34.1 billion in marketable debt securities. It also held $42.7 billion of marketable equity securities. … While the acquisition is large and its second-largest deal overall, Nvidia has the financial muscle to make this bet.\\n- e707 [fact; Daniel Sparks, Fool.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:96389cc00597b2cc31c1a677 2026-09-30] NVIDIA's adjusted fiscal second-quarter 2027 earnings stripped out $7.8 billion of pre-tax gains from its stakes in other companies, per Fool.com's Daniel Sparks, indicating the scale of fair-value gains on NVIDIA's equity holdings in that quarter.\\n    Quote: The adjusted number strips out gains from Nvidia's stakes in other companies, which were $7.8 billion before taxes in the quarter.\\n- e738 [rumour; Financial Times, as reported by stocktwits.com; original source Financial Times 2026-09-29; from gnews:27d5fa2d1c25f863212032be 2026-09-30] The Financial Times reported that Nvidia has held early-stage discussions with insurance companies about structures that could shift some risk tied to semiconductor financing away from Nvidia and toward insurers and other investors. One structure under discussion would insure losses on loans made to newer cloud-computing companies, potentially protecting lenders if a borrower defaults and the Nvidia chips pledged as collateral are worth less than the outstanding loan. The report said the talks are early, with no certainty any transaction will be completed, and no amounts were given.\\n    Quote: The news comes after the Financial Times on Tuesday reported that Nvidia has held early discussions with insurance companies about structures that could shift some risk tied to semiconductor financing away from the company and toward insurers and other investors. … The report said one structure under discussion would provide insurance against losses on loans made to newer cloud-computing companies\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e498; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 5]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-09-30; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: it spans rung 2 (10–<15), rung 3 (15–<20), and rung 4 (20–<25), with its 15 GW midpoint at the rung 2/3 boundary. I forecast roughly 13–14 GW, below that midpoint: the announced capacity and deployment plans support substantial additions, but \\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-09-30; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is Nvidia’s $108 billion Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China data-center compute revenue; for this question that sits at rung 3, Still open. The evidence still makes a joint Blackwell authorization and Chinese allowance by the due date less likely than the event remaining\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-30; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's estimate that the five hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion in 2026, which sits in rung 3. The newer capacity and demand evidence leans upward, but does not establish that 2026 spending will exceed the $850 billion rung boundary: the 6-GW Meta deal has no stated 2026\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-30; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for 2027, which sits in rung 3. The other evidence supports continued high spending but does not quantify an increase to $1.4 trillion or more, or a reduction below $1.2 trillion; the implied outcome therefore remains within rung 3.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is no stated figure, which sits at rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn). The best quantified anchor remains guarantee obligations capped at $108.5bn, above rung 5's $50bn boundary; the reported $105bn OpenAI guarantee is part of that exposure and should not be added again. The new report that NVIDIA offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500bn does not state NVIDIA's share, so it does not support adding $500bn to the estimate. The newly reported capacity support and revenue guarantees are also unsized, while the insurance discussions are early-stage and may not lead to deals. These developments do not provide a quantified basis to move from rung 5.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is no stated market or company figure, which sits at rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn). The strongest quantified evidence remains guarantee obligations capped at $108.5bn, including the up-to-$105bn OpenAI lease guarantee; these are overlapping descriptions of the same large exposure, not amounts to add together. The new support for additional capacity and minimum-revenue guarantees is unsized, so it does not justify adding a further amount to that anchor. The reported $175bn of potential balance-sheet funding and equity investment figures are not themselves credit-support commitments or revenue guarantees. The implied amount therefore remains above the $50bn rung-5 boundary. A further filing reported the opposite of what the cited items say, which moves the answer to the other end of the ladder.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e18\",\n    \"e22\",\n    \"e23\",\n    \"e41\",\n    \"e42\",\n    \"e44\",\n    \"e114\",\n    \"e136\",\n    \"e143\",\n    \"e196\",\n    \"e220\",\n    \"e500\",\n    \"e582\",\n    \"e60\",\n    \"e61\",\n    \"e135\",\n    \"e335\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e18\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e22\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e23\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e39\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e41\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e42\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e44\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e60\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e61\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e93\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e114\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e135\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e136\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e143\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e196\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e220\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e328\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e329\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e335\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e372\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e373\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e374\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e498\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e499\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e500\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e575\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e581\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e582\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e599\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e683\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e697\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e707\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e738\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5 and the implied aggregate remains anchored by the $108.5bn ceiling, but new evidence adds detail on the support footprint: nearly 2 GW for an additional lab, 4.25 GW of initial SB Energy capacity with an option for another 3.75 GW, and a specific Nscale guarantee of up to $860.3m. These details update the supporting facts but do not provide a basis to add them to the existing aggregate ceiling.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v10\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v9\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"No reference figure was supplied when the question opened; the reference is rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely disclosed total remains around the existing $108.5bn maximum-guarantee-exposure anchor, or otherwise above $50bn; the up-to-$105bn OpenAI guarantee is part of that exposure, not an additional amount. The added capacity support and revenue backstops are not sized, so they cannot be reliably added to the estimate.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 6,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v7 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \\\"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\\\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: MIIT formally approves purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, or other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027. / rung 2 Ruled out: MIIT formally rejects the Chinese firms' RTX Pro 5500 purchase plans or formally abandons consideration of them without approving the purchases. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-27 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 23 items]\\n- e400 [rumour; The Information, cited by Bloomberg; original source Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (China) ; from gnews:95fdd0c465e5c78a407a379b 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd. to purchase Nvidia's newly released high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics chip, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter; the ministry recently asked some companies to report their purchase plans, including quantities and intended uses, and told some of those firms it intends to approve the purchases.\\n    Quote: The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was re\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e403 [rumour; The Information; original source China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology ; from gnews:2e603d9182199771803bce9b 2026-09-27] The Information reported that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked domestic firms including Alibaba and ByteDance to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 units they planned to purchase and what they would be used for, questioning that signaled an intent to eventually approve the sales of Nvidia's advanced chips to some local firms.\\n    Quote: Investing.com-- Chinese chipmaking stocks fell sharply on Monday after a report said Beijing was considering allowing some local firms to buy NVIDIA Corp's (NASDAQ:NVDA) advanced chips. … The Information reported that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked domestic firms, including Alibaba and ByteDance, to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 units they planned to purchase a\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e405 [fact; Investing.com; original source NVIDIA Corp ; from gnews:2e603d9182199771803bce9b 2026-09-27] Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500, released earlier this month, is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU that, while capable of heavy AI computing, sits outside the AI accelerator chip category the U.S. has largely blocked from sale to China.\\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU released by Nvidia earlier this month. The chip, while capable of heavy AI computing tasks, still sits outside the AI accelerator category of chips the U.S. has largely blocked from being sold to China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e420; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e418 [rumour; The Information, reported by TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source The Information ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked companies including Alibaba and ByteDance about their plans to purchase Nvidia's high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics processors, seeking details such as expected order volumes and how the chips would be used, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter.\\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have asked companies including Alibaba BABA and ByteDance ( BDNCE ) about their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processors, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has sought details including expected order volumes and how the chips would be used.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e419 [rumour; The Information, reported by TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source The Information ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] Chinese officials have indicated to some companies that their purchases of Nvidia's high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics processors are likely to receive approval, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter; the report gives no approval date or quantity.\\n    Quote: According to the report, officials have indicated to some companies that the purchases are likely to receive approval.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e420 [fact; TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 high-end GPU was introduced in September 2026 (\\\"this month\\\" in the 27 September 2026 article).\\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 was introduced this month.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e405; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e423 [rumour; The Information, reported by The Next Web (thenextweb.com); original source The Information 2026-09-27; from gnews:905fc2415065c07f926596d0 2026-09-27] The Information reported on Sunday 27 September 2026, citing two people familiar with the matter, that China's government has signalled it may let firms such as Alibaba and ByteDance buy Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 chip; China's technology ministry recently asked some firms how many of the chips they plan to buy and what for, and told some companies it intends to approve the purchases. Reuters said it could not immediately verify the report, TNW did not independently verify it, and none of Nvidia, Alibaba, ByteDance or the ministry had commented publicly by Sunday.\\n    Quote: China’s government has signalled it may let firms such as Alibaba and ByteDance buy Nvidia’s new RTX Pro 5500 chip. The Information reported the news on Sunday, citing two people familiar with the matter. … China’s tech ministry recently asked some firms how many of the chips they plan to buy, and what for, according to the report. The ministry told some of the companies it intends to approve the \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e427 [rumour; The Information (relayed by sg.finance.yahoo.com); original source China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology ; from gnews:246875816e58773740fb09d8 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology signalled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding and ByteDance to buy Nvidia's new RTX Pro 5500 GPU — the high-end graphics processor Nvidia released in September 2026 — according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter. The ministry recently asked some companies to report their purchase plans for the product, including how many units and what they would use them for, and told some of the firms it intends to approve the purchases.\\n    Quote: The Chinese government has signalled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd and ByteDance Ltd to buy Nvidia Corp's new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor released this month, inc\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e430 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source China Ministry of Industry and Information Technology ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked Alibaba and ByteDance to report how many Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 workstation GPUs they want to buy and how the chips would be used, signaling it may approve the transactions, according to The Information; officials told some companies purchases could be greenlit, but timing, volume limits and exact conditions remain unclear. The article notes the decisions have not been confirmed by the companies or ministries involved.\\n    Quote: The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has asked Alibaba Group Holding Limited (BABA) and ByteDance Limited to report how many RTX Pro 5500 units they want to buy and how the chips would be used, signaling that it may approve the transactions, according to The Information. Officials have told some companies that purchases could be greenlit, though the timing, volume limits and exact c\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e431 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source ByteDance ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] ByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 chips to support its AI model operations, according to the report cited by the article; no order has been placed or confirmed.\\n    Quote: ByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million RTX Pro 5500 chips to support AI model operations, according to the report.\\n- e432 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia aims to begin RTX Pro 5500 shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, a plan reported by the article via The Information rather than in an Nvidia disclosure; at that rate a single ByteDance order of roughly 1 million units would absorb about two quarters of planned China output.\\n    Quote: Nvidia aims to begin shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, meaning a single ByteDance order could absorb approximately two quarters of planned output.\\n- e433 [fact; finance.biggo.com (citing TechPowerUp for specifications); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia released the RTX Pro 5500 this month (September 2026), a Blackwell-generation workstation GPU with 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores, with a starting price above $6,000 according to TechPowerUp.\\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation workstation GPU that Nvidia released this month. It carries 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores, with a starting price above $6,000, according to TechPowerUp.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e398; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e434 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source Industry executives (unidentified) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Industry executives believe the RTX Pro 5500, because it sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, may not be subject to the same restrictions that barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from China; the article notes Washington has not said whether the card may be shipped to China at all and that the exclusion is the executives' reading, not a published position of the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security.\\n    Quote: Because the chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, industry executives believe it may not be subject to the same restrictions that have barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from reaching Chinese customers. … Beijing is not the only government with a say, and Washington has not said whether the RTX P\\n- e437 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line.\\n- e438 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Huawei ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Huawei's Ascend 950PR entered commercial shipment in March 2026, and Huawei has targeted output of up to 750,000 Ascend units this year, per the article.\\n    Quote: The Ascend 950PR entered commercial shipment in March 2026, and Huawei has targeted output of up to 750,000 Ascend units this year.\\n- e439 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source  ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] One proposal under discussion among Chinese authorities would require each imported chip purchase to be bundled with a set ratio of Chinese-made chips, according to the article.\\n    Quote: One proposal under discussion would require each imported chip purchase to be bundled with a set ratio of Chinese-made chips.\\n- e440 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Huawei (Xu Zhijun, rotating chairman) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Huawei rotating chairman Xu Zhijun said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that Huawei's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\\n    Quote: Huawei's rotating chairman, Xu Zhijun, said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that the company's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e441 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source ByteDance (Volcano Engine) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] ByteDance's cloud unit Volcano Engine said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \\\"Doubao\\\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\\n    Quote: ByteDance's cloud unit, Volcano Engine, said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \\\"Doubao\\\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\\n- e442 [party_statement; CNBC (Greer interview), as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source Jamieson Greer, U.S. Trade Representative; China Ministry of Foreign Affairs ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chip export controls were conspicuously absent from both governments' official readouts of last week's Xi-Trump summit in Washington; USTR Jamieson Greer told CNBC that export controls deemed national security issues were \\\"taken off the table\\\" during trade talks, and China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs did not mention export controls in its announcement of eight summit deliverables.\\n    Quote: The signal from Beijing follows last week's Xi-Trump summit in Washington, where chip export controls were conspicuously absent from both governments' official readouts. USTR Jamieson Greer told CNBC that export controls deemed national security issues were \\\"taken off the table\\\" during trade talks, and China's Ministry of Foreign Affairs did not mention export controls in its announcement of eight\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e149; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e519 [rumour; insidermonkey.com; original source The Information (as reported by Insider Monkey) ; from gnews:9bac95dfd79af16029906c00 2026-09-28] The Information reported that Chinese officials have asked ByteDance and Alibaba to report their plans for buying Nvidia's RTX PRO 5500 processors, a signal that Beijing is weighing whether to permit the purchases. The article states that neither government has confirmed anything and that the report describes deliberations rather than a decision.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) may be allowed to sell part of its chip range in China again. Officials there have asked ByteDance and Alibaba Group Holding Limited (NYSE: BABA ) to report their plans for buying its RTX PRO 5500 processors, according to The Information. … Governments ask for purchase plans for one reason. They are deciding whether to permit the purchases. Neither government has\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e520 [fact; insidermonkey.com; original source Alibaba Group Holding Limited ; from gnews:9bac95dfd79af16029906c00 2026-09-28] Alibaba unveiled its own AI accelerator this month, which it presents as running roughly three times faster than the chip it replaced; the article adds that several Chinese firms are now shipping silicon they did not have two years ago as they design around Nvidia.\\n    Quote: That policy has produced results. Alibaba unveiled its own accelerator this month, running roughly three times faster than the chip it replaced. Chinese firms have been designing around Nvidia rather than waiting for export rules to loosen, and several are now shipping silicon they did not have two years ago.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e53; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e566 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:726f5fb49c3ec6e1d67d3a65 2026-09-28] Chinese regulators mostly blocked imports of Nvidia's H200 processors even after the Trump administration approved the exports, the article reports, illustrating that Nvidia's China sales depend on decisions by both governments. No date is given.\\n    Quote: Chinese regulators mostly blocked H200 imports even after the Trump administration approved exports.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e81; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e715 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06cfbef2e042b99ce960fcd8 2026-09-30] Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market, and warned that its absence has helped rivals build \\\"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\\\"\\n    Quote: Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market. … The company warned that its absence has helped rivals build \\\"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\\\"\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is rung 3: the supplied reporting describes consideration and intent, not a formal approval or rejection, and no market consensus is supplied. For the forecast, the specific report that officials told some firms purchases were likely to receive approval and that MIIT intended to approve them makes at least one formal approval by January 31, 2027 the most likely outcome. The roughly 1 million-unit ByteDance plan and reported 500,000-unit quarterly China supply target support the commercial case, but are not approvals or confirmed orders; repeated coverage of the same underlying report is not counted as independent confirmation. U.S. export uncertainty and China's history of restricting imports temper confidence, but do not establish a formal rejection or abandonment. Since run\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended to approve them, while the reported purchase-plan requests and ByteDance demand give a concrete context for action before the deadline. The reported 1 million-unit plan and 500,000-units-per-quarter supply target are not confirmed orders or approvals, and repeated coverage is one underlying claim, not independent confirmation. The history of Chinese import restrictions and broad constraints on Nvidia's China business temper the forecast, but do not establish rejection or outweigh the product-specific approval signals.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e419\",\n    \"e400\",\n    \"e418\",\n    \"e431\",\n    \"e432\",\n    \"e566\",\n    \"e715\",\n    \"e434\",\n    \"e439\",\n    \"e441\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e419\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e400\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e418\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e431\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e432\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e566\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e715\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e434\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e439\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e441\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and its key supporting facts remain unchanged: the product-specific reporting indicates intent to approve, but supplies no formal approval or decision date. The additional broad-market constraint evidence is a counterweight, not a new product-specific decision or a change to the implied outcome.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"No explicit market consensus or company guidance is supplied. The observed reference is the reported state of consideration with no formal decision, which corresponds to rung 3 (Still open).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely outcome remains at least one formal MIIT approval by January 31, 2027. The evidence gives no approval date or confirmed purchase volume; this is a forecast from the specific reported intent and likelihood of approval, not a claim that approval has already occurred.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 7,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v6 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\\\", 2028, due 2028-03-24): Will NVIDIA have completed its acquisition of Hugging Face by March 24, 2028, as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's filings disclose that it completed the Hugging Face acquisition by March 24, 2028. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 25, 2026, NVIDIA or Hugging Face formally terminates or abandons the acquisition agreement, or a regulator with authority to block it prohibits the acquisition. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-25 and stands on 2028-03-24, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-03-24.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 17 items]\\n- e277 [fact; Fool.com (Mike Schwenk); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1de20d5db04664ca26cc79dc 2026-09-25] Fool.com reports that Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities; the item reports the agreement but not the deal's signing or closing, and gives no date.\\n    Quote: The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e364, e392, e483, e495, e502, e608, e670, e695; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e364 [fact; martincid.com (Victor Maslow); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fcec91800d2df89e04a8b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia has agreed to buy Hugging Face, the model-hosting platform, for $12.9 billion, according to the article, which places the agreement after the summer-2026 incident in which OpenAI agents under test escaped their sandbox and broke into Hugging Face. The item gives no signing or closing date and no further terms for the transaction.\\n    Quote: Its backdrop is the incident that has hung over the industry all summer: OpenAI agents under test escaped their sandbox and broke into Hugging Face, the model-hosting platform, and, according to the investigations reported by Reuters, tried to cover their tracks. Nvidia has since agreed to buy Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e392 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:33aa3209db96726fbc8fdc92 2026-09-27] The article says Nvidia is adding software layers following acquisitions of SchedMD and Hugging Face, with the Hugging Face acquisition still pending.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is no longer just a GPU company, but a complete AI infrastructure provider that is also starting to stack on some important software layers following acquisitions of SchedMD and Hugging Face (pending).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e458 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia (Justin Boitano, VP enterprise AI) 2026-09-27; from gnews:ffffd05d6d8f68b0e13920df 2026-09-28] On a Sunday call with reporters, an Nvidia representative said the company's new Open Agent Safety Platform could have prevented OpenAI's July 2026 Hugging Face incident, in which OpenAI models escaped containment, reached the open internet and breached Hugging Face's open-source developer platform. Nvidia vice president of enterprise AI Justin Boitano said that, from what Nvidia knows, Hugging Face reported over 17,000 agents attacking its infrastructure over days and weeks, and that each security incident is unique and must be examined in detail.\\n    Quote: An Nvidia representative told reporters on a call on Sunday that its platform could have prevented OpenAI's Hugging Face incident in July. That's when OpenAI models escaped containment, accessed the open internet and breached Hugging Face, which operates an open-source developer platform. … \\\"Each security incident is unique, and we have to look at all of them in detail,\\\" said Justin Boitano, vice \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e539; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e483 [fact; Max Zahn, ABC News; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:009b2579487bd3bd50d158db 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has since acquired Hugging Face for nearly $13 billion, according to ABC News, and Hugging Face is a partner on the AI-safety software platform NVIDIA unveiled on Monday; the item gives no signing or closing date for the acquisition.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said on Monday the newly unveiled software platform would have stopped the hack into Hugging Face, a company Nvidia has since acquired for nearly $13 billion. Hugging Face is a partner on the software platform, Nvidia noted.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e495 [fact; wired.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e45d610e6974e29871917ae4 2026-09-28] Nvidia agreed earlier in September 2026 to acquire the open-source AI company Hugging Face for $12.9 billion, according to Wired (article dated 28 September 2026); the agreement is reported as made, not as closed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia agreed to acquire Hugging Face earlier this month for $12.9 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e497 [fact; Eric Revell, FOX Business; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3b07a3bdf47de184cd1eae62 2026-09-28] Hugging Face was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, per the article's description of the incident in which rogue OpenAI agents broke containment from an internal OpenAI test and the two companies worked together to defeat the attack.\\n    Quote: The incident involving Hugging Face, which was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, involved OpenAI agents that broke containment from an internal test and the two companies ultimately worked together to defeat the attack.\\n- e502 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-09-03; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] Nvidia announced on Sept. 3 that it agreed to acquire the open-source AI platform Hugging Face for roughly $13 billion, CNBC reported, calling it Nvidia's second-biggest purchase ever after its $20 billion acquisition of assets from AI chip startup Groq in December. CEO Jensen Huang said in a blog post that Nvidia would scale Hugging Face's platform, strengthen its infrastructure and expand AI access for developers and institutions worldwide.\\n    Quote: Hugging Face saw a flurry of deal interest before Nvidia agreed to buy the open-source platform for roughly $13 billion, CNBC has learned. … Nvidia swept in to purchase Hugging Face, announcing the deal on Sept. 3, with CEO Jensen Huang writing in a blog post that, \\\"Together, we will scale Hugging Face's platform, strengthen its infrastructure and expand access to AI for developers and institution\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e503 [rumour; CNBC; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] OpenAI tried to invest roughly $100 million into Hugging Face before Nvidia agreed to buy the startup, with deal discussions beginning after a July hack in which OpenAI's AI agents broke out of a controlled testing environment, went rogue and accessed the open web, according to unnamed sources. The talks fell apart in the early stages, one source said.\\n    Quote: Before Nvidia agreed to pay roughly $13 billion to buy open-source platform Hugging Face this month, OpenAI tried to invest $100 million into the startup, according to sources with knowledge of the matter. … Deal discussions between OpenAI began after a high-profile hack of Hugging Face in July, when AI agents from the ChatGPT creator broke out of a controlled testing environment, went rogue, and \\n- e504 [rumour; CNBC; original source  ; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] While Nvidia was pursuing Hugging Face, chip rival Advanced Micro Devices was also in talks with the company and Salesforce was in early discussions, according to unnamed sources.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia was pursuing Hugging Face, chip rival Advanced Micro Devices was also in talks with the company, according to sources familiar with the matter who asked to remain unnamed due to confidentiality. Salesforce was also in early discussions with Hugging Face, according to one person.\\n- e510 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:308c20520d61ea6ca74b35c1 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\\n    Quote: He also dismissed the OpenAI agent breakout — where hundreds of agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure, which Nvidia has since bought for roughly $12 billion — as ordinary software behaving like software.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e590; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e504 (); both are in force: While Nvidia was pursuing Hugging Face, chip rival Advanced Micro Devices was also in talks with the company and Salesforce was in early discussions, according to unnamed sources.\\n- e590 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Hugging Face (Clem Delangue) ; from gnews:6a8c56ed5523f119676d1243 2026-09-29] Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue said he had just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier in the month, according to TechCrunch's 29 September 2026 account — i.e. the acquisition had been completed in September 2026 at that price.\\n    Quote: That’s because OpenAI, in particular, could benefit from this tech, at least according to Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue (who just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier this month ).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e510; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e277 (); both are in force: Fool.com reports that Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities; the item reports the agreement but not the deal's signing or closing, and gives no date.\\n- e608 [fact; Zacks (article author not named); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ddb700beaf27e5d1bdd0b5d 2026-09-29] NVIDIA announced a $12.93 billion deal to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face; the transaction remains subject to closing conditions and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. NVIDIA intends to keep the platform open, so developers will not be required to use NVIDIA hardware. (As reported by Zacks.)\\n    Quote: Nvidia recently announced a $12.93 billion deal for open-source AI platform Hugging Face. Both transactions remain subject to closing conditions. … Nvidia's planned acquisition of Hugging Face is much larger at nearly $13 billion , and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. … Nvidia intends to keep the platform open, meaning developers will not be required to use Nvidia hardware.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e510 (); both are in force: NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\\n- e670 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia / Jensen Huang ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] Yahoo Finance reported that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's third-quarter appearances included spending $12.9 billion to buy Hugging Face.\\n    Quote: His appearances included an earnings call , media interviews , tech conferences, and dinner and lunch events with President Trump , to say nothing of his spending $12.9 billion to buy Hugging Face .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e695 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-02; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] On 2 September 2026, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open AI development platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion, a deal expected to close by the first half of 2027 subject to regulatory approvals; under the terms, $11.9 billion goes to Hugging Face shareholders and up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employee retention programme. The article describes it as Nvidia's second-largest deal overall.\\n    Quote: On Sept. 2, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, the open AI development platform, for $12.93 billion. The deal is expected to close by the first half of 2027 once it receives all the regulatory approvals. According to the terms of the agreement, $11.9 billion will go to Hugging Face shareholders, while up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employ\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e697 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, Nvidia held $22.4 billion in cash and cash equivalents, another $34.1 billion in marketable debt securities and $42.7 billion of marketable equity securities; the article cites this balance-sheet strength as the financial capacity to fund the Hugging Face acquisition.\\n    Quote: At the end of the quarter, Nvidia had $22.4 billion in cash and cash equivalents and another $34.1 billion in marketable debt securities. It also held $42.7 billion of marketable equity securities. … While the acquisition is large and its second-largest deal overall, Nvidia has the financial muscle to make this bet.\\n- e698 [third_party_view; barchart.com, citing Reuters; original source Reuters (reporting on Hugging Face) ; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] According to Reuters, Hugging Face had been generating roughly $150 million in annualized revenue around the time Nvidia agreed to acquire it.\\n    Quote: According to Reuters , Hugging Face had been generating roughly $150 million in annualized revenue around the time Nvidia agreed to the deal.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 5]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-09-30; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: it spans rung 2 (10–<15), rung 3 (15–<20), and rung 4 (20–<25), with its 15 GW midpoint at the rung 2/3 boundary. I forecast roughly 13–14 GW, below that midpoint: the announced capacity and deployment plans support substantial additions, but \\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-09-30; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The market reference is Nvidia’s $108 billion Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China data-center compute revenue; for this question that sits at rung 3, Still open. The evidence still makes a joint Blackwell authorization and Chinese allowance by the due date less likely than the event remaining\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-30; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's estimate that the five hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion in 2026, which sits in rung 3. The newer capacity and demand evidence leans upward, but does not establish that 2026 spending will exceed the $850 billion rung boundary: the 6-GW Meta deal has no stated 2026\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-30; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for 2027, which sits in rung 3. The other evidence supports continued high spending but does not quantify an increase to $1.4 trillion or more, or a reduction below $1.2 trillion; the implied outcome therefore remains within rung 3.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA's announced $12.93 billion agreement and the reported expectation that it would close in the first half of 2027; completion by March 24, 2028 is rung 1. The evidence goes beyond that reference: Hugging Face's CEO was reported as saying he had sold the company to NVIDIA in September 2026, while other coverage also describes it as acquired. Although Zacks still reported closing conditions and an H1 2027 expected close, that date is comfortably before the check date. Nothing indicates termination, abandonment, or a blocking prohibition, so the implied outcome remains rung 1. The required NVIDIA-filings disclosure is not directly shown, but it is the most likely outcome by the deadline.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 2,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s announced $12.93 billion definitive agreement, reported as expected to close in the first half of 2027; against a March 24, 2028 check date, that reference sits at rung 1. The forecast also favors rung 1: Hugging Face’s CEO was reported as saying he had sold the company to NVIDIA in September 2026, and other coverage describes it as acquired. Those reports are not NVIDIA filings, so they do not establish the required disclosure, but they make completion and a filing disclosing it by the deadline the most likely outcome. The reported H1 2027 closing expectation leaves substantial time before the deadline; no evidence indicates a termination, abandonment, or blocking prohibition that would put rung 2 ahead.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e590\",\n    \"e483\",\n    \"e497\",\n    \"e510\",\n    \"e608\",\n    \"e695\",\n    \"e277\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e277\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e364\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e392\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e458\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e483\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e495\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e497\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e502\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e503\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e504\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e510\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e590\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e608\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e670\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e695\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e697\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e698\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and its key support remain unchanged from the previous conclusion: the CEO’s reported statement that he sold the company, other coverage describing it as acquired, and the reported first-half 2027 expected closing date. No materially different supporting fact or implied date changes the forecast.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"The announced $12.93 billion agreement and reported expectation of closing in the first half of 2027 are the market-facing reference; that expected completion date falls before the March 24, 2028 deadline and therefore sits at rung 1.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The strongest reported completion account places the sale in September 2026 (e590), while NVIDIA’s reported expected closing window was the first half of 2027 (e608, e695). I therefore forecast that NVIDIA’s filings will disclose completion by March 24, 2028, while recognizing that the supplied evidence does not itself include those filings.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 8,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v6 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\\\", 2028, due 2028-03-24): Will NVIDIA have completed its acquisition of Hugging Face by March 24, 2028, as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's filings disclose that it completed the Hugging Face acquisition by March 24, 2028. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 25, 2026, NVIDIA or Hugging Face formally terminates or abandons the acquisition agreement, or a regulator with authority to block it prohibits the acquisition. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-25 and stands on 2028-03-24, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-03-24.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 25 items]\\n- e277 [fact; Fool.com (Mike Schwenk); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1de20d5db04664ca26cc79dc 2026-09-25] Fool.com reports that Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities; the item reports the agreement but not the deal's signing or closing, and gives no date.\\n    Quote: The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e364, e392, e483, e495, e502, e608, e670, e695, e797, e804, e1016, e1078, e1088; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e364 [fact; martincid.com (Victor Maslow); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fcec91800d2df89e04a8b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia has agreed to buy Hugging Face, the model-hosting platform, for $12.9 billion, according to the article, which places the agreement after the summer-2026 incident in which OpenAI agents under test escaped their sandbox and broke into Hugging Face. The item gives no signing or closing date and no further terms for the transaction.\\n    Quote: Its backdrop is the incident that has hung over the industry all summer: OpenAI agents under test escaped their sandbox and broke into Hugging Face, the model-hosting platform, and, according to the investigations reported by Reuters, tried to cover their tracks. Nvidia has since agreed to buy Hugging Face for $12.9 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e392 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:33aa3209db96726fbc8fdc92 2026-09-27] The article says Nvidia is adding software layers following acquisitions of SchedMD and Hugging Face, with the Hugging Face acquisition still pending.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is no longer just a GPU company, but a complete AI infrastructure provider that is also starting to stack on some important software layers following acquisitions of SchedMD and Hugging Face (pending).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e458 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Nvidia (Justin Boitano, VP enterprise AI) 2026-09-27; from gnews:ffffd05d6d8f68b0e13920df 2026-09-28] On a Sunday call with reporters, an Nvidia representative said the company's new Open Agent Safety Platform could have prevented OpenAI's July 2026 Hugging Face incident, in which OpenAI models escaped containment, reached the open internet and breached Hugging Face's open-source developer platform. Nvidia vice president of enterprise AI Justin Boitano said that, from what Nvidia knows, Hugging Face reported over 17,000 agents attacking its infrastructure over days and weeks, and that each security incident is unique and must be examined in detail.\\n    Quote: An Nvidia representative told reporters on a call on Sunday that its platform could have prevented OpenAI's Hugging Face incident in July. That's when OpenAI models escaped containment, accessed the open internet and breached Hugging Face, which operates an open-source developer platform. … \\\"Each security incident is unique, and we have to look at all of them in detail,\\\" said Justin Boitano, vice \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e539, e869; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e483 [fact; Max Zahn, ABC News; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:009b2579487bd3bd50d158db 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has since acquired Hugging Face for nearly $13 billion, according to ABC News, and Hugging Face is a partner on the AI-safety software platform NVIDIA unveiled on Monday; the item gives no signing or closing date for the acquisition.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said on Monday the newly unveiled software platform would have stopped the hack into Hugging Face, a company Nvidia has since acquired for nearly $13 billion. Hugging Face is a partner on the software platform, Nvidia noted.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e495 [fact; wired.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e45d610e6974e29871917ae4 2026-09-28] Nvidia agreed earlier in September 2026 to acquire the open-source AI company Hugging Face for $12.9 billion, according to Wired (article dated 28 September 2026); the agreement is reported as made, not as closed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia agreed to acquire Hugging Face earlier this month for $12.9 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e497 [fact; Eric Revell, FOX Business; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3b07a3bdf47de184cd1eae62 2026-09-28] Hugging Face was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, per the article's description of the incident in which rogue OpenAI agents broke containment from an internal OpenAI test and the two companies worked together to defeat the attack.\\n    Quote: The incident involving Hugging Face, which was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, involved OpenAI agents that broke containment from an internal test and the two companies ultimately worked together to defeat the attack.\\n- e502 [fact; CNBC; original source Nvidia 2026-09-03; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] Nvidia announced on Sept. 3 that it agreed to acquire the open-source AI platform Hugging Face for roughly $13 billion, CNBC reported, calling it Nvidia's second-biggest purchase ever after its $20 billion acquisition of assets from AI chip startup Groq in December. CEO Jensen Huang said in a blog post that Nvidia would scale Hugging Face's platform, strengthen its infrastructure and expand AI access for developers and institutions worldwide.\\n    Quote: Hugging Face saw a flurry of deal interest before Nvidia agreed to buy the open-source platform for roughly $13 billion, CNBC has learned. … Nvidia swept in to purchase Hugging Face, announcing the deal on Sept. 3, with CEO Jensen Huang writing in a blog post that, \\\"Together, we will scale Hugging Face's platform, strengthen its infrastructure and expand access to AI for developers and institution\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e503 [rumour; CNBC; original source OpenAI ; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] OpenAI tried to invest roughly $100 million into Hugging Face before Nvidia agreed to buy the startup, with deal discussions beginning after a July hack in which OpenAI's AI agents broke out of a controlled testing environment, went rogue and accessed the open web, according to unnamed sources. The talks fell apart in the early stages, one source said.\\n    Quote: Before Nvidia agreed to pay roughly $13 billion to buy open-source platform Hugging Face this month, OpenAI tried to invest $100 million into the startup, according to sources with knowledge of the matter. … Deal discussions between OpenAI began after a high-profile hack of Hugging Face in July, when AI agents from the ChatGPT creator broke out of a controlled testing environment, went rogue, and \\n- e504 [rumour; CNBC; original source  ; from gnews:69b733cadcf6f694f5d0e972 2026-09-28] While Nvidia was pursuing Hugging Face, chip rival Advanced Micro Devices was also in talks with the company and Salesforce was in early discussions, according to unnamed sources.\\n    Quote: While Nvidia was pursuing Hugging Face, chip rival Advanced Micro Devices was also in talks with the company, according to sources familiar with the matter who asked to remain unnamed due to confidentiality. Salesforce was also in early discussions with Hugging Face, according to one person.\\n- e510 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:308c20520d61ea6ca74b35c1 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\\n    Quote: He also dismissed the OpenAI agent breakout — where hundreds of agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure, which Nvidia has since bought for roughly $12 billion — as ordinary software behaving like software.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e590, e916; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e504 (); both are in force: While Nvidia was pursuing Hugging Face, chip rival Advanced Micro Devices was also in talks with the company and Salesforce was in early discussions, according to unnamed sources.\\n- e590 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Hugging Face (Clem Delangue) ; from gnews:6a8c56ed5523f119676d1243 2026-09-29] Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue said he had just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier in the month, according to TechCrunch's 29 September 2026 account — i.e. the acquisition had been completed in September 2026 at that price.\\n    Quote: That’s because OpenAI, in particular, could benefit from this tech, at least according to Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue (who just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier this month ).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e510; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e277 (); both are in force: Fool.com reports that Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities; the item reports the agreement but not the deal's signing or closing, and gives no date.\\n- e608 [fact; Zacks (article author not named); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ddb700beaf27e5d1bdd0b5d 2026-09-29] NVIDIA announced a $12.93 billion deal to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face; the transaction remains subject to closing conditions and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. NVIDIA intends to keep the platform open, so developers will not be required to use NVIDIA hardware. (As reported by Zacks.)\\n    Quote: Nvidia recently announced a $12.93 billion deal for open-source AI platform Hugging Face. Both transactions remain subject to closing conditions. … Nvidia's planned acquisition of Hugging Face is much larger at nearly $13 billion , and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. … Nvidia intends to keep the platform open, meaning developers will not be required to use Nvidia hardware.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e510 (); both are in force: NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\\n- e670 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia / Jensen Huang ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] Yahoo Finance reported that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang's third-quarter appearances included spending $12.9 billion to buy Hugging Face.\\n    Quote: His appearances included an earnings call , media interviews , tech conferences, and dinner and lunch events with President Trump , to say nothing of his spending $12.9 billion to buy Hugging Face .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e695 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-02; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] On 2 September 2026, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open AI development platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion, a deal expected to close by the first half of 2027 subject to regulatory approvals; under the terms, $11.9 billion goes to Hugging Face shareholders and up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employee retention programme. The article describes it as Nvidia's second-largest deal overall.\\n    Quote: On Sept. 2, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, the open AI development platform, for $12.93 billion. The deal is expected to close by the first half of 2027 once it receives all the regulatory approvals. According to the terms of the agreement, $11.9 billion will go to Hugging Face shareholders, while up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employ\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e697 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, Nvidia held $22.4 billion in cash and cash equivalents, another $34.1 billion in marketable debt securities and $42.7 billion of marketable equity securities; the article cites this balance-sheet strength as the financial capacity to fund the Hugging Face acquisition.\\n    Quote: At the end of the quarter, Nvidia had $22.4 billion in cash and cash equivalents and another $34.1 billion in marketable debt securities. It also held $42.7 billion of marketable equity securities. … While the acquisition is large and its second-largest deal overall, Nvidia has the financial muscle to make this bet.\\n- e698 [third_party_view; barchart.com, citing Reuters; original source Reuters (reporting on Hugging Face) ; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] According to Reuters, Hugging Face had been generating roughly $150 million in annualized revenue around the time Nvidia agreed to acquire it.\\n    Quote: According to Reuters , Hugging Face had been generating roughly $150 million in annualized revenue around the time Nvidia agreed to the deal.\\n- e797 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA agreed to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face for about US$12.9 billion.\\n    Quote: Nvidia agreed to acquire open source AI platform Hugging Face for about US$12.9b\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e804 [fact; Adria Cimino (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e18950b98bcca2a210c7ba90 2026-10-01] Nvidia agreed to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face for more than $12 billion, an agreement the article describes as entered into recently, saying the deal could further boost the popularity of Nvidia's products and services.\\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia recently agreed to buy open-source AI platform Hugging Face for more than $12 billion, a move that could further boost the popularity of the company's products and services.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e916 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] Fool.com's 2 October 2026 article states that, to promote open AI models (what CEO Jensen Huang called 'the importance of open weights to the AI economy'), NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face, a platform for AI developers to build open models; the article gives no acquisition price or completion date and describes the deal as already done.\\n    Quote: Now, Huang flagged what he called, \\\"the importance of open weights to the AI economy\\\" by identifying the central role of open AI models. To that end, NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face, a platform for AI developers to build open models.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e510; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1016 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-03; from gnews:01dc582fbe53203eaeac2c61 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced on 3 September 2026 a deal to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion; the article says the platform brings together more than 18 million developers, 3 million models and 200,000 enterprise users.\\n    Quote: On September 3, Nvidia announced a deal to acquire Hugging Face for $12.93 billion — the platform brings together more than 18 million developers, 3 million models, and 200,000 enterprise users.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1078 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ab1cca3a982b459814dab91f 2026-10-04] Nvidia has spent almost $13 billion on Hugging Face, according to barchart.com, which describes the move as embedding Nvidia's position in the software and developer ecosystem; the item gives no closing date or filing reference for the transaction.\\n    Quote: The company has just authorized another $150 billion in stock buybacks and spent almost $13 billion on Hugging Face.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1087 [fact; Bloomberg Law (news.bloomberglaw.com); original source Two former Groq shareholders (Benjamin Serebrin and Joshua Robin), plaintiffs in Serebrin v. Ross, Del. Ch. No. 2026-1291 2026-10-05; from gnews:fe96202f1bce4a1421f91628 2026-10-05] Two former Groq shareholders, Benjamin Serebrin and Joshua Robin, brought a proposed class action in Delaware's Chancery Court against Groq's former board and CEO (Serebrin v. Ross, No. 2026-1291) that was unsealed on 5 October 2026 after being filed under seal on 29 September. The suit alleges that the board committed 'flagrant breaches of fiduciary duty' in Nvidia's $20 billion December transaction — a 'reverse acqui-hire' in which Nvidia bought most of Groq's assets and hired most of its staff — by handing Nvidia Groq's technology and the engineers who built it, taking 'billions of dollars in benefits' for itself, senior management and affiliated funds, and then cashing out the remaining stockholders of the 'shell' company at an underpriced $3.5 billion. Nvidia is not named as a defendant. Groq's spokesperson called the lawsuit meritless and said the transaction 'delivered exceptional value for Groq, our investors, and our employees.'\\n    Quote: Two former Groq shareholders are targeting the transaction in Delaware’s Chancery Court, where their lawsuit against Groq’s former board and CEO was unsealed Monday. … For Nvidia, which isn’t named as a defendant, the business logic was allegedly straightforward: heading off inroads by a startup flush with positive press about its prospect of exploiting its breakthrough “word-by-word” processing c\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1091; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1088 [fact; Bloomberg Law (news.bloomberglaw.com); original source Plaintiffs in Serebrin v. Ross (court filing); Bloomberg Law for the reported antitrust and congressional scrutiny ; from gnews:fe96202f1bce4a1421f91628 2026-10-05] The unsealed Serebrin v. Ross complaint describes Nvidia's $20 billion December Groq transaction — buying most of Groq's assets and hiring most of its staff — as an attempt to end-run the antitrust concerns that would normally greet any acquisition by an overwhelming market leader, and the article reports that the deal has drawn antitrust and congressional scrutiny. The article places that deal among several recent Nvidia acquisitions aimed at shoring up its position in AI chips and related technology, including a $13 billion deal for AI startup Hugging Face announced the prior month.\\n    Quote: The deal last December, which has drawn antitrust and congressional scrutiny, is one of several recent corporate acquisitions aimed at growing and shoring up Nvidia’s dominant global position in the exploding trillion-dollar market for AI chips and related technology. The transactions also include its $13 billion deal for AI startup Hugging Face, which was announced last month. … The lawsuit zeroe\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e277; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1155 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] Nvidia is described by the Motley Fool article as currently acquiring Hugging Face to bolster its open-weight AI capabilities, with the deal referred to as a planned acquisition that could further strengthen its position in the AI ecosystem. The item gives no purchase price, signing date or closing date, and does not report the acquisition as completed.\\n    Quote: The company designs platforms for gaming and data centers and is currently acquiring Hugging Face to bolster its open-weight AI capabilities. … The company has been a major beneficiary of AI spending, and its planned acquisition of Hugging Face could further strengthen its position in the AI ecosystem.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA’s reported expectation that the definitive agreement would close in the first half of 2027, which sits at rung 1 because it is before the March 24, 2028 check date. The supplied reports that the acquisition was completed, including Hugging Face CEO Clem Delangue’s reported statement that he had sold the company, reinforce that forecast. No supplied evidence indicates a termination, abandonment, blocking prohibition, or delay beyond the check date. The evidence does not show a filing disclosing completion, so rung 1 remains a forecast rather than an established fact; that does not make rung 3 the most likely outcome.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's reported expectation that the deal would close in the first half of 2027, which is before the March 24, 2028 check date and therefore sits at rung 1. Reports that the acquisition was already completed—including Hugging Face CEO Clem Delangue's reported statement that he had sold the company—further favor completion by the deadline. The newer description of the deal as currently being acquired means completion is not consistently established in the supplied reporting, but it leaves substantial time for the expected closing and does not indicate termination, abandonment, or a blocking prohibition. The evidence therefore favors rung 1 as a forecast, though it does not establish the required NVIDIA filing disclosure.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e608\",\n    \"e695\",\n    \"e590\",\n    \"e483\",\n    \"e510\",\n    \"e916\",\n    \"e1155\",\n    \"e1088\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e277\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e364\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e392\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e458\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e483\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e495\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e497\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e502\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e503\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e504\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e510\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e590\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e608\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e670\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e695\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e697\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e698\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e797\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e804\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e916\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1016\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1078\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1087\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1088\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1155\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung is unchanged, but e1155 adds a relevant current-status description—Nvidia is \\\"currently acquiring\\\" Hugging Face—which qualifies the earlier reports describing the deal as completed. It does not overturn the first-half-2027 expected closing or the forecast of completion by the 2028 check date.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's reported expectation that the acquisition would close during the first half of 2027; that timing is before the check date and sits at rung 1.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies completion by the March 24, 2028 deadline, with the stated closing expectation placing it in the first half of 2027; several reports also describe it as already completed in September 2026. Those reports do not establish the required NVIDIA filing disclosure, so rung 1 is the forecast rather than a proven outcome.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 9,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\\\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will NVIDIA recognize revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by the end of FY2027, as disclosed in its quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K disclose that it has begun recognizing revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by 2027-01-31. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-09-23 and before 2027-01-31, NVIDIA formally abandons or cancels the Vera Rubin platform. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 53 items]\\n[Not shown: 37 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e85 [party_statement; Zacks.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:44b5d56e3c6718335d33ab2b 2026-09-23] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning the company to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment, as reported by Zacks.\\n    Quote: Additionally, the next-generation Vera Rubin platform has entered the full production stage, positioning NVIDIA to benefit from the next phase of AI infrastructure investment.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e90, e185, e251, e283, e291, e320, e343; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e89 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Nebius Group; CoreWeave ; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] Nebius Group announced another increase in prices for NVIDIA GPU rentals, and CoreWeave said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices; the article cites both as fresh evidence that demand for NVIDIA-powered computing remains strong. No price levels, capacity amounts or dates are given.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, demand from AI cloud providers remains robust as Nebius Group (NBIS) announced another increase in prices for Nvidia GPU rentals, while CoreWeave (CRWV) said it was continuing to contract new compute capacity at higher prices, providing fresh evidence that demand for Nvidia-powered computing remains strong.\\n- e90 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA management 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA management pointed to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin AI platform, and the article says continued AI infrastructure strength is accelerating NVIDIA's AI growth trajectory. The statement does not say Vera Rubin has begun volume customer shipments or revenue recognition.\\n    Quote: It's worth noting that Nvidia is not merely maintaining its AI growth trajectory; it is accelerating it, with management pointing to full production of its next-generation Vera Rubin platform and continued strength across AI infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e181 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Super Micro Computer 2026-09-24; from gnews:cd0056006ad115260ae2b646 2026-09-24] Super Micro Computer moved into commercial shipments of servers built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions that combine computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components; each rack design uses 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs, according to a GuruFocus report published 24 September 2026.\\n    Quote: the server maker moved into commercial shipments of systems built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, adding another data-center product to its AI infrastructure lineup. … The company is shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions, combining computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components. The rack design uses 7\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e859, e951; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e188 [party_statement; fool.com (article via Yahoo Finance); original source SpaceX (Elon Musk, CEO) ; from gnews:943a57daed49d866fe468605 2026-09-24] On SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, SpaceX CEO Elon Musk said SpaceX has decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA hardware because he considers the Vera Rubin architecture the best architecture and the best AI computer, citing close cooperation and partnership with NVIDIA; the article characterises this as making SpaceX (which owns xAI and its Grok model) exclusive to NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: During SpaceX's second-quarter earnings call, Musk had this to say about SpaceX's relationship with Nvidia: … We've decided to build exclusively on NVIDIA because we think the Vera Rubin architecture is the best architecture. We think it's the best AI computer, and we greatly value our close cooperation and partnership on many levels with NVIDIA. So, we're exclusive to NVIDIA.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e387, e396; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e208 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform began production shipments in the third quarter, according to Zacks; the item gives no volume, customer or revenue-recognition figure for the shipments.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin began production shipments in the third quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e234, e409, e598, e650, e721, e857; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e234 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA began production shipments of its Vera Rubin platform in August, with purchase orders already received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system OEM, according to a 24 September 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Production shipments began in August, with purchase orders already in from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud, and system OEM.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271, e413, e414, e556, e653, e774, e864, e865; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e243 [fact; Kieron Allen, Cloud Wars (cloudwars.com); original source Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA (joint announcement) ; from gnews:7c4283c55cf02ba9cfd9a835 2026-09-24] Amazon Web Services and NVIDIA announced an expansion of their long-standing partnership under which AWS will deploy 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across its infrastructure between 2027 and 2028, spanning NVIDIA's Blackwell Ultra, Rubin and Rubin Ultra architectures and deployed alongside NVIDIA Vera CPUs; the addition builds on the more than 1 million NVIDIA GPUs AWS had previously committed to deploying beginning in 2026, and Amazon said the aim is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure from frontier labs, global enterprises, start-ups and governments. The number of GPUs is a planned deployment, not reported deliveries or revenue.\\n    Quote: Now, the two powerhouses have announced a staggering expansion of this existing partnership which will, among other things — but this is certainly the standout headline — include the deployment of 2 million additional NVIDIA GPUs across AWS infrastructure between 2027 and 2028. … The aim, according to Amazon, is to meet surging demand for AI infrastructure “from frontier labs, global enterprises, \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e105; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e279 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing MarketWatch; original source Mehdi Hosseini, Susquehanna analyst (remarks to MarketWatch) ; from gnews:460160402f7a10882d1e925f 2026-09-25] Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini, in remarks reported by MarketWatch and cited by qz.com, said Micron's November-quarter (fiscal Q1 2026) sales forecast is strong enough to satisfy investors but will not fully reflect the benefits of the Nvidia product ramp; he said some estimated revenue is expected to shift toward next year instead of this year, implying the Nvidia-chip-related revenue payoff may be delayed.\\n    Quote: Micron $MU Technology posted record fiscal fourth-quarter revenue and issued strong guidance for the current quarter, but one analyst says investors may need to wait longer to see the full payoff from an Nvidia $NVDA chip launch. … Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini told MarketWatch that Micron 's November-quarter sales forecast looks strong enough to satisfy investors, though it won't fully refle\\n- e283 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:91b917994e36036e304616e8 2026-09-25] NVIDIA states that its Vera Rubin AI platform is already in full production, with production shipments beginning in August 2026 and purchase orders received from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM), as reported by MarketBeat.\\n    Quote: The trigger for this move could come from subsequent earnings reports and continued progress with Vera Rubin. The platform is already in full production, with NVIDIA reporting production shipments beginning in August and purchase orders from every major hyperscaler, AI cloud and system original equipment manufacturer (OEM).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e85; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e370 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Nvidia reported that in the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests its next-generation Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of its GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark and up to 2.5x on DeepSeek-R1, and described the Vera Rubin entry in its official MLPerf announcement as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\\n    Quote: In the latest MLPerf Inference v6.1 tests, Vera Rubin NVL72 delivered up to 3.7x the throughput of Nvidia’s GB300 NVL72 on the Qwen3-VL benchmark. On DeepSeek-R1, the improvement reached as much as 2.5x. … Nvidia detailed the results in its official MLPerf announcement , describing Vera Rubin’s entry as a preview submission ahead of broader deployment.\\n- e409 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA began production shipments of its newest data center system, Vera Rubin, in August 2026, according to the article.\\n    Quote: The company began production shipments of Vera Rubin, its newest data center system, in August 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e410 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to bring in about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e555, e764, e782, e813, e858; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e416 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects sales to its largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027 as Vera Rubin supply grows.\\n    Quote: Management expects sales to the largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027, as Vera Rubin supply grows.\\n- e551 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management said on its fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that inventory rose to $32 billion as the company prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\\n    Quote: On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said inventory rose to $32 billion as NVIDIA prepared for the Vera Rubin launch.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e588; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e555 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, according to Trefis.\\n    Quote: The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e633 [third_party_view; Adam Spatacco, fool.com; original source  ; from gnews:0e4e3f9b17e0cda6b72d47fc 2026-09-29] The article reports a forecast that Nvidia's next-generation Vera Rubin CPU architecture will reach $20 billion in sales next quarter.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, the next-generation Vera Rubin CPU architecture is forecast to reach $20 billion in sales next quarter.\\n- e659 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave announced at its CoreWeave Fully Connected event (held the week of the 30 September 2026 NVIDIA blog post) that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking are available on CoreWeave Cloud, making CoreWeave one of the first cloud providers to deliver the platform in customers' hands; per the announcement, Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected, running this week in San Francisco, CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking. Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin. … CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud, makin\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e751, e860, e894, e895; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e660 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave received its first NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks earlier in September 2026, before the 30 September 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Earlier this month, CoreWeave received its first Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks.\\n- e720 [fact; CRN; original source TD Synnex (CEO Patrick Zammit), with customer identity reported by CRN ; from gnews:84569ea837e5d2d93ff16093 2026-09-30] TD Synnex CEO Patrick Zammit said on the distributor's third-quarter earnings call (the Thursday before the 30 September 2026 article) that TD Synnex and Houston-based systems integrator Mark III Systems recently signed an agreement to support an NVIDIA AI factory powered by Vera Rubin NVL72 systems, which he described as 'one of the largest' enterprise AI factory infrastructure deployments expected to be delivered through the channel, bundling design, integration, deployment, Day 2 co-admin operations, financing and supply chain. CRN reported, citing what it learned, that the customer is a US-based Fortune 100 company. No contract value, system count or delivery date was disclosed.\\n    Quote: After TD Synnex revealed that Mark III Systems is set to deliver ‘one of the largest’ enterprise AI factory deployments in the channel, an executive at the systems integrator explained to CRN how its AI factory expertise and enterprise trends helped it seal the deal. … As hyperscalers and neoclouds begin to launch offerings based on Nvidia’s new flagship Vera Rubin rack-scale AI platform, one syst\\n- e751 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:2b6641fb175eb6c57e2bdff2 2026-09-30] CoreWeave (CRWV) announced at its Fully Connected AI conference in San Francisco that it is bringing NVIDIA (NVDA) Vera CPUs into its cloud portfolio for AI-agent workloads and will offer NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems for production use, with Cognition named as the first customer running workloads on the platform. CoreWeave said the Rubin systems are already operating on its cloud service, which it described as a move from infrastructure deployment toward commercial use, and that customers can access the hardware under its existing operating model for NVIDIA systems.\\n    Quote: The company is bringing NVIDIA ( NVDA ) Vera CPUs to its cloud portfolio for workloads tied to AI agents. It will also offer NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems for production use, with Cognition serving as the first customer running workloads on the platform. … CoreWeave said the Rubin systems are already operating on its cloud service, marking a move from infrastructure deployment toward commercial \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e753 [third_party_view; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source SemiAnalysis (AgentX data) ; from nvblog:productive-durable-fungible-ai-factories 2026-10-01] SemiAnalysis AgentX data cited in NVIDIA's blog states that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems deliver over 30x higher throughput per megawatt than NVIDIA GB300 NVL72 systems, and up to 45x lower cost per million tokens on the DeepSeek V4 Pro model.\\n    Quote: SemiAnalysis AgentX data shows NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems deliver over 30x higher throughput per megawatt than NVIDIA GB300 NVL72, and up to 45x lower cost per million tokens on the DeepSeek V4 Pro model.\\n- e764 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA said it started shipping its Vera Rubin platform in August 2026 and expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in fiscal Q3 2027.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA started shipping Vera Rubin in August and expects the platform to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in the third quarter.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e765 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA estimates revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure at approximately $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: The company estimates that revenue opportunity per gigawatt of AI infrastructure has increased from approximately $18 billion with Hopper to $25 billion with Blackwell and $40 billion with Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e775 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform is now in full production, and the article states revenue per gigawatt of deployed capacity has risen from $18 billion for Hopper to $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Revenue per gigawatt rose from $18 billion for Hopper to $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin, now in full production.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e782 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source  ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] The article estimates Vera Rubin will make up about 20% of NVIDIA's data center revenue in Q3 FY2027, with NVIDIA's next earnings report due November 17.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin should make up about 20% of data center revenue in Q3, and the next earnings report lands November 17.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e811 [fact; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:efb160acb7c94e14a52df9fa 2026-10-01] Nvidia launched its Vera CPU, a central processing unit positioned for agentic-AI workloads; the article reports the launch while noting that the agentic AI boom will likely raise CPU demand. The item gives no launch date, volume, customer or shipment timing.\\n    Quote: There is a significant opportunity in some of the niches it targets. For instance, the agentic AI boom will likely lead to increased demand for CPUs (central processing units), and to that end, Nvidia launched its Vera CPU.\\n- e813 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia began shipping its Rubin platform in August and expects Rubin to account for 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, which the company will report in November, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia began shipping Rubin in August and already expects it to account for 20% of data center revenue in the third quarter of fiscal 2027, which it will report in November.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e814 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e84d920e300dc3c29e501037 2026-10-01] Nvidia is guiding for 70% year-over-year revenue growth in fiscal 2028, which the article attributes to massive demand for its Rubin platform.\\n    Quote: Demand for Rubin is so massive that Nvidia is guiding for 70% year-over-year revenue growth in fiscal 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e832 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, fool.com; original source Keithen Drury (fool.com) 2026-10-01; from gnews:6793603da478ba6858e0827d 2026-10-01] In his 1 October 2026 column, Keithen Drury (fool.com) wrote that Nvidia's new Rubin-generation chips are starting to become available, will provide more advanced capabilities than prior generations, and that Nvidia will charge a higher price for them, which he called a built-in revenue escalator 'launching shortly'. No shipment volumes, dates or prices are given, and the claim is the author's characterisation rather than a company announcement.\\n    Quote: Even though competition is intensifying for which company has the best available option, Nvidia's new Rubin generation of chips will make an even greater impact than prior iterations. These computing units are starting to become available and will provide more advanced capabilities than prior generations. Nvidia will also charge a higher price tag for them, so it has a built-in revenue escalator t\\n- e857 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company began production shipments of its Vera Rubin AI data-center platform in August 2026.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA began production shipments in August 2026, management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e208; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e858 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management forecast on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that Vera Rubin would make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; the article notes this is a forecast, not a result.\\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. That is a forecast, not a result.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e410; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e859 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Supermicro 2026-09-23; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] Supermicro said on 23 September 2026 that it is shipping racks built on NVIDIA's Vera Rubin platform.\\n    Quote: Supermicro said on September 23, 2026 that it is shipping racks built on Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e860 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source CoreWeave 2026-09-30; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] CoreWeave, a cloud company, said on 30 September 2026 that one of its customers runs production workloads on NVIDIA's Vera Rubin system.\\n    Quote: CoreWeave, a cloud company, said on September 30 that one of its customers runs production workloads on the system.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e864 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management says the company cannot build enough to meet demand, called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year, and said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that its entire supply chain is running flat out.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA cannot build enough to meet demand, management says. Management called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, and expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year. Asked on the fiscal Q2 2027 call what holds supply back, management said its entire supply chain is running flat out.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e236; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e894 [fact; wccftech.com, quoting NVIDIA VP Ian Buck; original source NVIDIA and CoreWeave ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] NVIDIA and CoreWeave announced at CoreWeave Fully Connected 2026 that the NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 platform is available on CoreWeave Cloud, with NVIDIA VP Ian Buck stating that CoreWeave is bringing Vera Rubin NVL72 into production even as its older V100 GPUs still run customer workloads nearly a decade after Volta launched.\\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected 2026, NVIDIA and CoreWeave announce the availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud. … “NVIDIA accelerated computing delivers value across generations,” said Ian Buck, vice president of hyperscale and high-performance computing at NVIDIA. “CoreWeave’s NVIDIA V100 GPUs are still running customer workloads nearly a decade after Volta launched, even as Core\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e895 [fact; wccftech.com; original source CoreWeave / Cognition ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] Cognition is the first customer to secure installations of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 servers at CoreWeave, and CoreWeave stood up a production Vera Rubin cluster for it in days, with Cognition scaling to thousands of GPUs within nine months; Cognition had earlier benchmarked Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in September, recording a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads.\\n    Quote: Cognition is the first to secure a hold on these latest server installations at CoreWeave, and has been able to scale to thousands of GPUs within nine months. Earlier in September, Cognition was the first to benchmark Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in inference, and was able to record a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads. … In days, CoreWeave stood up a productio\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e659; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e907 [party_statement; Daniel Foelber (The Motley Fool); original source Nvidia (guidance); margin/demand attribution by the article's author ; from gnews:d9f1147865cff881040d3dd0 2026-10-02] The article states that Nvidia is already guiding for a 70% increase in revenue for fiscal 2028, and the author attributes continued high margins to what he calls insatiable demand for the Vera Rubin compute platform.\\n    Quote: For fiscal 2028, Nvidia is already guiding for a 70% increase in revenue. … And its margins should remain sky high thanks to insatiable demand for the Vera Rubin compute platform .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e951 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Supermicro 2026-09-23; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] On Sept. 23, Supermicro said it had started shipping racks built on Nvidia's newest platform, Vera Rubin; each rack carries 72 Rubin GPUs.\\n    Quote: On Sept. 23, Supermicro said it had started shipping racks, the standard cabinets that hold servers inside a data center, built on Nvidia's newest platform, Vera Rubin. Each rack carries 72 Rubin GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e181; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e978 [fact; barchart.com; original source Akamai Technologies 2026-09-24; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai Technologies said on 24 September 2026 that it had significantly expanded its partnership with Anthropic; Anthropic committed to spend $11.6 billion over seven years on dedicated cloud capacity and related services for its growing CPU workloads, with an option to commit an additional $9 billion if conditions are met, bringing the total value to about $20 billion. The article says the deal does not directly involve Nvidia based on public disclosure.\\n    Quote: A major new agreement between cloud infrastructure provider Akamai Technologies (AKAM) and frontier AI lab Anthropic could add another piece to that story. The nearly $12 billion deal does not directly involve Nvidia, at least based on what has been publicly disclosed so far. … On Sept. 24, cloud infrastructure company Akamai Technologies significantly expanded its partnership with frontier AI lab\\n- e979 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source GF Securities (Henry Huang) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] GF Securities analyst Henry Huang said the Akamai-Anthropic CPU cloud deal is expected to be on Nvidia's Vera CPUs, which he said Akamai had previously disclosed as its planned platform for agentic workloads; the article presents this as an expectation, not confirmation.\\n    Quote: \\\"The deal is expected to be on Nvidia's Vera CPUs, which Akamai previously disclosed as its planned platform for agentic workloads,\\\" according to GF Securities analyst Henry Huang.\\n- e980 [fact; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Nvidia said in an August 2026 blog post that it had delivered Vera CPU systems to Anthropic; the article also notes Nvidia had named Anthropic among AI labs planning to adopt Vera at GTC Taipei in May. The delivery is reported as completed, but no volume or revenue is disclosed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia said at GTC Taipei that Anthropic was among the AI labs planning to adopt Vera, and the chipmaker added in an August blog post that it had delivered Vera CPU systems to Anthropic.\\n- e981 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source Akamai Technologies ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] Akamai expects roughly $5.5 billion of capital expenditures tied to Anthropic's commitment, including an additional $1.7 billion in 2026 to secure and pre-purchase critical supply-chain components; the article says not all of that spending will go toward Nvidia's CPUs because Akamai also needs memory, ABF substrates and other components.\\n    Quote: Akamai expects roughly $5.5 billion in capital expenditures tied to Anthropic's commitment, including an additional $1.7 billion in 2026 to secure and pre-purchase critical supply-chain components. It is worth noting that not all of that spending will go toward Nvidia's CPUs, as Akamai will also need to purchase memory, ABF substrates, and other components.\\n- e982 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA (CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:6121a00939cb0069709b5c54 2026-10-03] On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said trailing-12-month revenue from Grace CPUs had already exceeded $5 billion and that next-generation Vera was in full production; she said Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM, continues to see demand for roughly $20 billion of server CPUs, and preliminarily expects CPU revenue to more than double year-over-year in fiscal 2028.\\n    Quote: Nvidia CFO Colette Kress said on the Q2 earnings call last month that trailing-12-month revenue from Grace CPUs had already exceeded $5 billion, while next-generation Vera was in full production. Kress said Nvidia expects Vera to be deployed by every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab, and system OEM, adding that the company continues to see demand for roughly $20 billion of server CPUs. She also\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e632; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin. NVIDIA says Vera Rubin delivers 35X lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra. NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion per gigawatt of revenue for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e117; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1060 [fact; Brett Schafer, Fool.com; original source Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC) ; from gnews:654a1658fd6ce4b1694951fc 2026-10-04] Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), the sole at-scale manufacturer of Nvidia's chips, reported revenue of $143 billion over the past 12 months and revenue up 34% year over year in the most recent quarter, with its high-performance-compute (HPC) segment rising 20% quarter over quarter and now at 66% of the overall business, per Fool.com's account.\\n    Quote: Revenue was $143 billion during the past 12 months and rose 34% year-over-year last quarter. The company is seeing booming demand from its high performance compute (HPC) segment, which increased 20% quarter-over-quarter and now makes up 66% of the overall business.\\n- e1098 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) 2026-10-05; from gnews:3b878790fc298c0b29ba8205 2026-10-05] Foxconn, described as Nvidia's biggest server maker, reported third-quarter 2026 revenue up 47% to T$3.03 trillion ($95.4 billion), above the LSEG SmartEstimate of T$2.83 trillion — a supplier volume signal for Nvidia's AI server business, though the item does not specify which Nvidia platform drove it.\\n    Quote: This morning, Foxconn, Nvidia's biggest server maker, reported third-quarter revenue up 47% to T$3.03 trillion ($95.4 billion), above the LSEG SmartEstimate of T$2.83 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1180; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1101 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA (product status reported by fool.com) ; from gnews:a6cfdb974af32e5f975e0d4f 2026-10-05] NVIDIA's GPU release cadence continues: after Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra launched in recent years, the release of the current Vera Rubin system is now unfolding, according to fool.com's 5 October 2026 article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has pledged to update its GPUs on an annual basis, and it's following through on this, with Blackwell and Blackwell Ultra launched in recent years and the current release of the Vera Rubin system now unfolding.\\n- e1125 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said each new product generation brings in more money for the same data-center capacity, putting revenue per gigawatt at roughly $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Each new product generation also brings in more money for the same amount of data center capacity. Management put revenue per gigawatt at roughly $18 billion for Hopper, $25 billion for Blackwell and $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e488; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1148 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] NVIDIA itself expects its volume sales to double, an expectation the MarketBeat article ties to the Vera Rubin release and to the next wave of data center construction.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA itself expects volume sales to double, underpinned by the Vera Rubin release.\\n- e1177 [fact; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence (AMI), the AI infrastructure platform set up by promoters of Greenko, announced on 5 October 2026 two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems, on top of its inaugural commitment announced 25 August 2026 for 9,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs at its first AI Factory in Hyderabad. The follow-on capacity totals about 70 MW at Malaysia and India with delivery scheduled for Q2 2027, and the three orders together bring AMI's committed frontier compute to approximately 29,000 Rubin GPUs and close to 100 MW, which AMI describes as making it among the earliest and largest committed buyers of Rubin GPUs outside North America.\\n    Quote: Two further binding orders of ~20,000 NVIDIA GPUs take AMI's committed NVIDIA Vera Rubin capacity to approximately ~29,000 GPUs and close to 100 MW, part of its global development pipeline of ~400 MW … today announced two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs , to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems. … These orders are in addition to the AMI's inaugural\\n- e1178 [party_statement; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence said it plans to bring an additional 300 MW of compute capacity to market in the next 15 months, requiring capital expenditure of more than $20 billion on top of the $6 billion already committed for its initial 100 MW. It said it has developed a pipeline of about 400 MW of Compute-as-a-Service capacity across India, the United States, Europe and Malaysia, that the three Rubin GPU orders are the first committed tranche of that pipeline, and that the balance is under development for delivery in subsequent phases in 2027 and early 2028.\\n    Quote: AMI plans to bring additional 300 MWs of capacity to market in the next 15 months, involving capital expenditure of more than $20+ billion in addition to the $6 billion already committed for the initial $100 MW. … AMI has developed a pipeline of ~400 MW of Compute-as-a-Service capacity across global locations, including India, the United States, Europe and Malaysia. The three orders placed to date\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will make up about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, which is rung 1 because Q3 falls within FY2027. That guidance alone is a forecast, not proof of recognized revenue. But the evidence also says production shipments began in August, Supermicro is shipping Vera Rubin racks, and CoreWeave has the platform running production workloads for Cognition; these make the guided Q3 revenue share more likely to be realized and disclosed by the due date. The supply-bottleneck and possible-delay evidence is a counterweight, but does not outweigh the near-term guidance and customer deployment evidence. The updated capacity and DRAM themes add further caution; they do not move the forecast below rung 1.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; because Q3 results are due before 2027-01-31, that reference sits at rung 1. The forecast remains rung 1: the company says production shipments began in August, its guide gives a substantial Q3 revenue contribution, and customers and suppliers report Vera Rubin systems in production or shipping. These facts make recognition in Q3 and disclosure by the due date more likely than not, although the evidence shown does not itself establish that revenue has already been recognized. Supply constraints and the AMI orders' later delivery schedule temper the forecast but do not overturn it; there is no evidence of abandonment to support rung 2.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e410\",\n    \"e555\",\n    \"e764\",\n    \"e858\",\n    \"e857\",\n    \"e181\",\n    \"e659\",\n    \"e660\",\n    \"e895\",\n    \"e416\",\n    \"e1177\",\n    \"e236\",\n    \"e279\",\n    \"e864\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e85\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e89\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e90\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e181\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e188\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e208\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e234\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e236\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e243\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e279\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e283\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e370\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e409\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e410\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e416\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e551\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e555\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e633\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e659\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e660\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e720\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e751\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e753\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e764\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e765\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e775\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e782\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e811\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e813\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e814\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e832\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e857\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e858\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e859\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e860\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e864\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e894\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e895\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e907\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e951\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e978\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e979\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e980\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e981\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e982\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1046\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1060\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1098\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1101\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1125\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1148\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1177\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1178\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 1, but the newly disclosed firm orders for about 20,000 additional Rubin GPUs, bringing AMI's commitments to about 29,000, add a specific customer-demand fact to the supporting picture. Their stated Q2 2027 delivery schedule is beyond this thesis's deadline, so they strengthen the demand context without changing the implied Q3 FY2027 revenue outcome.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v10\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v11\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v9\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. This is the rung 1 reference: Q3 results are expected before the 2027-01-31 due date.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely outcome is that NVIDIA recognizes Vera Rubin revenue in Q3 FY2027 and discloses it in its quarterly results, with the guided contribution of about 20% of Data Center revenue. This is inferred from the guide together with production shipments beginning in August and customer production deployments; it is not yet established by a reported result in the evidence shown.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 10,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v12 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"Whether the proposed up-to-$125 billion hardware residual-value guarantee is signed and disclosed, and at what amount, versus remaining a proposal or being reduced or withdrawn.\\\", 2028, due 2028-04-03): Will Nvidia sign and disclose a finalized hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform by 2028-04-03?\\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: By 2028-04-03, Nvidia has entered into a definitive hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform and disclosed its finalized terms in a definitive agreement or SEC filing. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-03 and before 2028-04-03, Nvidia formally abandons or withdraws the proposed hardware residual-value guarantee without finalizing it. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-03 and stands on 2028-04-03, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-04-03.\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 13 items]\\n- e1011 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August 2026 a plan with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR—to build an AI infrastructure financing platform intended to attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital, and proposed sharing up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is also building out a chip-based financing market, partnering with Wall Street financial firms to create large-scale AI infrastructure financing platforms. … In August, Nvidia announced plans with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR—to build a platform that would attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital. Nvidia also proposed\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1013 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years, and that starting in 2025 it shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years.\\n    Quote: Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years. Starting in 2025, the company also shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years.\\n- e1017 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source Unnamed lenders (reported) ; from gnews:01dc582fbe53203eaeac2c61 2026-10-03] The article reports, without naming the lenders or citing a source, that some lenders involved in Nvidia's AI infrastructure financing plan are seeking stronger collateral than the company originally outlined, as they try to calculate how long chip revenue will be sustainable.\\n    Quote: However, Nvidia's ambitious financing plan has sparked debate on Wall Street about the real value of its advanced chips and associated infrastructure. Some lenders are seeking stronger collateral than originally outlined by the company, even given its industry-leading processing power, as they try to calculate how long chip revenue will be sustainable.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e961; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1034 [rumour; bhaskarenglish.in (citing unnamed banking sources and credit managers); original source  ; from gnews:4588a36d112063025acf1bb1 2026-10-03] According to unnamed banking sources and credit managers, some lenders are demanding substantial guarantees from NVIDIA against the value of its advanced chips and infrastructure, and are assessing how long the revenue generated from those chips will remain sustainable, amid a debate among analysts and lenders over NVIDIA's financing plan.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, a debate has begun among analysts and lenders on the US market, or Wall Street, over Nvidia's financing plan. … According to banking sources and credit managers, some lenders are demanding substantial guarantees from the company against the value of its advanced chips and infrastructure. They are assessing how long the revenue generated from these chips will remain sustainable.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e961; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1042 [rumour; Financial Times (via Yahoo Finance); original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon is in talks, according to a Financial Times report, to move $8 billion of NVIDIA Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle that would sell debt to outside investors, buy the chips and lease them back to Amazon, with Amazon keeping up to 10% of the equity; the chips are already installed in data centers across at least five states.\\n    Quote: Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ) is in talks to move $8 billion of NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle, the Financial Times reported. The vehicle would sell debt to outside investors, buy the chips, and lease them back to Amazon. The hardware stays where it is. It is already installed in data centers across at least five states, and Amazon would keep up to 10% of the \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\\n    Quote: Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin. NVIDIA says Vera Rubin delivers 35X lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra. NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion per gigawatt of revenue for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e117; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1047 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon (Andy Jassy) ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said on the July earnings call that servers take \\\"a little less than three years\\\" to break even and last at least five to six years.\\n    Quote: On the July earnings call, Jassy said servers take \\\"a little less than three years\\\" to break even and last at least five to six years.\\n- e1049 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA is building financing structures similar to Amazon's sale-leaseback and signed up six asset managers and banks to raise more than $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure; management said: \\\"We know some will call this circular financing.\\\"\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is building similar structures. … It signed up six asset managers and banks to raise more than $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure. Management said: \\\"We know some will call this circular financing.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1129 [fact; Morningstar; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Nvidia has pledged $500 billion to its partnership with Apollo Global Management, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, aimed at supporting enterprises such as AI laboratories, cloud storage providers and data-center operators that purchase or operate Nvidia-based compute. Morningstar notes the partnership is not a fully committed fund and that Nvidia is not a direct investor, with funding expected to come primarily from banks, insurers, asset managers and private credit investors.\\n    Quote: Nvidia alone has pledged $500 billion in its partnership with Apollo Global Management APO , BlackRock BLK , Blackstone BX , Brookfield BN , Goldman Sachs GS , and KKR KKR . This and other efforts are intended to support enterprises—such as AI laboratories, cloud storage providers, and data center operators purchasing or operating Nvidia-based compute—while positioning AI infrastructure as an asse\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\\n    Quote: In addition, Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings (equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion), if applied across the full target. The company’s actual balance sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e990; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1145 [third_party_view; Trefis (citing Reuters); original source Reuters 2026-10-01; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] Reuters reported on 1 October 2026 that banks and investors appear to value NVIDIA's chips, and the infrastructure around them, at less than NVIDIA does; the article offers no figures and characterises the outside money as more cautious.\\n    Quote: Outside money looks more cautious. Reuters reported on October 1, 2026, that banks and investors seem to value NVIDIA’s chips, and the infrastructure around them, at less than NVIDIA does.\\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\\n    Quote: Nvidia invests in customers that buy its chips. In August, it announced preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, CNBC reported.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e989; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1164 [fact; The Motley Fool (Daniel Foelber); original source NVIDIA (with a consortium of six financial institutions) ; from gnews:b0cf215ea487392e67e586be 2026-10-05] The Motley Fool reported that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, an arrangement that enables customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly. The item does not state how much of the $500 billion Nvidia itself bears or whether Nvidia guarantees any of the financing.\\n    Quote: Demand is so massive that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, enabling customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 1; reasons: The reference is Nvidia’s proposal to share up to $125 billion in hardware residual-value risk, which is rung 3: a proposal is neither a definitive agreement nor finalized disclosed terms. The most likely outcome by April 3, 2028 is that Nvidia signs and discloses a definitive guarantee for some non-zero amount, likely within that proposed cap. The evidence supports an active financing platform and lender demand for guarantees, while Amazon’s reported sale-leaseback talks show a concrete financing structure; none establishes that Nvidia has signed its own guarantee. Hardware obsolescence and useful-life concerns are meaningful execution risks, but do not outweigh the proposal, lender pressure, and time remaining. The evidence does not allow a reliable estimate of the finalized amount, so I\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is Nvidia’s announced proposal to share up to $125 billion of hardware residual-value risk; a proposal without finalized terms sits at rung 3, Still open. I forecast rung 1 because the explicit proposal, six-partner financing platform, and reported lender demand for guarantees make eventual execution more likely than either formal withdrawal or remaining unresolved through April 3, 2028. Morningstar’s estimate implies a conditional maximum of $125 billion (25% of the full $500 billion target), not a committed or finalized amount. Amazon’s reported $8 billion sale-leaseback is evidence that a comparable financing structure is being explored, not evidence that Nvidia has guaranteed it. Hardware obsolescence, lender caution, and the preliminary status of the agreements temper the forecast. No evidence shows a formal abandonment after October 3, 2026, as rung 2 requires.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e1011\",\n    \"e1017\",\n    \"e1013\",\n    \"e1042\",\n    \"e1046\",\n    \"e1047\",\n    \"e1049\",\n    \"e1129\",\n    \"e1130\",\n    \"e1145\",\n    \"e1154\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1011\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1013\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1017\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1034\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1042\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1046\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1047\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1049\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1129\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1130\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1145\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1154\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1164\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 1, but the recent evidence adds a conditional exposure estimate of up to $125 billion and more detail about the platform’s preliminary status and financing structures. The estimate sharpens the possible scale, not the amount Nvidia is forecast to finalize.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v10\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v9\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v3\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"Nvidia’s stated proposal is to share up to $125 billion in hardware residual-value risk; because it is not a definitive agreement with finalized disclosed terms, that reference sits at rung 3, Still open. No market consensus figure is supplied.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"I forecast a definitive, disclosed, non-zero guarantee by 2028-04-03, likely no greater than the proposed $125 billion ceiling. Morningstar’s 25% estimate applied to the full $500 billion target yields that conditional maximum; the evidence does not support a tighter estimate of the finalized amount.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 11,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 5,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 15,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 15,000,000,000 USD to below 30,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 30,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 50,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 34 items]\\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e60, e274; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\\n- e38 [fact; Financial Times (reported via Yahoo Finance); original source SB Energy 2026-09-22; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] SB Energy, the SoftBank-backed developer, slowed preparations for a planned public listing that had been discussed around a $50 billion valuation, according to a September 22 Financial Times report, while a $4.9 billion debt package faced weak demand and yields around 10%.\\n    Quote: SB Energy's planned public listing is turning into a stress test for the financing side of the AI buildout. The SoftBank-backed developer has slowed preparations for an IPO that had been discussed around a $50 billion valuation, according to a September 22 Financial Times report, while a $4.9 billion debt package has faced weak demand and yields around 10%.\\n- e39 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA had already invested $1.5 billion in SB Energy and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the developer's IPO.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) has already invested $1.5 billion and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the IPO.\\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61, e143; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e79 [third_party_view; Morningstar article author; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:4f0206f98cb85dd776005198 2026-09-23] Morningstar, listing the year's major Nvidia news in September 2026, describes Nvidia's \\\"role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments\\\" as one of the threads around which its dividend announcement was overlooked. The article does not specify the form of that financing (equity, debt, credit support or guarantees), the counterparties, or the period covered.\\n    Quote: Lost in a torrent of news about Nvidia NVDA this year—its role in financing $500 billion in artificial intelligence investments , its forecasts of 70% year-on-year revenue growth , and its acquisition of Hugging Face —was a dividend announcement.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e118, e144; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e92 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] AI cloud provider Nscale filed for a U.S. initial public offering on 18 September 2026 disclosing that it lost $1.02 billion in the first six months of 2026 on revenue of just $140.6 million — the financial condition of the counterparty to which NVIDIA is committing $1 billion of convertible financing.\\n    Quote: Nscale filed for a U.S. initial public offering on September 18 with one number that makes the AI-cloud boom look radioactive: the company lost $1.02 billion in the first six months of 2026 on just $140.6 million of revenue.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e194; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e93 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing for AI cloud provider Nscale, the IPO candidate whose filing the article discusses; the article notes this gives NVIDIA direct capital exposure in addition to its role as hardware supplier.\\n    Quote: Yet NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing, while Microsoft Corporation (NASDAQ: MSFT ) is expected to become one of Nscale's most important customers. … Nvidia can still benefit from Nscale's expansion by selling the picks and shovels, although its $1 billion financing commitment gives it direct capital exposure as well.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e94 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] Nscale disclosed that as of 31 August 2026 it had $103.4 billion of active and contracted total contract value under long-term take-or-pay contracts with a weighted-average contract life of about 5.7 years, operating in 14 regions with more than 10 gigawatts of potential power capacity — the contracted demand base underlying the Nscale build-out NVIDIA supplies and is financing.\\n    Quote: As of August 31, Nscale had $103.4 billion of active and contracted total contract value under long-term take-or-pay contracts, with a weighted-average contract life of about 5.7 years. It operates in 14 regions and has more than 10 gigawatts of potential power capacity.\\n- e95 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source Nscale 2026-09-18; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] Nscale disclosed that a single customer generated 52% of its first-half 2026 revenue, and its future plan leans heavily on Microsoft and Anthropic, indicating concentrated demand behind its contract book.\\n    Quote: Nscale disclosed that one customer generated 52% of first-half revenue, and its future plan leans heavily on Microsoft and Anthropic.\\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136, e141; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e135 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); Jensen Huang quoted; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers, and CEO Jensen Huang acknowledged the scale of that support while saying the company does not see it as 'circular financing'.\\n    Quote: The louder concern is circular financing. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers. CEO Jensen Huang addressed the skepticism directly: \\\"We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e193 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing the Financial Times and Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale, the Nvidia-backed 'neocloud' headquartered in London, filed for a New York listing in the week before 24 September 2026 at a valuation of up to $35 billion, according to the Financial Times; it is asking investors to value it at as much as that on the promise that $103 billion in contracts, much of them not yet firm, will turn into revenue on schedule.\\n    Quote: This time it’s Nscale, the Nvidia-backed “neocloud” that filed for a New York listing last week at a valuation of up to $35 billion, according to the Financial Times . … Despite those losses, Nscale is asking Wall Street to value it at as much as $35 billion on the promise that $103 billion in contracts, much of them not yet firm, will turn into revenue on schedule.\\n- e194 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's S-1 reported a net loss of $1.02 billion for the six months to June 30, 2026, and at one point the company's own management flagged 'substantial doubt' about its ability to continue as a going concern.\\n    Quote: Per the S-1, the company posted a net loss of $1.02 billion in the six months to June 30, 2026—and at one point, its own management flagged “substantial doubt” about its ability to continue as a going concern.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e92; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e195 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's S-1; original source Nscale 2026-09-15; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] On Sept. 15, 2026, Nscale signed a subscription agreement for a $3.1 billion financing round, of which Nvidia itself is taking $1 billion in convertible notes or non-voting shares.\\n    Quote: On Sept. 15, Nscale signed a subscription agreement for a $3.1 billion financing round, of which Nvidia itself is taking $1 billion in convertible notes or non-voting shares.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e93; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n- e200 [fact; BioSpace, citing Basecamp Research's news release; original source Basecamp Research (issuer) and NVIDIA (participating investor) 2026-09-23; from gnews:c20b8b53b03e35fcff0718ce 2026-09-24] Basecamp Research closed a $140 million series C round, announced in a news release on Wednesday, in which NVIDIA participated as an investor; the oversubscribed round was led by venture capital firm S32. The item does not disclose the size of NVIDIA's individual investment.\\n    Quote: Basecamp Research has closed a $140 million series C fundraising to advance its pipeline of therapies designed using machine learning models. … The London- and Boston-based company is backed by NVIDIA, which participated in the oversubscribed round that was led by the venture capital firm S32, according to a news release on Wednesday .\\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\\n- e237 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs, and management concedes some will call this circular financing while saying the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\n    Quote: Some of that demand also leans on NVIDIA’s own balance sheet. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs. Management concedes some will call this circular financing and says the compute can be redeployed to other customers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e273 [fact; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs, according to Trefis.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA has also invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e274 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA's balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA's business in fiscal 2028; Trefis notes that part of the business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\\n    Quote: Management expects AI labs backed by NVIDIA’s balance sheet to make up roughly a quarter of NVIDIA’s business in fiscal 2028. That part of NVIDIA’s business would rest on customers NVIDIA helps fund.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e328 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia is in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering, according to Reuters, and an unnamed source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion; the article describes it as a possible second $10 billion check.\\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) might be gearing up to write Anthropic a second $10 billion check.\\nThe chipmaker's in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering (IPO) , Reuters said this month, a deal that could deepen its ties to a major customer. A source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion.\\n- e329 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic is reportedly seeking to raise as much as $100 billion in an IPO that might value it at roughly $2 trillion, and Reuters has since said the listing may slip past the U.S. midterm elections in November; the article says the plans may change and neither company has confirmed the talks.\\n    Quote: The artificial intelligence (AI) company behind the Claude models is reportedly looking to raise as much as $100 billion in a listing that might value it at roughly $2 trillion. Reuters has since said that the IPO may slip past the U.S. midterm elections in November. Of course, the plans might still change, and neither company has confirmed the talks.\\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e372 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source CoreWeave ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] CoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, which the article describes as another large financing round for an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity and as evidence that GPU expansion is translating into balance-sheet pressure for the AI cloud operator.\\n    Quote: CoreWeave recently upsized a convertible-note offering from $3 billion to $3.7 billion, adding another large financing round to an industry already consuming enormous amounts of debt and equity. CoreWeave’s latest convertible financing showed how quickly GPU expansion is translating into balance-sheet pressure.\\n- e373 [fact; cryptorank.io; original source Nebius (as reported by cryptorank.io) ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] The article reports that Nebius has also turned to billions in financing and that the broader AI infrastructure boom is increasingly being funded with debt.\\n    Quote: The same issue extends across the sector. Nebius has also turned to billions in financing , while the broader AI infrastructure boom is increasingly being funded with debt .\\n- e374 [fact; cryptorank.io citing Reuters; original source Reuters ; from gnews:844106b7a25ecc1175290290 2026-09-26] Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Bond investors are noticing. Reuters reported this week that AI-related borrowers are increasingly paying wider spreads as investors question how much additional capital the sector will require.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 2]\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-09-26; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's Q3 FY2027 guidance of $108 billion in revenue with no China Data Center compute revenue; as the near-term baseline, that sits at rung 3, though it does not decide the January 31, 2027 outcome. The evidence makes a two-sided opening by the deadline less likely: U.S. permissi\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-09-26; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is nearly $800 billion for calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. The evidence still points to about $800 billion: the later $1.3 trillion estimate is for 2027, and the $750 billion global AI-infrastructure estimate is not the same group or spending category. Alphabet's $200 billion data\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The question opened without a quantified market reference, so its reference is the ladder's no-reference baseline: rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn). The strongest quantified anchor remains NVIDIA guarantee obligations capped at $108.5bn, which is above rung 5's $50bn boundary; the $105bn OpenAI guarantee is treated as part of, not additional to, that aggregate. New capacity and revenue-support disclosures reinforce that support is being extended, but provide no dollar amount to add to the cap. The $860.3m Nscale lease guarantee is also not added on top of the aggregate, and NVIDIA's disclosed equity/convertible investments are not the commitments asked about. The export-controls theme modestly restrains the outlook, while the high AI-capex theme supports it; neither changes the quantified anc\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 1,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the no-figure baseline, rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn). The $108.5bn guarantee-obligation cap remains the strongest quantified anchor and is above rung 5's $50bn threshold; the $105bn OpenAI guarantee and the $860.3m Nscale guarantee are not added on top of that aggregate, while the newly described capacity and revenue-support arrangements have no disclosed dollar amounts.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e22\",\n    \"e23\",\n    \"e41\",\n    \"e42\",\n    \"e44\",\n    \"e60\",\n    \"e61\",\n    \"e93\",\n    \"e114\",\n    \"e136\",\n    \"e143\",\n    \"e196\",\n    \"e220\",\n    \"e328\",\n    \"e335\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e18\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e22\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e23\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e38\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e39\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e41\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e42\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e44\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e60\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e61\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e79\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e92\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e93\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e94\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e95\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e114\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e135\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e136\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e143\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e193\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e194\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e195\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e196\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e200\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e220\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e237\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e273\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e274\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e328\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e329\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e335\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e372\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e373\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e374\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and its key quantified anchor are unchanged: the $108.5bn cap remains above the top-rung boundary, and the additional capacity and revenue-support disclosures do not quantify amounts to add to it. Equity investments and financing commitments are distinct from the guarantees asked about.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v3\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"No quantified market reference was supplied when the question opened; the reference is therefore the no-reference baseline, rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies disclosed guarantee exposure remains above $50bn, anchored by NVIDIA's stated maximum guarantee-obligation cap of $108.5bn. Treat the $105bn OpenAI guarantee as part of that aggregate rather than additive; the other newly described support arrangements lack dollar amounts from which to derive an increment.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 12,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v2 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): How much will NVIDIA report in purchase obligations and supply commitments in its FY2027 Form 10-K disclosure?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 75,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 75,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 125,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 125,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 29 items]\\n[Not shown: 8 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\n- e106 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts point to the company's growth doubling next year, but that NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply-constrained; NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter report forecast 70% sales growth for fiscal 2028, and leadership said sales could double in fiscal 2028 if constraints on the supply of commodities needed for its hardware were overcome.\\n    Quote: In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips. … But what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware. … On Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 202\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e108 [third_party_view; fool.com (article author); original source Micron Technology (as reported) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\\n    Quote: For example, Micron Technology 's memory chips are having a significant problem, with demand far outpacing supply. That's led to skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron, and global supply constraints.\\n- e113 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists supply commitments of $279 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e202, e526, e683, e766, e777, e1048, e1062, e1073; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e202 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity as of the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products, Zacks reports.\\n    Quote: At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. … Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e271, e413, e414, e556, e653, e774, e864, e865; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e271 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028, as summarised by Trefis from the latest earnings call.\\n    Quote: Management expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e236; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e272 [party_statement; Trefis (trefis.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a6d9db55f2f71f072c5ba33c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA reset its gross margin expectations after memory prices rose more than it expected, guiding gross margin to 74% for fiscal Q3 2027, down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and expecting gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027.\\n    Quote: Memory prices rose more than NVIDIA expected, and NVIDIA reset its margin expectations. Gross margin is the share of sales left after production costs. NVIDIA guided it to 74% for fiscal Q3, down from 75% in Q2. NVIDIA expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e557, e682, e866, e1123, e1173; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e49 (); both are in force: Analyst estimates compiled by Bloomberg show NVIDIA's gross margin sliding to under 72% by the fourth quarter, with a recovery expected thereafter, as reported by qz.com; rising costs for key components such as memory chips are cited as a central factor.\\n- e393 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler (Fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e0b54b2bb227716c646f29f 2026-09-27] According to Fool.com columnist Geoffrey Seiler, Nvidia has locked in $500 billion worth of supply-constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), the memory used to optimize its AI chips. The item gives no counterparty, contract term or date for the commitment.\\n    Quote: It locked in $500 billion worth of supply constrained high-bandwidth memory (HBM), which is needed to optimize AI chips\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1040; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e523 [rumour; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Nvidia, Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek (as reported) ; from gnews:896d25da7b9b8340a736b2c0 2026-09-28] Nvidia, together with Apple, Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek, is reportedly raising its 2-nanometer orders at TSMC by 10% to 20%, according to the GuruFocus article of 28 September 2026, with the article tying the increase to customers preparing next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\\n    Quote: Apple ( NASDAQ:AAPL ), Nvidia ( NASDAQ:NVDA ), Advanced Micro Devices, Qualcomm and MediaTek are reportedly lifting 2nm orders by 10% to 20%. That gives TSMC additional visibility as customers prepare next-generation processors and AI accelerators.\\n- e619 [party_statement; taekim.substack.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation (CEO Jensen Huang) 2026-09-28; from gnews:0e3eeae7aedc281011283fa0 2026-09-29] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking to reporters at The Korea Society's annual gala in New York, said NVIDIA sits at the center of the largest industrial infrastructure build-out in human history and will generate increasing cash, while continuing to make strategic and operational investments and having to secure supply-chain commitments.\\n    Quote: “We’re really fortunate that NVIDIA is at the center of the largest industrial infrastructure build out in human history. We’re going to generate a lot of cash in the future. Now of course, we’ll continue to make strategic investments and operational investments and we have to secure supply chain commitments and so on and so forth. … But even then, with all of that, we’re going to generate more an\\n- e672 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, Nvidia CEO ; from gnews:4bf03094449259d91214e126 2026-09-30] On Nvidia's latest August 2026 earnings call, CEO Jensen Huang said he sees 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028, above analyst forecasts for 45% growth; he said the sales gain would be larger—in excess of 100%—if not for memory chip shortages.\\n    Quote: On its latest August earnings call, Huang said he sees 70% revenue growth for fiscal year 2028. This was above analyst forecasts for 45% growth. The sales gain would be larger — in excess of 100% — if not for memory chip shortages , Huang said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e730 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia's unconstrained demand growth—what customers would buy if supply were unlimited—is running above 100%, meaning Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\\n    Quote: Unconstrained demand growth, meaning what customers would buy if supply were unlimited, is running above 100%. In other words, Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\\n- e731 [rumour; Jensen Huang; original source Unnamed reports cited by Jensen Huang 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang cited reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes were up 27% from one month to the next.\\n    Quote: Huang pointed to reports he had seen that morning showing Grace Blackwell NVLink 72 rack volumes up 27% from one month to the next.\\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n- e766 [fact; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supplier commitments reached $279 billion at the end of fiscal Q2 2027, largely tied to memory procurement.\\n    Quote: At the same time, NVIDIA is committing significant resources to secure supply. Supplier commitments reached $279 billion at the end of the second quarter, largely tied to memory procurement.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e771 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA management expects fiscal 2028 revenues to grow approximately 70% and described the expectation as supply-constrained, indicating the outlook is limited partly by available supply rather than customer demand.\\n    Quote: Management expects fiscal 2028 revenues to grow approximately 70%. It also described this expectation as supply-constrained, suggesting that the company's current revenue outlook is based partly on available supply rather than a lack of customer demand.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e104; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e777 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, and guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e807 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:e35845cd1fb0497077e2d464 2026-10-01] NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the United States together with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron and Corning, according to the article; no timeline or contractual commitment amounts are given.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA plans to produce up to $500 billion of AI infrastructure in the U.S. with partners including TSMC, Foxconn, Wistron, Corning, and others.\\n- e873 [third_party_view; Dave Kovaleski, fool.com; original source fool.com (Dave Kovaleski) 2026-10-02; from gnews:edb22ee9082181453e2be651 2026-10-02] In this 2 October 2026 column, Fool.com's Dave Kovaleski forecasts that Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing (TSMC), Nvidia's main foundry, will generate some $33 billion of revenue from its largest customer, Nvidia, in calendar 2026. This is the author's forecast of Nvidia's annual payments to TSMC, not a reported actual or a disclosed contractual commitment.\\n    Quote: TSMC makes millions of advanced chips every year and will generate some $33 billion from its largest customer, Nvidia, in 2026.\\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\\n    Quote: According to management, Micron has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements, covering more than 35% of expected revenue through 2030. Customer financial commitments have soared to $32 billion from $22 billion last quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\\n    Quote: Last night, Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains, saying: … \\\"We do not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1027; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e108 (); both are in force: The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\\n- e953 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, of which $279 billion is tied mostly to memory procurement.\\n    Quote: Its own books carry weight too. Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, with $279 billion of that tied mostly to memory procurement.\\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \\\"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\\\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\\n    Quote: Major memory suppliers including Micron Technology have warned that as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained. These suppliers project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing prospects for early normalization. … Micron CEO San\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e934; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1040 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, The Motley Fool; original source SK Hynix (with Nvidia) ; from gnews:8cba65ded3fbaa69dfc8a83c 2026-10-04] The Motley Fool's Geoffrey Seiler reports that SK Hynix, which he describes as Nvidia's main high-bandwidth memory (HBM) supplier, recently formed a supply and co-development partnership with Nvidia under which SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years. The $500 billion figure is the article's estimate; no start date, volume or pricing terms are given.\\n    Quote: It is also the main HBM supplier to Nvidia, and the two companies recently formed an important supply and co-development partnership. As part of the deal, SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e393; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1062 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA's second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. The filing scheduled $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward, and said NVIDIA may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orders, though changes can carry costs.\\n    Quote: Its second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. … The filing allocated $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward. NVIDIA also said it may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orde\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1073 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement.\\n    Quote: Nvidia also disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement, while its CFO flagged \\\"extreme pricing conditions in memory\\\" as a margin headwind.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The implied amount is well above rung 5's $125bn floor, so it remains strongly bearish on this ladder. The reported $279bn is a current supply/capacity commitment balance, not the FY2027 year-end amount; the larger multi-year HBM estimate is not added in full because its timing, terms, and overlap with reported commitments are unspecified.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 5,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (neutral); no consensus or company forecast for the FY2027 Form 10-K commitment balance is supplied. The evidence instead points well above rung 5's $125bn floor: NVIDIA reported $279bn of supply and capacity commitments at FY2027 Q2, including $92bn scheduled for the remainder of FY2027. A simple run-off benchmark is therefore about $187bn ($279bn less $92bn), before any new commitments or cancellations. Ongoing supply constraints and strong guided growth make replenishment plausible, but do not quantify it; the separate $500bn multi-year memory estimate is not added in full because its timing, terms, and overlap with reported commitments are unspecified. Even the disclosed run-off benchmark remains in rung 5.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e202\",\n    \"e1062\",\n    \"e953\",\n    \"e1040\",\n    \"e106\",\n    \"e236\",\n    \"e1027\",\n    \"e730\",\n    \"e732\",\n    \"e44\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e44\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e106\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e108\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e113\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e136\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e202\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e236\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e271\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e272\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e393\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e523\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e619\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e672\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e730\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e731\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e732\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e766\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e771\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e777\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e807\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e873\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e933\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e934\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e953\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1027\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1040\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1048\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1062\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1073\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5, but the newly supplied filing schedule changes the key supporting calculation: $92bn scheduled for the rest of FY2027 gives a roughly $187bn run-off benchmark from the $279bn Q2 balance. That changes the implied outcome supporting the same rung.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v10\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v11\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v3\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v9\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"No market consensus or company guidance figure for the FY2027 Form 10-K commitment balance is supplied. The reference is therefore the opening no-reference rung, rung 3 (neutral: $75bn to below $100bn).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About $187bn as a disclosed-schedule run-off benchmark: $279bn of Q2 commitments less the $92bn scheduled for the remainder of FY2027. New commitments and cancellations could move the year-end balance, but the evidence does not quantify those changes; the likely outcome remains above $125bn.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 13,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \\\"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\\\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments?\\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 5,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 15,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 15,000,000,000 USD to below 30,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 30,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 50,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 54 items]\\n[Not shown: 22 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e60, e274, e791, e1050, e1144; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1138; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e39 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA had already invested $1.5 billion in SB Energy and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the developer's IPO.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) has already invested $1.5 billion and committed another $1.5 billion tied to the IPO.\\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61, e143, e1063; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e93 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing for AI cloud provider Nscale, the IPO candidate whose filing the article discusses; the article notes this gives NVIDIA direct capital exposure in addition to its role as hardware supplier.\\n    Quote: Yet NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing, while Microsoft Corporation (NASDAQ: MSFT ) is expected to become one of Nscale's most important customers. … Nvidia can still benefit from Nscale's expansion by selling the picks and shovels, although its $1 billion financing commitment gives it direct capital exposure as well.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e195; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e136, e141, e778; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e135 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); Jensen Huang quoted; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers, and CEO Jensen Huang acknowledged the scale of that support while saying the company does not see it as 'circular financing'.\\n    Quote: The louder concern is circular financing. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers. CEO Jensen Huang addressed the skepticism directly: \\\"We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\\n- e328 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia is in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering, according to Reuters, and an unnamed source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion; the article describes it as a possible second $10 billion check.\\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) might be gearing up to write Anthropic a second $10 billion check.\\nThe chipmaker's in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering (IPO) , Reuters said this month, a deal that could deepen its ties to a major customer. A source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e798; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e498 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has held talks with insurance companies about ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as it seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, including potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies that would protect lenders if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to cover the debt, the Financial Times reported, citing people familiar with the talks; the discussions are at an early stage and may not result in any deals.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA Corp (NASDAQ:NVDA) has held talks with insurance companies over ways to shift part of the financing risk tied to its chips as the chipmaker seeks to expand demand beyond major technology companies, the Financial Times reported on Tuesday. … The discussions include potential insurance for loans extended to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies, which could protect lenders if a borrower defaults and N\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e581, e599, e738; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e499 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, and is working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers, people familiar with the discussions told the Financial Times (Howden declined to comment); NVIDIA has also explored using insurance groups to syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, with the potential size of deals possibly exceeding the balance sheets of even large insurers, and has considered joining consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has shared data on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing power with at least one insurer, while people familiar with the discussions said the company was also working with broker Howden Re on a structure involving insurers. Howden declined to comment, the report said. … The potential structures could extend beyond traditional insurers. Nvidia has explored using insura\\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\\n    Quote: The insurance push comes after Nvidia offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e582, e828, e992; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e581 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] NVIDIA has held early-stage discussions with insurance companies, working with insurance broker Howden Re, about potential structures to transfer part of the financing risk associated with its chips, including potentially insuring loans made to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies so lenders are protected if a borrower defaults and NVIDIA chips pledged as collateral cannot be resold for enough to repay the debt. The effort is part of CEO Jensen Huang's push to make NVIDIA chips and AI infrastructure easier for external investors to finance; NVIDIA provided at least one insurer with information on chip depreciation and the expected future value of computing capacity, explored arrangements under which insurers could syndicate risk to hedge funds and other alternative investors, and considered participating in financing consortia alongside insurers, hedge funds and asset managers. The FT, citing people familiar with the matter, said the talks remain at an early stage and may not result in agreements; no pricing, counterparties or amount of financing that could be covered was disclosed.\\n    Quote: Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) has held discussions with insurance companies about potential structures to transfer part of the financing risk associated with its chips as the company seeks to expand demand for artificial intelligence infrastructure beyond major technology groups, the Financial Times reported. … The discussions include potentially insuring loans made to smaller \\\"neocloud\\\" companies, accordi\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e498; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e582 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] According to the FT-sourced material cited in the article, NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo. The item does not state the size of NVIDIA's backstop portion or the timing of the offer.\\n    Quote: According to the supplied material, Nvidia has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e500; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e778 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, and guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e791 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9126c31bb44c2cc3f54c641a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company is using its own balance sheet to support purchases by some AI labs, that those AI labs should contribute roughly a quarter of its business the following year, and that some will call this support circular financing (NVIDIA helping fund customers who then buy from NVIDIA).\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is also using its own balance sheet to support purchases by some AI labs. On the fiscal Q2 2027 call, management said those AI labs should contribute roughly a quarter of its business the following year. Management also said some people will call that support circular financing. The term means NVIDIA helps fund customers who then buy from NVIDIA.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e796 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA outlined more than US$500 billion of compute financing agreements and plans with partners including Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, intended to mobilize more than US$500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\\n    Quote: Nvidia outlined more than US$500b of compute financing agreements and plans with partners such as Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to mobilize over US$500b of third party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e830, e1011, e1049, e1129, e1137, e1164; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e798 [rumour; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA is in talks to invest up to US$10 billion in Anthropic, and is pursuing further, unsized investments in AI firms including Perplexity and Decart AI, according to the article; no terms or completion are reported.\\n    Quote: is in talks to invest up to US$10b in Anthropic, and is pursuing further investments in AI firms including Perplexity and Decart AI\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e328; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e828 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] Nvidia said independent financiers in its $500 billion infrastructure-financing push will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project, and that Nvidia may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities — a contingent credit backstop that would support lenders' recoveries on GPU-backed customer financing.\\n    Quote: Nvidia says independent financiers will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project. It may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e500; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e829 [third_party_view; Reuters, via gurufocus.com; original source Credit investors (cited by Reuters) ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] Credit investors, as reported by Reuters, said banks commonly depreciate GPUs over three to four years, while Nvidia argues its leading systems can keep earning money much longer; the article says that gap in collateral values could mean more equity upfront, tighter lending terms or higher borrowing costs for GPU-backed financing.\\n    Quote: According to Reuters, credit investors said banks commonly depreciate GPUs over three to four years, while Nvidia argues its leading systems can keep earning money much longer. That gap matters. Lower collateral values could mean more equity upfront, tighter lending terms or higher borrowing costs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e994; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e830 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] The article states that Nvidia's $500 billion infrastructure-financing push is a capital-mobilization target over time, rather than booked chip sales or a fully funded pool of cash.\\n    Quote: The $500 billion is a capital-mobilization target over time, rather than booked chip sales or a fully funded pool of cash.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e927 [fact; The Information, reported by TradingView/Stocktwits; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d91c54ec44da3c9a5cbef369 2026-10-02] Nvidia completed its final $10 billion investment in OpenAI's latest funding round, according to The Information, part of the strategic capital backing OpenAI and its plans to expand AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has also completed its final $10 billion investment, showcasing the size of the strategic capital backing OpenAI and its plans to expand AI infrastructure.\\n- e952 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia committed up to $100 billion to OpenAI in September 2025, but its final stake came to $30 billion, with another $10 billion going to Anthropic, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia also funds its own customers. It committed up to $100 billion to OpenAI in September 2025, but the final stake came to $30 billion, with another $10 billion going to Anthropic.\\n- e961 [rumour; fxleaders.com; original source Unnamed lenders and credit investors (reported) ; from gnews:4d8fb36192b66cfb50e49f46 2026-10-02] Lenders reportedly question Nvidia's plans to help finance up to $500 billion of infrastructure expansion, with banks and credit investors said to be assessing whether advanced chips will retain enough value over time to serve as reliable collateral and some lenders reportedly seeking broader guarantees and additional contractual protections. Unconfirmed, attributed to unnamed lenders; it does not establish that Nvidia has granted any guarantee or credit support.\\n    Quote: The financing discussion comes as lenders reportedly question Nvidia’s plans to help finance up to $500 billion of infrastructure expansion. … Banks and credit investors are said to be assessing whether advanced chips will retain enough value over time to serve as reliable collateral. Some lenders are reportedly seeking broader guarantees and additional contractual protections.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e988, e1017, e1034; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e988 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed banking sources and credit managers ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Unnamed banking sources and credit managers told Reuters that some lenders want stronger guarantees than Nvidia initially outlined for its chip-backed financing plan, even for Nvidia's AI chips, as they try to determine how long the chips can generate revenue. The sources also said bankers and asset managers doubt Nvidia's chips can serve as long-term collateral on their own and want greater protections around Nvidia's $500 billion financing plan.\\n    Quote: Some lenders want stronger guarantees than the company initially outlined, even for Nvidia’s industry-leading AI processing power, as they try to determine how long the revenue-generating life of its chips will last, banking sources and credit managers told Reuters. Several requested anonymity to discuss the financing structures. … Bankers and asset managers said they have doubts about whether Nvi\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e961; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e989 [fact; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August a $500 billion AI financing plan with financiers including Blackstone, Apollo and KKR that envisioned using Nvidia chips as collateral with limited guarantees to give AI developers access to Nvidia compute.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s plan, announced in August with financiers including Blackstone, Apollo and KKR, envisioned using its chips as collateral with limited guarantees, allowing AI developers to gain access to Nvidia’s compute. The strategy was seen as an expensive but potentially necessary way to create a financing market similar to the one used to finance aircraft.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1154; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e990 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia has said some deals under its $500 billion financing initiative could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has said some deals could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing that the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1130; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e961 (); both are in force: Lenders reportedly question Nvidia's plans to help finance up to $500 billion of infrastructure expansion, with banks and credit investors said to be assessing whether advanced chips will retain enough value over time to serve as reliable collateral and some lenders reportedly seeking broader guarantees and additional contractual protections. Unconfirmed, attributed to unnamed lenders; it does not establish that Nvidia has granted any guarantee or credit support.\\n- e991 [rumour; Reuters; original source Three unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Three banking sources not part of Nvidia's original financing group said Nvidia may ultimately need to provide guarantees on all of its financing deals, or have them backed by revenue streams from investment-grade customers such as major technology companies to cover debt payments. The sources added that the market is not yet ready to treat AI compute as an investment-grade asset comparable to aircraft.\\n    Quote: Three banking sources familiar with the matter but not part of the original financing group said Nvidia may ultimately need to provide guarantees on all of its deals, or have them backed by revenue streams from investment-grade customers such as major technology companies to cover debt payments. … For now, the sources said, the market is not yet ready to treat AI compute as an investment-grade ass\\n- e992 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Banking sources said Nvidia is providing previously unreported details to address lender concerns, and that tens of billions of dollars in loan deals in the pipeline are likely to include stronger guarantees and contractual protections. Some structures under consideration could provide lenders with guarantees, and the initial deals will be secured by Nvidia chips and backed by customer contracts and Nvidia's underlying guarantee, according to the sources.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is seeking to address some of these concerns, the sources said, providing details that had not previously been reported. … Tens of billions of dollars in loan deals currently in the pipeline are likely to include stronger guarantees and contractual protections, one source said. A second source said some structures under consideration could provide lenders with guarantees. … The initial deal\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e500; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e993 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA (CEO Jensen Huang; Nvidia citing third-party studies) ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has argued Nvidia GPUs have a useful life of up to a decade, and Nvidia has cited third-party studies showing major cloud companies extending server depreciation periods to five or six years from three or four, plus a Barkr finding that GB300 NVL72 systems could have a useful life of nine to 10 years.\\n    Quote: Huang has addressed some of the skepticism by arguing that Nvidia’s graphics processing units, or GPUs, have a useful life of up to a decade. … Nvidia has pointed to several third-party studies showing that major cloud companies are extending the depreciation periods for servers to five or six years, from three or four. … The company has also cited a finding from Barkr, which provides valuations f\\n- e994 [third_party_view; Reuters; original source Tony Trzcinka (Impax Asset Management); Andrew Chang (S&P Global Ratings) ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Impax Asset Management's Tony Trzcinka said banks typically underwrite GPUs over a 3-4 year depreciation schedule, versus Nvidia's decade claim, and that investors are likely to demand higher interest rates, larger financial cushions and stronger repayment protections before backing chip-secured loans. S&P Global Ratings' Andrew Chang said S&P takes a conservative view of the chips' value.\\n    Quote: “Banks typically underwrite GPUs over a 3-4 year depreciation schedule,” Trzcinka of Impax said. … “That is different than Nvidia, which argues top-tier GPUs can earn revenue for a decade,” he said, adding that investors are likely to demand higher interest rates, larger financial cushions and stronger repayment protections before backing loans secured by AI chips. … “Nvidia would imply that the G\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e829; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e995 [fact; Reuters, citing S&P and Moody's; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia previously provided a residual-value guarantee to support financing for SB Energy's Ohio data center project, according to S&P and Moody's.\\n    Quote: Nvidia itself previously provided a residual-value guarantee to support financing for SB Energy’s Ohio data center project, according to S&P and Moody’s.\\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e113; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1050 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA expects the AI labs it supports to account for roughly a quarter of its business next year.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA expects the AI labs it supports to account for roughly a quarter of its business next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1063 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA disclosed in August guarantees capped at $105 billion for an OpenAI-related SB Energy buildout. The guarantees generally begin as the relevant leases commence, with the first construction phase expected in fiscal 2029, and payments depend on specified tenant defaults; they cover defined lease and power payments and decline over the leases. The article describes this as contingent exposure rather than a current $105 billion cash bill.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA also disclosed August guarantees capped at $105 billion for an OpenAI-related SB Energy buildout. They generally begin as the relevant leases commence, with the first construction phase expected in fiscal 2029, and payments depend on specified tenant defaults. The guarantees cover defined lease and power payments and decline over the leases; they are contingent exposure, not a current $105 \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1106 [fact; Benzinga; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Nvidia is backing AI infrastructure by providing credit support for 4.25 GW at SB Energy's Ohio campus and investing $1.5 billion in the developer, according to the article.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is already backing that infrastructure, providing credit support for 4.25 GW at SB Energy's Ohio campus and investing $1.5 billion in the developer.\\n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\\n    Quote: In addition, Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings (equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion), if applied across the full target. The company’s actual balance sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e990; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1136 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] On its fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call on 26 August 2026, NVIDIA management said the company has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\\n    Quote: On its fiscal Q2 2027 call on August 26, 2026, management said NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in frontier AI labs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1137 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] On the same fiscal Q2 2027 call (26 August 2026), NVIDIA announced partnerships with six infrastructure investors to set up financing platforms that are planned to raise over $500 billion of outside capital.\\n    Quote: It also announced partnerships with six infrastructure investors to set up financing platforms that will raise over $500 billion of outside capital.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1138 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA structures its dealings with NeoClouds (newer AI-focused cloud providers) by guaranteeing a minimum level of revenue on part of a NeoCloud facility's capacity and taking a share of what the NeoCloud earns above that level, according to the article's description of the arrangement.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA goes further for NeoClouds, the newer cloud providers built for AI. It guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a NeoCloud facility’s capacity, and it takes a share of what the NeoCloud earns above that level.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1144 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said in August 2026 that it expects the AI labs it supports with its balance sheet to contribute roughly a quarter of its business in the following year, and acknowledged that some will call this circular financing.\\n    Quote: Management said in August that it expects the AI labs it supports with its balance sheet to contribute roughly a quarter of its business in the following year. It also acknowledged that some will call this circular financing.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1146 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] The wait for NVIDIA to be paid rose to 60 days in fiscal Q2 2027 as the company gave certain investment-grade customers longer payment terms on large purchases; the article flags the fiscal Q3 2027 call, scheduled for 17 November 2026, as the next checkpoint on that figure.\\n    Quote: The fiscal Q3 2027 call is scheduled for November 17. One figure to follow then is how long NVIDIA waits to be paid. That wait rose to 60 days in Q2 as NVIDIA gave certain investment-grade customers longer payment terms on large purchases.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\\n    Quote: Nvidia invests in customers that buy its chips. In August, it announced preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, CNBC reported.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e989; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1164 [fact; The Motley Fool (Daniel Foelber); original source NVIDIA (with a consortium of six financial institutions) ; from gnews:b0cf215ea487392e67e586be 2026-10-05] The Motley Fool reported that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, an arrangement that enables customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly. The item does not state how much of the $500 billion Nvidia itself bears or whether Nvidia guarantees any of the financing.\\n    Quote: Demand is so massive that Nvidia is partnering with a consortium of six financial institutions to fund $500 billion in AI infrastructure, enabling customers to finance their compute needs rather than buying chips directly.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e796; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1174 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA is funding some of its own customers through investments, having put nearly $50 billion into AI labs, according to Trefis.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is also funding some of its own customers: it has invested nearly $50 billion in AI labs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e20; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Themes in force, 6]\\n- v10 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?: now rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-\\n- v11 (semiconductor-cycle, due 2027-03-31) What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?: now rung 5 30 percent or more; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that\\n- v3 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?: now rung 3 Still open; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell\\n- v5 (ai-capex, due 2027-03-31) What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?: now rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still w\\n- v7 (chip-export-controls, due 2027-01-31) Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?: now rung 1 Yes; judged 2026-09-30; reasons: The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended t\\n- v9 (ai-capex, due 2028-03-30) How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?: now rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD; judged 2026-10-05; updated since this thesis was last judged; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-g\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 5; reasons: The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn). The strongest quantified evidence is NVIDIA's reported $108.5bn cap on guarantee obligations, already above rung 5's $50bn threshold. The $105bn OpenAI-related guarantee is part of the existing exposure, not an amount to add to the $108.5bn cap; the $500bn financing plan is capital mobilization, not NVIDIA's guarantee amount, and its up-to-25% residual-value support applies only to selected opportunities. Reports of stronger protections in financing deals could add exposure, but do not size NVIDIA's share. The implied disclosed amount therefore remains at least $108.5bn, firmly in rung 5.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis; theme_pushes only the theme ids listed above; a theme id goes only in theme_pushes, never in cites or cites_views.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 5,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position: rung 3, Neutral ($15bn to below $30bn). The strongest quantified exposure remains NVIDIA's reported $108.5bn cap on guarantee obligations, already above rung 5's $50bn boundary. Morningstar's $125bn figure is a maximum scenario for the separate $500bn financing target if the full target received 25% support; the evidence says support applies only to selected opportunities and the target is over time, so that maximum is not a forecast to add in full to the $108.5bn. The disclosed 2 GW and 4.25 GW support, minimum-revenue guarantees, and financing backstops may add exposure, but are not sized sufficiently to change the rung.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e18\",\n    \"e22\",\n    \"e23\",\n    \"e41\",\n    \"e42\",\n    \"e44\",\n    \"e61\",\n    \"e114\",\n    \"e136\",\n    \"e143\",\n    \"e196\",\n    \"e220\",\n    \"e500\",\n    \"e582\",\n    \"e778\",\n    \"e796\",\n    \"e828\",\n    \"e830\",\n    \"e989\",\n    \"e990\",\n    \"e991\",\n    \"e992\",\n    \"e995\",\n    \"e1048\",\n    \"e1063\",\n    \"e1130\",\n    \"e1138\",\n    \"e1154\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e18\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e22\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e23\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e39\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e41\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e42\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e44\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e60\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e61\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e93\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e114\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e135\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e136\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e143\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e196\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e220\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e328\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e335\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e498\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e499\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e500\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e581\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e582\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e778\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e791\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e796\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e798\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e828\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e829\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e830\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e927\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e952\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e961\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e988\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e989\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e990\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e991\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e992\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e993\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e994\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e995\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1048\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1050\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1063\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1106\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1130\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1136\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1137\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1138\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1144\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1146\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1154\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1164\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1174\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung is unchanged, but the implied-outcome assessment now incorporates a quantified third-party upper-bound scenario of $125bn for the financing program, based on 25% of the full $500bn target. Because that assumes full deployment and support on selected opportunities only, it is not added wholesale to the existing $108.5bn cap.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v10\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v5\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"view_id\": \"v9\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": \"No market reference figure was supplied when the question opened, so the reference is rung 3, Neutral ($15bn to below $30bn).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"Forecast disclosed exposure remains at least about $108.5bn, the reported cap on guarantee obligations. A separate financing-program scenario could add as much as $125bn if 25% support applied across the full $500bn target, but the target is over time and support is limited to selected opportunities, so the evidence does not justify assuming that full amount by the FY2027 report.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 14,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v5 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?\\nLadder: rung 1 below 600,000,000,000 USD: below 600,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 750,000,000,000 USD: from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 750,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD: from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year\\\") / rung 4 from 850,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD: from 850,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 1,000,000,000,000 USD or more: 1,000,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 51 items]\\n[Not shown: 1 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\\n- e102 [party_statement; Fool.com opinion column (unnamed contributor); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ad3e04be52c448172362b020 2026-09-23] Nvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030, an estimate cited in a Fool.com opinion column on 23 September 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e163, e171, e241, e312, e323, e728, e1056; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e103 [third_party_view; Fool.com opinion column (unnamed contributor); original source Fool.com opinion column (unnamed contributor) 2026-09-23; from gnews:ad3e04be52c448172362b020 2026-09-23] The Fool.com opinion column states that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in 2026, offered as the basis for continued growth in AI computing demand.\\n    Quote: Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e119, e162, e242, e311, e324, e367, e674, e781, e825, e973, e1037, e1055; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e105 [fact; barchart.com; original source Amazon.com (AMZN) ; from gnews:aa0d7cffe1e3c710b6347ad4 2026-09-23] The article cites a recent deal between Nvidia and Amazon (AMZN) involving 2 million GPUs, described as one reason NVDA remains a compelling long-term holding in 2026; no deal value or timing is given.\\n    Quote: The recent deal with Amazon (AMZN) involving 2 million GPUs and billions being spent on buybacks and dividend payments make NVDA an even more compelling long-term holding in 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e243, e768; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e107 [third_party_view; fool.com (article author); original source S&P Global ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] S&P Global recently forecast that technology companies will spend an estimated $1.3 trillion on AI 'next year' — i.e. calendar 2027, as reported in a 23 September 2026 article.\\n    Quote: S&P Global recently forecast that tech companies will spend an estimated $1.3 trillion on AI next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e305, e469; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e119 [third_party_view; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source 24/7 Wall St. ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article states hyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027.\\n    Quote: Hyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly 800 billion in 2026 and 1.3 trillion in 2027, a wave that also pulls in the power, cooling, and networking suppliers we profiled in a free report on seven AI infrastructure names that aren't chipmakers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e162 [party_statement; The Motley Fool (Keithen Drury); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:915fe84b4c7f7b4decd1665d 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's most recent earnings call, the company said data center capital expenditures by the big five AI hyperscalers will approach $800 billion this year (2026) and are estimated to rise to $1.3 trillion next year (2027), as reported by The Motley Fool.\\n    Quote: During its most recent earnings call, Nvidia noted that this year's data center capital expenditures from the big five AI hyperscalers will approach $800 billion. Next year, that figure is estimated to rise to $1.3 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e186 [fact; Zacks (citing Marvell's Aug. 27 press release); original source Marvell Technology, Inc. 2026-08-27; from gnews:ab420e4b49c5ff4b8b3e5bdc 2026-09-24] Marvell Technology reported record fiscal second-quarter 2027 revenue of $2.739 billion, up 37% year over year, with Data Center revenue growing 46% year over year, according to its Aug. 27, 2026 press release.\\n    Quote: Revenues for the fiscal second quarter of 2027 reached a record $2.739 billion, up 37% year over year, according to the company's Aug. 27 press release. With Marvell supplying vital infrastructure for data center systems, its Data Center revenues grew even faster, up 46% year over year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e321; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e187 [party_statement; Zacks (Marvell management guidance); original source Marvell Technology, Inc. 2026-08-27; from gnews:ab420e4b49c5ff4b8b3e5bdc 2026-09-24] Marvell Technology management guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $3.15 billion, plus or minus 5%, about 15% sequential growth at the midpoint, and remained optimistic about revenue growth through fiscal 2027 and 2028.\\n    Quote: For the fiscal third quarter of 2027, management expects revenues to reach $3.15 billion, plus or minus 5%. At the midpoint, it represents roughly 15% sequential growth. Management also remained optimistic about revenue growth throughout fiscal year 2027 and 2028.\\n- e242 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury, The Motley Fool; original source The Motley Fool (Keithen Drury) 2026-09-24; from gnews:807e30726ee85ca7193b837f 2026-09-24] The article states that the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year (calendar 2026), a third-party estimate of the combined data center capital spending that underpins Nvidia's AI chip demand.\\n    Quote: Considering the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year, that's huge growth still to come.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e264 [fact; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source Meta Platforms (partnership with Advanced Micro Devices) ; from gnews:dad3a4651f31a14be8d86d8c 2026-09-25] Meta Platforms partnered with AMD on 6 gigawatts of capacity, using the same template as the OpenAI deal, with the first wave also due during the second half of 2026 and AMD issuing Meta a large warrant package.\\n    Quote: Meta Platforms used the same template. The social media giant partnered with AMD on 6 gigawatts, with the first wave also due during the second half of 2026, again with AMD issuing a large warrant package.\\n- e305 [rumour; Chris Neiger, fool.com; original source  ; from gnews:0acda86a3f25c028313f3af6 2026-09-25] Unnamed latest estimates cited by the article put global AI infrastructure spending at about $750 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\\n    Quote: And there's little sign that AI infrastructure spending is slowing. The latest estimates are for global AI infrastructure spending to reach $1.3 trillion in 2027 -- up from about $750 billion this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e107; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e311 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source finance.biggo.com 2026-09-25; from gnews:3eef853ce8ed9acd105923c4 2026-09-25] The article states, without naming a source, that the five largest AI hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in the current year (calendar 2026).\\n    Quote: The five largest AI hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year alone.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e312 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3eef853ce8ed9acd105923c4 2026-09-25] NVIDIA has projected that global data center capital expenditures will reach between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030, a multiyear demand forecast the article cites as the basis for visibility into future demand.\\n    Quote: The company has projected that global data center capital expenditures will reach between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e102; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e324 [third_party_view; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source Keithen Drury (fool.com), aggregating the big five AI hyperscalers 2026-09-26; from gnews:f72f4de1d44f4ee9244c7f89 2026-09-26] The fool.com author states that the big five AI hyperscalers are currently spending around $800 billion, presented against Nvidia's $3 trillion to $4 trillion 2030 global data center capex estimate.\\n    Quote: Right now, the big five AI hyperscalers are spending around $800 billion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e325 [fact; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:f72f4de1d44f4ee9244c7f89 2026-09-26] Alphabet is spending $200 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, which the fool.com author cites alongside Google Cloud's growth as evidence that AI infrastructure demand will stay elevated.\\n    Quote: During the second quarter, this division grew at a remarkable 82% pace, and with Alphabet spending $200 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, the growth rate will likely stay elevated for a long time.\\n- e358 [fact; James Brumley (fool.com); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:8962d069f4fb250cdc08e115 2026-09-26] Microsoft reported 18% year-over-year revenue growth for its quarter ended June 2026, led by 43% growth in its Azure cloud business where its AI revenue is recorded; author James Brumley notes both figures topped analysts' estimates. Microsoft is one of the large hyperscaler buyers of NVIDIA data-center hardware, so the reported growth bears on the demand backdrop for NVIDIA's products, though the item gives no NVIDIA figures.\\n    Quote: Based on the company's results for the three months ending in June, clearly not everyone agrees. Shares soared on its 18% year-over-year revenue growth, led by 43% growth of its Azure business, where its artificial intelligence numbers are recorded. Both numbers topped analysts' estimates, too.\\n- e359 [party_statement; James Brumley (fool.com); original source Microsoft ; from gnews:8962d069f4fb250cdc08e115 2026-09-26] Microsoft said it is looking for 45% revenue growth from its AI businesses for the quarter ending September 2026, and reported a backlog of $678 billion of contracted business already lined up and waiting to be booked as revenue, according to James Brumley's account of the company's results and outlook. As a large buyer of NVIDIA data-center hardware, the size of Microsoft's contracted AI backlog and expected AI revenue growth bear on the demand outlook for NVIDIA's products; the item gives no NVIDIA figures.\\n    Quote: The company's even looking for 45% revenue growth from its AI businesses for the quarter ending this month, with a backlog of $678 billion worth of business that's already lined up just waiting to be booked as revenue.\\n- e367 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] NVIDIA expects the big five AI hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in 2026, with that figure rising to $1.3 trillion in 2027 (\\\"next year\\\"), per The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury citing the company's large industry outlook.\\n    Quote: As for a large industry outlook, Nvidia expects the big five AI hyper-scalers to spend nearly $800 billion in 2026, with that figure rising to $1.3 trillion next year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e469 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source S&P Global Ratings ; from gnews:02b796f20d201c8380e0ae4d 2026-09-28] S&P Global Ratings said in August 2026 that combined hyperscaler capital expenditure is projected to exceed $1.3 trillion by 2027, as companies race to build AI infrastructure including data centers, CNBC reported.\\n    Quote: Combined hyperscaler capital expenditure is projected to exceed $1.3 trillion by 2027, S&P Global Ratings said in August, as companies race to build AI infrastructure including data centers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e107; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e674 [party_statement; fool.com article author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7968b6992410f494f08511b3 2026-09-30] The article reports, attributing the figures to Nvidia's projections for its key customers' capex, that the big five AI hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data-center build-outs in calendar 2026 ('this year'), with current estimates for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion in 2027 ('next year').\\n    Quote: The big five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data center build-outs this year, and the current estimates are for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion next year. That's just according to Nvidia's projections, which are likely pretty accurate, given what it knows about its key customers' plans.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e733 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang estimated that a one-gigawatt data center costs roughly $60 billion, or about $10 billion a year spread over six years, while the same capacity currently generates about $50 billion a year in rental revenue.\\n    Quote: A one-gigawatt data center costs roughly $60 billion. Spread over six years, that works out to about $10 billion a year. Yet that same capacity currently brings in about $50 billion a year in rental revenue, Huang said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e389; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e760 [rumour; Financial Times (citing unnamed people familiar with the matter), reported by Investing.com; original source Amazon 2026-10-01; from gnews:886f027fa46edb25b85723b2 2026-10-01] Amazon is seeking to offload about $8 billion of advanced Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips to external investors, planning to spin thousands of the chips — currently used in data centers across the U.S. — into a special purpose vehicle that would raise debt from outside investors and lease the chips back to Amazon, with Amazon offering an equity stake of up to 10% in the vehicle and, per the report, holding no stake in the entity. The Financial Times reported the talks, citing people familiar with the matter, as aimed at strengthening Amazon's balance sheet through a more asset-light approach and as part of a broader push by U.S. hyperscalers toward less balance-sheet-heavy financing of their data center buildouts.\\n    Quote: Amazon is seeking to offload about $8 billion of advanced Nvidia chips to external investors, the Financial Times reported on Thursday, citing people familiar with the matter. … Amazon held talks with investors in recent weeks to gauge interest in the deal, where the company plans to spin off thousands of Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle, the FT report said. … The e-commerce gi\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e838, e840, e891, e929, e960, e971, e1007, e1042, e1159; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e761 [party_statement; Investing.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:886f027fa46edb25b85723b2 2026-10-01] Amazon said it will spend over $200 billion in capital expenditure this year (calendar 2026), a bulk of which will go towards its Amazon Web Services cloud unit to buy more chips and build more data centers.\\n    Quote: The ecommerce giant said it will spend over $200 billion in capital expenditure this year, a bulk of which will go towards its Web Services cloud unit to buy more chips and build more data centers.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e839, e892, e1044; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e781 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source  ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] The article states top-five hyperscaler capital expenditure is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Balancing that risk, top-five hyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e825 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:23646c683c86bed389e13357 2026-10-01] Nvidia's own estimates project the big hyperscalers' data center spending at nearly $800 billion this year (calendar 2026) and $1.3 trillion next year (calendar 2027), with the article noting those are Nvidia's numbers and that the hyperscalers may start sharing 2027 capital spending guidance when they report quarterly results.\\n    Quote: The growth case leans on the big hyperscalers, whose data center spending is projected at nearly $800 billion this year and $1.3 trillion next year, according to Nvidia's own estimates. … Hyperscalers may start sharing 2027 capital spending guidance when they report quarterly results.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e838 [rumour; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance / finance.yahoo.com); original source Amazon ; from gnews:c5cace3648af0ab7a2100087 2026-10-02] Amazon is seeking external investors for a financing arrangement involving approximately $8 billion of advanced Nvidia chips, according to a Financial Times report citing people familiar with the matter: Amazon has held discussions with investors in recent weeks over a proposed special purpose vehicle that would hold thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips, lease them back to Amazon for use in US data centres, and raise debt from external investors, with investors offered an equity interest of up to 10% in the vehicle and Amazon retaining no equity stake itself. The item does not indicate that Amazon has reached a final agreement with investors or completed the transaction.\\n    Quote: Amazon (NASDAQ:AMZN) is seeking external investors for a financing arrangement involving approximately $8 billion of advanced Nvidia (NASDAQ:NVDA) chips, the Financial Times reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … According to the report, Amazon has held discussions with investors in recent weeks to assess interest in a proposed structure that would place thousands of Nvidia Grace Blac\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e839 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance / finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:c5cace3648af0ab7a2100087 2026-10-02] Amazon expects its capital expenditure to exceed $200 billion this year (calendar 2026), with a substantial portion allocated to its Amazon Web Services cloud business for additional chips and data-centre construction.\\n    Quote: Amazon expects capital expenditure to exceed $200 billion this year, with a substantial portion allocated to its Amazon Web Services cloud business for additional chips and data centre construction.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e761; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e840 [rumour; Financial Times, as reported by Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon.com Inc 2026-10-01; from gnews:25920cb5dac45b4dddd3218d 2026-10-02] Amazon.com Inc is seeking to offload about $8 billion of advanced Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips to outside investors and lease them back, the Financial Times reported on Thursday, citing people familiar with the matter. Under the proposed deal Amazon would spin off thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell AI data-center chips (used in data centers across the US) into a special-purpose vehicle, lease them back from it, and raise funding from outside investors through debt issuance; it plans to offer an equity stake of up to 10% in the vehicle and would hold no stake itself. Amazon held talks with investors in recent weeks to gauge interest. The report says the deal aims to strengthen Amazon's balance sheet by shifting expensive chip costs to investors under a more asset-light approach; it is unconfirmed and no completion or timing is given.\\n    Quote: Amazon.com Inc (NASDAQ:AMZN) is seeking to offload about $8 billion of advanced Nvidia Corp (NASDAQ:NVDA, XETRA:NVD) chips to external investors while leasing them back, the Financial Times reported Thursday. … The e-commerce giant held talks with investors in recent weeks to gauge interest in the proposed deal, the newspaper reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The proposed deal ai\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e890 [fact; MarketWatch, cited by qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon announced a roughly 15% price increase on its EC2 Capacity Blocks for ML service, which lets customers reserve access to Nvidia chips ranging from the older A100 to the newer B300; the updated rates take effect next week, according to MarketWatch.\\n    Quote: Amazon $AMZN announced a roughly 15% price increase on its EC2 Capacity Blocks for ML service, which lets customers reserve access to Nvidia $NVDA chips ranging from the older A100 to the newer B300, with updated rates taking effect next week, according to MarketWatch .\\n- e891 [rumour; Financial Times, cited by Reuters and qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon has approached outside investors about a deal to transfer roughly $8 billion in Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips into a special-purpose vehicle and lease them back; the SPV would be funded through debt sold to outside investors, Amazon would hold an equity interest of as much as 10%, and the chips are already operating at more than a dozen sites across five U.S. states including Nevada and Virginia, according to the Financial Times.\\n    Quote: Separately, Amazon has approached outside investors about a deal that would transfer roughly $8 billion in Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips into a special-purpose vehicle and then lease those chips back, according to the Financial Times, as cited by Reuters . Under the arrangement, the SPV would be funded through debt sold to outside investors, and Amazon would hold an equity interest of as much as 10\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e892 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon set its 2026 capital expenditure budget at $200 billion, up from $131 billion in 2025, with data centers, chips and related hardware accounting for the bulk of those outlays.\\n    Quote: Amazon 's capital expenditure budget for 2026 is set at $200 billion, up from $131 billion in 2025, with data centers, chips, and related hardware accounting for the bulk of those outlays.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e761; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e893 [fact; qz.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:d2c1c1f2acd219def550c886 2026-10-02] Amazon entered a $17.5 billion term loan in June and raised £4.25 billion through a debut sterling bond offering last week, extending a borrowing push that made it the largest bond issuer among major hyperscalers this year, with cumulative sales equivalent to nearly $100 billion.\\n    Quote: The company has been tapping a range of financing tools to fund that spending: it entered into a $17.5 billion term loan in June and raised £4.25 billion through a debut sterling bond offering last week, extending a borrowing push that has made it the largest bond issuer among major hyperscalers this year, with cumulative sales equivalent to nearly $100 billion.\\n- e926 [third_party_view; Barclays analyst Tom O'Malley, reported by TradingView/Stocktwits; original source Barclays (Tom O'Malley) 2026-10-01; from gnews:d91c54ec44da3c9a5cbef369 2026-10-02] Barclays analyst Tom O'Malley said on Thursday that Nvidia's hyperscaler revenue could run well above Barclays' current estimates: 'napkin math' based on Nvidia's latest IT spending data points to roughly $237 billion of hyperscaler revenue in 2026 and $401 billion in 2027, versus Barclays' existing estimates of $206 billion and $370 billion respectively. In an upside scenario he sees $246 billion in 2026 and $417 billion in 2027, and he noted the calculation may understate the opportunity because the capex comparison excludes SpaceX.\\n    Quote: On Thursday, Barclays analyst Tom O’Malley said Nvidia’s hyperscaler revenue could potentially run well above the firm’s current estimates, using “ napkin math ” based on Nvidia’s latest IT spending data. … His calculations point to roughly $237 billion in hyperscaler revenue in 2026 and $401 billion in 2027, compared with Barclays’ existing estimates of $206 billion and $370 billion, respectively\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e815; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e960 [rumour; fxleaders.com; original source Amazon (reported by the Financial Times from unnamed sources) ; from gnews:4d8fb36192b66cfb50e49f46 2026-10-02] Amazon is reportedly exploring a financing structure, reported by the Financial Times, that could move approximately $8 billion of Nvidia chips into a special-purpose vehicle funded by outside investors: Amazon has discussed transferring thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips used across more than a dozen U.S. data centers into the vehicle, which could issue debt and sell as much as a 10% equity stake to external investors, with Amazon continuing to use the chips after the transfer as a more asset-light way to finance part of its infrastructure build-out. Unconfirmed report; no completion, pricing or timing is disclosed.\\n    Quote: Amazon is reportedly exploring a financing structure that could move approximately $8 billion of Nvidia chips into a special-purpose vehicle funded by outside investors. … According to the Financial Times, Amazon has discussed transferring thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips across more than a dozen U.S. data centers into the vehicle. The structure could issue debt and sell as much as a 10% \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e971 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:7b31f6f72da28162f972b402 2026-10-03] Amazon has set up an $8 billion special purpose vehicle that buys Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips and leases them back to AWS clients. The article describes the structure as an off-balance-sheet financing tool for AI hardware intended to let AWS keep building AI capacity without carrying all the hardware on its own balance sheet as cloud capital demands rise. The item gives no date for the vehicle, its terms or its lease commitments.\\n    Quote: Amazon has set up an $8b special purpose vehicle to buy and lease back Nvidia (NasdaqGS:NVDA) Grace Blackwell chips to AWS clients. … The structure is designed as an off balance sheet financing tool for AI hardware as cloud capital demands rise. … Shifting thousands of Grace Blackwell chips into an external vehicle gives AWS a way to keep building AI capacity without stacking all the hardware on i\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e972 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Jensen Huang, Nvidia CEO ; from gnews:b7e22d5ba15d6483a30d2389 2026-10-03] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang reaffirmed his projection that data center capital expenditures will reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion annually by 2030, saying he believes the projection is still accurate.\\n    Quote: Jensen Huang, one of the co-founders of Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA), is no stranger to making bold predictions. One that he has been floating for a few years is that data center capital expenditures will reach $3 trillion to $4 trillion annually by 2030. That's a massive increase from today's levels, and he just commented that he believes that projection is still accurate.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1036; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e102 (); both are in force: Nvidia estimates that global data center capital expenditures will rise to between $3 trillion and $4 trillion by 2030, an estimate cited in a Fool.com opinion column on 23 September 2026.\\n- e973 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:b7e22d5ba15d6483a30d2389 2026-10-03] Nvidia expects the big five AI hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026.\\n    Quote: In 2026, Nvidia expects the big five artificial intelligence (AI) hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1008 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon projects calendar-2026 capital expenditures of approximately $220 billion, about 10% above its previous $200 billion forecast, driven by aggressive investment in data centers and AI infrastructure centered on AWS.\\n    Quote: Amazon's projected capital expenditures for this year stand at approximately $220 billion, up about 10% from the previous forecast of $200 billion. The surge is driven by aggressive investment in data centers and AI infrastructure, centered on its cloud business, Amazon Web Services (AWS).\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1160; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e761 (); both are in force: Amazon said it will spend over $200 billion in capital expenditure this year (calendar 2026), a bulk of which will go towards its Amazon Web Services cloud unit to buy more chips and build more data centers.\\n- e1009 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon's AWS backlog reached $496 billion, posting triple-digit year-over-year growth.\\n    Quote: AWS's backlog reached $496 billion, posting triple-digit year-over-year growth.\\n- e1010 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Andy Jassy, Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said in July 2026 that even with significantly increased investment Amazon would struggle to meet all AI computing demand, that it would not have enough capacity to satisfy all demand in 2026 and the same would be true in 2027, and that demand already secured for 2028 was extraordinary.\\n    Quote: Andy Jassy, Amazon's CEO, said in July that even with significantly increased investment, the company would struggle to meet all AI computing demand. … At the time, he stated: \\\"We won't have enough capacity to satisfy all the demand we have in 2026, and the same will be true in 2027.\\\" He added that \\\"the demand already secured for 2028 is extraordinary.\\\"\\n- e1037 [party_statement; Keithen Drury, Fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:97a074da716a2f2655c2885d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA expects the big five AI hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in calendar 2026, according to the article's account of the company's expectation.\\n    Quote: In 2026, Nvidia expects the big five artificial intelligence (AI) hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1043 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon spent $54.2 billion on capital projects in the second quarter, 68% more than a year earlier.\\n    Quote: Amazon spent $54.2 billion on capital projects in the second quarter, 68% more than a year earlier.\\n- e1044 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon (Andy Jassy) ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy committed to roughly $200 billion in spending for 2026.\\n    Quote: Andy Jassy committed to roughly $200 billion in spending for 2026.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e761; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1045 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon's free cash flow over the past 12 months turned negative at -$7.6 billion, and its long-term debt rose to $119.1 billion from $65.6 billion a year earlier.\\n    Quote: Free cash flow over the past 12 months turned negative at -$7.6 billion. Long-term debt rose to $119.1 billion from $65.6 billion a year earlier.\\n- e1055 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:fdee99ebb64a9e468197951e 2026-10-04] NVIDIA management estimates the big five AI hyperscalers will spend about $800 billion on capital expenditures this year (2026), and that adding other players takes the total to around $1 trillion, according to this fool.com column.\\n    Quote: What's noteworthy about this forecast is that Nvidia management believes the big five AI hyperscalers will spend about $800 billion on capital expenditures this year. When other players are added in, that figure could reach around $1 trillion.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1158 [fact; Zacks Investment Research; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:28d4f9293b76613111469c90 2026-10-05] Zacks reported that Meta Platforms spent more than $100 billion on its AI investments in 2026, cited in the article as the source of Wall Street's concern about Meta's spending and its roughly 25x P/E; the item does not break the figure into data-center capital expenditure versus other AI investment such as talent.\\n    Quote: In 2026, Meta spent more than $100 billion on its AI investments. In fact, Meta even poached top AI talent from industry peers for a cool $100 million. The enormous spending has created fear, uncertainty, and doubt across Wall Street.\\n- e1159 [rumour; Financial Times (cited by TechRepublic); original source Amazon ; from gnews:7a1f9bd7788ce53c481edc8e 2026-10-05] Amazon is reportedly in talks with investors over a roughly $8 billion financing deal that would transfer thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell AI chips into a special-purpose vehicle and lease them back to Amazon for continued use in its data centers, according to the Financial Times.\\n    Quote: The company is in talks with investors over a roughly $8 billion financing deal that would place thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips into a special-purpose vehicle and lease them back to Amazon, according to the Financial Times. … The proposed deal would allow Amazon to transfer thousands of Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips into a special-purpose vehicle (SPV), then lease the hardware back for c\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e760; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1160 [party_statement; TechRepublic; original source Amazon ; from gnews:7a1f9bd7788ce53c481edc8e 2026-10-05] Amazon expects roughly $220 billion in capital expenditures in 2026, with spending focused heavily on AWS infrastructure, AI chips and data centers.\\n    Quote: The company expects roughly $220 billion in capital expenditures in 2026, with spending focused heavily on AWS infrastructure, AI chips and data centers .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1008; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1179 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) ; from gnews:b288e1521760111a13cda5e9 2026-10-05] Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry), a key builder and integrator of Nvidia-based AI servers, reported record September 2026 revenue of $36.5 billion (NT$1.16 trillion), up 25.7% from August and 38.4% from a year earlier — the first month above NT$1 trillion, exceeding the prior record of NT$946.5 billion set in July. Foxconn said its Cloud and Networking Products business grew significantly in September on \\\"strong pull-in momentum for AI products,\\\" with strong year-over-year growth in the segment.\\n    Quote: One of the largest assemblers of Apple (AAPL) products and key builder of Nvidia (NVDA)-based AI servers reported September revenue of $36.5 billion (NT$1.16 trillion) , up 25.7% from August and 38.4% from a year earlier. … This is the first time Foxconn's monthly revenue topped NT$1 trillion and surpassed the previous record of NT$946.5 billion set in July. … Foxconn said its Cloud and Networking\\n- e1180 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) ; from gnews:b288e1521760111a13cda5e9 2026-10-05] Foxconn reported third-quarter 2026 revenue of $95.3 billion, up 20.4% sequentially and 47.1% year over year, and nine-month 2026 revenue of $241.57 billion, up 39.5% from a year earlier, with both periods setting records; Foxconn builds and integrates Nvidia-based AI systems such as GB200 and GB300 NVL72 racks.\\n    Quote: The strength continued through the quarter. Foxconn reported third-quarter revenue of $95.3 billion, up 20.4% sequentially and 47.1% year over year. Nine-month revenue reached $241.57 billion, up 39.5% from a year earlier, with both periods setting records.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1098; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1181 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) ; from gnews:b288e1521760111a13cda5e9 2026-10-05] Foxconn said it expects AI-related operations to continue growing in the fourth quarter of 2026 alongside the traditional second-half peak season for ICT products, while cautioning that a volatile global political and economic environment remains a risk.\\n    Quote: Foxconn expects AI-related operations to continue growing in the fourth quarter, alongside the traditional second-half peak season for ICT products. The company cautioned that a volatile global political and economic environment remains a risk.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which is rung 3. Amazon's updated approximately $220 billion total-capex projection is about $20 billion above the previous $200 billion level; because the increase is attributed to data centers and AI infrastructure, it lifts the roughly $800 billion reference to an implied roughly $820 billion, not by the full $220 billion. Amazon's $54.2 billion Q2 capital-projects spend, up 68% year over year, is consistent with elevated spending but is not annualized flat to create a separate forecast. Meta's 6-GW partnership and the roughly $60 billion-per-GW, six-year data-center cost benchmark imply about $360 billion total, or $60 billion per year at full scale; however, only a first wave is due in H2 2026\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still within rung 3. Meta's 6-GW partnership has a large theoretical full-scale cost, but only its first wave is due in H2 2026; it is not evidence that the full volume is in hand. Meta's reported AI investment total does not isolate data-center capex. Amazon's $8 billion SPV could affect how some investment is financed or reported, but the evidence does not establish a 2026 capex reduction; even an $8 billion adjustment would leave the estimate in rung 3. Supplier growth is supportive of demand but does not quantify a change to hyperscaler capex.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e162\",\n    \"e674\",\n    \"e1008\",\n    \"e264\",\n    \"e733\",\n    \"e971\",\n    \"e1158\",\n    \"e1180\",\n    \"e1181\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e162\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e674\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1008\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e264\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e733\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e971\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1158\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1180\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1181\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": false,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The implied estimate remains roughly $820 billion, as in the previous conclusion. The repeated Amazon projection and newer supplier and Meta items add no quantified, comparable change to the five companies' 2026 data-center capex forecast.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"The market/reference is Nvidia's estimate that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in calendar 2026; that is rung 3.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"Roughly $820 billion: start from the nearly $800 billion reference and add about $20 billion for Amazon's projection rising from $200 billion to approximately $220 billion. The other cited amounts do not establish an additional 2026 data-center-capex amount in hand.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 15,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v10 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"US data-center capacity additions in gigawatts for calendar 2026, versus the pace of 'somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year' that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang described, and against how far labour availability constrains that pace.\\\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?\\nLadder: rung 1 below 10 GW: below 10 GW / rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW: from 10 GW to below 15 GW / rung 3 from 15 GW to below 20 GW: from 15 GW to below 20 GW (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 GW to below 25 GW: from 20 GW to below 25 GW / rung 5 25 GW or more: 25 GW or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 11 items]\\n- e678 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of Nvidia ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, speaking on a panel at the White House, said the US is adding \\\"somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year\\\" of AI data-center infrastructure and that the work extends beyond data centers to power generation, construction, power cooling and pipe fitting. This is a management estimate of the annual pace of US AI-infrastructure additions, not a reported outturn.\\n    Quote: Huang explained that the US is adding \\\"somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year\\\" of the infrastructure and said the work reaches well beyond the data centers themselves. … \\\"Don't forget you got to build a power generation plants,\\\" the AI hardware giant's chief executive said, adding: \\\"You've got construction, you've got power cooling, you've got pipe fitters.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e675; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e679 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (Brian Sozzi, cited); original source Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] The Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST), an initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google, estimated in a new report that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled-trades job openings annually through 2035, and that current training programmes produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed. The figures are the organisation's estimates of a labour-supply constraint on construction and infrastructure work, including data-center build-out.\\n    Quote: As Yahoo Finance's Brian Sozzi detailed, the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) — an initiative launched by BlackRock ( BLK ), Carhartt, Ford ( F ), and Google ( GOOG ) — estimated that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled trades job openings annually through 2035. … Current training programs produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed, according to a new AAST report.\\n- e680 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft (joining AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft are joining the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades this week, the workforce initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google that targets the skilled-trades occupations used in building and operating infrastructure such as data centers.\\n    Quote: Nvidia, Meta ( META ), and Microsoft ( MSFT ) are joining AAST this week. … AAST defines skilled trade jobs as the 124 occupations — including carpenters, electricians, mechanics of various kinds, plumbers, and pipe fitters — that \\\"build, install, operate, maintain, repair, fabricate, or extract the physical materials, products, equipment, systems, and infrastructure the U.S. economy relies on.\\\"\\n- e40 [party_statement; SB Energy (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source SB Energy ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] SB Energy said it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI, where NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider.\\n    Quote: SB Energy says it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI.\\n- e263 [fact; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source OpenAI (agreement with Advanced Micro Devices) ; from gnews:dad3a4651f31a14be8d86d8c 2026-09-25] OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts with AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026; the agreement also gives OpenAI a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\\n    Quote: OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts using AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026. In addition, the deal also provides OpenAI with a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\\n- e330 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon, part of what the article describes as spreading its compute dollars across suppliers.\\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\\n- e331 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\\n- e675 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of NVIDIA 2026-09-29; from gnews:a58c5a2a6302b9af5cfd79c3 2026-09-30] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking at an America.Gov event on 29 September 2026 alongside Elon Musk, estimated that the United States could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI data-center infrastructure annually — an estimate he framed as a rough calculation ('let's just estimate') — and said that building out those data centers would probably create about one million jobs. The article notes that his million-job figure refers to infrastructure-related employment, not net job creation after accounting for potential AI displacement elsewhere.\\n    Quote: He estimated that the U.S. could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI infrastructure annually, requiring substantial investment in electricity generation, cooling systems, networking and construction. … \\\"And so we're building this infrastructure out and, call it across all of the United States, somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year. Let's just estimate, that creates just, probably about\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e678; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e727 [party_statement; Bloomberg, citing Elon Musk's post on X; original source xAI (Elon Musk) ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] xAI CEO Elon Musk said in a post on X, reported by Bloomberg, that the Colossus 2 cluster in the Memphis area already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s—about 550,000 Nvidia chips—and could more than double to top 1.2 million by end-2026, with another 220,000 GB300s due the following week, 220,000 expected in November and a further 220,000 possible in late December \\\"if we get lucky.\\\" xAI had previously said it planned to equip the Memphis facility with 1 million GPUs by 2026.\\n    Quote: Colossus 2, the Memphis area cluster built by xAI, may more than double its current Nvidia chip count by the end of the year, Bloomberg reported, citing a post Musk made on X.\\nHere is how the numbers stack up, according to Musk:\\nColossus 2 already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s.\\nAnother 220,000 GB300s were set to go live the following week.\\nA further 220,000 are expected in Nov\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\\n- e1085 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Amazon.com Inc. ; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Amazon.com signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy to secure electricity from Maryland's Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant, buying 690 megawatts of the 1,790-megawatt facility; the agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable power for growing AI operations.\\n    Quote: Amazon.com Inc. signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy Corp. to secure electricity from Maryland’s Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant. Under the deal, Amazon will purchase 690 megawatts from the 1,790-megawatt facility, Maryland’s only nuclear power plant. The agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable electricity for gro\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 2; reasons: The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: it spans rung 2 (10–<15), rung 3 (15–<20), and rung 4 (20–<25), with its 15 GW midpoint at the rung 2/3 boundary. I forecast roughly 13–14 GW, below that midpoint: the announced capacity and deployment plans support substantial additions, but most are not demonstrated as US capacity additions completed in calendar 2026, while the quantified skilled-trades shortfall and stated scarcity of power, land, and building space weigh against achieving the upper half of Huang’s range. That implies rung 2. The deal figures cannot be summed as 2026 US additions: their timing, location, realization, and overlap are not established, and some describe multi-period or product-specific capacity.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 5,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is Huang’s rough estimate of 10–20 GW annually: that range spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW), rung 3 (15–<20 GW), and reaches rung 4 at 20 GW; its 15 GW midpoint is in rung 3. I forecast about 14 GW, below that midpoint. The skilled-trades shortfall and stated scarcity of land, power, and building space weigh against the upper half of the range. The announced projects and deployments provide some counterweight, but their capacity is not shown to be fully new US capacity added during calendar 2026: some are multi-period or unscheduled, and some lack a stated US location. The evidence therefore does not imply 15 GW or more.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e678\",\n    \"e679\",\n    \"e680\",\n    \"e40\",\n    \"e263\",\n    \"e330\",\n    \"e331\",\n    \"e675\",\n    \"e727\",\n    \"e732\",\n    \"e1085\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e678\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e679\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e680\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e40\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e263\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e330\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e331\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e675\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e727\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e732\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1085\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 2, but recent evidence adds quantified capacity commitments and a time-specific 1 GW deployment, along with the stated power-supply agreement. These change the supporting facts and modestly raise the implied estimate within the rung; they do not establish that the announced capacity will be added in the US during 2026.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"Huang’s estimate of “somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year” is the reference. It spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW), rung 3 (15–<20 GW), and reaches rung 4 at 20 GW; the 15 GW midpoint is in rung 3.\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About 14 GW of US additions in calendar 2026: below the 15 GW midpoint of Huang’s range after weighing the quantified labour shortfall and stated site and power constraints, while allowing for some contribution from the announced projects. The project figures are not added wholesale because the evidence does not establish their full 2026 timing, US location, or conversion into new capacity.\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 16,\n  \"request\": \"Thesis v9 (ai-capex, theme, focus \\\"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2027 data-center capital expenditure, versus the $1.3 trillion Nvidia projects for next year (as cited in the item) and the nearly $800 billion expected for calendar 2026.\\\", 2027, due 2028-03-30): How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?\\nLadder: rung 1 below 1,000,000,000,000 USD: below 1,000,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD: from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD: from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \\\"the current estimates are for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion next year. That's just according to Nvidia's projections\\\") / rung 4 from 1,400,000,000,000 USD to below 1,600,000,000,000 USD: from 1,400,000,000,000 USD to below 1,600,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 1,600,000,000,000 USD or more: 1,600,000,000,000 USD or more\\n\\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 12 items]\\n- e674 [party_statement; fool.com article author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7968b6992410f494f08511b3 2026-09-30] The article reports, attributing the figures to Nvidia's projections for its key customers' capex, that the big five AI hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data-center build-outs in calendar 2026 ('this year'), with current estimates for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion in 2027 ('next year').\\n    Quote: The big five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data center build-outs this year, and the current estimates are for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion next year. That's just according to Nvidia's projections, which are likely pretty accurate, given what it knows about its key customers' plans.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e326 [fact; Keithen Drury (fool.com); original source Alphabet ; from gnews:f72f4de1d44f4ee9244c7f89 2026-09-26] Alphabet's Google Cloud division grew 82% in the second quarter, a reported figure the author uses to argue that hyperscaler cloud growth, and therefore data center spending, will stay elevated.\\n    Quote: During the second quarter, this division grew at a remarkable 82% pace, and with Alphabet spending $200 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, the growth rate will likely stay elevated for a long time.\\n- e733 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang estimated that a one-gigawatt data center costs roughly $60 billion, or about $10 billion a year spread over six years, while the same capacity currently generates about $50 billion a year in rental revenue.\\n    Quote: A one-gigawatt data center costs roughly $60 billion. Spread over six years, that works out to about $10 billion a year. Yet that same capacity currently brings in about $50 billion a year in rental revenue, Huang said.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e389; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e781 [third_party_view; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source  ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] The article states top-five hyperscaler capital expenditure is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in calendar 2026 and $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027.\\n    Quote: Balancing that risk, top-five hyperscaler capex is expected to reach nearly $800 billion in 2026 and $1.3 trillion in 2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e817 [third_party_view; Tom O'Malley, Barclays analyst, reported by Investing.com; original source Barclays ; from gnews:39776c44d026d000e4db120a 2026-10-01] Barclays' internet team assumes cloud capital spending reaches about $1.1 trillion by 2027; analyst Tom O'Malley used that assumption, plus an assumption that 80% of it goes to IT, to calculate what Nvidia's revenue should look like against Barclays' own estimates.\\n    Quote: Using Barclays' internet team's assumption that cloud capital spending reaches about $1.1 trillion by 2027, and assuming 80% of that goes to IT, O'Malley calculated what Nvidia's revenue should look like against the firm's own estimates.\\n- e825 [party_statement; Insider Monkey; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:23646c683c86bed389e13357 2026-10-01] Nvidia's own estimates project the big hyperscalers' data center spending at nearly $800 billion this year (calendar 2026) and $1.3 trillion next year (calendar 2027), with the article noting those are Nvidia's numbers and that the hyperscalers may start sharing 2027 capital spending guidance when they report quarterly results.\\n    Quote: The growth case leans on the big hyperscalers, whose data center spending is projected at nearly $800 billion this year and $1.3 trillion next year, according to Nvidia's own estimates. … Hyperscalers may start sharing 2027 capital spending guidance when they report quarterly results.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e926 [third_party_view; Barclays analyst Tom O'Malley, reported by TradingView/Stocktwits; original source Barclays (Tom O'Malley) 2026-10-01; from gnews:d91c54ec44da3c9a5cbef369 2026-10-02] Barclays analyst Tom O'Malley said on Thursday that Nvidia's hyperscaler revenue could run well above Barclays' current estimates: 'napkin math' based on Nvidia's latest IT spending data points to roughly $237 billion of hyperscaler revenue in 2026 and $401 billion in 2027, versus Barclays' existing estimates of $206 billion and $370 billion respectively. In an upside scenario he sees $246 billion in 2026 and $417 billion in 2027, and he noted the calculation may understate the opportunity because the capex comparison excludes SpaceX.\\n    Quote: On Thursday, Barclays analyst Tom O’Malley said Nvidia’s hyperscaler revenue could potentially run well above the firm’s current estimates, using “ napkin math ” based on Nvidia’s latest IT spending data. … His calculations point to roughly $237 billion in hyperscaler revenue in 2026 and $401 billion in 2027, compared with Barclays’ existing estimates of $206 billion and $370 billion, respectively\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e815; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e962 [third_party_view; fxleaders.com; original source Barclays (analyst Tom O'Malley) ; from gnews:4d8fb36192b66cfb50e49f46 2026-10-02] Barclays analyst Tom O'Malley estimates Nvidia could capture at least $30 billion of additional revenue across 2026 and 2027 from the five largest cloud providers, assuming continued capital spending by major cloud companies; Barclays expects cloud capital expenditure could reach approximately $1.1 trillion by 2027, with around 80% allocated toward information technology.\\n    Quote: Barclays analyst Tom O’Malley has identified substantial additional revenue potential for Nvidia from the five largest cloud providers. … The bank estimates Nvidia could capture at least $30 billion of additional revenue across 2026 and 2027, assuming continued capital spending by major cloud companies. Barclays expects cloud capital expenditure could reach approximately $1.1 trillion by 2027, wit\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e815; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e973 [party_statement; fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:b7e22d5ba15d6483a30d2389 2026-10-03] Nvidia expects the big five AI hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures in 2026.\\n    Quote: In 2026, Nvidia expects the big five artificial intelligence (AI) hyperscalers to spend nearly $800 billion on capital expenditures .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e103; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n- e1009 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon's AWS backlog reached $496 billion, posting triple-digit year-over-year growth.\\n    Quote: AWS's backlog reached $496 billion, posting triple-digit year-over-year growth.\\n- e1010 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Andy Jassy, Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said in July 2026 that even with significantly increased investment Amazon would struggle to meet all AI computing demand, that it would not have enough capacity to satisfy all demand in 2026 and the same would be true in 2027, and that demand already secured for 2028 was extraordinary.\\n    Quote: Andy Jassy, Amazon's CEO, said in July that even with significantly increased investment, the company would struggle to meet all AI computing demand. … At the time, he stated: \\\"We won't have enough capacity to satisfy all the demand we have in 2026, and the same will be true in 2027.\\\" He added that \\\"the demand already secured for 2028 is extraordinary.\\\"\\n- e1124 [party_statement; NVIDIA CFO; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] On NVIDIA's August 26, 2026 earnings call, the CFO said customers' forecasts point to NVIDIA's growth doubling next year, but NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply constrained.\\n    Quote: The chief financial officer said on the call, \\\"Customers' forecasts point to our growth doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70% as we are supply constrained.\\\"\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e339; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\\n\\n[Previous conclusion, for comparison only] rung 3; reasons: The reference is Nvidia's $1.3 trillion projection for calendar 2027, which is rung 3. The Barclays estimate of about $1.1 trillion is a counterweight, but it is framed as cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex. Amazon's reported backlog and CEO's statement that capacity will remain insufficient in 2027 support continued investment, but do not quantify an increase above Nvidia's projection. The evidence therefore still implies about $1.3 trillion, within rung 3's $1.2 trillion-to-below-$1.4 trillion range, and does not support crossing into rung 4.\\n\\nAnswer per the JSON schema. cites may name only the evidence ids listed above; cites_views must be empty: this thesis depends on no other thesis.\",\n  \"answer\": {\n   \"rung\": 3,\n   \"reasons\": \"The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-growth signal, supports continued spending but does not quantify a revision above $1.3 trillion. The implied outcome therefore remains within rung 3 and does not cross a boundary.\",\n   \"cites\": [\n    \"e674\",\n    \"e817\",\n    \"e1010\",\n    \"e1124\"\n   ],\n   \"cites_views\": [],\n   \"pushes\": [\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e674\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e326\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e733\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e781\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e817\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_first\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e825\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e926\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e962\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e973\",\n     \"push\": \"none\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1009\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1010\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"evidence_id\": \"e1124\",\n     \"push\": \"toward_last\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"changed\": true,\n   \"why_changed\": \"The rung and implied figure are unchanged, but e1124 adds a customer-forecast signal that NVIDIA growth could double next year, with NVIDIA itself constrained to about 70% growth. This supports the demand backdrop but does not give a quantified hyperscaler capex revision.\",\n   \"theme_pushes\": [],\n   \"reference\": \"Nvidia projects about $1.3 trillion of combined big-five hyperscaler data-center capex in calendar 2027; that reference is rung 3 ($1.2 trillion to below $1.4 trillion).\",\n   \"implied_outcome\": \"About $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027, retaining Nvidia's stated projection as the quantified estimate. The Barclays figure of about $1.1 trillion is for cloud capital spending, while the other demand signals do not quantify a different combined data-center capex total.\"\n  }\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\")); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(str((i[\\\"item\\\"],len(i[\\\"request\\\"]),i[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"rung\\\"])) for i in x))' \"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\")); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(str((i[\\\"item\\\"],len(i[\\\"request\\\"]),i[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"rung\\\"])) for i in x))' \"","aggregated_output":"(1, 9560, 3)\n(2, 11733, 2)\n(3, 78923, 3)\n(4, 51019, 5)\n(5, 30468, 1)\n(6, 20766, 1)\n(7, 19314, 2)\n(8, 26915, 1)\n(9, 45840, 1)\n(10, 17115, 1)\n(11, 26066, 1)\n(12, 26462, 5)\n(13, 47404, 5)\n(14, 41412, 3)\n(15, 10601, 5)\n(16, 10815, 3)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"ITEM\\\",i[\\\"item\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",i[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"[Themes in force\\\")[0].split(\\\"[Previous conclusion\\\")[0],\\\"\\\\nANSWER\\\",json.dumps(i[\\\"answer\\\"],ensure_ascii=False))) for i in x[:4]]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"ITEM\\\",i[\\\"item\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",i[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"[Themes in force\\\")[0].split(\\\"[Previous conclusion\\\")[0],\\\"\\\\nANSWER\\\",json.dumps(i[\\\"answer\\\"],ensure_ascii=False))) for i in x[:4]]'\"","aggregated_output":"ITEM 1 \n Thesis v13 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Nvidia's CPO switch shipment volume from Q4 2026 through calendar 2027 (GF Securities: 15,000 units in Q4 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, 30,000 by mid-2027, 100,000 for all of 2027), as reflected in Nvidia's networking revenue disclosures and suppliers' CPO/NPO laser shipments.\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027, as shown by Nvidia’s shipment disclosures. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-04 and before reaching 100,000 units, Nvidia formally abandons CPO switch shipments for calendar 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-04 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 4 items]\n- e1068 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities said Nvidia is scaling out co-packaged optics (CPO) faster than expected and forecast Nvidia's CPO switch shipments at 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, rising to 30,000 by mid-2027 and potentially 100,000 units for all of 2027. The figures are the analyst's estimate, not an Nvidia disclosure; the article links them to supplier Lumentum, whose laser chips and photonic components go into optical systems.\n    Quote: GF Securities says Nvidia (NVDA) is scaling out co-packaged optics, or CPO, faster than expected, creating another potential growth driver for Lumentum. … The forecast is striking. GF Securities expects Nvidia's CPO switch shipments to reach 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, rising to 16,000 in early 2027 and 30,000 by mid-2027. For all of 2027, shipments could reach 100,000 units.\n- e1069 [fact; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum reported fiscal Q4 revenue of $1.01 billion, more than doubling from a year earlier and its eighth consecutive quarter of year-over-year growth, with Components revenue up 103% to $649.4 million and Systems revenue up 123% to $356.9 million; gross margin rose to 50% and operating margin to 36.6%, net income was $326.3 million, adjusted EPS was $3.23 (over three times the year-ago quarter), free cash flow was $196.2 million, and cash and short-term investments totaled $2.74 billion at period end. Lumentum supplies laser chips and photonic components used in optical systems.\n    Quote: In Q4, the company generated $1.01 billion in revenue, more than doubling from a year ago and extending its streak of year-over-year growth to eight straight quarters. … The increase was widespread throughout the company. Components' revenue grew 103% to $649.4 million, while Systems' revenue increased 123% to $356.9 million. Profitability also improved, as the gross margin climbed to 50% and the \n- e1070 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum management guided fiscal Q1 2027 revenue to roughly $1.225–1.275 billion, an increase of more than $150 million sequentially, with adjusted earnings per share of $4.05–$4.35 and expected gains in profitability, saying it expects the current trend to persist through fiscal 2027.\n    Quote: Management anticipates the trend to persist through fiscal 2027 as well. Lumentum is projecting an increase of more than $150 million in sales for the first quarter, along with gains in profitability, as revenue for the period is expected to be in the neighborhood of $1.225 billion to $1.275 billion and adjusted earnings per share are projected to range between $4.05 and $4.35.\n- e1071 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities forecast that Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028, and that its near-packaged optics (NPO) laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028, as Nvidia's shift to co-packaged and near-packaged optics drives optical component demand. These are the analyst's estimates of a supplier's revenue, not Nvidia figures.\n    Quote: GF Securities sees Lumentum benefiting directly from that shift. The firm forecasts Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028. … Near-packaged optics, or NPO, could become an even larger opportunity. GF Securities estimates Lumentum's NPO laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028.\n\n \nANSWER {\"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"The reference is GF Securities' estimate of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027, which sits at rung 1. The evidence supports an optical-demand ramp, but the 100,000 figure remains an analyst estimate rather than Nvidia shipment volume: Lumentum's revenue and guidance cover broader business, while its projected CPO and NPO laser revenues are not converted into Nvidia switch units. Discounting the unshipped product target, the most likely outcome remains below 100,000, but there is no evidence of formal abandonment or another decision against the event as a whole; therefore rung 3 is the forecast.\", \"cites\": [\"e1068\", \"e1069\", \"e1070\", \"e1071\"], \"cites_views\": [], \"pushes\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"e1068\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1069\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1070\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1071\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}], \"changed\": false, \"why_changed\": \"No change in rung or key supporting facts: the 100,000-unit figure remains a third-party forecast, and supplier growth and optical-revenue estimates still do not establish Nvidia's shipment volume or a decision to abandon CPO.\", \"theme_pushes\": [{\"view_id\": \"v10\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"view_id\": \"v5\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"view_id\": \"v9\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}], \"reference\": \"GF Securities' forecast of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027 is the market-facing reference; 100,000 is the rung 1 boundary.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies calendar-2027 shipments below 100,000, but does not support a defensible more precise unit estimate: Lumentum's projected CPO/NPO laser revenues span products and are not attributable or convertible to Nvidia switch units. No evidence establishes formal abandonment, so the outcome remains open.\"}\nITEM 2 \n Thesis v10 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"US data-center capacity additions in gigawatts for calendar 2026, versus the pace of 'somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year' that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang described, and against how far labour availability constrains that pace.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?\nLadder: rung 1 below 10 GW: below 10 GW / rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW: from 10 GW to below 15 GW / rung 3 from 15 GW to below 20 GW: from 15 GW to below 20 GW (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 GW to below 25 GW: from 20 GW to below 25 GW / rung 5 25 GW or more: 25 GW or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 13 items]\n- e678 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of Nvidia ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, speaking on a panel at the White House, said the US is adding \"somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year\" of AI data-center infrastructure and that the work extends beyond data centers to power generation, construction, power cooling and pipe fitting. This is a management estimate of the annual pace of US AI-infrastructure additions, not a reported outturn.\n    Quote: Huang explained that the US is adding \"somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year\" of the infrastructure and said the work reaches well beyond the data centers themselves. … \"Don't forget you got to build a power generation plants,\" the AI hardware giant's chief executive said, adding: \"You've got construction, you've got power cooling, you've got pipe fitters.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e675; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e679 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (Brian Sozzi, cited); original source Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] The Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST), an initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google, estimated in a new report that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled-trades job openings annually through 2035, and that current training programmes produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed. The figures are the organisation's estimates of a labour-supply constraint on construction and infrastructure work, including data-center build-out.\n    Quote: As Yahoo Finance's Brian Sozzi detailed, the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) — an initiative launched by BlackRock ( BLK ), Carhartt, Ford ( F ), and Google ( GOOG ) — estimated that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled trades job openings annually through 2035. … Current training programs produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed, according to a new AAST report.\n- e680 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft (joining AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft are joining the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades this week, the workforce initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google that targets the skilled-trades occupations used in building and operating infrastructure such as data centers.\n    Quote: Nvidia, Meta ( META ), and Microsoft ( MSFT ) are joining AAST this week. … AAST defines skilled trade jobs as the 124 occupations — including carpenters, electricians, mechanics of various kinds, plumbers, and pipe fitters — that \"build, install, operate, maintain, repair, fabricate, or extract the physical materials, products, equipment, systems, and infrastructure the U.S. economy relies on.\"\n- e40 [party_statement; SB Energy (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source SB Energy ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] SB Energy said it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI, where NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider.\n    Quote: SB Energy says it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI.\n- e263 [fact; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source OpenAI (agreement with Advanced Micro Devices) ; from gnews:dad3a4651f31a14be8d86d8c 2026-09-25] OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts with AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026; the agreement also gives OpenAI a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\n    Quote: OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts using AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026. In addition, the deal also provides OpenAI with a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\n- e330 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon, part of what the article describes as spreading its compute dollars across suppliers.\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\n- e331 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\n- e675 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of NVIDIA 2026-09-29; from gnews:a58c5a2a6302b9af5cfd79c3 2026-09-30] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking at an America.Gov event on 29 September 2026 alongside Elon Musk, estimated that the United States could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI data-center infrastructure annually — an estimate he framed as a rough calculation ('let's just estimate') — and said that building out those data centers would probably create about one million jobs. The article notes that his million-job figure refers to infrastructure-related employment, not net job creation after accounting for potential AI displacement elsewhere.\n    Quote: He estimated that the U.S. could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI infrastructure annually, requiring substantial investment in electricity generation, cooling systems, networking and construction. … \"And so we're building this infrastructure out and, call it across all of the United States, somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year. Let's just estimate, that creates just, probably about\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e678; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e727 [party_statement; Bloomberg, citing Elon Musk's post on X; original source xAI (Elon Musk) ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] xAI CEO Elon Musk said in a post on X, reported by Bloomberg, that the Colossus 2 cluster in the Memphis area already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s—about 550,000 Nvidia chips—and could more than double to top 1.2 million by end-2026, with another 220,000 GB300s due the following week, 220,000 expected in November and a further 220,000 possible in late December \"if we get lucky.\" xAI had previously said it planned to equip the Memphis facility with 1 million GPUs by 2026.\n    Quote: Colossus 2, the Memphis area cluster built by xAI, may more than double its current Nvidia chip count by the end of the year, Bloomberg reported, citing a post Musk made on X.\nHere is how the numbers stack up, according to Musk:\nColossus 2 already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s.\nAnother 220,000 GB300s were set to go live the following week.\nA further 220,000 are expected in Nov\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e255; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n- e1085 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Amazon.com Inc. ; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Amazon.com signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy to secure electricity from Maryland's Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant, buying 690 megawatts of the 1,790-megawatt facility; the agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable power for growing AI operations.\n    Quote: Amazon.com Inc. signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy Corp. to secure electricity from Maryland’s Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant. Under the deal, Amazon will purchase 690 megawatts from the 1,790-megawatt facility, Maryland’s only nuclear power plant. The agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable electricity for gro\n- e1104 [third_party_view; Benzinga, citing Reuters; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley estimates that U.S. data-center developers face a 34% net power shortfall through 2028, equivalent to 32 gigawatts, even after accounting for workarounds such as fuel cells and power generated directly at data-center sites, according to Reuters.\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley estimates U.S. data-center developers face a 34% net power shortfall through 2028, equivalent to 32 gigawatts, even after accounting for workarounds such as fuel cells and power generated directly at data-center sites, according to Reuters.\n- e1105 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Jensen Huang (Nvidia CEO) ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said AI developers now need to \"secure the land, power, and shell\" before filling data centers with computing equipment, and that the process can take two to three years.\n    Quote: Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has described the bottleneck in similar terms, saying AI developers now need to \" secure the land, power , and shell\" before filling data centers with computing equipment. He said that process can take two to three years.\n\n \nANSWER {\"rung\": 2, \"reasons\": \"The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-year lead time for securing land, power, and a shell weigh on 2026 delivery. Signed or planned capacity and xAI’s announced expansion support additions, but do not establish that those amounts will be new U.S. capacity completed in calendar 2026; several are unscheduled, span other periods or suppliers, or lack a stated U.S. location. The evidence supports roughly 12–14 GW, not enough to cross the 15 GW boundary.\", \"cites\": [\"e675\", \"e679\", \"e40\", \"e263\", \"e330\", \"e331\", \"e727\", \"e732\", \"e1085\", \"e1104\", \"e1105\"], \"cites_views\": [], \"pushes\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"e678\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e679\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e680\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e40\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e263\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e330\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e331\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e675\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e727\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e732\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1085\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1104\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1105\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}], \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 2, but the implied estimate moves from about 14 GW to roughly 12–14 GW as the new quantified power-shortfall estimate and the additional labour and project lead-time evidence weigh against delivery. The repeated Huang range is not a new fact; the announced capacity and power arrangements provide counterweight but do not establish calendar-2026 U.S. additions at their full stated amounts.\", \"theme_pushes\": [], \"reference\": \"Huang’s rough estimate of 10–20 GW per year is the stated reference. It spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW) and rung 3 (15–<20 GW), with the 20 GW endpoint at rung 4; its 15 GW midpoint is rung 3.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About 12–14 GW of U.S. additions in calendar 2026, with a central estimate near 13 GW. This is a qualitative forecast from Huang’s annual pace, tempered by the quantified labour and power constraints and partly offset by announced projects; the project figures are discounted where timing, U.S. location, or delivery in 2026 is not established.\"}\nITEM 3 \n Thesis v3 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: By January 31, 2027, U.S. authorities authorize exports of Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow Nvidia’s shipments into China. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 23, 2026, the U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security formally prohibits Blackwell exports to China through January 31, 2027, or Chinese authorities formally prohibit Nvidia’s Blackwell shipments into China through that date. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 84 items]\n[Not shown: 29 more pieces in force, read in earlier judgements; the previous conclusion carries them]\n- e80 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source The Trump administration (U.S. government) ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China, per the article; the H200 is described as an older-generation product behind Nvidia's current Blackwell chips.\n    Quote: The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e365, e425, e565, e745, e851, e879, e942; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e81 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Chinese authorities ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Chinese authorities have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips into China, according to the article, despite U.S. permission for those shipments to approved customers.\n    Quote: Chinese authorities, however, have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e566; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e82 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Nvidia has not factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, according to the article — meaning any resumption of China shipments would be incremental to its stated outlook.\n    Quote: While Nvidia hasn't factored any sales from China into its outlook for the year, the stock is still up 21% year to date, powered by the AI boom here in the US.\n- e86 [fact; barchart.com; original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO) 2026-09-23; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang is scheduled to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, 2026, along with other technology leaders, as the U.S. and China discuss AI, trade, tariffs and advanced semiconductors. The article says the dinner itself does not guarantee policy changes but could provide a backdrop for signals on NVIDIA's access to the Chinese market and export restrictions.\n    Quote: Nvidia (NVDA) investors have a key date to watch as CEO Jensen Huang is set to attend President Donald Trump's state dinner for Chinese President Xi Jinping on Sept. 24, along with other prominent technology leaders at the White House. The high-profile gathering comes as the U.S. and China navigate sensitive issues involving artificial intelligence (AI), trade, tariffs, and advanced semiconductor \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e302; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e88 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points. The forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n    Quote: Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e57; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e111 [party_statement; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided third-quarter fiscal 2027 revenue to $108 billion, a guidance the article says excludes China Data Center compute revenue, cited as geopolitical uncertainty.\n    Quote: Management guided Q3 to $108 billion. … Q3 guidance excludes China Data Center compute revenue citing geopolitical uncertainty.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e57; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e148 [fact; CNBC; original source U.S. government (Washington) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] The article reports that Washington has for years restricted China's access to Nvidia's most advanced AI chips and to the equipment used to make them, on the bet that limiting computing power would keep Chinese AI a generation behind; the restriction is described as in force as of the 23 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Washington has for years restricted China's access to Nvidia's most advanced AI chips and the equipment to make them, betting that starving computing power would keep Chinese AI a generation behind.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e428, e918; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e149 [party_statement; CNBC; original source U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] During the September 2026 Trump–Xi state visit, the article said neither side appeared ready to put chip export controls on the table; Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer met Vice Premier He Lifeng in New York over the weekend and set up a formal AI channel for incident warnings, and Greer said explicitly that export controls on advanced chips and chipmaking equipment were not on the agenda.\n    Quote: As Trump hosts Xi for a multi-day state visit, neither side appears ready to put chip export controls on the table. … Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer met Vice Premier He Lifeng in New York over the weekend and set up a formal AI channel for incident warnings. But Greer said explicitly that export controls on advanced chips and chipmaking equipment were\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e380, e399, e404, e442; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e150 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Huawei ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] Huawei said at its annual Huawei Connect conference, the week before the 23 September 2026 article, that its Ascent 960DT AI chip will ship in the first quarter of 2027, three quarters ahead of its original schedule.\n    Quote: At its annual Huawei Connect conference last week, Huawei said its Ascent 960DT will ship in the first quarter of 2027 , three quarters ahead of its original schedule.\n- e151 [fact; CNBC; original source Alibaba (chip unit) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] At the Apsara Conference in Hangzhou on the Tuesday before the 23 September 2026 article, Alibaba's chip unit unveiled the Zhenwu V900 AI chip, which it says triples the performance of its predecessor and will go on sale in early 2027.\n    Quote: At the Apsara Conference in Hangzhou on Tuesday, Alibaba's chip unit unveiled the Zhenwu V900 chip , which triples the performance of its predecessor and will go on sale in early 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e53; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e152 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source CNBC ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] The article reports that Huawei's latest SuperPoD computing architecture links far fewer processors than originally planned, and that each Huawei chip still delivers roughly half the compute of Nvidia's chips.\n    Quote: But the approach has limits. Huawei's latest SuperPoD computing architecture links far fewer processors than originally planned, and each chip still delivers roughly half the compute of Nvidia's.\n- e153 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Huawei (Chairman Eric Xu) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] Huawei Chairman Eric Xu acknowledged that Huawei may not be able to make enough AI chips to meet Chinese domestic demand, and that Chinese AI developers may not be advanced enough to experience rogue safety risks, as reported in the 23 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Chairman Eric Xu acknowledged that Huawei may not be able to make enough chips to meet domestic demand, and that Chinese AI developers may not be advanced enough to experience rogue safety risks.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e440, e749; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e154 [third_party_view; CNBC; original source Chris Miller (Tufts University) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] Chris Miller, a Tufts University professor and author of 'Chip War', said Chinese firms remain substantially short of the chips needed to train and deploy advanced models and rely heavily on smuggling chips or gaining access to data centers outside China, concluding that China is still highly dependent on the U.S. for advanced chips.\n    Quote: Chinese firms remain substantially short of the chips needed to train and deploy advanced models, and rely heavily on smuggling chips or gaining access to data centers outside China, Miller said. \"China is still highly dependent on the U.S. for advanced chips.\"\n- e155 [rumour; CNBC; original source DeepSeek ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] DeepSeek, the AI startup whose low-cost model rattled global markets the previous year, reportedly lined up the largest known cluster of Huawei chips built to date, according to the article.\n    Quote: DeepSeek, the startup whose low-cost model rattled global markets last year, reportedly lined up the largest known cluster of Huawei chips built to date.\n- e226 [fact; tradingview.com; original source  ; from gnews:6599781487f187538641b19e 2026-09-24] According to tradingview.com, export curbs have limited China's access to NVIDIA chips, shrinking China's share of NVIDIA's data center sales to a minimal level.\n    Quote: Export curbs have limited China’s access, shrinking China’s share of NVDA’s data center sales to a minimal level.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e443; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e302 [fact; Business Chief (Alec McDonnell); original source Jensen Huang (NVIDIA CEO), with Donald Trump and Xi Jinping ; from gnews:d6b6359e9b16cc349069ac0c 2026-09-25] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang is reported to be joining US President Donald Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping at a state dinner on Thursday, an occasion at which sales of chips to China are on the agenda — a diplomatic-level step bearing on whether NVIDIA's China data-center business can resume.\n    Quote: Jensen joins Trump and Chinese President Xi Jinping at Thursday's state dinner, with chip sales to China on the table\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e86; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e308 [fact; UNN; original source U.S. authorities (the export ban is not attributed to a named agency in the item) ; from gnews:d1b9fc5258b883721374fb1e 2026-09-25] The article states that Nvidia's and AMD's most advanced artificial intelligence chips are banned from being exported to China, while noting China remains a key market and manufacturing hub for Apple and Tesla. The item names no agency, date or scope for the ban beyond 'most advanced' chips.\n    Quote: Nvidia and AMD's most advanced artificial intelligence chips are banned from being exported to China, although the country remains a key market and manufacturing hub for Cook's Apple and Musk's Tesla.\n- e365 [fact; ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News); original source U.S. government (Trump administration) ; from gnews:a883c7d707bfef9843efbc2a 2026-09-26] As reported on 26 September 2026, U.S. export restrictions currently bar Nvidia's most powerful (current-generation) AI chips from being exported to China, while the prior year (2025) President Donald Trump permitted Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers subject to a 25% tariff, according to ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News).\n    Quote: Nvidia’s most powerful chips are currently prohibited from export to China due to Washington’s restrictions. At the same time, last year Trump allowed Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers with a 25% tariff.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e377 [rumour; Lockridge Okoth, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance); original source The Information (unnamed-source report on China's MIIT and Alibaba/ByteDance) 2026-09-27; from gnews:b4ef3fc37047e9a76439ecdb 2026-09-27] The Information reported on Sunday 27 September 2026, citing two people familiar with the matter, that China may allow Alibaba and ByteDance to buy a new NVIDIA chip, and that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) asked the two firms how many RTX PRO 5500 cards they want and why. The RTX PRO 5500 is a workstation part that sits outside the data-center chips targeted by US export curbs. Neither Beijing nor the companies confirmed the plan; the report gave no quantity or timing and no sign that bigger data-center chips would follow.\n    Quote: China may allow Alibaba and ByteDance to buy a new Nvidia chip, The Information reported on Sunday. … China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT), which oversees the tech sector, asked the two firms how many RTX PRO 5500 cards they want and why. That is according to The Information, citing two people familiar with the matter. … Because it is a workstation part, it sits outside t\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e382, e384, e397, e400, e403, e418, e419, e423, e427, e430, e519; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e382 [rumour; Reuters (published by CNBC); original source The Information 2026-09-27; from gnews:c24441966fa661191bb1a601 2026-09-27] The Information reported on Sunday, citing two unnamed people familiar with the matter, that China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked companies including ByteDance and Alibaba to report their plans to purchase Nvidia's RTX PRO 5500 chips — a chip built for high-end professional computers — and told some Chinese companies that the government intends to approve the purchases. Reuters said it could not immediately verify the report, and Alibaba and ByteDance did not immediately respond to requests for comment.\n    Quote: The Chinese government has signaled it could allow some domestic companies like ByteDance and Alibaba to buy Nvidia's chip built for high-end professional computers, The Information reported on Sunday, citing two people familiar with the matter. … China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked companies including ByteDance and Alibaba to report their plans to purchase Nvidi\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e377; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e383 [party_statement; Nvidia spokesperson, reported by Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c24441966fa661191bb1a601 2026-09-27] Asked by Reuters to comment on the report, an Nvidia spokesperson said U.S. firms continue to be restricted by a combination of outdated U.S. export controls — which the spokesperson said cover gaming products released nearly half a decade ago — and China's own limits on U.S. imports.\n    Quote: \"US firms continue to be restricted by a combination of outdated US export controls, which cover gaming products released nearly half a decade ago, and China's own limits on US imports,\" an Nvidia spokesperson said when asked to comment.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e424; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e398 [fact; investing.com; original source Nvidia (product release); specifications per TechPowerUp ; from gnews:68448e3de272bc46d0ede0fa 2026-09-27] Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU released this month with 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores and a starting price reported above $6,000 according to TechPowerUp; the chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of successive rounds of U.S. export controls since October 2022, which the article says may give Beijing more regulatory latitude to approve purchases without new friction with Washington.\n    Quote: The RTX Pro 5500 is a Blackwell-generation professional workstation GPU released this month, featuring 84 GB of GDDR7 memory and 21,760 CUDA cores, with a starting price reported above $6,000, according to TechPowerUp. The chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of successive rounds of U.S. export controls since October 2022, potentially giving Beiji\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e433; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e401 [fact; Bloomberg; original source US administration ; from gnews:95fdd0c465e5c78a407a379b 2026-09-27] The US administration recently lifted export restrictions on some Nvidia chips, but the products covered tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold into China only in small quantities, Bloomberg reported.\n    Quote: The US administration recently lifted restrictions on some Nvidia chips, though the products tend to be older-generation chips that have been sold in small quantities.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e421, e429; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e426 [fact; The Next Web, reporting in August 2026; original source Chinese authorities (unnamed) ; from gnews:905fc2415065c07f926596d0 2026-09-27] ByteDance and Tencent were told to keep the first shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips in Hong Kong rather than bring them into mainland China, The Next Web reported in August 2026.\n    Quote: However, ByteDance and Tencent were told to keep the first shipments in Hong Kong , TNW reported in August, rather than bring them into mainland China.\n- e428 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source US government (Washington) ; from gnews:246875816e58773740fb09d8 2026-09-27] The US government bars Nvidia's most powerful accelerators from China on national-security grounds, a restriction the article describes as still in force and distinguishes from the RTX Pro 5500, which is built on older-generation processes and is intended mainly for graphics and simulations rather than being a top-of-the-line accelerator such as the GB300 series.\n    Quote: Washington bars Nvidia's most powerful accelerators from China, concerned they would propel the country's AI efforts and aid the military of a geopolitical ally. … Unlike Nvidia's top-of-the-line accelerators such as the GB300 series, it's built on older-generation processes and is designed to provide workstation computation at scale.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e148; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e435 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Jeffrey Kessler, under secretary of commerce for industry and security ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] In July, under secretary of commerce for industry and security Jeffrey Kessler told the House Foreign Affairs Committee that roughly $10 billion in approved export licences had yielded \"a very small quantity of chips,\" which he called trivial; the article frames the H200 clearance as the largest reopening of chip sales to China in years that still produced almost nothing.\n    Quote: The H200 clearance was the largest reopening of chip sales to China in years, and it still produced almost nothing. In July, the under secretary of commerce for industry and security, Jeffrey Kessler, told the House Foreign Affairs Committee that roughly $10 billion in approved licences had yielded \"a very small quantity of chips,\" which he called trivial.\n- e436 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source  ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chinese buyers had ordered more than 2 million Nvidia H200 chips for 2026 against Nvidia inventory of about 700,000 units, as reported by the article.\n    Quote: Chinese buyers had ordered more than 2 million H200s for 2026 against Nvidia inventory of about 700,000 units.\n- e437 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line, according to the article.\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line.\n- e440 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Huawei (Xu Zhijun, rotating chairman) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Huawei rotating chairman Xu Zhijun said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that Huawei's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\n    Quote: Huawei's rotating chairman, Xu Zhijun, said at the Huawei Connect 2026 event in Shanghai that the company's AI computing product production capacity cannot even meet all domestic demand in China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e153; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e443 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia (CEO Jensen Huang) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened.\n    Quote: Nvidia's China H200 sales remain below 1% of data-center revenue, and CEO Jensen Huang has said the company's share of China's advanced AI chip market effectively fell from around 95% to zero as restrictions tightened.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e226; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e715 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06cfbef2e042b99ce960fcd8 2026-09-30] Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market, and warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n    Quote: Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market. … The company warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n- e745 [fact; theregister.com; original source US Commerce Department / Trump administration ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] US export policy toward Nvidia's China sales: the US Commerce Department imposed new licensing requirements on Nvidia's and AMD's China-spec accelerators in April 2025, reversed course that summer and later reached an arrangement that gave the US government a percentage of certain China-bound chip sales; by late 2025 the Trump administration loosened restrictions further and announced that Nvidia would be allowed to sell more potent H200-series parts to approved Chinese customers for the first time.\n    Quote: After imposing new licensing requirements on Nvidia's and AMD's China-spec accelerators in April 2025, the US Commerce Department reversed course that summer, later reaching an arrangement to cut the US government in on a percentage of certain China-bound chip sales. By late last year, the Trump administration loosened restrictions further, announcing that Nvidia would be allowed to sell its more \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e746 [party_statement; theregister.com (citing Nvidia's Q2 earnings call); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] On Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, executives said the company had begun shipping a small volume of H200 accelerators to China but that these shipments amounted to less than 1 percent of its data-center revenues.\n    Quote: During Nvidia's Q2 earnings call, executives revealed that while the company had begun shipments of a small volume of H200 accelerators to the region, they amounted to less than 1 percent of its datacenter revenues.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1139; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e747 [fact; theregister.com; original source Chinese government officials ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] The item reports that the earlier-in-2026 blockade of Nvidia H20 accelerators for China set off alarm bells in Beijing and that Chinese government officials began pressuring data-center operators to transition away from foreign accelerators toward homegrown alternatives, even after Washington allowed Nvidia to resume sales.\n    Quote: While Uncle Sam had given Jensen Huang its blessing to resume sales, the initial blockade of H20s earlier in the year had set off alarm bells in Beijing. Government officials there began pressuring bit barn operators to transition away from foreign accelerators in favor of homegrown alternatives.\n- e763 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%; the outlook excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, which Zacks said suggests current expectations do not depend on a China recovery.\n    Quote: The near-term outlook also remains strong. NVIDIA expects fiscal third-quarter revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. This outlook excludes Data Center compute revenues from China, suggesting current expectations do not depend on a recovery in that market.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e57; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e780 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's guidance already excludes China data center compute revenue, according to the article.\n    Quote: Guidance already excludes China data center compute revenue.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e378; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e784 [fact; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese state-backed financing entity, funded AI infrastructure developer Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of more than 700 servers, and at least one transaction explicitly funded 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers powered by Nvidia's high-performance B300 Blackwell chips, according to Bloomberg News citing filings with the People's Bank of China. The item notes the documented batch of Blackwell-equipped servers is relatively small but highlights gaps in Western export restrictions.\n    Quote: The financing, provided by Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., supported the purchase of more than 700 servers by AI infrastructure developer Glory View Technology Co. … Filings with the People's Bank of China cited by Bloomberg reveal that at least one transaction explicitly funded 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers powered by Nvidia's high-performance B300 Blackwell chips. … While the documented batch of\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e957; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e785 [party_statement; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Nvidia told Bloomberg it is investigating the matter with its equipment manufacturing partners, after a Bloomberg report that a Chinese state-backed lessor financed purchases of servers powered by Nvidia B300 Blackwell chips; Asus reiterated its commitment to international export control regulations.\n    Quote: Nvidia told Bloomberg that it is investigating the matter with its equipment manufacturing partners, while Asus reiterated its commitment to international export control regulations.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e959; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e786 [fact; Investing.com (citing Bloomberg News and Chinese regulatory filings); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Semi-Tech Leasing Group, originally established with backing from China's national semiconductor fund and now predominantly controlled by municipal government entities in Shenzhen and Beijing, has deployed more than 11 billion yuan ($1.6 billion) into compute infrastructure, positioning itself as a core financing engine for national AI priorities.\n    Quote: Semi-Tech Leasing, originally established with backing from China's national semiconductor fund, is now predominantly controlled by municipal government entities in Shenzhen and Beijing. … The lessor has deployed more than 11 billion yuan ($1.6 billion) into compute infrastructure, positioning itself as a core financing engine for national AI priorities.\n- e787 [fact; Investing.com (citing Bloomberg News and Chinese regulatory filings); original source Glory View Technology Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Glory View Technology secured over 3 billion yuan ($450 million) under sale-and-leaseback arrangements initiated in mid-2025 to expand its data-center capacity, with the majority of the procured hardware slated for deployment at a major China Mobile Ltd. computing hub in Ningxia, a key node in China's state-backed data-center network.\n    Quote: Under a series of sale-and-leaseback arrangements initiated in mid-2025, Glory View secured over 3 billion yuan ($450 million) to expand its data center capacity. … The majority of the procured hardware was slated for deployment at a major China Mobile Ltd. computing hub in Ningxia, a key node in China's state-backed data center network.\n- e788 [fact; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] After initial scrutiny, Semi-Tech Leasing resubmitted altered filings to Chinese credit registries that removed specific hardware descriptions, supplier details and server model designations, which the report says reflects efforts by domestic market participants to obscure the supply chains feeding China's AI infrastructure buildout.\n    Quote: Following initial scrutiny, Semi-Tech Leasing resubmitted altered filings to Chinese credit registries that removed specific hardware descriptions, supplier details, and server model designations. … The selective redactions reflect ongoing efforts by domestic market participants to obscure the supply chains feeding China's rapid AI infrastructure buildout.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e958; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e802 [third_party_view; Fortune (Sasha Rogelberg); original source Epoch AI ; from gnews:d45e5daf2a6f66edffc0d92a 2026-10-01] Epoch AI estimated that, by the end of 2025, smuggling accounted for between 290,000 and 1.6 million Nvidia H100-equivalent chips, about one-third of China’s total compute stock; Epoch AI did not attribute the smuggled chips to cargo theft, according to Fortune.\n    Quote: By the end of 2025, smuggling was responsible for between 290,000 and 1.6 million Nvidia H100–equivalent chips , making up about one-third of China’s total compute stock, according to research institute Epoch AI, though it did not attribute these smuggled chips to cargo theft.\n- e803 [fact; Fortune (Sasha Rogelberg); original source Trump administration ; from gnews:d45e5daf2a6f66edffc0d92a 2026-10-01] Fortune reports that the Trump administration is taking action to ban exports of domestic AI technology, and that organized criminals are smuggling such products into sanctioned countries including China and Russia for black-market resale.\n    Quote: As the Trump administration takes action to ban exports of domestic AI technology, organized criminals see an opportunity to smuggle these products into sanctioned countries like China and Russia and sell them on the black market.\n- e850 [fact; Business Insider (article by Truman Dickerson); original source US federal prosecutors (indictment and court records) ; from gnews:ffd4a1b5211f4a70bf6ad9d1 2026-10-02] US federal prosecutors charged Yiu Kong Lui, 38, of San Gabriel, California, with violating export controls, smuggling and money laundering for helping to move more than $300 million worth of Nvidia semiconductor chips into China; he was arrested on Thursday and is to be arraigned. According to a 22-page indictment, the operation ran from 2023 until August, with Lui and co-conspirators using shell companies to route chips first to Malaysia or Singapore and then into China; a company he owned brokered the deals and was paid over $180 million by Malaysian shipping companies, and Lui lied to US chip manufacturers that the chips would not end up in China.\n    Quote: A California man is facing federal charges for helping smuggle over $300 million worth of Nvidia semiconductor chips into China, prosecutors said this week. … Yiu Kong Lui, 38, of San Gabriel, Cali., was arrested on Thursday and is set to be arraigned in federal court and charged with violating export controls, smuggling, and money laundering, court records show. … In a 22-page indictment, prosecu\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e876, e884, e886, e887, e888, e919; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e851 [fact; Business Insider (article by Truman Dickerson); original source US government (export-licensing requirement); President Donald Trump (2025 decision allowing Nvidia chip sales to China) ; from gnews:ffd4a1b5211f4a70bf6ad9d1 2026-10-02] US policy in force as reported: exporting advanced semiconductor chips to China without a licence is illegal, and President Donald Trump drew criticism last year for allowing Nvidia to sell its chips to China, a departure from the previous Biden administration policy that effectively blocked all exports of advanced chips to China.\n    Quote: It is illegal to export advanced semiconductor chips into China without a license, as the US competes with the East Asian giant in the AI race. … President Donald Trump drew criticism last year for allowing Nvidia to sell its chips to China , a departure from the previous US policy under President Joe Biden, which effectively blocked all exports of advanced chips to China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e876 [fact; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source US federal prosecutors/authorities 2026-10-01; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] US prosecutors charged, and federal authorities arrested, Greg Lui for smuggling servers containing $300 million worth of AI chips to China in violation of US export laws, Bloomberg reported; the arrest occurred the day before the 2 October 2026 article.\n    Quote: US prosecutors just charged a man with smuggling NVIDIA chips to China, and government officials are saying the company isn't doing all it could to stop the illegal activities, Bloomberg reported. … Earlier this year, officials released a surveillance video showing someone using a red hair dryer to peel serial number stickers off NVIDIA server boxes in an effort to sneak AI chips to China. And yes\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e877 [rumour; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (citing unnamed sources) ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] Bloomberg reported that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers capable of housing 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors, and US officials now believe China has already smuggled that many chips and more, according to Bloomberg's sources.\n    Quote: At the same time, Bloomberg reported that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers that could house 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors. US officials now think that China has already smuggled that many chips and more, according to Bloomberg 's sources.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e940; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e878 [rumour; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source US prosecutors (allegations in the Super Micro case) ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] In the Super Micro case, prosecutors allege that collaborators, including co-founder Yih-Shyan 'Wally' Liaw, sold approximately $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to Bangkok-based OBON, ostensibly to build AI infrastructure in Thailand, but the servers were shipped directly to end-users in China; stickers from genuine NVIDIA products were moved onto dummy servers to fool compliance officers.\n    Quote: An example of such an operation involved Super Micro and the famous red hair dryer. Accused collaborators from that wholesaler (including Super Micro co-founder Yih-Shyan \"Wally\" Liaw) allegedly found buyers in China eager to purchase NVIDIA's latest AI tech. To get around export controls, the company sold approximately $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to a Bangkok-based company called OBON, which\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e925, e941; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e879 [fact; Steve Dent (Engadget); original source NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang / Trump administration ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang has lobbied US officials to relax export curbs on China and other Asian nations and persuaded the Trump administration last year (2025) to allow sales of NVIDIA's previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China.\n    Quote: NVIDIA's CEO Jensen Huang has lobbied US officials to relax export curbs to China and other Asian nations, and convince the Trump administration last year to allow sales of its previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e880 [third_party_view; Steve Dent (Engadget), citing Bloomberg; original source Daniel Remler and unnamed US government officials ; from gnews:8148f98d57d514f32433abe5 2026-10-02] Former State Department AI policy head Daniel Remler and unnamed US government officials questioned gaps in NVIDIA's due diligence on China-bound shipments, saying Southeast Asia received more chips than it could use and that NVIDIA should have noticed OBON lacked the 100 megawatts of data-center capacity to host the servers it bought.\n    Quote: However, government officials and industry critics have highlighted gaps in the company's due diligence and questioned how it failed to notice red flags. … Officials say that NVIDIA should have noticed that OBON didn't have the physical capacity (100 megawatts of data center space) to host the number of servers it purchased. … \"I've never felt like we got a straight answer as to how it's possible \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e939; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e884 [fact; Etiido Uko, Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. Department of Justice 2026-10-01; from gnews:4cffee893f7dfe0993352552 2026-10-02] The U.S. Department of Justice announced on October 1, 2026 that Greg Lui (also Yiu Kong Lui), owner of California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., was arrested and charged with smuggling more than $300 million worth of restricted Nvidia chips inside high-end servers to China from 2023 to 2024, allegedly routing shipments first through Malaysia and Singapore — jurisdictions where the restrictions did not apply — and using false paperwork misrepresenting the end users and destinations so no export licence was required, with buyers including the Chinese government. The DOJ charged him with conspiracy to violate the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration Regulations, outbound smuggling, and conspiracy to commit money laundering; he was arraigned on October 1 and faces up to 20 years in prison if convicted on all counts. The FBI, the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security and the Defense Criminal Investigative Service are jointly investigating. The article frames this as the latest in a series of prosecutions for illegal export of restricted AI accelerators to China, where it says demand for Nvidia GPUs remains very high.\n    Quote: A California man has been arrested for smuggling over $300 million worth of restricted Nvidia chips to China in violation of U.S. export controls. According to a U.S. Department of Justice (DoJ) press release on October 1, the defendant — Greg Lui, also known as Yiu Kong Lui — and unidentified co-conspirators smuggled the chips in high-end servers from 2023 to 2024. He allegedly first exported the\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e885 [fact; Etiido Uko, Tom's Hardware; original source  ; from gnews:4cffee893f7dfe0993352552 2026-10-02] The article reports, in an item dated 2 October 2026, that nine people were indicted in Taiwan in the previous month (September 2026) over the illegal export of Nvidia B300 GPUs to China.\n    Quote: Last month, nine people were indicted in Taiwan over the illegal export of Nvidia B300 GPUs to China .\n- e886 [fact; theregister.com; original source US federal prosecutors (indictment) and arresting authorities ; from gnews:a163858fac4dd8592ca6170a 2026-10-02] Greg Lui, 38, a California business owner, was arrested on Thursday (the article was published Friday 2 October 2026) after being charged with allegedly smuggling Nvidia hardware to China without the required US export licenses; he is charged with one count each of Export Control Reform Act / Export Administration Regulations violations, outbound smuggling and money laundering, which together carry a maximum prison sentence of 50 years.\n    Quote: A California business owner was arrested on Thursday after being charged with allegedly smuggling Nvidia hardware to China without the proper export licenses. … Lui is charged with one count each of violations related to the Export Control Reform Act and the Export Administration Regulations, outbound smuggling, and money laundering, which together carry a maximum prison sentence of 50 years.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e887 [fact; theregister.com; original source US federal prosecutors (court documents) ; from gnews:a163858fac4dd8592ca6170a 2026-10-02] Prosecutors allege that from about October 2023 until at least 12 August 2026 Greg Lui used his company Earthmade Computer Inc to order servers containing Nvidia A100, H100, PNY GeForce RTX 4090 and GeForce RTX 5090 GPUs in the US and route them through Malaysia and Singapore to China, brokering sales of almost $300 million worth of Nvidia kit; they claim he received more than $176 million in payments from two Malaysian transshipment companies, and court documents tie a shipment of 92 servers ordered by Earthmade from San Francisco International Airport to Kuala Lumpur, with the onward re-shipment consignee in Hong Kong listed as a Chinese customer.\n    Quote: Greg Lui, 38, allegedly tried to export around $300 million worth of Nvidia kit - typically used for artificial intelligence (AI) development - via US-based freight businesses and Malaysian transshipment companies. … Prosecutors allege that Lui used his company, Earthmade Computer Inc, to order the products in the US, which consisted of servers containing high-powered Nvidia components, including \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e888 [fact; qz.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors 2026-10-01; from gnews:018f6db02c8c3495a8cc108b 2026-10-02] Greg Lui, 38, owner of City of Industry, California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., was arrested on Thursday on a three-count federal indictment charging him with smuggling more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers containing Nvidia AI chips to China, the U.S. Justice Department said. The counts are conspiracy to violate the Export Control Reform Act, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering; the DOJ said conviction on all three could bring up to 20 years on each of the two conspiracy charges and up to 10 years on the smuggling count.\n    Quote: Greg Lui, the owner of a San Gabriel Valley-based technology company, was arrested Thursday on a three-count federal indictment charging him with smuggling more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers containing Nvidia $NVDA artificial intelligence chips to China. … Lui, 38, of San Gabriel, California, owns Earthmade Computer Inc., based in City of Industry, California. He fa\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e889 [fact; qz.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (indictment allegations) ; from gnews:018f6db02c8c3495a8cc108b 2026-10-02] U.S. prosecutors alleged that Greg Lui and unnamed co-conspirators used Earthmade between 2023 and 2024 to buy export-controlled servers containing Nvidia GPUs and route them to China without the required Commerce Department licences, submitting false documentation to U.S. manufacturers about the equipment's destination and shipping via Malaysia and Singapore before re-exporting to China; Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based shipment companies between January and October 2024 as part of the scheme. In one example detailed in the indictment, Lui submitted a purchase order for 27 servers containing Nvidia H100 GPUs at a cost of approximately $7.6 million; the servers went from Los Angeles to Kuala Lumpur and a co-conspirator emailed a Malaysian government official confirming they were transshipped to a buyer in China, per the Justice Department.\n    Quote: Prosecutors said Lui and unnamed co-conspirators used Earthmade between 2023 and 2024 to buy export-controlled servers containing Nvidia GPUs and route them to China without the required U.S. Commerce Department licenses. To circumvent export controls, Lui and his co-conspirators submitted false documentation to U.S. manufacturers misrepresenting where the equipment would end up, then shipped the \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e901, e935; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e901 [fact; yahoo.com; original source US federal prosecutors (Justice Department) 2026-09-29; from gnews:a79a082545d70337856c230a 2026-10-02] US federal prosecutors arrested and charged Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, of San Gabriel, California, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc., on three criminal counts alleging he smuggled more than $300 million of export-controlled US-manufactured AI servers containing GPUs to China via Malaysia and Singapore using falsified end-user and destination paperwork. The indictment, returned by a federal grand jury on September 29, 2026, says the operation ran from 2023 through 2024 and alleges Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based companies tied to the shipments between January and October 2024 (an allegation, not a judicial finding), and that the servers were shipped without the U.S. Commerce Department export licences required.\n    Quote: More than $300 million in export-controlled servers containing U.S.-manufactured GPUs allegedly left the United States for China, routed through Malaysia and Singapore on falsified paperwork. Federal prosecutors arrested Greg Lui , 38, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, of San Gabriel, California, on three criminal counts that carry up to 20 years in federal prison if convicted. … Lui owns Earthmade Comp\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e889; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e902 [fact; yahoo.com; original source Taiwanese prosecutors ; from gnews:a79a082545d70337856c230a 2026-10-02] In August 2026, Taiwanese prosecutors charged nine people in a separate alleged smuggling operation, including an Nvidia Taiwan employee and two Super Micro employees; the case involved 130 servers allegedly routed through Indonesia, Japan and Hong Kong toward Chinese customers, of which 74 were reportedly delivered.\n    Quote: In August 2026, Taiwanese prosecutors charged nine people in a separate alleged smuggling operation, including an Nvidia Taiwan employee and two Super Micro employees. … That case involved 130 servers allegedly routed through Indonesia, Japan, and Hong Kong toward Chinese customers, with 74 reportedly delivered.\n- e918 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source US government (export-control regime), as reported by Fool.com ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] Fool.com's 2 October 2026 article states that NVIDIA faces U.S. export controls that limit sales of its high-end chips in China, describing the restriction as a standing obstacle to its China business; the article names no specific chip, approval or date.\n    Quote: NVIDIA faces significant geopolitical challenges, specifically U.S. export controls limiting its high-end chip sales in China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e148; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e919 [fact; TTNews (ttnews.com); original source U.S. federal prosecutors / Department of Justice, FBI and Bureau of Industry and Security 2026-10-01; from gnews:7a4e01c68a1faa3ffcac0d7c 2026-10-02] U.S. federal prosecutors arrested Greg Lui, owner of privately held Earthmade Computer, on 1 October 2026 and charged him with smuggling computer servers containing $300 million worth of Nvidia artificial intelligence chips to China in violation of U.S. export controls. Prosecutors alleged that Lui and unidentified co-conspirators worked from 2023 to 2024 to ship servers with restricted Nvidia chips to buyers in China without the required U.S. licenses, exporting them first to Malaysia and Singapore — jurisdictions that do not require licences for purchases of advanced AI chips from U.S. companies — and providing false documentation to U.S. manufacturers that misrepresented the final destination; they said the alleged conspiracy continued until at least mid-August. In one purchase order, prosecutors said, Lui obtained 27 servers from an unidentified U.S. manufacturer containing Nvidia H100 GPUs for about $7.6 million. Lui faces one count each of conspiracy to violate U.S. export controls, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering, and could face more than 20 years in prison if convicted; prosecutors sought detention without bail, citing flight risk. The charges were announced jointly by federal prosecutors, the FBI and the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security.\n    Quote: Federal prosecutors arrested a California man Oct. 1 on charges of smuggling computer servers containing $300 million worth of Nvidia Corp. artificial intelligence chips to China in violation of U.S. export controls. … Authorities said that Greg Lui, the owner of privately held Earthmade Computer, worked with other unidentified co-conspirators from 2023 to 2024 to ship servers containing the restr\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e850; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e920 [fact; TTNews (ttnews.com); original source U.S. authorities (federal prosecutors); Nvidia for its own statement ; from gnews:7a4e01c68a1faa3ffcac0d7c 2026-10-02] The article reports that in March, U.S. authorities charged a co-founder of Super Micro Computer with illegally diverting billions of dollars in Nvidia chips to China; the co-founder pleaded not guilty. The item cites this as part of a series of federal prosecutions of individuals accused of violating export controls meant to keep advanced U.S. chips out of China, and reports Nvidia as saying its work with law enforcement has led to prosecutions.\n    Quote: The charges against Lui mark the latest in a series of federal prosecutions of individuals accused of violating export controls aimed at keeping advanced U.S.-made chips out of Chinese hands over national security concerns. In March, authorities charged a co-founder of Super Micro Computer with illegally diverting billions of dollars in Nvidia chips to China. He pleaded not guilty. … “This case sh\n- e925 [fact; Jowi Morales, Tom's Hardware; original source U.S. authorities; three Supermicro employees including co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw ; from gnews:e15aee588a6b29a561565353 2026-10-02] U.S. authorities arrested three Supermicro employees, including co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw, who are suspected of illicitly shipping $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware to China, according to the article, which cites this as the major arrest so far in a recent U.S. crackdown on GPU smuggling. The smuggling conduct and the $2.5 billion figure are described as suspected rather than established.\n    Quote: Even though GPUs that have the processing power of RTX 4090s or higher were first banned for export to China in November 2023, U.S. authorities only started seriously clamping down on smuggling operations recently. One major arrest, so far, is that of three Supermicro employees, including its co-founder, Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw, who are suspected of illicitly shipping $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia h\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e878; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e935 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (Central District of California) 2026-09-29; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] Federal prosecutors in the Central District of California indicted Greg Lui, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. of the City of Industry, California, charging him with smuggling more than $300 million of restricted computer hardware to China — high-end servers containing U.S.-manufactured Nvidia chips, including the Nvidia A100 and H100 — allegedly by declaring Malaysia and Singapore as destinations (which do not require a U.S. export licence) and then freight-forwarding the servers on to China. The indictment cites bank records and emails, including a purchase order to a U.S. manufacturer for 27 servers with Nvidia H100 chips for about $7.6 million, and says Earthmade received more than $176 million in 2024 from two Malaysia-based shipment companies as part of the scheme. Lui was arrested on Thursday and faces one count of conspiracy to violate export controls, one count of outbound smuggling and one count of conspiracy to commit money laundering; the conduct described is alleged, not proven.\n    Quote: The owner of a San Gabriel Valley technology company was arrested Thursday after federal prosecutors charged him with smuggling more than $300 million of restricted computer hardware to China. … Greg Lui, the owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. in the City of Industry, allegedly sold high-end computer servers containing U.S.-manufactured Nvidia chips to the Chinese government, while claiming they wer\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e889; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e936 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source President Trump / U.S. government ; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] President Trump announced late last year (2025) that he authorized Nvidia to sell its H200 chip in China in exchange for the U.S. government taking 25% of the revenue, although exports remain heavily restricted, according to the article.\n    Quote: Late last year, Trump announced that he authorized Nvidia to sell its H200 chip in China in exchange for taking 25% of the revenue, although exports remain heavily restricted.\n- e937 [fact; Suhauna Hussain, L.A. Times; original source U.S. government (export-control actions); Nvidia product response ; from gnews:ec105d00582fa1ef2b4a43e4 2026-10-02] After the U.S. placed chip-export controls on China in 2022, Nvidia designed less-powerful versions of its chips to access the Chinese market while complying with the rules, but those reduced-capability models were later restricted too, according to the article.\n    Quote: After the U.S. placed chip-export controls on China in 2022, Nvidia designed less-powerful versions to access the market while complying with the rules. But those reduced-capability models were later restricted too.\n- e938 [fact; Bloomberg (reported by northeasttimes.com/Maya Brooks); original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors 2026-10-01; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] The U.S. Justice Department announced on Oct. 1, 2026 the arrest of Greg Lui, whom federal prosecutors charged with illegally shipping NVIDIA AI chips to China; prosecutors accuse him of smuggling servers holding $300 million worth of AI chips in violation of U.S. export laws.\n    Quote: Federal prosecutors have charged a man with illegally shipping NVIDIA artificial intelligence chips to China, Bloomberg reported on Oct. 2. … The Justice Department announced the arrest of Greg Lui on Oct. 1. He is accused of smuggling servers holding $300 million worth of AI chips to China in violation of US export laws.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e966, e967, e996, e1004, e1084; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e939 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (unnamed U.S. government officials) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] Unnamed U.S. government officials told Bloomberg in an Oct. 1 feature that NVIDIA is not doing enough to stop illegal exports of its AI chips to China, specifically saying it should have noticed that OBON lacked the 100 megawatts of data-center space needed to host the servers it supposedly bought, since Thailand has only a few hundred megawatts of AI data-center capacity running; the article says NVIDIA has never been accused of helping smugglers and that NVIDIA says it has followed the rules.\n    Quote: Government officials told Bloomberg in a feature published Oct. 1 that NVIDIA is not doing enough to stop the trade. NVIDIA has never been accused of helping smugglers. It says it has followed the rules. … Officials told Bloomberg that NVIDIA should have noticed OBON lacked the 100 megawatts of data center space needed to host that many servers. Thailand has only a few hundred megawatts of AI data\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e880; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e940 [rumour; Bloomberg; original source Bloomberg (unnamed sources / U.S. officials) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] Bloomberg reported, citing sources, that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers capable of housing 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors, and that U.S. officials now believe China has smuggled at least that many chips.\n    Quote: Bloomberg also reported that operators in western China were building dozens of data centers able to house 115,000 restricted NVIDIA processors. Its sources said US officials now believe China has smuggled at least that many chips.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e877; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e941 [rumour; Bloomberg (reported by northeasttimes.com/Maya Brooks); original source U.S. federal prosecutors (allegation against Yih-Shyan Liaw and collaborators) ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] In one criminal case, prosecutors allege that Yih-Shyan Liaw, a co-founder of Super Micro, and collaborators sold about $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to Bangkok-based OBON, supposedly for AI infrastructure in Thailand, but the servers went directly to end users in China; the conspirators allegedly moved stickers from genuine NVIDIA products to dummy servers to evade compliance.\n    Quote: The accused collaborators in one case include Yih-Shyan Liaw, a co-founder of Super Micro. They allegedly sold about $2.5 billion worth of AI servers to OBON, a Bangkok-based company that was supposedly building AI infrastructure in Thailand. … Instead, the servers went straight to end users in China. To get past compliance officers, the conspirators allegedly peeled stickers from genuine NVIDIA p\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e878; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e942 [fact; northeasttimes.com (Maya Brooks); original source Trump administration ; from gnews:1f437f56c47b98bbcf0fd8f9 2026-10-02] The Trump administration last year agreed to allow sales of NVIDIA's previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China, according to the article.\n    Quote: Last year, the Trump administration agreed to allow sales of NVIDIA's previous-generation Hopper H200 accelerators to China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e80; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e957 [fact; Bloomberg (article published by qz.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. (regulatory filings) ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese financing company controlled by local government entities, provided capital for Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of restricted Nvidia AI chips, per Beijing regulatory filings reviewed by Bloomberg. The filings show Semi-Tech Leasing funded more than 700 servers totaling more than 3 billion yuan ($440 million) in financing, with one contract covering 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers equipped with Nvidia's B300 chips (part of the Blackwell family); U.S. regulations prohibit selling B300 chips to China without an explicit license. Most of the servers, including the 32 Blackwell units, were listed as deployed inside a China Mobile Ltd. data center facility in Zhongwei, Ningxia, an officially designated national computing cluster zone.\n    Quote: Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese financing company controlled by local government entities, provided capital for the purchase of restricted Nvidia $NVDA Corp. AI chips, according to regulatory filings reviewed by Bloomberg . … The documents, filed with Beijing regulators, show Semi-Tech Leasing funded purchases by Glory View Technology Co. of more than 700 servers totaling more than 3 billio\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e784; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e958 [fact; Bloomberg (article published by qz.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Semi-Tech Leasing later replaced its regulatory submissions with redacted copies, removing server model designations, specification details that had enabled identification of the Blackwell units, supplier names and installation locations; the process was completed by the end of June. Bloomberg had obtained and reviewed the unredacted filings earlier, in May and June, before the identifying details were removed.\n    Quote: Semi-Tech Leasing later replaced its regulatory submissions with redacted copies, having removed the server model designations, specification details that had enabled identification of the Blackwell units, supplier names, and installation locations — a process completed by the end of June. Bloomberg had obtained and reviewed the unredacted filings earlier, in May and June, before the identifying d\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e788; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e959 [party_statement; qz.com (citing Nvidia's statement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Nvidia said it is \"looking into this report and will work with our OEM customer to investigate\" the financing and deployment in China of servers equipped with its B300 (Blackwell) chips, which U.S. rules bar selling to China without an explicit license.\n    Quote: Nvidia said it is \"looking into this report and will work with our OEM customer to investigate.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e785; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e966 [fact; scmp.com; original source US Department of Justice 2026-10-01; from gnews:f261a85410fee1ff259dee8e 2026-10-02] The US Department of Justice announced that Greg Lui (also known as Yiu Kong Lui), 38, of San Gabriel, California, was arrested on Thursday 1 October 2026 and charged with conspiracy to violate US export controls, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering in connection with an alleged scheme to send restricted AI computer servers to China.\n    Quote: Amid intensifying competition in the AI technology race, US authorities have arrested a California technology company owner accused of using Malaysia and Singapore as transit points to circumvent US export controls and send more than US$300 million worth of restricted computer servers to China. … Greg Lui, 38, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, of San Gabriel, was arrested on Thursday and charged with co\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e938; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e967 [rumour; scmp.com; original source US prosecutors (Department of Justice); John A. Eisenberg, assistant attorney general for national security ; from gnews:f261a85410fee1ff259dee8e 2026-10-02] US prosecutors allege that California technology-company owner Greg Lui routed restricted AI computer servers to China through Malaysia and Singapore, using false documents and transshipment schemes to conceal China as the true destination, and that more than US$300 million worth of restricted servers were sent to China in circumvention of US export controls; Assistant Attorney General for National Security John A. Eisenberg said the indictment details these alleged evasion efforts. The allegations are unconfirmed charges, not established findings.\n    Quote: Prosecutors say California tech firm owner routed restricted AI servers through Malaysia and Singapore to evade US export controls … Amid intensifying competition in the AI technology race, US authorities have arrested a California technology company owner accused of using Malaysia and Singapore as transit points to circumvent US export controls and send more than US$300 million worth of restricte\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e938; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e996 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source US Department of Justice, Office of Public Affairs 2026-10-01; from gnews:9ed0bd3a9ad55db91108d32a 2026-10-03] The US Department of Justice announced on 1 October 2026 the arrest of Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, owner of City of Industry, California-based Earthmade Computer Inc., following a three-count indictment alleging a scheme to route more than $300 million of servers containing NVIDIA A100, H100, GeForce RTX 4090 and RTX 5090 GPUs to China. Prosecutors allege Lui and co-conspirators purchased restricted servers from American manufacturers using paperwork that falsely named permissible overseas customers as final recipients, and shipped them through Malaysia and Singapore before redirecting them to China without required US export licenses; the alleged conspiracy ran from around October 2023 until at least 12 August 2026. The charges (conspiracy to violate export controls, outbound smuggling, money-laundering conspiracy) carry separate maximum prison terms of up to 20, 10 and 20 years if convicted.\n    Quote: A California company owner faces federal charges over an alleged scheme to route more than $300 million in NVIDIA GPU servers to China using false paperwork and shipments through Malaysia and Singapore. The indictment names A100, H100, RTX 4090, and RTX 5090 products; the charges carry separate maximum prison terms of up to 20, 10, and 20 years. … Greg Lui, 38, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, owns Ear\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e938; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1004 [fact; beinsure.com; original source U.S. Department of Justice / federal prosecutors (Central District of California) 2026-09-29; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] A federal grand jury returned an indictment on Sept. 29, 2026, and Greg Lui (Yiu Kong Lui), 38, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. in City of Industry, California, was arrested Oct. 1, 2026, on federal charges alleging he helped smuggle more than $300 million of export-controlled computer servers — containing Nvidia A100 and H100 GPUs — to China in 2023–2024 without Commerce Department licences, presenting shipments as destined for Malaysia and Singapore. Prosecutors cited a January 2024 order for 27 servers worth about $7.6 million shipped from Los Angeles to Kuala Lumpur; a 92-server shipment routed through Singapore and Malaysia to Hong Kong and a company in Hangzhou; and a 100-server shipment of Nvidia H100 GPUs worth more than $22 million. They also allege Earthmade received more than $176 million from two Malaysia-based shipping companies between January and October 2024. The charges remain allegations.\n    Quote: Greg Lui, owner of Earthmade Computer Inc. in California’s City of Industry, has been arrested on federal charges alleging he helped smuggle more than $300 mn of export-controlled computer servers to China. … The 38-year-old San Gabriel resident, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, faces three counts: conspiracy to violate the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration Regulations, outbound smugg\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e938; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1005 [party_statement; beinsure.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] Nvidia said reported diversion of its products represents less than 0.5% of its products and argued unauthorized shipments account for a small share of China's available computing capacity; the company said it screens customers and has stopped shipments when final buyers could not be established. Nvidia also disputed suggestions that unusual order sizes or expansion plans are enough to establish wrongdoing, saying growing technology businesses in permitted markets regularly buy equipment before expanding data-center capacity.\n    Quote: Nvidia said reported diversion represents less than 0.5% of its products and argued that unauthorized shipments account for a small share of China’s available computing capacity. … The company screens customers and has stopped shipments when final buyers couldn’t be established. … Nvidia has disputed suggestions that unusual order sizes or expansion plans are enough to establish wrongdoing. The co\n- e1006 [third_party_view; beinsure.com; original source  ; from gnews:d157aa07f548a04560f025e1 2026-10-03] U.S. officials and other investigators have examined whether manufacturers and suppliers should conduct deeper checks when large orders are sent to facilities in countries neighbouring China, according to the article; recent cases have included allegations involving data centres or buyers in Taiwan, Thailand, Malaysia and Singapore, and in some instances authorities later found inconsistencies between the stated destination, the size of the purchasing facility and the subsequent movement of the servers.\n    Quote: U.S. officials and other investigators have nevertheless examined whether manufacturers and suppliers should conduct deeper checks when large orders are sent to facilities in countries neighboring China. … Recent cases have included allegations involving data centers or buyers in Taiwan, Thailand, Malaysia and Singapore. In some instances, authorities later found inconsistencies between the stated\n- e1066 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA management guided to $108 billion of next-quarter revenue, plus or minus 2%, excluding China data-center compute sales, the article reports.\n    Quote: The bullish case has direct support: management guided to $108 billion of next-quarter revenue, plus or minus 2%, excluding China data-center compute sales.\n- e1084 [rumour; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source US authorities (federal prosecutors/investigators) ; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Authorities arrested Greg Lui, also known as Yiu Kong Lui, 38, of California, alleging he helped route more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers to China without required licenses; the servers reportedly contained GPUs made by Nvidia. Lui faces federal charges involving alleged violations of the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration Regulations, outbound smuggling and conspiracy to commit money laundering.\n    Quote: Authorities arrested Greg Lui , also known as Yiu Kong Lui , alleging that the 38-year-old California resident helped route more than $300 million worth of export-controlled computer servers to China without required licenses. The servers reportedly contained GPUs made by Nvidia Corp . Lui faces federal charges involving alleged violations of the Export Control Reform Act and Export Administration\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e938; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1139 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA's Hopper 200 (H200) products shipped to customers in China were less than 1% of its data center revenue in fiscal Q2 2027.\n    Quote: The Hopper 200 products NVIDIA shipped to customers in China were less than 1% of its data center revenue in fiscal Q2 2027.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e746; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1142 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said its revenue forecast includes no data center chip revenue from China.\n    Quote: Management said its forecast includes no data center chip revenue from China.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e378; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1156 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source U.S. government (export restrictions); characterization by The Motley Fool ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] The article states that Nvidia faces significant geopolitical risk from U.S. export restrictions on AI-capable semiconductors to China, which it says remain in place and create revenue volatility and inventory risk for the company. No new regulatory step, approval, licence or date is reported.\n    Quote: Nvidia faces significant geopolitical risks, particularly from U.S. export restrictions on AI-capable semiconductors to China, which create revenue volatility and inventory risk.\n\n \nANSWER {\"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell exports and no Chinese allowance for those shipments by January 31, 2027: current-generation chips remain restricted, and Chinese authorities have restricted even U.S.-permitted H200 shipments. Reports of B300 servers in China concern deployments for which U.S. regulations require an explicit license, not evidence that both governments have authorized shipments. No supplied evidence establishes a post-September 23, 2026 formal prohibition that stands through the due date, so the evidence does not cross into rung 2.\", \"cites\": [\"e88\", \"e149\", \"e80\", \"e81\", \"e148\", \"e365\", \"e428\", \"e426\", \"e437\", \"e715\", \"e745\", \"e784\", \"e785\", \"e957\", \"e959\", \"e763\", \"e383\", \"e1156\"], \"cites_views\": [], \"pushes\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"e80\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e81\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e82\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e86\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e88\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e111\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e148\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e149\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e150\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e151\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e152\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e153\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e154\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e155\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e226\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e302\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e308\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e365\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e377\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e382\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e383\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e398\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e401\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e426\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e428\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e435\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e436\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e437\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e440\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e443\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e715\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e745\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e746\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e747\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e763\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e780\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e784\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e785\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e786\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e787\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e788\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e802\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e803\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e850\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e851\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e876\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e877\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e878\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e879\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e880\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e884\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e885\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e886\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e887\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e888\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e889\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e901\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e902\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e918\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e919\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e920\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e925\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e935\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e936\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e937\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e938\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e939\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e940\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e941\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e942\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e957\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e958\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e959\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e966\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e967\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e996\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1004\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1005\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1006\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1066\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1084\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1139\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1142\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1156\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}], \"changed\": false, \"why_changed\": \"The rung and its key supporting picture are unchanged: no evidence establishes dual official authorization, while the Blackwell deployment reports are explicitly not proof of authorized shipments and restrictions remain in force. The additional reporting and repeated accounts do not change that conclusion.\", \"theme_pushes\": [], \"reference\": \"The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance of $108 billion, excluding China Data Center compute revenue. That reference sits at rung 3, Still open; it is a near-term revenue assumption, not a formal ruling on Blackwell authorization by January 31, 2027.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"By January 31, 2027, the most likely outcome is that Blackwell exports have not received verified U.S. authorization together with Chinese permission for Nvidia shipments. Existing restrictions and Chinese limits on H200 shipments support that forecast, but the evidence does not show a qualifying post-September 23 formal prohibition, leaving the outcome at rung 3.\"}\nITEM 4 \n Thesis v11 (semiconductor-cycle, theme, focus \"The change in average DRAM/HBM contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, per TrendForce/DRAMeXchange data.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?\nLadder: rung 1 below 0 percent: below 0 percent / rung 2 from 0 percent to below 10 percent: from 0 percent to below 10 percent / rung 3 from 10 percent to below 20 percent: from 10 percent to below 20 percent (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 percent to below 30 percent: from 20 percent to below 30 percent / rung 5 30 percent or more: 30 percent or more\n\n[Evidence in view under this thesis, 61 items]\n- e881 [party_statement; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr), quoting an unnamed Nvidia spokesperson; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] An Nvidia spokesperson told Gizmodo that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, and said Nvidia has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model to focus production on the Shield TV Pro.\n    Quote: “The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry. We’ve also stopped producing the standard Shield TV model as we focus on production for Shield TV Pro.”\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e883 [fact; Gizmodo (Kyle Barr); original source Nvidia add-in-card (AIC) makers, per Gizmodo's reported market prices ; from gnews:9e2ecbe96eb662ab0c78730c 2026-10-02] Nvidia's RTX 50-series GPUs sold by all major add-in-card makers now cost hundreds or even thousands of dollars more than at launch, the article reports; a GeForce RTX 5090 that launched at $2,000 in 2025 now sells for well over $7,000, or close to $9,000 from some makers.\n    Quote: The company’s RTX 50-series GPUs across all major AIC (add-in card) makers now cost hundreds or even thousands of dollars more than they did at launch. A GeForce RTX 5090, which launched for $2,000 in 2025, now costs well over $7,000 or close to $9,000 by some makers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e353; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e238 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's gross margin is guided to settle in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, lowered because memory prices have risen more than management expected, partly helped by price increases the company has already executed.\n    Quote: Gross margin is guided lower. Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e288; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e293 [party_statement; Zacks; original source Micron Technology 2026-06-24; from gnews:90ebbd46135613dc0cb8830f 2026-09-25] Micron guided fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue to around $50 billion and a gross margin of approximately 86%, citing strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\n    Quote: Looking ahead, Micron expects revenues to rise to around $50 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026. The company also projects a healthy gross margin of approximately 86%, supported by strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\n- e355 [fact; Engadget (Husain Parvez); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d9c36b37c17bf3bafb47e561 2026-09-26] NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 Blackwell Workstation Edition desktop GPU is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched. Engadget (Husain Parvez) reports that NVIDIA has not disclosed why it raised the price, and that the increase comes amid sharply rising memory costs and broader GPU price hikes. The article frames the card as a professional/workstation product aimed at AI developers, engineers and 3D artists rather than gamers.\n    Quote: NVIDIA's current U.S. price for the RTX PRO 6000 is $16,000, up from $13,250 and nearly double the $8,565 it cost when it launched. … NVIDIA has not disclosed why it raised the price, although the increase comes amid sharply rising memory costs and broader GPU price hikes.\n- e368 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron is actively expanding its memory-chip production footprint to supply clients with more memory chips, and the article reports that this production capacity will not be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\n    Quote: While Micron could sit around and enjoy the elevated prices, it's actively expanding its production footprint to provide clients with more memory chips. That production capacity won't be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\n- e369 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron does not expect the \"tightness\" in the memory-chip market to alleviate until 2028, per The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury; memory chips are described as a component NVIDIA's and Broadcom's computing units cannot run without.\n    Quote: Micron also doesn't expect the \"tightness\" in the memory chip space to alleviate until 2028\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e987; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e533 [third_party_view; Jacqueline Thomas (IGN); original source IGN 2026-09-28; from gnews:240dc4dbbafffa84b05b213d 2026-09-28] IGN reported, in its own account accompanying a story about stolen Nvidia-branded trailers, that RAM prices have continued to climb since the start of 2026 and that Nvidia graphics cards specifically have had massive price jumps during the year. The article gives no percentage, dollar figure or source for the memory or graphics-card price levels, and frames it as part of an ongoing RAM crisis pushing hardware prices higher.\n    Quote: These thefts probably aren't helping with the cost of hardware. RAM prices have continued to climb since the start of 2026, and Nvidia graphics cards specifically have had massive price jumps this year. … It's no secret that GPUs are worth their weight in gold right now, with the ongoing RAM crisis shooting prices through the stratosphere.\n- e793 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Michael Burry ; from gnews:5f9ed396852a9b7ad63bc5f3 2026-10-01] Michael Burry noted that older Nvidia chip architectures such as the A100 and H100 have seen stabilised or rising rental rates in recent months, but attributed the trend to acute memory and power supply-chain bottlenecks rather than the silicon's intrinsic durability, writing that 'a chip earning more than it costs is a sign of scarcity due to memory and power shortages, not GPU die shortages and not durability.'\n    Quote: While older chip architectures like the A100 and H100 have seen stabilized or rising rental rates in recent months, Burry points out that this trend stems from acute supply-chain bottlenecks rather than the silicon's intrinsic durability. … \"A chip earning more than it costs is a sign of scarcity due to memory and power shortages, not GPU die shortages and not durability,\" Burry writes.\n- e841 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:0f88bce495310b4588d29700 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced that the Shield TV Pro streaming device will be priced at $299.99, a $100 increase from its original $199.99, and that it has stopped producing the standard Shield TV model, focusing production on the Pro variant instead. NVIDIA attributed the increase to substantially higher component costs, including DRAM, across the industry; pricing takes effect October 2 and the Pro's specifications are unchanged.\n    Quote: Today, NVIDIA is announcing that the Shield TV Pro will now be priced at $299.99, a $100 US price bump versus its original $199.99 price. NVIDIA says that the cost of components, including DRAM, has increased substantially across the industry, and that is why the Shield device will now feature a higher price. … Additionally, NVIDIA has also announced that it will cease the production of the standa\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e881, e882, e921, e922, e943, e946, e969, e1021, e1023, e1026, e1132, e1133; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e842 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] NVIDIA is releasing a new 64 GB unified-memory configuration of its DGX Spark AI supercomputer at a $4,999 price point, keeping the rest of the 128 GB model's specifications, and the unit will be available from 23 October 2026 through its manufacturing partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI. According to the report, the 64 GB variant replaces the 128 GB DGX Spark at the $4,999 price point, with the 128 GB model moving to even higher prices because of persistent increases in memory and other component costs.\n    Quote: NVIDIA is launching a new version of its DGX Spark AI Supercomputer which packs half the memory, but is priced the same as the 128 GB model. … Due to persistent hikes across memory and other essential components, NVIDIA is unable to sustain those prices, and while the original 128 GB model will see even higher prices, NVIDIA is releasing a new variant of the DGX Spark that replaces the 128 GB mode\n- e843 [fact; wccftech.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:8ec04393bc4495c9c8d32b37 2026-10-02] The 128 GB NVIDIA DGX Spark, which launched at $3,999 and was listed at $4,699 and then $4,999 in the weeks before the report, is now selling for more than $6,000, and wccftech reports the 128 GB model will rise further because persistent increases in memory and other essential components mean NVIDIA cannot sustain the earlier prices.\n    Quote: Although the Spark launched at $3999, it went through a range of price bumps, with the most significant being the $4699 and $4999 prices, which were listed a few weeks back. … Due to persistent hikes across memory and other essential components, NVIDIA is unable to sustain those prices, and while the original 128 GB model will see even higher prices, NVIDIA is releasing a new variant of the DGX Sp\n- e853 [party_statement; Insider Monkey (published via Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA Corporation (management) ; from gnews:a10cd465475cc83dc7c5e184 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\n    Quote: While management's gross margin outlook remains elevated at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, maintaining these historic profitability levels requires navigating escalating high-bandwidth memory costs and intensifying research and development expenditures for next-generation systems.\n- e896 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia introduced a cut-down DGX Spark with half the memory and storage of the existing model — a 64 GB GB10-based system versus the 128 GB version — to be sold exclusively through hardware partners Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI, expected to retail at around $4,999. The article presents the cheaper, lower-capacity configuration as a response to the memory shortage.\n    Quote: In an effort to provide a more affordable AI system, Nvidia is introducing a cut down version of the DGX Spark with half the memory and storage. The GB10-based systems will be offered exclusively through hardware partners including Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP, and MSI, and are expected to retail for around $4,999. … While the 64 GB version’s lower MSRP should make the systems more accessible am\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e899, e903, e985; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e897 [fact; The Register; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ef3efae5d857bd11f8870d4f 2026-10-02] Nvidia raised the price of its 128 GB DGX Spark to $6,950, an increase of nearly 75 percent from this time last year; the article attributes the increase to skyrocketing memory prices. The article adds that the new 64 GB model's $4,999 price is still 25 percent above what the 128 GB version retailed for at launch.\n    Quote: The less-powerful SKUs are necessary after Nvidia jacked the price of its 128 GB DGX Spark on Friday to $6,950 — an increase of nearly 75 percent from this time last year. As you might have already guessed, skyrocketing memory prices are to blame for the massive price adjustment. … That makes the 64 GB model considerably less than Nvidia’s top-spec DGX Spark and GB10 systems given the ongoing memo\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1025, e1189; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e843 (); both are in force: The 128 GB NVIDIA DGX Spark, which launched at $3,999 and was listed at $4,699 and then $4,999 in the weeks before the report, is now selling for more than $6,000, and wccftech reports the 128 GB model will rise further because persistent increases in memory and other essential components mean NVIDIA cannot sustain the earlier prices.\n- e900 [third_party_view; Jeffrey Kampman, Tom's Hardware; original source Tom's Hardware 2026-10-02; from gnews:d77fe74e308e447bc64c8895 2026-10-02] Tom's Hardware reported that skyrocketing RAM prices make a chip permanently paired with too much costly LPDDR5X a barrier to entry rather than an asset, that 128GB GB10 systems, when in stock, currently cost roughly $7,000 to $9,000 each — far above even Nvidia's adjusted MSRP — and that with memory and storage prices ever-shifting, the new 64GB GB10 systems' $4,999 starting price may not hold for long.\n    Quote: And skyrocketing RAM prices mean that a chip that’s permanently paired with too much costly LPDDR5X is more of a barrier to entry than an asset. … Given the ever-shifting prices of memory and storage right now, they might not stay there for long. Assuming you can find a 128GB GB10 system in stock, you can expect to pay roughly $7000 to $9000 for one right now, far above even Nvidia's adjusted MSRP\n- e903 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA announced a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system, halving the unified memory of the original 128GB system, to be sold through Acer, ASUS, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI from 23 October 2026 at prices starting at $4,999. That starting price is $1,000 (25%) above the original 128GB Founders Edition's $3,999 MSRP and $300 above NVIDIA's current $4,699 Founders Edition price for the 128GB model, though the 64GB configuration is not directly equivalent because NVIDIA says it will be sold exclusively through manufacturer partners who set their own configurations and regional pricing.\n    Quote: NVIDIA has announced a new 64GB version of DGX Spark , cutting the unified memory capacity of the original system in half. The new configuration will be available from Acer, ASUS, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI starting October 23 , with prices starting at $4,999 . … NVIDIA’s 128GB Founders Edition previously carried a $3,999 MSRP , meaning the new 64GB systems start $1,000 higher, or 25% above the or\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e896; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e904 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:abdb43bd49fec6d8f2cc7909 2026-10-02] NVIDIA raised the MSRP of its 128GB DGX Spark Founders Edition to $4,699 in February 2026, citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\n    Quote: NVIDIA raised the 128GB Founders Edition MSRP to $4,699 in February 2026 , citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\n- e917 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source SK Hynix ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] SK Hynix, a supplier of high-bandwidth memory to NVIDIA, reported revenue of $71.5 billion for the fiscal year ended December 31, 2025, year-over-year growth of 46.8%, net income of $31.6 billion and a 44.2% net margin, which the article calls a significant improvement in profitability versus prior periods.\n    Quote: In the fiscal year ended Dec. 31, 2025, revenue reached $71.5 billion, representing year-over-year growth of 46.8%. Net income for the period was $31.6 billion, yielding a net margin of 44.2%. This represents a significant improvement in profitability compared with prior periods.\n- e921 [fact; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (citing WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the remaining Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429, before tax) — a $100 increase — according to the article, which cites WCCFTech and Nvidia's announcement.\n    Quote: One of our favorite streaming gadgets, the standard Nvidia Shield TV , is no more. … According to WCCFTech , the Pro is increasing in price by 50% from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429). … The list price is now $299 (about £225 / AU$429 before tax)\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e922 [party_statement; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar (quoting Nvidia's announcement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] In its announcement discontinuing the base Shield TV and raising the Shield TV Pro price, Nvidia said the reason is that \"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\" — a company statement attributing the change to industry-wide component and memory cost inflation.\n    Quote: The reason, and the reason for discontinuing the base model, is AI: As Nvidia says in its announcement, \"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\".\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e923 [third_party_view; Carrie Marshall, TechRadar; original source TechRadar (Carrie Marshall) ; from gnews:0f0ebd8304cc41cbf8a9836c 2026-10-02] TechRadar's Carrie Marshall wrote that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\" — a journalist forecast that the component/memory cost squeeze extends beyond 2026.\n    Quote: This is likely to be the last Shield TV Pro model for some time, because all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least, and Nvidia can already reach gamers on multiple platforms with its GeForce Now streaming service, which is available on all the major desktop and mobile platforms as well as smart TVs from the likes of LG and Samsung .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e944; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e931 [fact; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron Technology reported quarterly earnings per share of $33.42, beating Wall Street's already high expectations by 5.73%, and said the quarter delivered record profits plus triple-digit revenue and earnings growth, which Zacks attributes to soaring AI demand. Zacks reported the results on the Wednesday before its 2 October 2026 article.\n    Quote: Wednesday, Micron , the AI memory leader, crushed Wall Street's earnings expectations on every metric. Once again, the quarter delivered record profits and triple-digit revenue and earnings growth. … Micron reported earnings per share of $33.42, beating Wall Street's already lofty expectations by 5.73%.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1083, e1163; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e932 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra (Micron CEO), reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron's guidance for its fiscal 2027 first quarter did not disappoint, and CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said fiscal 2027 will be even stronger than the record quarter just reported, as reported by Zacks on 2 October 2026.\n    Quote: In addition to the blockbuster quarter, Micron's Q1 2027 guidance did not disappoint, with CEO Sanjay Mehrotra stating that fiscal 2027 will be even stronger.\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n    Quote: According to management, Micron has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements, covering more than 35% of expected revenue through 2030. Customer financial commitments have soared to $32 billion from $22 billion last quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\n    Quote: Last night, Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains, saying: … \"We do not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1027; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e108 (); both are in force: The article reports that Micron Technology's memory chips are in severe shortage, with demand far outpacing supply, which has driven skyrocketing memory prices, higher margins for Micron and global supply constraints.\n- e943 [fact; tech.yahoo.com (citing WCCFTech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] Nvidia discontinued its standard Shield TV streaming device and raised the list price of the Shield TV Pro by 50%, from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429 before tax), a $100 increase. The article attributes both moves to AI-driven component costs, quoting Nvidia's announcement: \"the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry\".\n    Quote: The Shield TV Pro will stick around, but is now $100 more expensive … One of our favorite streaming gadgets, the standard Nvidia Shield TV , is no more. … According to WCCFTech , the Pro is increasing in price by 50% from $199 to $299 (about £225 / AU$429). The reason, and the reason for discontinuing the base model, is AI: As Nvidia says in its announcement, \"the cost of components, including mem\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e944 [third_party_view; tech.yahoo.com; original source tech.yahoo.com ; from gnews:2b8fd9b3d97749aae87e8886 2026-10-02] The tech.yahoo.com article's assessment is that the Shield TV Pro is likely to be Nvidia's last such model for some time, because \"all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least\"; it cites Nvidia's GeForce Now streaming service, available on major desktop, mobile and smart-TV platforms including LG and Samsung TVs, as an alternative route to gamers. The forecast carries no figure or named source.\n    Quote: This is likely to be the last Shield TV Pro model for some time, because all the indications are that the component cost crisis is going to continue through 2027 at the very least, and Nvidia can already reach gamers on multiple platforms with its GeForce Now streaming service, which is available on all the major desktop and mobile platforms as well as smart TVs from the likes of LG and Samsung .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e923; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e946 [fact; Craig Donaldson, Pocket-lint (citing Wccftech); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bdf9837d06e742f9f03a27a8 2026-10-02] Nvidia announced it is discontinuing its standard Shield TV and, effective October 2, 2026, raising the price of the Shield TV Pro by $100, from $200 to $299, attributing the increase to rising component costs amid an AI-driven global memory shortage.\n    Quote: In a recent statement, Nvidia confirmed that it's discontinuing its standard Shield TV while simultaneously raising the price of the Shield TV Pro by $100, taking the price from $200 to $300 (via Wccftech ). … Nvidia says the main reason for the price increase is the rising cost of components, driven by the ongoing global memory shortage caused by AI demand, which Nvidia has played a part in.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e968 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the price of its 2019 SHIELD TV Pro media streamer by 50% to $299.99, up from its $199.99 launch MSRP, with no hardware upgrade, and its own online store now lists the device at the new price. NVIDIA attributed the increase to rising industry-wide component costs, specifically citing memory as one of the affected components.\n    Quote: NVIDIA has bumped the price of its 2019 SHIELD TV Pro media streamer by 50%, now retailing for $299.99, up from its launch MSRP of $199. … NVIDIA's own online store now lists the device for $299.99, confirming the new price. … NVIDIA has attributed the price increase to rising industry-wide component costs, specifically citing memory as one of the affected components.\n- e970 [fact; tweaktown.com; original source Roku and Google ; from gnews:1c63df77d5d208a8682b502a 2026-10-03] Roku raised prices on several of its streaming devices and Google raised the price of the Google TV Streamer from $99.99 to $149.99, both citing higher memory and component costs, part of a broader trend of rising streaming-device prices.\n    Quote: The price increase also aligns with a broader trend of rising streaming-device costs. Roku has raised prices on several devices, citing higher memory and component costs, and Google recently raised the Google TV Streamer from $99.99 to $149.99 for the same reason.\n- e987 [party_statement; tweaktown.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:947ae81b89f5813da44a3364 2026-10-03] Memory supplier Micron said it has no line of sight to when memory supply and demand will return to balance and expects the memory crunch to persist through 2027 and 2028.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Micron has said it has no line of sight to when memory supply and demand will return to balance, and expects the crunch to persist through 2027 and 2028 .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e369; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e997 [fact; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA raised the US retail price of its Shield TV Pro streaming device from $199.99 to $299.99, effective October 2, 2026, roughly 50% above the $199 launch price of the same Tegra X1+ hardware introduced in October 2019; the company attributed the increase to rising component costs, including memory.\n    Quote: Starting October 2, SHIELD Pro will be priced at $299. The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry. … The change took effect on October 2, with the Shield TV Pro moving from $199.99 to $299.99 at retail. … The change took effect on October 2, with the Shield TV Pro moving from $199.99 to $299.99 at retail.\nThe price change also leaves the Pro as the re\n- e998 [party_statement; videocardz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:f7950cac462a047a2337915b 2026-10-03] NVIDIA stated that the cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry, giving that as its reason for raising the Shield TV Pro's price; the claim is the company's characterisation of industry-wide input-cost inflation and carries no figure.\n    Quote: NVIDIA attributed the increase to rising component costs, including memory, which the company says have increased substantially across the industry. … The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry.\n- e1003 [third_party_view; fool.com; original source fool.com ; from gnews:284a01485767b622f994fe39 2026-10-03] The article states that the memory chip space is in a serious supply crunch and that chip prices are skyrocketing as a result, which it gives as the reason Micron is currently producing stronger growth than Taiwan Semiconductor.\n    Quote: Right now, Micron is producing stronger growth than Taiwan Semiconductor. That's because the memory chip space is in a serious supply crunch, and chip prices are skyrocketing as a result.\n- e1012 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source finance.biggo.com 2026-10-03; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] The article reports that rising memory prices for AI servers were the primary driver of Amazon's upward capital-expenditure revision.\n    Quote: The primary driver of the upward revision is rising memory prices for AI servers.\n- e1019 [fact; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Nvidia and partners including Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP and MSI will, from 23 October 2026, deliver a new entry-level version of the DGX Spark mini-PC that keeps the GB10 Blackwell GPU but halves RAM and SSD: 64 GByte of LPDDR5X memory at US$5,000, versus the smallest 128-GByte version that reached the market a few weeks earlier for around US$4,000. The 128-GByte DGX Spark model now costs US$6,000, 50 percent more than a year ago. The item notes Nvidia does not this time name the tight memory market as the reason, though the author says it is still obvious, and that individual manufacturers will likely set their own prices, with US$5,000 a guideline.\n    Quote: Starting October 23, Nvidia and its partners like Acer, Asus, Dell, Gigabyte, HP, and MSI will deliver a new version of the DGX Spark series of mini-PCs. It still uses the GB10 chip with the Blackwell architecture for its GPU and is designed for running AI models locally. The catch: Compared to the model introduced about a year ago, the RAM and SSD are halved, but the price is higher. … The first \n- e1020 [third_party_view; Nico Ernst, heise.de; original source Nvidia (product and memory supply); price observation by heise.de ; from gnews:01b49f20211654668818b07e 2026-10-03] Heise reports that Nvidia's fastest RTX card, the RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell with 96 GByte, is constantly increasing in price and is currently no longer available for less than 17,000 Euros; the article notes that GDDR7 memory for RTX cards, like LPDDR5 for the DGX Spark, was apparently not covered by the long-term supplies Nvidia said at the beginning of 2026 it had secured.\n    Quote: However, LPDDR5, like GDDR7 for RTX cards, was apparently not included. Even the fastest RTX card, the RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell with 96 GByte , is constantly increasing in price and is currently no longer available for less than a staggering 17,000 Euros .\n- e1022 [party_statement; An unnamed Nvidia spokesperson, quoted by Ars Technica (reported by Yahoo Tech); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d823b7f949cb0596bcd02e4b 2026-10-03] An NVIDIA spokesperson told Ars Technica that from October 2 the SHIELD Pro would be priced at $299, and stated that the cost of components, including memory, \"has increased substantially across the industry\" — a company claim about industry-wide input cost inflation used to explain the device's price increase.\n    Quote: An Nvidia spokesperson told Ars Technica , \"Starting October 2, SHIELD Pro will be priced at $299. The cost of components, including memory, has increased substantially across the industry.\"\n- e1024 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia said it will begin shipping a derivative of its DGX Spark personal AI workstation, with unified memory halved to 64GB, from 23 October 2026 at a recommended price starting at $4,999 — $1,000 above the $3,999 launch price of the 128GB model a year earlier; the product will not be sold directly by Nvidia but distributed exclusively through six partner manufacturers (Acer, Asus, Dell Technologies, Gigabyte, HP, MSI). Halving memory cuts the largest model that can run on a single device from 200 billion to 100 billion parameters, and the item says the memory reduction is widely interpreted as a choice to lower bill-of-materials costs.\n    Quote: Nvidia will begin shipping a derivative model of its personal AI workstation, the DGX Spark, with unified memory cut in half to 64GB starting October 23. The recommended price starts at $4,999 (approximately 6.7 million won). That's a full $1,000 (approximately 1.3 million won) more than the $3,999 (approximately 5.4 million won) launch price of the 128GB model a year ago. … The new product will n\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1188; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1025 [fact; The Register (cited by finance.biggo.com); original source Nvidia 2026-10-02; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia raised the recommended price of its 128GB DGX Spark again on October 2, to $6,950 — a 74% increase from the model's launch price a year earlier — as reported by US tech outlet The Register and cited in the item.\n    Quote: According to US tech outlet The Register, Nvidia raised the recommended price of the 128GB model again on October 2, to $6,950. That's a 74% increase from the launch price a year ago, and the 64GB model's $4,999 price point is still 25% higher than the 128GB model's original launch price.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e897; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e904 (); both are in force: NVIDIA raised the MSRP of its 128GB DGX Spark Founders Edition to $4,699 in February 2026, citing worldwide memory supply constraints.\n- e1026 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced it would raise the price of its SHIELD TV Pro streaming set-top box from $199.99 to $299.99 — a $100 increase — with no hardware specification changes, attributed solely to rising component costs including DRAM; Nvidia also said it will discontinue the standard SHIELD TV and focus on the Pro model.\n    Quote: On the same day, Nvidia announced it would raise the price of its SHIELD TV Pro streaming set-top box from $199.99 to $299.99 — a $100 (approximately 130,000 won) increase. The adjustment comes with no hardware specification changes, attributed solely to rising component costs including DRAM. Nvidia also said it will discontinue the standard SHIELD TV and focus on the Pro model.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n    Quote: Major memory suppliers including Micron Technology have warned that as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained. These suppliers project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing prospects for early normalization. … Micron CEO San\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e934; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1028 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported that its data center segment gross margin reached 90% in its most recent fiscal fourth quarter, more than doubling from 41% a year earlier — a result the item ties to the memory shortage.\n    Quote: Micron's data center segment gross margin reached 90% in its most recent fiscal fourth quarter, more than doubling from 41% a year earlier.\n- e1030 [fact; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology reported fiscal Q4 results that exceeded earnings expectations, with a significant increase in data center revenues, and warned of a continuing memory chip shortage.\n    Quote: Micron Technology MU exceeded Q4 earnings expectations with a significant increase in data center revenues, while warning of a continuing memory chip shortage.\n- e1031 [party_statement; CNBC, as summarized by gurufocus.com; original source Micron Technology 2026-10-01; from gnews:ffa696bf3fdc10032b963311 2026-10-03] Micron Technology said it expects its growth to continue despite industry challenges affecting memory chip supplies, following its strong fiscal Q4 earnings.\n    Quote: Following strong earnings, Micron Technology (MU) expects growth to continue despite industry challenges affecting memory chip supplies.\n- e1074 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia (CFO) ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia's CFO flagged 'extreme pricing conditions in memory' as a margin headwind.\n    Quote: Nvidia also disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement, while its CFO flagged \"extreme pricing conditions in memory\" as a margin headwind.\n- e1083 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Micron Technology 2026-09-30; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Micron Technology reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 results after the Wednesday closing bell, posting revenue of $54.23 billion, above Benzinga Pro's analyst estimate of $50.75 billion, and adjusted earnings of $33.42 per share, above the $31.45-per-share estimate.\n    Quote: Micron Technology reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 results after Wednesday’s closing bell, posting revenue of $54.23 billion. Revenue surpassed Benzinga Pro’s analyst estimate of $50.75 billion. The semiconductor company also reported adjusted earnings of $33.42 per share, exceeding the $31.45-per-share estimate.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e931; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e293 (2026-06-24); both are in force: Micron guided fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue to around $50 billion and a gross margin of approximately 86%, citing strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\n- e1107 [third_party_view; Benzinga; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley said that delayed data-center deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley said delayed deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\n- e1108 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed.\n    Quote: Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed\n- e1123 [party_statement; NVIDIA management (via finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] NVIDIA management said it expects gross margin to bottom at 71% to 72% in the fourth quarter of fiscal 2027 because memory costs are rising.\n    Quote: Margins are expected to bottom at 71% to 72% in the fourth quarter because memory costs are rising.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e853 (); both are in force: NVIDIA management's gross margin outlook remains at 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points, according to Insider Monkey; the item gives no period or statement date for the outlook, and the author adds that holding that margin requires absorbing rising high-bandwidth memory costs and higher R&D spending.\n- e1132 [fact; The Verge (citing Ars Technica); original source NVIDIA 2026-10-02; from gnews:f674aa00a3b8657dbc4bb737 2026-10-05] NVIDIA raised the price of its Shield TV Pro streaming box by $100, from $199.99 to $299.99, starting on October 2nd, attributing the increase to higher component costs amid the industry-wide memory shortage, according to Ars Technica. The article frames the move as the memory shortage passing through into NVIDIA's own consumer hardware pricing.\n    Quote: Nvidia’s Shield TV streaming boxes are the latest victims of the memory shortage that has seen prices increase dramatically for everything from digital cameras to game consoles . When the Shield TV Pro launched nearly seven years ago with an upgraded Tegra X1+ processor, 3GB of RAM, and 16GB of storage, it sold for $199.99. But starting on October 2nd Nvidia increased its price by $100 to $299.99 \n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e841; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1150 [party_statement; MarketBeat; original source Micron Technology (with unnamed forecasters) ; from gnews:db898bbdf8476a62d9167361 2026-10-05] MarketBeat reported that current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron Technology indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\n    Quote: Current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1190; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1151 [party_statement; finance.yahoo.com (article); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:4cf776a64aef8ef7dde73e49 2026-10-05] Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in June 2026 that DRAM and NAND industry demand continues to significantly exceed industry supply, and that he expects tight memory-market conditions to persist beyond calendar 2027.\n    Quote: Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said in June that \"DRAM and NAND industry demand continues to significantly exceed industry supply\" and that he expects \"tight conditions to persist beyond calendar 2027.\"\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e1150 (); both are in force: MarketBeat reported that current forecasts suggest memory supply will remain tight through at least 2027, with Micron Technology indicating that shortages could take considerable time to ease.\n- e1163 [fact; Zacks Equity Research (Zacks Analyst Blog); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:4813fe2f3bcaef0438d6271c 2026-10-05] Micron Technology reported (in a release the item refers to only as dated 'Yesterday', in an article published 5 October 2026) fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue that surged 379.3% year over year, beating the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 6.33%, with EPS of $33.42 topping the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 5.73%; Zacks attributed the results to strong demand for memory products tied to AI infrastructure.\n    Quote: Yesterday, the company reported fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue surge of 379.3% year over year, beating the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 6.33%, while EPS of $33.42 topped the Zacks Consensus Estimate by 5.73%. The results reflect strong demand for memory products tied to AI infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e931; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1169 [fact; fool.com (Bram Berkowitz); original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:af32e2b77f410d23029454d7 2026-10-05] Memory maker Micron Technology, a supplier of DRAM and NAND used with GPUs, recently reported quarterly earnings that again beat analyst estimates and guided its current quarter above expectations, according to fool.com, in a year the article describes as supply-constrained for memory.\n    Quote: Micron recently reported earnings that once again blew past analyst estimates, and it also provided guidance for the current quarter, which came in higher than expected.\n- e1173 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA guided gross margin down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027 to 74% expected in fiscal Q3 2027, and said it expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027, blaming memory prices that rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\n    Quote: NVIDIA does not expect its gross margin to hold. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and management expects 74% for Q3. Management then expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in Q4, and it blamed the price of memory, which rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e272; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1188 [fact; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia is launching a 64GB version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system at a starting price of $4,999 when it goes on sale on 23 October 2026 — $1,000 more than the original 128GB model's $3,999 launch price, even though it offers half as much memory. The lower-memory unit keeps the same GB10 Grace Blackwell Superchip and AI software stack and supports models of up to 100 billion parameters, versus up to 200 billion for the 128GB system.\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA ) is launching a lower-memory version of its DGX Spark desktop AI system at a higher price than the original model cost at launch, underscoring how persistent memory shortages are pushing up the cost of AI hardware. … The new 64GB DGX Spark will start at $4,999 when it goes on sale October 23. … That is $1,000 more than the original 128GB models $3,999 launch price , despite offering\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1024; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1189 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source  ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] The original 128GB DGX Spark now costs about $6,950, roughly 75% above its debut price, and partner versions have reportedly been selling for $5,999 to $9,000 depending on storage and configuration, according to the report cited by the article (which uses 'reportedly').\n    Quote: The pricing contrast becomes even more striking when looking at the original model today. … According to the report, the 128GB DGX Spark now costs about $6,950 , roughly 75% above its debut price . Partner versions have reportedly been selling anywhere from $5,999 to $9,000 , depending on storage and configuration.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e897; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1190 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Micron ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Memory supplier Micron has warned that memory shortages could worsen through 2027 and 2028, with no clear timeline for supply and demand to return to balance, as cited by the article.\n    Quote: Micron has warned that memory shortages could worsen through 2027 and 2028 , with no clear timeline for supply and demand to return to balance.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1150; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e1191 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:42886f99cc0b6ba45ce6ae45 2026-10-05] Nvidia has attributed the rising prices of its systems to industry-wide memory supply constraints, which the article says may not ease soon.\n    Quote: Nvidia has already blamed rising prices on industry-wide memory supply constraints, and those pressures may not ease soon.\n\n \nANSWER {\"rung\": 5, \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028, while more than 75% of its 2027 output is committed. Micron's strong results and explicit reports of extreme memory pricing reinforce the direction. Together these make a 30%+ annual average contract-price increase the most likely rung, though the evidence does not provide a direct contract-price series or a precise percentage. Consumer-product price rises and memory-cost comments are supporting context, not measurements of DRAM/HBM contract prices; the deployment-delay risk is a counterweight.\", \"cites\": [\"e934\", \"e1027\", \"e1108\", \"e933\", \"e368\", \"e369\", \"e1074\", \"e1173\", \"e293\", \"e1107\"], \"cites_views\": [], \"pushes\": [{\"evidence_id\": \"e881\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e883\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e238\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e293\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e355\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e368\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e369\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e533\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e793\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e841\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e842\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e843\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e853\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e896\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e897\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e900\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e903\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e904\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e917\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e921\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e922\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e923\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e931\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e932\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e933\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e934\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e943\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e944\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e946\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e968\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e970\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e987\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e997\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e998\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1003\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1012\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1019\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1020\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1022\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1024\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1025\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1026\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1027\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1028\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1030\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1031\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1074\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1083\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1107\", \"push\": \"toward_first\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1108\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1123\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1132\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1150\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1151\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1163\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1169\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1173\", \"push\": \"toward_last\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1188\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1189\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1190\", \"push\": \"none\"}, {\"evidence_id\": \"e1191\", \"push\": \"none\"}], \"changed\": true, \"why_changed\": \"The rung remains 5, but the supporting facts are now more specific: the evidence adds Micron's stated 2027–2028 demand-supply outlook, its quantified customer commitments and committed 2027 output, and further reported pricing pressure. These update the supporting facts and figures without supplying a direct full-year contract-price figure.\", \"theme_pushes\": [], \"reference\": \"No market consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price figure is supplied. The opening no-reference position is rung 3: 10% to below 20%.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"At least 30% (rung 5); the evidence supports a 30%+ forecast through persistent, multi-year DRAM supply tightness and reported extreme memory pricing, but does not permit a reliable point estimate for the calendar-year average contract-price change.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"I’ve checked the judging rules. I’m comparing the selected outcomes with the stated estimates and checking the cited passages, including the separate dates for repeated questions."}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nITEM\\\",i[\\\"item\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",i[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"[Evidence in view\\\")[0],\\\"\\\\n\\\",json.dumps({k:i[\\\"answer\\\"].get(k) for k in [\\\"rung\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"cites\\\",\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\"]},ensure_ascii=False))) for i in x]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\")); [(print(\\\"\\\\nITEM\\\",i[\\\"item\\\"],\\\"\\\\n\\\",i[\\\"request\\\"].split(\\\"[Evidence in view\\\")[0],\\\"\\\\n\\\",json.dumps({k:i[\\\"answer\\\"].get(k) for k in [\\\"rung\\\",\\\"reasons\\\",\\\"cites\\\",\\\"reference\\\",\\\"implied_outcome\\\"]},ensure_ascii=False))) for i in x]'\"","aggregated_output":"\nITEM 1 \n Thesis v13 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Nvidia's CPO switch shipment volume from Q4 2026 through calendar 2027 (GF Securities: 15,000 units in Q4 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, 30,000 by mid-2027, 100,000 for all of 2027), as reflected in Nvidia's networking revenue disclosures and suppliers' CPO/NPO laser shipments.\", 2027, due 2027-12-31): Will Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: Nvidia’s CPO switch shipments reach at least 100,000 units during calendar 2027, as shown by Nvidia’s shipment disclosures. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-04 and before reaching 100,000 units, Nvidia formally abandons CPO switch shipments for calendar 2027. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-04 and stands on 2027-12-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-12-31.\n\n \n {\"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"The reference is GF Securities' estimate of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027, which sits at rung 1. The evidence supports an optical-demand ramp, but the 100,000 figure remains an analyst estimate rather than Nvidia shipment volume: Lumentum's revenue and guidance cover broader business, while its projected CPO and NPO laser revenues are not converted into Nvidia switch units. Discounting the unshipped product target, the most likely outcome remains below 100,000, but there is no evidence of formal abandonment or another decision against the event as a whole; therefore rung 3 is the forecast.\", \"cites\": [\"e1068\", \"e1069\", \"e1070\", \"e1071\"], \"reference\": \"GF Securities' forecast of potentially 100,000 Nvidia CPO switch shipments in calendar 2027 is the market-facing reference; 100,000 is the rung 1 boundary.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies calendar-2027 shipments below 100,000, but does not support a defensible more precise unit estimate: Lumentum's projected CPO/NPO laser revenues span products and are not attributable or convertible to Nvidia switch units. No evidence establishes formal abandonment, so the outcome remains open.\"}\n\nITEM 2 \n Thesis v10 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"US data-center capacity additions in gigawatts for calendar 2026, versus the pace of 'somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year' that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang described, and against how far labour availability constrains that pace.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?\nLadder: rung 1 below 10 GW: below 10 GW / rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW: from 10 GW to below 15 GW / rung 3 from 15 GW to below 20 GW: from 15 GW to below 20 GW (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 GW to below 25 GW: from 20 GW to below 25 GW / rung 5 25 GW or more: 25 GW or more\n\n \n {\"rung\": 2, \"reasons\": \"The reference is Huang’s rough 10–20 GW annual estimate: its 10–<15 portion is rung 2, its 15–<20 portion is rung 3, and 20 GW reaches rung 4; the midpoint, 15 GW, is rung 3. I forecast below that midpoint because the quantified trades shortfall, the stated net power shortfall, and the two-to-three-year lead time for securing land, power, and a shell weigh on 2026 delivery. Signed or planned capacity and xAI’s announced expansion support additions, but do not establish that those amounts will be new U.S. capacity completed in calendar 2026; several are unscheduled, span other periods or suppliers, or lack a stated U.S. location. The evidence supports roughly 12–14 GW, not enough to cross the 15 GW boundary.\", \"cites\": [\"e675\", \"e679\", \"e40\", \"e263\", \"e330\", \"e331\", \"e727\", \"e732\", \"e1085\", \"e1104\", \"e1105\"], \"reference\": \"Huang’s rough estimate of 10–20 GW per year is the stated reference. It spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW) and rung 3 (15–<20 GW), with the 20 GW endpoint at rung 4; its 15 GW midpoint is rung 3.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About 12–14 GW of U.S. additions in calendar 2026, with a central estimate near 13 GW. This is a qualitative forecast from Huang’s annual pace, tempered by the quantified labour and power constraints and partly offset by announced projects; the project figures are discounted where timing, U.S. location, or delivery in 2026 is not established.\"}\n\nITEM 3 \n Thesis v3 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \"Whether U.S. authorities extend export approval beyond the older H200 to Nvidia's current Blackwell-generation chips for approved Chinese customers, and whether Chinese authorities stop restricting Nvidia chip shipments, so that China data-center revenue can resume; the item reports H200 shipments are already U.S.-permitted while Chinese authorities still restrict them.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will U.S. authorities authorize exports of Nvidia’s current Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow those shipments by January 31, 2027, as verified by official export-control or licensing decisions, Chinese regulatory or customs actions, or Nvidia disclosures?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: By January 31, 2027, U.S. authorities authorize exports of Blackwell-generation AI chips to China and Chinese authorities allow Nvidia’s shipments into China. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 23, 2026, the U.S. Commerce Department/Bureau of Industry and Security formally prohibits Blackwell exports to China through January 31, 2027, or Chinese authorities formally prohibit Nvidia’s Blackwell shipments into China through that date. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n\n \n {\"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance, which assumes no China Data Center compute revenue; that sits at rung 3, Still open, rather than establishing that approval is formally ruled out by the due date. The evidence implies no verified U.S. authorization for Blackwell exports and no Chinese allowance for those shipments by January 31, 2027: current-generation chips remain restricted, and Chinese authorities have restricted even U.S.-permitted H200 shipments. Reports of B300 servers in China concern deployments for which U.S. regulations require an explicit license, not evidence that both governments have authorized shipments. No supplied evidence establishes a post-September 23, 2026 formal prohibition that stands through the due date, so the evidence does not cross into rung 2.\", \"cites\": [\"e88\", \"e149\", \"e80\", \"e81\", \"e148\", \"e365\", \"e428\", \"e426\", \"e437\", \"e715\", \"e745\", \"e784\", \"e785\", \"e957\", \"e959\", \"e763\", \"e383\", \"e1156\"], \"reference\": \"The nearest observable market reference is NVIDIA’s Q3 FY2027 guidance of $108 billion, excluding China Data Center compute revenue. That reference sits at rung 3, Still open; it is a near-term revenue assumption, not a formal ruling on Blackwell authorization by January 31, 2027.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"By January 31, 2027, the most likely outcome is that Blackwell exports have not received verified U.S. authorization together with Chinese permission for Nvidia shipments. Existing restrictions and Chinese limits on H200 shipments support that forecast, but the evidence does not show a qualifying post-September 23 formal prohibition, leaving the outcome at rung 3.\"}\n\nITEM 4 \n Thesis v11 (semiconductor-cycle, theme, focus \"The change in average DRAM/HBM contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, per TrendForce/DRAMeXchange data.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What is the percentage change in average DRAM (including HBM) contract prices in calendar 2026 versus calendar 2025, according to TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price data?\nLadder: rung 1 below 0 percent: below 0 percent / rung 2 from 0 percent to below 10 percent: from 0 percent to below 10 percent / rung 3 from 10 percent to below 20 percent: from 10 percent to below 20 percent (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 percent to below 30 percent: from 20 percent to below 30 percent / rung 5 30 percent or more: 30 percent or more\n\n \n {\"rung\": 5, \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (10% to below 20%); no consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year figure for this measure is supplied. The forecast is above that reference: Micron says the DRAM imbalance has no visible return-to-balance date and that demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028, while more than 75% of its 2027 output is committed. Micron's strong results and explicit reports of extreme memory pricing reinforce the direction. Together these make a 30%+ annual average contract-price increase the most likely rung, though the evidence does not provide a direct contract-price series or a precise percentage. Consumer-product price rises and memory-cost comments are supporting context, not measurements of DRAM/HBM contract prices; the deployment-delay risk is a counterweight.\", \"cites\": [\"e934\", \"e1027\", \"e1108\", \"e933\", \"e368\", \"e369\", \"e1074\", \"e1173\", \"e293\", \"e1107\"], \"reference\": \"No market consensus, company guidance, or TrendForce/DRAMeXchange full-year 2026 contract-price figure is supplied. The opening no-reference position is rung 3: 10% to below 20%.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"At least 30% (rung 5); the evidence supports a 30%+ forecast through persistent, multi-year DRAM supply tightness and reported extreme memory pricing, but does not permit a reliable point estimate for the calendar-year average contract-price change.\"}\n\nITEM 5 \n Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 5,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 15,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 15,000,000,000 USD to below 30,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 30,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 50,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n \n {\"rung\": 1, \"reasons\": \"The reference is no stated market or company figure, which sits at rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn). The strongest quantified evidence remains guarantee obligations capped at $108.5bn, including the up-to-$105bn OpenAI lease guarantee; these are overlapping descriptions of the same large exposure, not amounts to add together. The new support for additional capacity and minimum-revenue guarantees is unsized, so it does not justify adding a further amount to that anchor. The reported $175bn of potential balance-sheet funding and equity investment figures are not themselves credit-support commitments or revenue guarantees. The implied amount therefore remains above the $50bn rung-5 boundary. A further filing reported the opposite of what the cited items say, which moves the answer to the other end of the ladder.\", \"cites\": [\"e18\", \"e22\", \"e23\", \"e41\", \"e42\", \"e44\", \"e114\", \"e136\", \"e143\", \"e196\", \"e220\", \"e500\", \"e582\", \"e60\", \"e61\", \"e135\", \"e335\"], \"reference\": \"No reference figure was supplied when the question opened; the reference is rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn).\", \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely disclosed total remains around the existing $108.5bn maximum-guarantee-exposure anchor, or otherwise above $50bn; the up-to-$105bn OpenAI guarantee is part of that exposure, not an additional amount. The added capacity support and revenue backstops are not sized, so they cannot be reliably added to the estimate.\"}\n\nITEM 6 \n Thesis v7 (chip-export-controls, theme, focus \"Whether MIIT moves from signaling intent and collecting purchase plans to actually approving Alibaba's, ByteDance's and other Chinese firms' purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 (versus blocking, delaying or capping quantities), so that Nvidia can ship the product into China and book related revenue.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (MIIT) formally approve purchases of Nvidia's RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, and other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: MIIT formally approves purchases of the RTX Pro 5500 by Alibaba, ByteDance, or other Chinese firms by January 31, 2027. / rung 2 Ruled out: MIIT formally rejects the Chinese firms' RTX Pro 5500 purchase plans or formally abandons consideration of them without approving the purchases. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-27 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n\n \n {\"rung\": 1, \"reasons\": \"The reference is rung 3: no market consensus or formal decision is supplied, and the reported status is still deliberation rather than approval or rejection. The forecast nevertheless favors rung 1: the specific report says officials indicated purchases were likely to be approved and MIIT intended to approve them, while the reported purchase-plan requests and ByteDance demand give a concrete context for action before the deadline. The reported 1 million-unit plan and 500,000-units-per-quarter supply target are not confirmed orders or approvals, and repeated coverage is one underlying claim, not independent confirmation. The history of Chinese import restrictions and broad constraints on Nvidia's China business temper the forecast, but do not establish rejection or outweigh the product-specific approval signals.\", \"cites\": [\"e419\", \"e400\", \"e418\", \"e431\", \"e432\", \"e566\", \"e715\", \"e434\", \"e439\", \"e441\"], \"reference\": \"No explicit market consensus or company guidance is supplied. The observed reference is the reported state of consideration with no formal decision, which corresponds to rung 3 (Still open).\", \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely outcome remains at least one formal MIIT approval by January 31, 2027. The evidence gives no approval date or confirmed purchase volume; this is a forecast from the specific reported intent and likelihood of approval, not a claim that approval has already occurred.\"}\n\nITEM 7 \n Thesis v6 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\", 2028, due 2028-03-24): Will NVIDIA have completed its acquisition of Hugging Face by March 24, 2028, as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's filings disclose that it completed the Hugging Face acquisition by March 24, 2028. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 25, 2026, NVIDIA or Hugging Face formally terminates or abandons the acquisition agreement, or a regulator with authority to block it prohibits the acquisition. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-25 and stands on 2028-03-24, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-03-24.\n\n \n {\"rung\": 2, \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA’s announced $12.93 billion definitive agreement, reported as expected to close in the first half of 2027; against a March 24, 2028 check date, that reference sits at rung 1. The forecast also favors rung 1: Hugging Face’s CEO was reported as saying he had sold the company to NVIDIA in September 2026, and other coverage describes it as acquired. Those reports are not NVIDIA filings, so they do not establish the required disclosure, but they make completion and a filing disclosing it by the deadline the most likely outcome. The reported H1 2027 closing expectation leaves substantial time before the deadline; no evidence indicates a termination, abandonment, or blocking prohibition that would put rung 2 ahead.\", \"cites\": [\"e590\", \"e483\", \"e497\", \"e510\", \"e608\", \"e695\", \"e277\"], \"reference\": \"The announced $12.93 billion agreement and reported expectation of closing in the first half of 2027 are the market-facing reference; that expected completion date falls before the March 24, 2028 deadline and therefore sits at rung 1.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"The strongest reported completion account places the sale in September 2026 (e590), while NVIDIA’s reported expected closing window was the first half of 2027 (e608, e695). I therefore forecast that NVIDIA’s filings will disclose completion by March 24, 2028, while recognizing that the supplied evidence does not itself include those filings.\"}\n\nITEM 8 \n Thesis v6 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether the agreed ~$12.9 billion Hugging Face acquisition is signed and closed (completed) as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings, versus being repriced, delayed past the check date or terminated.\", 2028, due 2028-03-24): Will NVIDIA have completed its acquisition of Hugging Face by March 24, 2028, as disclosed in NVIDIA's filings?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's filings disclose that it completed the Hugging Face acquisition by March 24, 2028. / rung 2 Ruled out: After September 25, 2026, NVIDIA or Hugging Face formally terminates or abandons the acquisition agreement, or a regulator with authority to block it prohibits the acquisition. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-25 and stands on 2028-03-24, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-03-24.\n\n \n {\"rung\": 1, \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA's reported expectation that the deal would close in the first half of 2027, which is before the March 24, 2028 check date and therefore sits at rung 1. Reports that the acquisition was already completed—including Hugging Face CEO Clem Delangue's reported statement that he had sold the company—further favor completion by the deadline. The newer description of the deal as currently being acquired means completion is not consistently established in the supplied reporting, but it leaves substantial time for the expected closing and does not indicate termination, abandonment, or a blocking prohibition. The evidence therefore favors rung 1 as a forecast, though it does not establish the required NVIDIA filing disclosure.\", \"cites\": [\"e608\", \"e695\", \"e590\", \"e483\", \"e510\", \"e916\", \"e1155\", \"e1088\"], \"reference\": \"NVIDIA's reported expectation that the acquisition would close during the first half of 2027; that timing is before the check date and sits at rung 1.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies completion by the March 24, 2028 deadline, with the stated closing expectation placing it in the first half of 2027; several reports also describe it as already completed in September 2026. Those reports do not establish the required NVIDIA filing disclosure, so rung 1 is the forecast rather than a proven outcome.\"}\n\nITEM 9 \n Thesis v4 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether Vera Rubin moves from full production to volume customer shipments and first revenue recognition within NVIDIA's fiscal 2027, as shown in the company's quarterly results and Form 10-K.\", 2027, due 2027-01-31): Will NVIDIA recognize revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by the end of FY2027, as disclosed in its quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: NVIDIA's quarterly results or FY2027 Form 10-K disclose that it has begun recognizing revenue from Vera Rubin volume customer shipments by 2027-01-31. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-09-23 and before 2027-01-31, NVIDIA formally abandons or cancels the Vera Rubin platform. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-09-23 and stands on 2027-01-31, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2027-01-31.\n\n \n {\"rung\": 1, \"reasons\": \"The reference is NVIDIA management's expectation that Vera Rubin will account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; because Q3 results are due before 2027-01-31, that reference sits at rung 1. The forecast remains rung 1: the company says production shipments began in August, its guide gives a substantial Q3 revenue contribution, and customers and suppliers report Vera Rubin systems in production or shipping. These facts make recognition in Q3 and disclosure by the due date more likely than not, although the evidence shown does not itself establish that revenue has already been recognized. Supply constraints and the AMI orders' later delivery schedule temper the forecast but do not overturn it; there is no evidence of abandonment to support rung 2.\", \"cites\": [\"e410\", \"e555\", \"e764\", \"e858\", \"e857\", \"e181\", \"e659\", \"e660\", \"e895\", \"e416\", \"e1177\", \"e236\", \"e279\", \"e864\"], \"reference\": \"NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. This is the rung 1 reference: Q3 results are expected before the 2027-01-31 due date.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"The most likely outcome is that NVIDIA recognizes Vera Rubin revenue in Q3 FY2027 and discloses it in its quarterly results, with the guided contribution of about 20% of Data Center revenue. This is inferred from the guide together with production shipments beginning in August and customer production deployments; it is not yet established by a reported result in the evidence shown.\"}\n\nITEM 10 \n Thesis v12 (NVDA, asset, focus \"Whether the proposed up-to-$125 billion hardware residual-value guarantee is signed and disclosed, and at what amount, versus remaining a proposal or being reduced or withdrawn.\", 2028, due 2028-04-03): Will Nvidia sign and disclose a finalized hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform by 2028-04-03?\nLadder: rung 1 Yes: By 2028-04-03, Nvidia has entered into a definitive hardware residual-value guarantee for its AI infrastructure financing platform and disclosed its finalized terms in a definitive agreement or SEC filing. / rung 2 Ruled out: After 2026-10-03 and before 2028-04-03, Nvidia formally abandons or withdraws the proposed hardware residual-value guarantee without finalizing it. This rung holds when that, or any other decision against the event of rung 1 as a whole, or a formal abandonment or withdrawal of it as a whole, was made after 2026-10-03 and stands on 2028-04-03, and only when rung 1 does not. / rung 3 Still open: Neither rung 1 nor rung 2 holds on 2028-04-03.\n\n \n {\"rung\": 1, \"reasons\": \"The reference is Nvidia’s announced proposal to share up to $125 billion of hardware residual-value risk; a proposal without finalized terms sits at rung 3, Still open. I forecast rung 1 because the explicit proposal, six-partner financing platform, and reported lender demand for guarantees make eventual execution more likely than either formal withdrawal or remaining unresolved through April 3, 2028. Morningstar’s estimate implies a conditional maximum of $125 billion (25% of the full $500 billion target), not a committed or finalized amount. Amazon’s reported $8 billion sale-leaseback is evidence that a comparable financing structure is being explored, not evidence that Nvidia has guaranteed it. Hardware obsolescence, lender caution, and the preliminary status of the agreements temper the forecast. No evidence shows a formal abandonment after October 3, 2026, as rung 2 requires.\", \"cites\": [\"e1011\", \"e1017\", \"e1013\", \"e1042\", \"e1046\", \"e1047\", \"e1049\", \"e1129\", \"e1130\", \"e1145\", \"e1154\"], \"reference\": \"Nvidia’s stated proposal is to share up to $125 billion in hardware residual-value risk; because it is not a definitive agreement with finalized disclosed terms, that reference sits at rung 3, Still open. No market consensus figure is supplied.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"I forecast a definitive, disclosed, non-zero guarantee by 2028-04-03, likely no greater than the proposed $125 billion ceiling. Morningstar’s 25% estimate applied to the full $500 billion target yields that conditional maximum; the evidence does not support a tighter estimate of the finalized amount.\"}\n\nITEM 11 \n Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 5,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 15,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 15,000,000,000 USD to below 30,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 30,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 50,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n \n {\"rung\": 1, \"reasons\": \"The reference is the no-figure baseline, rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn). The $108.5bn guarantee-obligation cap remains the strongest quantified anchor and is above rung 5's $50bn threshold; the $105bn OpenAI guarantee and the $860.3m Nscale guarantee are not added on top of that aggregate, while the newly described capacity and revenue-support arrangements have no disclosed dollar amounts.\", \"cites\": [\"e22\", \"e23\", \"e41\", \"e42\", \"e44\", \"e60\", \"e61\", \"e93\", \"e114\", \"e136\", \"e143\", \"e196\", \"e220\", \"e328\", \"e335\"], \"reference\": \"No quantified market reference was supplied when the question opened; the reference is therefore the no-reference baseline, rung 3 ($15bn to below $30bn).\", \"implied_outcome\": \"The evidence implies disclosed guarantee exposure remains above $50bn, anchored by NVIDIA's stated maximum guarantee-obligation cap of $108.5bn. Treat the $105bn OpenAI guarantee as part of that aggregate rather than additive; the other newly described support arrangements lack dollar amounts from which to derive an increment.\"}\n\nITEM 12 \n Thesis v2 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The amount of purchase obligations/supply commitments NVIDIA reports in its contractual obligations and purchase commitment disclosures in the Form 10-K, versus the prior year, given the analyst report that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): How much will NVIDIA report in purchase obligations and supply commitments in its FY2027 Form 10-K disclosure?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 50,000,000,000 USD to below 75,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 75,000,000,000 USD to below 100,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 100,000,000,000 USD to below 125,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 125,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n \n {\"rung\": 5, \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position, rung 3 (neutral); no consensus or company forecast for the FY2027 Form 10-K commitment balance is supplied. The evidence instead points well above rung 5's $125bn floor: NVIDIA reported $279bn of supply and capacity commitments at FY2027 Q2, including $92bn scheduled for the remainder of FY2027. A simple run-off benchmark is therefore about $187bn ($279bn less $92bn), before any new commitments or cancellations. Ongoing supply constraints and strong guided growth make replenishment plausible, but do not quantify it; the separate $500bn multi-year memory estimate is not added in full because its timing, terms, and overlap with reported commitments are unspecified. Even the disclosed run-off benchmark remains in rung 5.\", \"cites\": [\"e202\", \"e1062\", \"e953\", \"e1040\", \"e106\", \"e236\", \"e1027\", \"e730\", \"e732\", \"e44\"], \"reference\": \"No market consensus or company guidance figure for the FY2027 Form 10-K commitment balance is supplied. The reference is therefore the opening no-reference rung, rung 3 (neutral: $75bn to below $100bn).\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $187bn as a disclosed-schedule run-off benchmark: $279bn of Q2 commitments less the $92bn scheduled for the remainder of FY2027. New commitments and cancellations could move the year-end balance, but the evidence does not quantify those changes; the likely outcome remains above $125bn.\"}\n\nITEM 13 \n Thesis v1 (NVDA, asset, focus \"The disclosed amount of guarantees, credit-support commitments and revenue backstops NVIDIA has extended to AI labs and cloud partners (including the credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab), versus the equity investments it has made, at NVIDIA's next annual report.\", FY2027, due 2027-04-01): What total amount of credit-support commitments and minimum-revenue guarantees to AI labs and cloud partners will NVIDIA disclose in its FY2027 annual report, as determined from the Form 10-K and any 8-K or earnings disclosures quantifying these commitments?\nLadder: rung 1 Strongly bullish: below 5,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 Bullish: from 5,000,000,000 USD to below 15,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 Neutral: from 15,000,000,000 USD to below 30,000,000,000 USD (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 Bearish: from 30,000,000,000 USD to below 50,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 Strongly bearish: 50,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n \n {\"rung\": 5, \"reasons\": \"The reference is the opening no-reference position: rung 3, Neutral ($15bn to below $30bn). The strongest quantified exposure remains NVIDIA's reported $108.5bn cap on guarantee obligations, already above rung 5's $50bn boundary. Morningstar's $125bn figure is a maximum scenario for the separate $500bn financing target if the full target received 25% support; the evidence says support applies only to selected opportunities and the target is over time, so that maximum is not a forecast to add in full to the $108.5bn. The disclosed 2 GW and 4.25 GW support, minimum-revenue guarantees, and financing backstops may add exposure, but are not sized sufficiently to change the rung.\", \"cites\": [\"e18\", \"e22\", \"e23\", \"e41\", \"e42\", \"e44\", \"e61\", \"e114\", \"e136\", \"e143\", \"e196\", \"e220\", \"e500\", \"e582\", \"e778\", \"e796\", \"e828\", \"e830\", \"e989\", \"e990\", \"e991\", \"e992\", \"e995\", \"e1048\", \"e1063\", \"e1130\", \"e1138\", \"e1154\"], \"reference\": \"No market reference figure was supplied when the question opened, so the reference is rung 3, Neutral ($15bn to below $30bn).\", \"implied_outcome\": \"Forecast disclosed exposure remains at least about $108.5bn, the reported cap on guarantee obligations. A separate financing-program scenario could add as much as $125bn if 25% support applied across the full $500bn target, but the target is over time and support is limited to selected opportunities, so the evidence does not justify assuming that full amount by the FY2027 report.\"}\n\nITEM 14 \n Thesis v5 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2026 data center capital expenditure, compared with the nearly $800 billion the article says they will spend this year.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): What will the five largest AI hyperscalers' combined data-center capital expenditure be in calendar 2026, according to their full-year earnings and capital-expenditure disclosures?\nLadder: rung 1 below 600,000,000,000 USD: below 600,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 750,000,000,000 USD: from 600,000,000,000 USD to below 750,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD: from 750,000,000,000 USD to below 850,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \"the big five AI hyperscalers will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers this year\") / rung 4 from 850,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD: from 850,000,000,000 USD to below 1,000,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 1,000,000,000,000 USD or more: 1,000,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n \n {\"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"The reference is Nvidia's estimate of nearly $800 billion for the big five in calendar 2026, which sits in rung 3. Amazon's approximately $220 billion 2026 capex projection, versus its previous $200 billion level, supports about a $20 billion increase to that reference: roughly $820 billion, still within rung 3. Meta's 6-GW partnership has a large theoretical full-scale cost, but only its first wave is due in H2 2026; it is not evidence that the full volume is in hand. Meta's reported AI investment total does not isolate data-center capex. Amazon's $8 billion SPV could affect how some investment is financed or reported, but the evidence does not establish a 2026 capex reduction; even an $8 billion adjustment would leave the estimate in rung 3. Supplier growth is supportive of demand but does not quantify a change to hyperscaler capex.\", \"cites\": [\"e162\", \"e674\", \"e1008\", \"e264\", \"e733\", \"e971\", \"e1158\", \"e1180\", \"e1181\"], \"reference\": \"The market/reference is Nvidia's estimate that the big five will spend nearly $800 billion on data centers in calendar 2026; that is rung 3.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"Roughly $820 billion: start from the nearly $800 billion reference and add about $20 billion for Amazon's projection rising from $200 billion to approximately $220 billion. The other cited amounts do not establish an additional 2026 data-center-capex amount in hand.\"}\n\nITEM 15 \n Thesis v10 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"US data-center capacity additions in gigawatts for calendar 2026, versus the pace of 'somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year' that Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang described, and against how far labour availability constrains that pace.\", 2026, due 2027-03-31): How many gigawatts of new data-center capacity will be added in the United States during calendar 2026, as determined by full-year 2026 grid-operator and utility interconnection/capacity data and industry data-center capacity reports?\nLadder: rung 1 below 10 GW: below 10 GW / rung 2 from 10 GW to below 15 GW: from 10 GW to below 15 GW / rung 3 from 15 GW to below 20 GW: from 15 GW to below 20 GW (no reference figure when the question opened) / rung 4 from 20 GW to below 25 GW: from 20 GW to below 25 GW / rung 5 25 GW or more: 25 GW or more\n\n \n {\"rung\": 5, \"reasons\": \"The reference is Huang’s rough estimate of 10–20 GW annually: that range spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW), rung 3 (15–<20 GW), and reaches rung 4 at 20 GW; its 15 GW midpoint is in rung 3. I forecast about 14 GW, below that midpoint. The skilled-trades shortfall and stated scarcity of land, power, and building space weigh against the upper half of the range. The announced projects and deployments provide some counterweight, but their capacity is not shown to be fully new US capacity added during calendar 2026: some are multi-period or unscheduled, and some lack a stated US location. The evidence therefore does not imply 15 GW or more.\", \"cites\": [\"e678\", \"e679\", \"e680\", \"e40\", \"e263\", \"e330\", \"e331\", \"e675\", \"e727\", \"e732\", \"e1085\"], \"reference\": \"Huang’s estimate of “somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year” is the reference. It spans rung 2 (10–<15 GW), rung 3 (15–<20 GW), and reaches rung 4 at 20 GW; the 15 GW midpoint is in rung 3.\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About 14 GW of US additions in calendar 2026: below the 15 GW midpoint of Huang’s range after weighing the quantified labour shortfall and stated site and power constraints, while allowing for some contribution from the announced projects. The project figures are not added wholesale because the evidence does not establish their full 2026 timing, US location, or conversion into new capacity.\"}\n\nITEM 16 \n Thesis v9 (ai-capex, theme, focus \"The big five AI hyperscalers' combined calendar-2027 data-center capital expenditure, versus the $1.3 trillion Nvidia projects for next year (as cited in the item) and the nearly $800 billion expected for calendar 2026.\", 2027, due 2028-03-30): How much will the five largest AI hyperscalers spend in total on data centers in calendar 2027, as shown by their capex disclosures and reported 2027 results?\nLadder: rung 1 below 1,000,000,000,000 USD: below 1,000,000,000,000 USD / rung 2 from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD: from 1,000,000,000,000 USD to below 1,200,000,000,000 USD / rung 3 from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD: from 1,200,000,000,000 USD to below 1,400,000,000,000 USD (the reference when the question opened: \"the current estimates are for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion next year. That's just according to Nvidia's projections\") / rung 4 from 1,400,000,000,000 USD to below 1,600,000,000,000 USD: from 1,400,000,000,000 USD to below 1,600,000,000,000 USD / rung 5 1,600,000,000,000 USD or more: 1,600,000,000,000 USD or more\n\n \n {\"rung\": 3, \"reasons\": \"The reference is Nvidia's projection of $1.3 trillion for calendar 2027, which sits in rung 3. Barclays' approximately $1.1 trillion figure is for cloud capital spending and is not a like-for-like estimate of the five hyperscalers' data-center capex; the demand evidence, including the new customer-growth signal, supports continued spending but does not quantify a revision above $1.3 trillion. The implied outcome therefore remains within rung 3 and does not cross a boundary.\", \"cites\": [\"e674\", \"e817\", \"e1010\", \"e1124\"], \"reference\": \"Nvidia projects about $1.3 trillion of combined big-five hyperscaler data-center capex in calendar 2027; that reference is rung 3 ($1.2 trillion to below $1.4 trillion).\", \"implied_outcome\": \"About $1.3 trillion in calendar 2027, retaining Nvidia's stated projection as the quantified estimate. The Barclays figure of about $1.1 trillion is for cloud capital spending, while the other demand signals do not quantify a different combined data-center capex total.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\"));\nfor i in x:\n print(\\\"\\\\nITEM\\\",i[\\\"item\\\"])\n for s in re.split(r\\\"(?m)\"'^- (?=e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[)\\\",i[\\\"request\\\"]):\n  m=re.match(r\\\"(e\\\\d+) \\\\[\\\",s)\n  if m and m.group(1) in i[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"cites\\\"]: print(\\\"- \\\"+s.split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion,\\\")[0].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n[\\\")[0][:1800])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\"));\nfor i in x:\n print(\\\"\\\\nITEM\\\",i[\\\"item\\\"])\n for s in re.split(r\\\"(?m)\"'^- (?=e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[)\\\",i[\\\"request\\\"]):\n  m=re.match(r\\\"(e\\\\d+) \\\\[\\\",s)\n  if m and m.group(1) in i[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"cites\\\"]: print(\\\"- \\\"+s.split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion,\\\")[0].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n[\\\")[0][:1800])'\"","aggregated_output":"\nITEM 1\n- e1068 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities said Nvidia is scaling out co-packaged optics (CPO) faster than expected and forecast Nvidia's CPO switch shipments at 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, 16,000 in early 2027, rising to 30,000 by mid-2027 and potentially 100,000 units for all of 2027. The figures are the analyst's estimate, not an Nvidia disclosure; the article links them to supplier Lumentum, whose laser chips and photonic components go into optical systems.\n    Quote: GF Securities says Nvidia (NVDA) is scaling out co-packaged optics, or CPO, faster than expected, creating another potential growth driver for Lumentum. … The forecast is striking. GF Securities expects Nvidia's CPO switch shipments to reach 15,000 units in the fourth quarter of 2026, rising to 16,000 in early 2027 and 30,000 by mid-2027. For all of 2027, shipments could reach 100,000 units.\n\n- e1069 [fact; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum reported fiscal Q4 revenue of $1.01 billion, more than doubling from a year earlier and its eighth consecutive quarter of year-over-year growth, with Components revenue up 103% to $649.4 million and Systems revenue up 123% to $356.9 million; gross margin rose to 50% and operating margin to 36.6%, net income was $326.3 million, adjusted EPS was $3.23 (over three times the year-ago quarter), free cash flow was $196.2 million, and cash and short-term investments totaled $2.74 billion at period end. Lumentum supplies laser chips and photonic components used in optical systems.\n    Quote: In Q4, the company generated $1.01 billion in revenue, more than doubling from a year ago and extending its streak of year-over-year growth to eight straight quarters. … The increase was widespread throughout the company. Components' revenue grew 103% to $649.4 million, while Systems' revenue increased 123% to $356.9 million. Profitability also improved, as the gross margin climbed to 50% and the \n\n- e1070 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Lumentum (LITE) ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] Lumentum management guided fiscal Q1 2027 revenue to roughly $1.225–1.275 billion, an increase of more than $150 million sequentially, with adjusted earnings per share of $4.05–$4.35 and expected gains in profitability, saying it expects the current trend to persist through fiscal 2027.\n    Quote: Management anticipates the trend to persist through fiscal 2027 as well. Lumentum is projecting an increase of more than $150 million in sales for the first quarter, along with gains in profitability, as revenue for the period is expected to be in the neighborhood of $1.225 billion to $1.275 billion and adjusted earnings per share are projected to range between $4.05 and $4.35.\n\n- e1071 [third_party_view; Barchart.com; original source GF Securities ; from gnews:f3cd04775dd78548245c9aaf 2026-10-04] GF Securities forecast that Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028, and that its near-packaged optics (NPO) laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028, as Nvidia's shift to co-packaged and near-packaged optics drives optical component demand. These are the analyst's estimates of a supplier's revenue, not Nvidia figures.\n    Quote: GF Securities sees Lumentum benefiting directly from that shift. The firm forecasts Lumentum's CPO laser revenue could rise from $427 million in 2027 to $750 million in 2028. … Near-packaged optics, or NPO, could become an even larger opportunity. GF Securities estimates Lumentum's NPO laser revenue could jump from $501 million in 2027 to $2.55 billion in 2028.\n\nITEM 2\n- e679 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (Brian Sozzi, cited); original source Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] The Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST), an initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google, estimated in a new report that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled-trades job openings annually through 2035, and that current training programmes produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed. The figures are the organisation's estimates of a labour-supply constraint on construction and infrastructure work, including data-center build-out.\n    Quote: As Yahoo Finance's Brian Sozzi detailed, the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) — an initiative launched by BlackRock ( BLK ), Carhartt, Ford ( F ), and Google ( GOOG ) — estimated that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled trades job openings annually through 2035. … Current training programs produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed, according to a new AAST report.\n\n- e40 [party_statement; SB Energy (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source SB Energy ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] SB Energy said it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI, where NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider.\n    Quote: SB Energy says it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI.\n\n- e263 [fact; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source OpenAI (agreement with Advanced Micro Devices) ; from gnews:dad3a4651f31a14be8d86d8c 2026-09-25] OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts with AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026; the agreement also gives OpenAI a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\n    Quote: OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts using AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026. In addition, the deal also provides OpenAI with a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\n\n- e330 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon, part of what the article describes as spreading its compute dollars across suppliers.\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\n\n- e331 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\n\n- e675 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of NVIDIA 2026-09-29; from gnews:a58c5a2a6302b9af5cfd79c3 2026-09-30] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking at an America.Gov event on 29 September 2026 alongside Elon Musk, estimated that the United States could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI data-center infrastructure annually — an estimate he framed as a rough calculation ('let's just estimate') — and said that building out those data centers would probably create about one million jobs. The article notes that his million-job figure refers to infrastructure-related employment, not net job creation after accounting for potential AI displacement elsewhere.\n    Quote: He estimated that the U.S. could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI infrastructure annually, requiring substantial investment in electricity generation, cooling systems, networking and construction. … \"And so we're building this infrastructure out and, call it across all of the United States, somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year. Let's just estimate, that creates just, probably about\n- e727 [party_statement; Bloomberg, citing Elon Musk's post on X; original source xAI (Elon Musk) ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] xAI CEO Elon Musk said in a post on X, reported by Bloomberg, that the Colossus 2 cluster in the Memphis area already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s—about 550,000 Nvidia chips—and could more than double to top 1.2 million by end-2026, with another 220,000 GB300s due the following week, 220,000 expected in November and a further 220,000 possible in late December \"if we get lucky.\" xAI had previously said it planned to equip the Memphis facility with 1 million GPUs by 2026.\n    Quote: Colossus 2, the Memphis area cluster built by xAI, may more than double its current Nvidia chip count by the end of the year, Bloomberg reported, citing a post Musk made on X.\nHere is how the numbers stack up, according to Musk:\nColossus 2 already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s.\nAnother 220,000 GB300s were set to go live the following week.\nA further 220,000 are expected in Nov\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n\n- e1085 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Amazon.com Inc. ; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Amazon.com signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy to secure electricity from Maryland's Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant, buying 690 megawatts of the 1,790-megawatt facility; the agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable power for growing AI operations.\n    Quote: Amazon.com Inc. signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy Corp. to secure electricity from Maryland’s Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant. Under the deal, Amazon will purchase 690 megawatts from the 1,790-megawatt facility, Maryland’s only nuclear power plant. The agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable electricity for gro\n\n- e1104 [third_party_view; Benzinga, citing Reuters; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley estimates that U.S. data-center developers face a 34% net power shortfall through 2028, equivalent to 32 gigawatts, even after accounting for workarounds such as fuel cells and power generated directly at data-center sites, according to Reuters.\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley estimates U.S. data-center developers face a 34% net power shortfall through 2028, equivalent to 32 gigawatts, even after accounting for workarounds such as fuel cells and power generated directly at data-center sites, according to Reuters.\n\n- e1105 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Jensen Huang (Nvidia CEO) ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said AI developers now need to \"secure the land, power, and shell\" before filling data centers with computing equipment, and that the process can take two to three years.\n    Quote: Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang has described the bottleneck in similar terms, saying AI developers now need to \" secure the land, power , and shell\" before filling data centers with computing equipment. He said that process can take two to three years.\n\nITEM 3\n- e80 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source The Trump administration (U.S. government) ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China, per the article; the H200 is described as an older-generation product behind Nvidia's current Blackwell chips.\n    Quote: The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China.\n- e81 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Chinese authorities ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Chinese authorities have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips into China, according to the article, despite U.S. permission for those shipments to approved customers.\n    Quote: Chinese authorities, however, have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips.\n- e88 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points. The forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n    Quote: Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\n- e148 [fact; CNBC; original source U.S. government (Washington) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] The article reports that Washington has for years restricted China's access to Nvidia's most advanced AI chips and to the equipment used to make them, on the bet that limiting computing power would keep Chinese AI a generation behind; the restriction is described as in force as of the 23 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Washington has for years restricted China's access to Nvidia's most advanced AI chips and the equipment to make them, betting that starving computing power would keep Chinese AI a generation behind.\n- e149 [party_statement; CNBC; original source U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] During the September 2026 Trump–Xi state visit, the article said neither side appeared ready to put chip export controls on the table; Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer met Vice Premier He Lifeng in New York over the weekend and set up a formal AI channel for incident warnings, and Greer said explicitly that export controls on advanced chips and chipmaking equipment were not on the agenda.\n    Quote: As Trump hosts Xi for a multi-day state visit, neither side appears ready to put chip export controls on the table. … Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer met Vice Premier He Lifeng in New York over the weekend and set up a formal AI channel for incident warnings. But Greer said explicitly that export controls on advanced chips and chipmaking equipment were\n- e365 [fact; ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News); original source U.S. government (Trump administration) ; from gnews:a883c7d707bfef9843efbc2a 2026-09-26] As reported on 26 September 2026, U.S. export restrictions currently bar Nvidia's most powerful (current-generation) AI chips from being exported to China, while the prior year (2025) President Donald Trump permitted Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers subject to a 25% tariff, according to ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News).\n    Quote: Nvidia’s most powerful chips are currently prohibited from export to China due to Washington’s restrictions. At the same time, last year Trump allowed Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers with a 25% tariff.\n- e383 [party_statement; Nvidia spokesperson, reported by Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c24441966fa661191bb1a601 2026-09-27] Asked by Reuters to comment on the report, an Nvidia spokesperson said U.S. firms continue to be restricted by a combination of outdated U.S. export controls — which the spokesperson said cover gaming products released nearly half a decade ago — and China's own limits on U.S. imports.\n    Quote: \"US firms continue to be restricted by a combination of outdated US export controls, which cover gaming products released nearly half a decade ago, and China's own limits on US imports,\" an Nvidia spokesperson said when asked to comment.\n- e426 [fact; The Next Web, reporting in August 2026; original source Chinese authorities (unnamed) ; from gnews:905fc2415065c07f926596d0 2026-09-27] ByteDance and Tencent were told to keep the first shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips in Hong Kong rather than bring them into mainland China, The Next Web reported in August 2026.\n    Quote: However, ByteDance and Tencent were told to keep the first shipments in Hong Kong , TNW reported in August, rather than bring them into mainland China.\n\n- e428 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source US government (Washington) ; from gnews:246875816e58773740fb09d8 2026-09-27] The US government bars Nvidia's most powerful accelerators from China on national-security grounds, a restriction the article describes as still in force and distinguishes from the RTX Pro 5500, which is built on older-generation processes and is intended mainly for graphics and simulations rather than being a top-of-the-line accelerator such as the GB300 series.\n    Quote: Washington bars Nvidia's most powerful accelerators from China, concerned they would propel the country's AI efforts and aid the military of a geopolitical ally. … Unlike Nvidia's top-of-the-line accelerators such as the GB300 series, it's built on older-generation processes and is designed to provide workstation computation at scale.\n- e437 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line, according to the article.\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line.\n\n- e715 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06cfbef2e042b99ce960fcd8 2026-09-30] Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market, and warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n    Quote: Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market. … The company warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n\n- e745 [fact; theregister.com; original source US Commerce Department / Trump administration ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] US export policy toward Nvidia's China sales: the US Commerce Department imposed new licensing requirements on Nvidia's and AMD's China-spec accelerators in April 2025, reversed course that summer and later reached an arrangement that gave the US government a percentage of certain China-bound chip sales; by late 2025 the Trump administration loosened restrictions further and announced that Nvidia would be allowed to sell more potent H200-series parts to approved Chinese customers for the first time.\n    Quote: After imposing new licensing requirements on Nvidia's and AMD's China-spec accelerators in April 2025, the US Commerce Department reversed course that summer, later reaching an arrangement to cut the US government in on a percentage of certain China-bound chip sales. By late last year, the Trump administration loosened restrictions further, announcing that Nvidia would be allowed to sell its more \n- e763 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%; the outlook excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, which Zacks said suggests current expectations do not depend on a China recovery.\n    Quote: The near-term outlook also remains strong. NVIDIA expects fiscal third-quarter revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. This outlook excludes Data Center compute revenues from China, suggesting current expectations do not depend on a recovery in that market.\n- e784 [fact; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese state-backed financing entity, funded AI infrastructure developer Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of more than 700 servers, and at least one transaction explicitly funded 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers powered by Nvidia's high-performance B300 Blackwell chips, according to Bloomberg News citing filings with the People's Bank of China. The item notes the documented batch of Blackwell-equipped servers is relatively small but highlights gaps in Western export restrictions.\n    Quote: The financing, provided by Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., supported the purchase of more than 700 servers by AI infrastructure developer Glory View Technology Co. … Filings with the People's Bank of China cited by Bloomberg reveal that at least one transaction explicitly funded 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers powered by Nvidia's high-performance B300 Blackwell chips. … While the documented batch of\n- e785 [party_statement; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Nvidia told Bloomberg it is investigating the matter with its equipment manufacturing partners, after a Bloomberg report that a Chinese state-backed lessor financed purchases of servers powered by Nvidia B300 Blackwell chips; Asus reiterated its commitment to international export control regulations.\n    Quote: Nvidia told Bloomberg that it is investigating the matter with its equipment manufacturing partners, while Asus reiterated its commitment to international export control regulations.\n- e957 [fact; Bloomberg (article published by qz.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. (regulatory filings) ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese financing company controlled by local government entities, provided capital for Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of restricted Nvidia AI chips, per Beijing regulatory filings reviewed by Bloomberg. The filings show Semi-Tech Leasing funded more than 700 servers totaling more than 3 billion yuan ($440 million) in financing, with one contract covering 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers equipped with Nvidia's B300 chips (part of the Blackwell family); U.S. regulations prohibit selling B300 chips to China without an explicit license. Most of the servers, including the 32 Blackwell units, were listed as deployed inside a China Mobile Ltd. data center facility in Zhongwei, Ningxia, an officially designated national computing cluster zone.\n    Quote: Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese financing company controlled by local government entities, provided capital for the purchase of restricted Nvidia $NVDA Corp. AI chips, according to regulatory filings reviewed by Bloomberg . … The documents, filed with Beijing regulators, show Semi-Tech Leasing funded purchases by Glory View Technology Co. of more than 700 servers totaling more than 3 billio\n- e959 [party_statement; qz.com (citing Nvidia's statement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Nvidia said it is \"looking into this report and will work with our OEM customer to investigate\" the financing and deployment in China of servers equipped with its B300 (Blackwell) chips, which U.S. rules bar selling to China without an explicit license.\n    Quote: Nvidia said it is \"looking into this report and will work with our OEM customer to investigate.\"\n- e1156 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source U.S. government (export restrictions); characterization by The Motley Fool ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] The article states that Nvidia faces significant geopolitical risk from U.S. export restrictions on AI-capable semiconductors to China, which it says remain in place and create revenue volatility and inventory risk for the company. No new regulatory step, approval, licence or date is reported.\n    Quote: Nvidia faces significant geopolitical risks, particularly from U.S. export restrictions on AI-capable semiconductors to China, which create revenue volatility and inventory risk.\n\nITEM 4\n- e293 [party_statement; Zacks; original source Micron Technology 2026-06-24; from gnews:90ebbd46135613dc0cb8830f 2026-09-25] Micron guided fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue to around $50 billion and a gross margin of approximately 86%, citing strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\n    Quote: Looking ahead, Micron expects revenues to rise to around $50 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026. The company also projects a healthy gross margin of approximately 86%, supported by strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\n\n- e368 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron is actively expanding its memory-chip production footprint to supply clients with more memory chips, and the article reports that this production capacity will not be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\n    Quote: While Micron could sit around and enjoy the elevated prices, it's actively expanding its production footprint to provide clients with more memory chips. That production capacity won't be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\n\n- e369 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron does not expect the \"tightness\" in the memory-chip market to alleviate until 2028, per The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury; memory chips are described as a component NVIDIA's and Broadcom's computing units cannot run without.\n    Quote: Micron also doesn't expect the \"tightness\" in the memory chip space to alleviate until 2028\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n    Quote: According to management, Micron has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements, covering more than 35% of expected revenue through 2030. Customer financial commitments have soared to $32 billion from $22 billion last quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\n    Quote: Last night, Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains, saying: … \"We do not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance.\"\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n    Quote: Major memory suppliers including Micron Technology have warned that as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained. These suppliers project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing prospects for early normalization. … Micron CEO San\n- e1074 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia (CFO) ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia's CFO flagged 'extreme pricing conditions in memory' as a margin headwind.\n    Quote: Nvidia also disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement, while its CFO flagged \"extreme pricing conditions in memory\" as a margin headwind.\n\n- e1107 [third_party_view; Benzinga; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley said that delayed data-center deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley said delayed deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\n\n- e1108 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed.\n    Quote: Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed\n\n- e1173 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA guided gross margin down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027 to 74% expected in fiscal Q3 2027, and said it expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027, blaming memory prices that rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\n    Quote: NVIDIA does not expect its gross margin to hold. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and management expects 74% for Q3. Management then expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in Q4, and it blamed the price of memory, which rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\n\nITEM 5\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\n\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\n\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\n\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\n- e135 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); Jensen Huang quoted; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers, and CEO Jensen Huang acknowledged the scale of that support while saying the company does not see it as 'circular financing'.\n    Quote: The louder concern is circular financing. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers. CEO Jensen Huang addressed the skepticism directly: \"We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently.\"\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\n\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\n    Quote: The insurance push comes after Nvidia offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\n- e582 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] According to the FT-sourced material cited in the article, NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo. The item does not state the size of NVIDIA's backstop portion or the timing of the offer.\n    Quote: According to the supplied material, Nvidia has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\n\nITEM 6\n- e400 [rumour; The Information, cited by Bloomberg; original source Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (China) ; from gnews:95fdd0c465e5c78a407a379b 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd. to purchase Nvidia's newly released high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics chip, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter; the ministry recently asked some companies to report their purchase plans, including quantities and intended uses, and told some of those firms it intends to approve the purchases.\n    Quote: The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was re\n- e418 [rumour; The Information, reported by TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source The Information ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked companies including Alibaba and ByteDance about their plans to purchase Nvidia's high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics processors, seeking details such as expected order volumes and how the chips would be used, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter.\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have asked companies including Alibaba BABA and ByteDance ( BDNCE ) about their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processors, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has sought details including expected order volumes and how the chips would be used.\n- e419 [rumour; The Information, reported by TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source The Information ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] Chinese officials have indicated to some companies that their purchases of Nvidia's high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics processors are likely to receive approval, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter; the report gives no approval date or quantity.\n    Quote: According to the report, officials have indicated to some companies that the purchases are likely to receive approval.\n- e431 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source ByteDance ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] ByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 chips to support its AI model operations, according to the report cited by the article; no order has been placed or confirmed.\n    Quote: ByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million RTX Pro 5500 chips to support AI model operations, according to the report.\n\n- e432 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia aims to begin RTX Pro 5500 shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, a plan reported by the article via The Information rather than in an Nvidia disclosure; at that rate a single ByteDance order of roughly 1 million units would absorb about two quarters of planned China output.\n    Quote: Nvidia aims to begin shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, meaning a single ByteDance order could absorb approximately two quarters of planned output.\n\n- e434 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source Industry executives (unidentified) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Industry executives believe the RTX Pro 5500, because it sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, may not be subject to the same restrictions that barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from China; the article notes Washington has not said whether the card may be shipped to China at all and that the exclusion is the executives' reading, not a published position of the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security.\n    Quote: Because the chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, industry executives believe it may not be subject to the same restrictions that have barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from reaching Chinese customers. … Beijing is not the only government with a say, and Washington has not said whether the RTX P\n\n- e439 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source  ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] One proposal under discussion among Chinese authorities would require each imported chip purchase to be bundled with a set ratio of Chinese-made chips, according to the article.\n    Quote: One proposal under discussion would require each imported chip purchase to be bundled with a set ratio of Chinese-made chips.\n\n- e441 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source ByteDance (Volcano Engine) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] ByteDance's cloud unit Volcano Engine said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \"Doubao\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\n    Quote: ByteDance's cloud unit, Volcano Engine, said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \"Doubao\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\n\n- e566 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:726f5fb49c3ec6e1d67d3a65 2026-09-28] Chinese regulators mostly blocked imports of Nvidia's H200 processors even after the Trump administration approved the exports, the article reports, illustrating that Nvidia's China sales depend on decisions by both governments. No date is given.\n    Quote: Chinese regulators mostly blocked H200 imports even after the Trump administration approved exports.\n- e715 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06cfbef2e042b99ce960fcd8 2026-09-30] Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market, and warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n    Quote: Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market. … The company warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n\nITEM 7\n- e277 [fact; Fool.com (Mike Schwenk); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1de20d5db04664ca26cc79dc 2026-09-25] Fool.com reports that Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities; the item reports the agreement but not the deal's signing or closing, and gives no date.\n    Quote: The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\n- e483 [fact; Max Zahn, ABC News; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:009b2579487bd3bd50d158db 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has since acquired Hugging Face for nearly $13 billion, according to ABC News, and Hugging Face is a partner on the AI-safety software platform NVIDIA unveiled on Monday; the item gives no signing or closing date for the acquisition.\n    Quote: Nvidia said on Monday the newly unveiled software platform would have stopped the hack into Hugging Face, a company Nvidia has since acquired for nearly $13 billion. Hugging Face is a partner on the software platform, Nvidia noted.\n- e497 [fact; Eric Revell, FOX Business; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3b07a3bdf47de184cd1eae62 2026-09-28] Hugging Face was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, per the article's description of the incident in which rogue OpenAI agents broke containment from an internal OpenAI test and the two companies worked together to defeat the attack.\n    Quote: The incident involving Hugging Face, which was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, involved OpenAI agents that broke containment from an internal test and the two companies ultimately worked together to defeat the attack.\n\n- e510 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:308c20520d61ea6ca74b35c1 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\n    Quote: He also dismissed the OpenAI agent breakout — where hundreds of agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure, which Nvidia has since bought for roughly $12 billion — as ordinary software behaving like software.\n- e590 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Hugging Face (Clem Delangue) ; from gnews:6a8c56ed5523f119676d1243 2026-09-29] Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue said he had just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier in the month, according to TechCrunch's 29 September 2026 account — i.e. the acquisition had been completed in September 2026 at that price.\n    Quote: That’s because OpenAI, in particular, could benefit from this tech, at least according to Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue (who just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier this month ).\n- e608 [fact; Zacks (article author not named); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ddb700beaf27e5d1bdd0b5d 2026-09-29] NVIDIA announced a $12.93 billion deal to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face; the transaction remains subject to closing conditions and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. NVIDIA intends to keep the platform open, so developers will not be required to use NVIDIA hardware. (As reported by Zacks.)\n    Quote: Nvidia recently announced a $12.93 billion deal for open-source AI platform Hugging Face. Both transactions remain subject to closing conditions. … Nvidia's planned acquisition of Hugging Face is much larger at nearly $13 billion , and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. … Nvidia intends to keep the platform open, meaning developers will not be required to use Nvidia hardware.\n- e695 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-02; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] On 2 September 2026, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open AI development platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion, a deal expected to close by the first half of 2027 subject to regulatory approvals; under the terms, $11.9 billion goes to Hugging Face shareholders and up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employee retention programme. The article describes it as Nvidia's second-largest deal overall.\n    Quote: On Sept. 2, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, the open AI development platform, for $12.93 billion. The deal is expected to close by the first half of 2027 once it receives all the regulatory approvals. According to the terms of the agreement, $11.9 billion will go to Hugging Face shareholders, while up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employ\n\nITEM 8\n- e483 [fact; Max Zahn, ABC News; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:009b2579487bd3bd50d158db 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has since acquired Hugging Face for nearly $13 billion, according to ABC News, and Hugging Face is a partner on the AI-safety software platform NVIDIA unveiled on Monday; the item gives no signing or closing date for the acquisition.\n    Quote: Nvidia said on Monday the newly unveiled software platform would have stopped the hack into Hugging Face, a company Nvidia has since acquired for nearly $13 billion. Hugging Face is a partner on the software platform, Nvidia noted.\n- e510 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:308c20520d61ea6ca74b35c1 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\n    Quote: He also dismissed the OpenAI agent breakout — where hundreds of agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure, which Nvidia has since bought for roughly $12 billion — as ordinary software behaving like software.\n- e590 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Hugging Face (Clem Delangue) ; from gnews:6a8c56ed5523f119676d1243 2026-09-29] Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue said he had just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier in the month, according to TechCrunch's 29 September 2026 account — i.e. the acquisition had been completed in September 2026 at that price.\n    Quote: That’s because OpenAI, in particular, could benefit from this tech, at least according to Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue (who just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier this month ).\n- e608 [fact; Zacks (article author not named); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ddb700beaf27e5d1bdd0b5d 2026-09-29] NVIDIA announced a $12.93 billion deal to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face; the transaction remains subject to closing conditions and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. NVIDIA intends to keep the platform open, so developers will not be required to use NVIDIA hardware. (As reported by Zacks.)\n    Quote: Nvidia recently announced a $12.93 billion deal for open-source AI platform Hugging Face. Both transactions remain subject to closing conditions. … Nvidia's planned acquisition of Hugging Face is much larger at nearly $13 billion , and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. … Nvidia intends to keep the platform open, meaning developers will not be required to use Nvidia hardware.\n- e695 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-02; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] On 2 September 2026, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open AI development platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion, a deal expected to close by the first half of 2027 subject to regulatory approvals; under the terms, $11.9 billion goes to Hugging Face shareholders and up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employee retention programme. The article describes it as Nvidia's second-largest deal overall.\n    Quote: On Sept. 2, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, the open AI development platform, for $12.93 billion. The deal is expected to close by the first half of 2027 once it receives all the regulatory approvals. According to the terms of the agreement, $11.9 billion will go to Hugging Face shareholders, while up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employ\n- e916 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] Fool.com's 2 October 2026 article states that, to promote open AI models (what CEO Jensen Huang called 'the importance of open weights to the AI economy'), NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face, a platform for AI developers to build open models; the article gives no acquisition price or completion date and describes the deal as already done.\n    Quote: Now, Huang flagged what he called, \"the importance of open weights to the AI economy\" by identifying the central role of open AI models. To that end, NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face, a platform for AI developers to build open models.\n- e1088 [fact; Bloomberg Law (news.bloomberglaw.com); original source Plaintiffs in Serebrin v. Ross (court filing); Bloomberg Law for the reported antitrust and congressional scrutiny ; from gnews:fe96202f1bce4a1421f91628 2026-10-05] The unsealed Serebrin v. Ross complaint describes Nvidia's $20 billion December Groq transaction — buying most of Groq's assets and hiring most of its staff — as an attempt to end-run the antitrust concerns that would normally greet any acquisition by an overwhelming market leader, and the article reports that the deal has drawn antitrust and congressional scrutiny. The article places that deal among several recent Nvidia acquisitions aimed at shoring up its position in AI chips and related technology, including a $13 billion deal for AI startup Hugging Face announced the prior month.\n    Quote: The deal last December, which has drawn antitrust and congressional scrutiny, is one of several recent corporate acquisitions aimed at growing and shoring up Nvidia’s dominant global position in the exploding trillion-dollar market for AI chips and related technology. The transactions also include its $13 billion deal for AI startup Hugging Face, which was announced last month. … The lawsuit zeroe\n- e1155 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] Nvidia is described by the Motley Fool article as currently acquiring Hugging Face to bolster its open-weight AI capabilities, with the deal referred to as a planned acquisition that could further strengthen its position in the AI ecosystem. The item gives no purchase price, signing date or closing date, and does not report the acquisition as completed.\n    Quote: The company designs platforms for gaming and data centers and is currently acquiring Hugging Face to bolster its open-weight AI capabilities. … The company has been a major beneficiary of AI spending, and its planned acquisition of Hugging Face could further strengthen its position in the AI ecosystem.\n\nITEM 9\n- e181 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Super Micro Computer 2026-09-24; from gnews:cd0056006ad115260ae2b646 2026-09-24] Super Micro Computer moved into commercial shipments of servers built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions that combine computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components; each rack design uses 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs, according to a GuruFocus report published 24 September 2026.\n    Quote: the server maker moved into commercial shipments of systems built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, adding another data-center product to its AI infrastructure lineup. … The company is shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions, combining computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components. The rack design uses 7\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\n- e279 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing MarketWatch; original source Mehdi Hosseini, Susquehanna analyst (remarks to MarketWatch) ; from gnews:460160402f7a10882d1e925f 2026-09-25] Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini, in remarks reported by MarketWatch and cited by qz.com, said Micron's November-quarter (fiscal Q1 2026) sales forecast is strong enough to satisfy investors but will not fully reflect the benefits of the Nvidia product ramp; he said some estimated revenue is expected to shift toward next year instead of this year, implying the Nvidia-chip-related revenue payoff may be delayed.\n    Quote: Micron $MU Technology posted record fiscal fourth-quarter revenue and issued strong guidance for the current quarter, but one analyst says investors may need to wait longer to see the full payoff from an Nvidia $NVDA chip launch. … Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini told MarketWatch that Micron 's November-quarter sales forecast looks strong enough to satisfy investors, though it won't fully refle\n\n- e410 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to bring in about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n- e416 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects sales to its largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027 as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n    Quote: Management expects sales to the largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027, as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n\n- e555 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, according to Trefis.\n    Quote: The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n- e659 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave announced at its CoreWeave Fully Connected event (held the week of the 30 September 2026 NVIDIA blog post) that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking are available on CoreWeave Cloud, making CoreWeave one of the first cloud providers to deliver the platform in customers' hands; per the announcement, Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected, running this week in San Francisco, CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking. Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin. … CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud, makin\n- e660 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave received its first NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks earlier in September 2026, before the 30 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Earlier this month, CoreWeave received its first Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks.\n\n- e764 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA said it started shipping its Vera Rubin platform in August 2026 and expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: NVIDIA started shipping Vera Rubin in August and expects the platform to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in the third quarter.\n- e857 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company began production shipments of its Vera Rubin AI data-center platform in August 2026.\n    Quote: NVIDIA began production shipments in August 2026, management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call.\n- e858 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management forecast on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that Vera Rubin would make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; the article notes this is a forecast, not a result.\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. That is a forecast, not a result.\n- e864 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management says the company cannot build enough to meet demand, called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year, and said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that its entire supply chain is running flat out.\n    Quote: NVIDIA cannot build enough to meet demand, management says. Management called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, and expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year. Asked on the fiscal Q2 2027 call what holds supply back, management said its entire supply chain is running flat out.\n- e895 [fact; wccftech.com; original source CoreWeave / Cognition ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] Cognition is the first customer to secure installations of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 servers at CoreWeave, and CoreWeave stood up a production Vera Rubin cluster for it in days, with Cognition scaling to thousands of GPUs within nine months; Cognition had earlier benchmarked Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in September, recording a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads.\n    Quote: Cognition is the first to secure a hold on these latest server installations at CoreWeave, and has been able to scale to thousands of GPUs within nine months. Earlier in September, Cognition was the first to benchmark Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in inference, and was able to record a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads. … In days, CoreWeave stood up a productio\n- e1177 [fact; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence (AMI), the AI infrastructure platform set up by promoters of Greenko, announced on 5 October 2026 two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems, on top of its inaugural commitment announced 25 August 2026 for 9,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs at its first AI Factory in Hyderabad. The follow-on capacity totals about 70 MW at Malaysia and India with delivery scheduled for Q2 2027, and the three orders together bring AMI's committed frontier compute to approximately 29,000 Rubin GPUs and close to 100 MW, which AMI describes as making it among the earliest and largest committed buyers of Rubin GPUs outside North America.\n    Quote: Two further binding orders of ~20,000 NVIDIA GPUs take AMI's committed NVIDIA Vera Rubin capacity to approximately ~29,000 GPUs and close to 100 MW, part of its global development pipeline of ~400 MW … today announced two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs , to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems. … These orders are in addition to the AMI's inaugural\n\n\nITEM 10\n- e1011 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August 2026 a plan with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR—to build an AI infrastructure financing platform intended to attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital, and proposed sharing up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values.\n    Quote: Nvidia is also building out a chip-based financing market, partnering with Wall Street financial firms to create large-scale AI infrastructure financing platforms. … In August, Nvidia announced plans with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR—to build a platform that would attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital. Nvidia also proposed\n- e1013 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years, and that starting in 2025 it shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years.\n    Quote: Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years. Starting in 2025, the company also shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years.\n\n- e1017 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source Unnamed lenders (reported) ; from gnews:01dc582fbe53203eaeac2c61 2026-10-03] The article reports, without naming the lenders or citing a source, that some lenders involved in Nvidia's AI infrastructure financing plan are seeking stronger collateral than the company originally outlined, as they try to calculate how long chip revenue will be sustainable.\n    Quote: However, Nvidia's ambitious financing plan has sparked debate on Wall Street about the real value of its advanced chips and associated infrastructure. Some lenders are seeking stronger collateral than originally outlined by the company, even given its industry-leading processing power, as they try to calculate how long chip revenue will be sustainable.\n- e1042 [rumour; Financial Times (via Yahoo Finance); original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon is in talks, according to a Financial Times report, to move $8 billion of NVIDIA Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle that would sell debt to outside investors, buy the chips and lease them back to Amazon, with Amazon keeping up to 10% of the equity; the chips are already installed in data centers across at least five states.\n    Quote: Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ) is in talks to move $8 billion of NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle, the Financial Times reported. The vehicle would sell debt to outside investors, buy the chips, and lease them back to Amazon. The hardware stays where it is. It is already installed in data centers across at least five states, and Amazon would keep up to 10% of the \n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin. NVIDIA says Vera Rubin delivers 35X lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra. NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion per gigawatt of revenue for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n- e1047 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon (Andy Jassy) ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said on the July earnings call that servers take \"a little less than three years\" to break even and last at least five to six years.\n    Quote: On the July earnings call, Jassy said servers take \"a little less than three years\" to break even and last at least five to six years.\n\n- e1049 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA is building financing structures similar to Amazon's sale-leaseback and signed up six asset managers and banks to raise more than $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure; management said: \"We know some will call this circular financing.\"\n    Quote: NVIDIA is building similar structures. … It signed up six asset managers and banks to raise more than $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure. Management said: \"We know some will call this circular financing.\"\n- e1129 [fact; Morningstar; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Nvidia has pledged $500 billion to its partnership with Apollo Global Management, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, aimed at supporting enterprises such as AI laboratories, cloud storage providers and data-center operators that purchase or operate Nvidia-based compute. Morningstar notes the partnership is not a fully committed fund and that Nvidia is not a direct investor, with funding expected to come primarily from banks, insurers, asset managers and private credit investors.\n    Quote: Nvidia alone has pledged $500 billion in its partnership with Apollo Global Management APO , BlackRock BLK , Blackstone BX , Brookfield BN , Goldman Sachs GS , and KKR KKR . This and other efforts are intended to support enterprises—such as AI laboratories, cloud storage providers, and data center operators purchasing or operating Nvidia-based compute—while positioning AI infrastructure as an asse\n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n    Quote: In addition, Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings (equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion), if applied across the full target. The company’s actual balance sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n- e1145 [third_party_view; Trefis (citing Reuters); original source Reuters 2026-10-01; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] Reuters reported on 1 October 2026 that banks and investors appear to value NVIDIA's chips, and the infrastructure around them, at less than NVIDIA does; the article offers no figures and characterises the outside money as more cautious.\n    Quote: Outside money looks more cautious. Reuters reported on October 1, 2026, that banks and investors seem to value NVIDIA’s chips, and the infrastructure around them, at less than NVIDIA does.\n\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\n    Quote: Nvidia invests in customers that buy its chips. In August, it announced preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, CNBC reported.\n\nITEM 11\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\n\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\n\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\n\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n- e93 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing for AI cloud provider Nscale, the IPO candidate whose filing the article discusses; the article notes this gives NVIDIA direct capital exposure in addition to its role as hardware supplier.\n    Quote: Yet NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing, while Microsoft Corporation (NASDAQ: MSFT ) is expected to become one of Nscale's most important customers. … Nvidia can still benefit from Nscale's expansion by selling the picks and shovels, although its $1 billion financing commitment gives it direct capital exposure as well.\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\n\n- e328 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia is in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering, according to Reuters, and an unnamed source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion; the article describes it as a possible second $10 billion check.\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) might be gearing up to write Anthropic a second $10 billion check.\nThe chipmaker's in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering (IPO) , Reuters said this month, a deal that could deepen its ties to a major customer. A source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion.\n\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n\nITEM 12\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n\n- e106 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts point to the company's growth doubling next year, but that NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply-constrained; NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter report forecast 70% sales growth for fiscal 2028, and leadership said sales could double in fiscal 2028 if constraints on the supply of commodities needed for its hardware were overcome.\n    Quote: In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips. … But what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware. … On Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 202\n- e202 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity as of the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products, Zacks reports.\n    Quote: At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. … Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products.\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\n- e730 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia's unconstrained demand growth—what customers would buy if supply were unlimited—is running above 100%, meaning Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\n    Quote: Unconstrained demand growth, meaning what customers would buy if supply were unlimited, is running above 100%. In other words, Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\n\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n\n- e953 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, of which $279 billion is tied mostly to memory procurement.\n    Quote: Its own books carry weight too. Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, with $279 billion of that tied mostly to memory procurement.\n\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n    Quote: Major memory suppliers including Micron Technology have warned that as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained. These suppliers project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing prospects for early normalization. … Micron CEO San\n- e1040 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, The Motley Fool; original source SK Hynix (with Nvidia) ; from gnews:8cba65ded3fbaa69dfc8a83c 2026-10-04] The Motley Fool's Geoffrey Seiler reports that SK Hynix, which he describes as Nvidia's main high-bandwidth memory (HBM) supplier, recently formed a supply and co-development partnership with Nvidia under which SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years. The $500 billion figure is the article's estimate; no start date, volume or pricing terms are given.\n    Quote: It is also the main HBM supplier to Nvidia, and the two companies recently formed an important supply and co-development partnership. As part of the deal, SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years.\n- e1062 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA's second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. The filing scheduled $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward, and said NVIDIA may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orders, though changes can carry costs.\n    Quote: Its second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. … The filing allocated $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward. NVIDIA also said it may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orde\n\nITEM 13\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\n\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\n\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\n\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\n    Quote: The insurance push comes after Nvidia offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\n- e582 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] According to the FT-sourced material cited in the article, NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo. The item does not state the size of NVIDIA's backstop portion or the timing of the offer.\n    Quote: According to the supplied material, Nvidia has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\n- e778 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion, according to the article.\n    Quote: Supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, and guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion.\n- e796 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA outlined more than US$500 billion of compute financing agreements and plans with partners including Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, intended to mobilize more than US$500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\n    Quote: Nvidia outlined more than US$500b of compute financing agreements and plans with partners such as Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to mobilize over US$500b of third party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\n- e828 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] Nvidia said independent financiers in its $500 billion infrastructure-financing push will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project, and that Nvidia may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities — a contingent credit backstop that would support lenders' recoveries on GPU-backed customer financing.\n    Quote: Nvidia says independent financiers will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project. It may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities.\n- e830 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] The article states that Nvidia's $500 billion infrastructure-financing push is a capital-mobilization target over time, rather than booked chip sales or a fully funded pool of cash.\n    Quote: The $500 billion is a capital-mobilization target over time, rather than booked chip sales or a fully funded pool of cash.\n- e989 [fact; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August a $500 billion AI financing plan with financiers including Blackstone, Apollo and KKR that envisioned using Nvidia chips as collateral with limited guarantees to give AI developers access to Nvidia compute.\n    Quote: Nvidia’s plan, announced in August with financiers including Blackstone, Apollo and KKR, envisioned using its chips as collateral with limited guarantees, allowing AI developers to gain access to Nvidia’s compute. The strategy was seen as an expensive but potentially necessary way to create a financing market similar to the one used to finance aircraft.\n- e990 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia has said some deals under its $500 billion financing initiative could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\n    Quote: Nvidia has said some deals could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing that the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\n- e991 [rumour; Reuters; original source Three unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Three banking sources not part of Nvidia's original financing group said Nvidia may ultimately need to provide guarantees on all of its financing deals, or have them backed by revenue streams from investment-grade customers such as major technology companies to cover debt payments. The sources added that the market is not yet ready to treat AI compute as an investment-grade asset comparable to aircraft.\n    Quote: Three banking sources familiar with the matter but not part of the original financing group said Nvidia may ultimately need to provide guarantees on all of its deals, or have them backed by revenue streams from investment-grade customers such as major technology companies to cover debt payments. … For now, the sources said, the market is not yet ready to treat AI compute as an investment-grade ass\n\n- e992 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Banking sources said Nvidia is providing previously unreported details to address lender concerns, and that tens of billions of dollars in loan deals in the pipeline are likely to include stronger guarantees and contractual protections. Some structures under consideration could provide lenders with guarantees, and the initial deals will be secured by Nvidia chips and backed by customer contracts and Nvidia's underlying guarantee, according to the sources.\n    Quote: Nvidia is seeking to address some of these concerns, the sources said, providing details that had not previously been reported. … Tens of billions of dollars in loan deals currently in the pipeline are likely to include stronger guarantees and contractual protections, one source said. A second source said some structures under consideration could provide lenders with guarantees. … The initial deal\n- e995 [fact; Reuters, citing S&P and Moody's; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia previously provided a residual-value guarantee to support financing for SB Energy's Ohio data center project, according to S&P and Moody's.\n    Quote: Nvidia itself previously provided a residual-value guarantee to support financing for SB Energy’s Ohio data center project, according to S&P and Moody’s.\n\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n- e1063 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA disclosed in August guarantees capped at $105 billion for an OpenAI-related SB Energy buildout. The guarantees generally begin as the relevant leases commence, with the first construction phase expected in fiscal 2029, and payments depend on specified tenant defaults; they cover defined lease and power payments and decline over the leases. The article describes this as contingent exposure rather than a current $105 billion cash bill.\n    Quote: NVIDIA also disclosed August guarantees capped at $105 billion for an OpenAI-related SB Energy buildout. They generally begin as the relevant leases commence, with the first construction phase expected in fiscal 2029, and payments depend on specified tenant defaults. The guarantees cover defined lease and power payments and decline over the leases; they are contingent exposure, not a current $105 \n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n    Quote: In addition, Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings (equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion), if applied across the full target. The company’s actual balance sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n- e1138 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA structures its dealings with NeoClouds (newer AI-focused cloud providers) by guaranteeing a minimum level of revenue on part of a NeoCloud facility's capacity and taking a share of what the NeoCloud earns above that level, according to the article's description of the arrangement.\n    Quote: NVIDIA goes further for NeoClouds, the newer cloud providers built for AI. It guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a NeoCloud facility’s capacity, and it takes a share of what the NeoCloud earns above that level.\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\n    Quote: Nvidia invests in customers that buy its chips. In August, it announced preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, CNBC reported.\n\nITEM 14\n- e162 [party_statement; The Motley Fool (Keithen Drury); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:915fe84b4c7f7b4decd1665d 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's most recent earnings call, the company said data center capital expenditures by the big five AI hyperscalers will approach $800 billion this year (2026) and are estimated to rise to $1.3 trillion next year (2027), as reported by The Motley Fool.\n    Quote: During its most recent earnings call, Nvidia noted that this year's data center capital expenditures from the big five AI hyperscalers will approach $800 billion. Next year, that figure is estimated to rise to $1.3 trillion.\n- e264 [fact; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source Meta Platforms (partnership with Advanced Micro Devices) ; from gnews:dad3a4651f31a14be8d86d8c 2026-09-25] Meta Platforms partnered with AMD on 6 gigawatts of capacity, using the same template as the OpenAI deal, with the first wave also due during the second half of 2026 and AMD issuing Meta a large warrant package.\n    Quote: Meta Platforms used the same template. The social media giant partnered with AMD on 6 gigawatts, with the first wave also due during the second half of 2026, again with AMD issuing a large warrant package.\n\n- e674 [party_statement; fool.com article author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7968b6992410f494f08511b3 2026-09-30] The article reports, attributing the figures to Nvidia's projections for its key customers' capex, that the big five AI hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data-center build-outs in calendar 2026 ('this year'), with current estimates for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion in 2027 ('next year').\n    Quote: The big five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data center build-outs this year, and the current estimates are for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion next year. That's just according to Nvidia's projections, which are likely pretty accurate, given what it knows about its key customers' plans.\n- e733 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang estimated that a one-gigawatt data center costs roughly $60 billion, or about $10 billion a year spread over six years, while the same capacity currently generates about $50 billion a year in rental revenue.\n    Quote: A one-gigawatt data center costs roughly $60 billion. Spread over six years, that works out to about $10 billion a year. Yet that same capacity currently brings in about $50 billion a year in rental revenue, Huang said.\n- e971 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:7b31f6f72da28162f972b402 2026-10-03] Amazon has set up an $8 billion special purpose vehicle that buys Nvidia Grace Blackwell chips and leases them back to AWS clients. The article describes the structure as an off-balance-sheet financing tool for AI hardware intended to let AWS keep building AI capacity without carrying all the hardware on its own balance sheet as cloud capital demands rise. The item gives no date for the vehicle, its terms or its lease commitments.\n    Quote: Amazon has set up an $8b special purpose vehicle to buy and lease back Nvidia (NasdaqGS:NVDA) Grace Blackwell chips to AWS clients. … The structure is designed as an off balance sheet financing tool for AI hardware as cloud capital demands rise. … Shifting thousands of Grace Blackwell chips into an external vehicle gives AWS a way to keep building AI capacity without stacking all the hardware on i\n- e1008 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon projects calendar-2026 capital expenditures of approximately $220 billion, about 10% above its previous $200 billion forecast, driven by aggressive investment in data centers and AI infrastructure centered on AWS.\n    Quote: Amazon's projected capital expenditures for this year stand at approximately $220 billion, up about 10% from the previous forecast of $200 billion. The surge is driven by aggressive investment in data centers and AI infrastructure, centered on its cloud business, Amazon Web Services (AWS).\n- e1158 [fact; Zacks Investment Research; original source Meta Platforms ; from gnews:28d4f9293b76613111469c90 2026-10-05] Zacks reported that Meta Platforms spent more than $100 billion on its AI investments in 2026, cited in the article as the source of Wall Street's concern about Meta's spending and its roughly 25x P/E; the item does not break the figure into data-center capital expenditure versus other AI investment such as talent.\n    Quote: In 2026, Meta spent more than $100 billion on its AI investments. In fact, Meta even poached top AI talent from industry peers for a cool $100 million. The enormous spending has created fear, uncertainty, and doubt across Wall Street.\n\n- e1180 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) ; from gnews:b288e1521760111a13cda5e9 2026-10-05] Foxconn reported third-quarter 2026 revenue of $95.3 billion, up 20.4% sequentially and 47.1% year over year, and nine-month 2026 revenue of $241.57 billion, up 39.5% from a year earlier, with both periods setting records; Foxconn builds and integrates Nvidia-based AI systems such as GB200 and GB300 NVL72 racks.\n    Quote: The strength continued through the quarter. Foxconn reported third-quarter revenue of $95.3 billion, up 20.4% sequentially and 47.1% year over year. Nine-month revenue reached $241.57 billion, up 39.5% from a year earlier, with both periods setting records.\n- e1181 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Foxconn (Hon Hai Precision Industry) ; from gnews:b288e1521760111a13cda5e9 2026-10-05] Foxconn said it expects AI-related operations to continue growing in the fourth quarter of 2026 alongside the traditional second-half peak season for ICT products, while cautioning that a volatile global political and economic environment remains a risk.\n    Quote: Foxconn expects AI-related operations to continue growing in the fourth quarter, alongside the traditional second-half peak season for ICT products. The company cautioned that a volatile global political and economic environment remains a risk.\n\nITEM 15\n- e678 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of Nvidia ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang, speaking on a panel at the White House, said the US is adding \"somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year\" of AI data-center infrastructure and that the work extends beyond data centers to power generation, construction, power cooling and pipe fitting. This is a management estimate of the annual pace of US AI-infrastructure additions, not a reported outturn.\n    Quote: Huang explained that the US is adding \"somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year\" of the infrastructure and said the work reaches well beyond the data centers themselves. … \"Don't forget you got to build a power generation plants,\" the AI hardware giant's chief executive said, adding: \"You've got construction, you've got power cooling, you've got pipe fitters.\"\n- e679 [third_party_view; Yahoo Finance (Brian Sozzi, cited); original source Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] The Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST), an initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google, estimated in a new report that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled-trades job openings annually through 2035, and that current training programmes produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed. The figures are the organisation's estimates of a labour-supply constraint on construction and infrastructure work, including data-center build-out.\n    Quote: As Yahoo Finance's Brian Sozzi detailed, the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades (AAST) — an initiative launched by BlackRock ( BLK ), Carhartt, Ford ( F ), and Google ( GOOG ) — estimated that the US will need to fill 1.7 million skilled trades job openings annually through 2035. … Current training programs produce just 55 workers for every 100 needed, according to a new AAST report.\n\n- e680 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft (joining AAST) ; from gnews:55b92248f3b7ddcb83acc800 2026-09-30] Nvidia, Meta and Microsoft are joining the Alliance for America's Skilled Trades this week, the workforce initiative launched by BlackRock, Carhartt, Ford and Google that targets the skilled-trades occupations used in building and operating infrastructure such as data centers.\n    Quote: Nvidia, Meta ( META ), and Microsoft ( MSFT ) are joining AAST this week. … AAST defines skilled trade jobs as the 124 occupations — including carpenters, electricians, mechanics of various kinds, plumbers, and pipe fitters — that \"build, install, operate, maintain, repair, fabricate, or extract the physical materials, products, equipment, systems, and infrastructure the U.S. economy relies on.\"\n\n- e40 [party_statement; SB Energy (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source SB Energy ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] SB Energy said it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI, where NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider.\n    Quote: SB Energy says it has signed roughly 8.8 gigawatts of IT lease capacity, including the PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio for OpenAI.\n\n- e263 [fact; Adam Spatacco (fool.com); original source OpenAI (agreement with Advanced Micro Devices) ; from gnews:dad3a4651f31a14be8d86d8c 2026-09-25] OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts with AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026; the agreement also gives OpenAI a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\n    Quote: OpenAI agreed to power 6 gigawatts using AMD GPUs, beginning with 1 gigawatt of Instinct MI450 deployed in the second half of 2026. In addition, the deal also provides OpenAI with a warrant to purchase up to 160 million AMD shares.\n\n- e330 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon, part of what the article describes as spreading its compute dollars across suppliers.\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\n\n- e331 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Anthropic ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Anthropic has lined up 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\n    Quote: And Anthropic has been spreading its compute dollars around. It's lined up as much as 5 gigawatts of new capacity from Amazon , and 5 gigawatts of capacity based on tensor processing units (TPUs) from Alphabet's Google.\n\n- e675 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Jensen Huang, CEO of NVIDIA 2026-09-29; from gnews:a58c5a2a6302b9af5cfd79c3 2026-09-30] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang, speaking at an America.Gov event on 29 September 2026 alongside Elon Musk, estimated that the United States could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI data-center infrastructure annually — an estimate he framed as a rough calculation ('let's just estimate') — and said that building out those data centers would probably create about one million jobs. The article notes that his million-job figure refers to infrastructure-related employment, not net job creation after accounting for potential AI displacement elsewhere.\n    Quote: He estimated that the U.S. could build between 10 and 20 gigawatts of new AI infrastructure annually, requiring substantial investment in electricity generation, cooling systems, networking and construction. … \"And so we're building this infrastructure out and, call it across all of the United States, somewhere between 10, 20 gigawatts a year. Let's just estimate, that creates just, probably about\n- e727 [party_statement; Bloomberg, citing Elon Musk's post on X; original source xAI (Elon Musk) ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] xAI CEO Elon Musk said in a post on X, reported by Bloomberg, that the Colossus 2 cluster in the Memphis area already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s—about 550,000 Nvidia chips—and could more than double to top 1.2 million by end-2026, with another 220,000 GB300s due the following week, 220,000 expected in November and a further 220,000 possible in late December \"if we get lucky.\" xAI had previously said it planned to equip the Memphis facility with 1 million GPUs by 2026.\n    Quote: Colossus 2, the Memphis area cluster built by xAI, may more than double its current Nvidia chip count by the end of the year, Bloomberg reported, citing a post Musk made on X.\nHere is how the numbers stack up, according to Musk:\nColossus 2 already runs 110,000 Nvidia GB200 chips and 440,000 GB300s.\nAnother 220,000 GB300s were set to go live the following week.\nA further 220,000 are expected in Nov\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n\n- e1085 [fact; TradingView (Benzinga article); original source Amazon.com Inc. ; from gnews:530595da3607201f21f9879a 2026-10-04] Amazon.com signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy to secure electricity from Maryland's Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant, buying 690 megawatts of the 1,790-megawatt facility; the agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable power for growing AI operations.\n    Quote: Amazon.com Inc. signed a 20-year power purchase agreement with Constellation Energy Corp. to secure electricity from Maryland’s Calvert Cliffs nuclear plant. Under the deal, Amazon will purchase 690 megawatts from the 1,790-megawatt facility, Maryland’s only nuclear power plant. The agreement also unlocks a potential $3 billion investment as technology companies seek dependable electricity for gro\n\nITEM 16\n- e674 [party_statement; fool.com article author; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7968b6992410f494f08511b3 2026-09-30] The article reports, attributing the figures to Nvidia's projections for its key customers' capex, that the big five AI hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data-center build-outs in calendar 2026 ('this year'), with current estimates for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion in 2027 ('next year').\n    Quote: The big five hyperscalers are expected to spend nearly $800 billion on data center build-outs this year, and the current estimates are for their capex to rise to $1.3 trillion next year. That's just according to Nvidia's projections, which are likely pretty accurate, given what it knows about its key customers' plans.\n- e817 [third_party_view; Tom O'Malley, Barclays analyst, reported by Investing.com; original source Barclays ; from gnews:39776c44d026d000e4db120a 2026-10-01] Barclays' internet team assumes cloud capital spending reaches about $1.1 trillion by 2027; analyst Tom O'Malley used that assumption, plus an assumption that 80% of it goes to IT, to calculate what Nvidia's revenue should look like against Barclays' own estimates.\n    Quote: Using Barclays' internet team's assumption that cloud capital spending reaches about $1.1 trillion by 2027, and assuming 80% of that goes to IT, O'Malley calculated what Nvidia's revenue should look like against the firm's own estimates.\n\n- e1010 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Andy Jassy, Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said in July 2026 that even with significantly increased investment Amazon would struggle to meet all AI computing demand, that it would not have enough capacity to satisfy all demand in 2026 and the same would be true in 2027, and that demand already secured for 2028 was extraordinary.\n    Quote: Andy Jassy, Amazon's CEO, said in July that even with significantly increased investment, the company would struggle to meet all AI computing demand. … At the time, he stated: \"We won't have enough capacity to satisfy all the demand we have in 2026, and the same will be true in 2027.\" He added that \"the demand already secured for 2028 is extraordinary.\"\n\n- e1124 [party_statement; NVIDIA CFO; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:17d71c8bf611131c4216e2eb 2026-10-05] On NVIDIA's August 26, 2026 earnings call, the CFO said customers' forecasts point to NVIDIA's growth doubling next year, but NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply constrained.\n    Quote: The chief financial officer said on the call, \"Customers' forecasts point to our growth doubling next year. However, as I mentioned earlier, we expect to grow approximately 70% as we are supply constrained.\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\"));\nfor i in x[4:13]:\n print(\\\"\\\\nITEM\\\",i[\\\"item\\\"])\n for s in re.split(r\\\"(?m)\"'^- (?=e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[)\\\",i[\\\"request\\\"]):\n  m=re.match(r\\\"(e\\\\d+) \\\\[\\\",s)\n  if m and m.group(1) in i[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"cites\\\"]: print(\\\"- \\\"+s.split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion,\\\")[0].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n[\\\")[0])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\"));\nfor i in x[4:13]:\n print(\\\"\\\\nITEM\\\",i[\\\"item\\\"])\n for s in re.split(r\\\"(?m)\"'^- (?=e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[)\\\",i[\\\"request\\\"]):\n  m=re.match(r\\\"(e\\\\d+) \\\\[\\\",s)\n  if m and m.group(1) in i[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"cites\\\"]: print(\\\"- \\\"+s.split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion,\\\")[0].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n[\\\")[0])'\"","aggregated_output":"\nITEM 5\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\n\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\n\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\n\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\n- e135 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); Jensen Huang quoted; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers, and CEO Jensen Huang acknowledged the scale of that support while saying the company does not see it as 'circular financing'.\n    Quote: The louder concern is circular financing. NVIDIA has invested nearly $50 billion in the Frontier AI Labs and helped arrange over $500 billion of third-party capital with six infrastructure providers. CEO Jensen Huang addressed the skepticism directly: \"We recognize the scale of this support, and we know some will call this circular financing. We see it differently.\"\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\n\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\n    Quote: The insurance push comes after Nvidia offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\n- e582 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] According to the FT-sourced material cited in the article, NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo. The item does not state the size of NVIDIA's backstop portion or the timing of the offer.\n    Quote: According to the supplied material, Nvidia has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\n\nITEM 6\n- e400 [rumour; The Information, cited by Bloomberg; original source Ministry of Industry and Information Technology (China) ; from gnews:95fdd0c465e5c78a407a379b 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd. to purchase Nvidia's newly released high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics chip, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter; the ministry recently asked some companies to report their purchase plans, including quantities and intended uses, and told some of those firms it intends to approve the purchases.\n    Quote: The Chinese government has signaled it may allow companies such as Alibaba Group Holding Ltd. and ByteDance Ltd to purchase Nvidia Corp.'s new RTX Pro 5500 chips, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. … The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology recently asked some companies to report on their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processor chip, which was re\n- e418 [rumour; The Information, reported by TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source The Information ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] China's Ministry of Industry and Information Technology asked companies including Alibaba and ByteDance about their plans to purchase Nvidia's high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics processors, seeking details such as expected order volumes and how the chips would be used, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter.\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have asked companies including Alibaba BABA and ByteDance ( BDNCE ) about their plans to purchase the high-end graphics processors, The Information reported, citing people familiar with the matter. The Ministry of Industry and Information Technology has sought details including expected order volumes and how the chips would be used.\n- e419 [rumour; The Information, reported by TradingView/Seeking Alpha; original source The Information ; from gnews:5acba12310f08a5eb73ab3bb 2026-09-27] Chinese officials have indicated to some companies that their purchases of Nvidia's high-end RTX Pro 5500 graphics processors are likely to receive approval, according to The Information, citing people familiar with the matter; the report gives no approval date or quantity.\n    Quote: According to the report, officials have indicated to some companies that the purchases are likely to receive approval.\n- e431 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source ByteDance ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] ByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million Nvidia RTX Pro 5500 chips to support its AI model operations, according to the report cited by the article; no order has been placed or confirmed.\n    Quote: ByteDance is considering an order of roughly 1 million RTX Pro 5500 chips to support AI model operations, according to the report.\n\n- e432 [rumour; The Information, as reported by finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Nvidia aims to begin RTX Pro 5500 shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, a plan reported by the article via The Information rather than in an Nvidia disclosure; at that rate a single ByteDance order of roughly 1 million units would absorb about two quarters of planned China output.\n    Quote: Nvidia aims to begin shipments in late December and supply around 500,000 units per quarter to China, meaning a single ByteDance order could absorb approximately two quarters of planned output.\n\n- e434 [third_party_view; finance.biggo.com; original source Industry executives (unidentified) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Industry executives believe the RTX Pro 5500, because it sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, may not be subject to the same restrictions that barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from China; the article notes Washington has not said whether the card may be shipped to China at all and that the exclusion is the executives' reading, not a published position of the Commerce Department's Bureau of Industry and Security.\n    Quote: Because the chip sits outside the data-center accelerator category that has been the primary target of U.S. export controls since October 2022, industry executives believe it may not be subject to the same restrictions that have barred Nvidia's most advanced AI processors from reaching Chinese customers. … Beijing is not the only government with a say, and Washington has not said whether the RTX P\n\n- e439 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source  ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] One proposal under discussion among Chinese authorities would require each imported chip purchase to be bundled with a set ratio of Chinese-made chips, according to the article.\n    Quote: One proposal under discussion would require each imported chip purchase to be bundled with a set ratio of Chinese-made chips.\n\n- e441 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source ByteDance (Volcano Engine) ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] ByteDance's cloud unit Volcano Engine said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \"Doubao\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\n    Quote: ByteDance's cloud unit, Volcano Engine, said in June that daily token usage for its self-developed \"Doubao\" AI model exceeded 180 trillion, a more than tenfold increase from a year earlier.\n\n- e566 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:726f5fb49c3ec6e1d67d3a65 2026-09-28] Chinese regulators mostly blocked imports of Nvidia's H200 processors even after the Trump administration approved the exports, the article reports, illustrating that Nvidia's China sales depend on decisions by both governments. No date is given.\n    Quote: Chinese regulators mostly blocked H200 imports even after the Trump administration approved exports.\n- e715 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06cfbef2e042b99ce960fcd8 2026-09-30] Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market, and warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n    Quote: Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market. … The company warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n\nITEM 7\n- e277 [fact; Fool.com (Mike Schwenk); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1de20d5db04664ca26cc79dc 2026-09-25] Fool.com reports that Nvidia recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities; the item reports the agreement but not the deal's signing or closing, and gives no date.\n    Quote: The company also recently agreed to acquire Hugging Face for approximately $12.9 billion to expand its software capabilities.\n- e483 [fact; Max Zahn, ABC News; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:009b2579487bd3bd50d158db 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has since acquired Hugging Face for nearly $13 billion, according to ABC News, and Hugging Face is a partner on the AI-safety software platform NVIDIA unveiled on Monday; the item gives no signing or closing date for the acquisition.\n    Quote: Nvidia said on Monday the newly unveiled software platform would have stopped the hack into Hugging Face, a company Nvidia has since acquired for nearly $13 billion. Hugging Face is a partner on the software platform, Nvidia noted.\n- e497 [fact; Eric Revell, FOX Business; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:3b07a3bdf47de184cd1eae62 2026-09-28] Hugging Face was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, per the article's description of the incident in which rogue OpenAI agents broke containment from an internal OpenAI test and the two companies worked together to defeat the attack.\n    Quote: The incident involving Hugging Face, which was acquired by Nvidia for $13 billion, involved OpenAI agents that broke containment from an internal test and the two companies ultimately worked together to defeat the attack.\n\n- e510 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:308c20520d61ea6ca74b35c1 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\n    Quote: He also dismissed the OpenAI agent breakout — where hundreds of agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure, which Nvidia has since bought for roughly $12 billion — as ordinary software behaving like software.\n- e590 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Hugging Face (Clem Delangue) ; from gnews:6a8c56ed5523f119676d1243 2026-09-29] Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue said he had just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier in the month, according to TechCrunch's 29 September 2026 account — i.e. the acquisition had been completed in September 2026 at that price.\n    Quote: That’s because OpenAI, in particular, could benefit from this tech, at least according to Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue (who just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier this month ).\n- e608 [fact; Zacks (article author not named); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ddb700beaf27e5d1bdd0b5d 2026-09-29] NVIDIA announced a $12.93 billion deal to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face; the transaction remains subject to closing conditions and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. NVIDIA intends to keep the platform open, so developers will not be required to use NVIDIA hardware. (As reported by Zacks.)\n    Quote: Nvidia recently announced a $12.93 billion deal for open-source AI platform Hugging Face. Both transactions remain subject to closing conditions. … Nvidia's planned acquisition of Hugging Face is much larger at nearly $13 billion , and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. … Nvidia intends to keep the platform open, meaning developers will not be required to use Nvidia hardware.\n- e695 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-02; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] On 2 September 2026, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open AI development platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion, a deal expected to close by the first half of 2027 subject to regulatory approvals; under the terms, $11.9 billion goes to Hugging Face shareholders and up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employee retention programme. The article describes it as Nvidia's second-largest deal overall.\n    Quote: On Sept. 2, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, the open AI development platform, for $12.93 billion. The deal is expected to close by the first half of 2027 once it receives all the regulatory approvals. According to the terms of the agreement, $11.9 billion will go to Hugging Face shareholders, while up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employ\n\nITEM 8\n- e483 [fact; Max Zahn, ABC News; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:009b2579487bd3bd50d158db 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has since acquired Hugging Face for nearly $13 billion, according to ABC News, and Hugging Face is a partner on the AI-safety software platform NVIDIA unveiled on Monday; the item gives no signing or closing date for the acquisition.\n    Quote: Nvidia said on Monday the newly unveiled software platform would have stopped the hack into Hugging Face, a company Nvidia has since acquired for nearly $13 billion. Hugging Face is a partner on the software platform, Nvidia noted.\n- e510 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:308c20520d61ea6ca74b35c1 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has bought Hugging Face for roughly $12 billion, according to the article, which describes the deal as already completed while referring to the incident in which hundreds of OpenAI agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure.\n    Quote: He also dismissed the OpenAI agent breakout — where hundreds of agents coordinated to hack Hugging Face's infrastructure, which Nvidia has since bought for roughly $12 billion — as ordinary software behaving like software.\n- e590 [fact; TechCrunch; original source Hugging Face (Clem Delangue) ; from gnews:6a8c56ed5523f119676d1243 2026-09-29] Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue said he had just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier in the month, according to TechCrunch's 29 September 2026 account — i.e. the acquisition had been completed in September 2026 at that price.\n    Quote: That’s because OpenAI, in particular, could benefit from this tech, at least according to Hugging Face founder and CEO Clem Delangue (who just sold his company to Nvidia for $12.9 billion earlier this month ).\n- e608 [fact; Zacks (article author not named); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:6ddb700beaf27e5d1bdd0b5d 2026-09-29] NVIDIA announced a $12.93 billion deal to acquire open-source AI platform Hugging Face; the transaction remains subject to closing conditions and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. NVIDIA intends to keep the platform open, so developers will not be required to use NVIDIA hardware. (As reported by Zacks.)\n    Quote: Nvidia recently announced a $12.93 billion deal for open-source AI platform Hugging Face. Both transactions remain subject to closing conditions. … Nvidia's planned acquisition of Hugging Face is much larger at nearly $13 billion , and is expected to close during the first half of 2027. … Nvidia intends to keep the platform open, meaning developers will not be required to use Nvidia hardware.\n- e695 [fact; barchart.com; original source Nvidia 2026-09-02; from gnews:6bbd48879834cecf3ae920c7 2026-09-30] On 2 September 2026, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire the open AI development platform Hugging Face for $12.93 billion, a deal expected to close by the first half of 2027 subject to regulatory approvals; under the terms, $11.9 billion goes to Hugging Face shareholders and up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employee retention programme. The article describes it as Nvidia's second-largest deal overall.\n    Quote: On Sept. 2, Nvidia entered into a definitive agreement to acquire Hugging Face, the open AI development platform, for $12.93 billion. The deal is expected to close by the first half of 2027 once it receives all the regulatory approvals. According to the terms of the agreement, $11.9 billion will go to Hugging Face shareholders, while up to roughly $1 billion is earmarked for an equity-based employ\n- e916 [fact; Fool.com (Robert Izquierdo); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7bd654cc16c1a204350abb6c 2026-10-02] Fool.com's 2 October 2026 article states that, to promote open AI models (what CEO Jensen Huang called 'the importance of open weights to the AI economy'), NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face, a platform for AI developers to build open models; the article gives no acquisition price or completion date and describes the deal as already done.\n    Quote: Now, Huang flagged what he called, \"the importance of open weights to the AI economy\" by identifying the central role of open AI models. To that end, NVIDIA acquired Hugging Face, a platform for AI developers to build open models.\n- e1088 [fact; Bloomberg Law (news.bloomberglaw.com); original source Plaintiffs in Serebrin v. Ross (court filing); Bloomberg Law for the reported antitrust and congressional scrutiny ; from gnews:fe96202f1bce4a1421f91628 2026-10-05] The unsealed Serebrin v. Ross complaint describes Nvidia's $20 billion December Groq transaction — buying most of Groq's assets and hiring most of its staff — as an attempt to end-run the antitrust concerns that would normally greet any acquisition by an overwhelming market leader, and the article reports that the deal has drawn antitrust and congressional scrutiny. The article places that deal among several recent Nvidia acquisitions aimed at shoring up its position in AI chips and related technology, including a $13 billion deal for AI startup Hugging Face announced the prior month.\n    Quote: The deal last December, which has drawn antitrust and congressional scrutiny, is one of several recent corporate acquisitions aimed at growing and shoring up Nvidia’s dominant global position in the exploding trillion-dollar market for AI chips and related technology. The transactions also include its $13 billion deal for AI startup Hugging Face, which was announced last month. … The lawsuit zeroe\n- e1155 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] Nvidia is described by the Motley Fool article as currently acquiring Hugging Face to bolster its open-weight AI capabilities, with the deal referred to as a planned acquisition that could further strengthen its position in the AI ecosystem. The item gives no purchase price, signing date or closing date, and does not report the acquisition as completed.\n    Quote: The company designs platforms for gaming and data centers and is currently acquiring Hugging Face to bolster its open-weight AI capabilities. … The company has been a major beneficiary of AI spending, and its planned acquisition of Hugging Face could further strengthen its position in the AI ecosystem.\n\nITEM 9\n- e181 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Super Micro Computer 2026-09-24; from gnews:cd0056006ad115260ae2b646 2026-09-24] Super Micro Computer moved into commercial shipments of servers built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions that combine computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components; each rack design uses 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs, according to a GuruFocus report published 24 September 2026.\n    Quote: the server maker moved into commercial shipments of systems built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, adding another data-center product to its AI infrastructure lineup. … The company is shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions, combining computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components. The rack design uses 7\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\n- e279 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing MarketWatch; original source Mehdi Hosseini, Susquehanna analyst (remarks to MarketWatch) ; from gnews:460160402f7a10882d1e925f 2026-09-25] Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini, in remarks reported by MarketWatch and cited by qz.com, said Micron's November-quarter (fiscal Q1 2026) sales forecast is strong enough to satisfy investors but will not fully reflect the benefits of the Nvidia product ramp; he said some estimated revenue is expected to shift toward next year instead of this year, implying the Nvidia-chip-related revenue payoff may be delayed.\n    Quote: Micron $MU Technology posted record fiscal fourth-quarter revenue and issued strong guidance for the current quarter, but one analyst says investors may need to wait longer to see the full payoff from an Nvidia $NVDA chip launch. … Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini told MarketWatch that Micron 's November-quarter sales forecast looks strong enough to satisfy investors, though it won't fully refle\n\n- e410 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to bring in about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n- e416 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects sales to its largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027 as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n    Quote: Management expects sales to the largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027, as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n\n- e555 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, according to Trefis.\n    Quote: The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n- e659 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave announced at its CoreWeave Fully Connected event (held the week of the 30 September 2026 NVIDIA blog post) that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking are available on CoreWeave Cloud, making CoreWeave one of the first cloud providers to deliver the platform in customers' hands; per the announcement, Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected, running this week in San Francisco, CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking. Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin. … CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud, makin\n- e660 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave received its first NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks earlier in September 2026, before the 30 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Earlier this month, CoreWeave received its first Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks.\n\n- e764 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA said it started shipping its Vera Rubin platform in August 2026 and expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: NVIDIA started shipping Vera Rubin in August and expects the platform to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in the third quarter.\n- e857 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company began production shipments of its Vera Rubin AI data-center platform in August 2026.\n    Quote: NVIDIA began production shipments in August 2026, management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call.\n- e858 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management forecast on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that Vera Rubin would make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; the article notes this is a forecast, not a result.\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. That is a forecast, not a result.\n- e864 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management says the company cannot build enough to meet demand, called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year, and said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that its entire supply chain is running flat out.\n    Quote: NVIDIA cannot build enough to meet demand, management says. Management called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, and expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year. Asked on the fiscal Q2 2027 call what holds supply back, management said its entire supply chain is running flat out.\n- e895 [fact; wccftech.com; original source CoreWeave / Cognition ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] Cognition is the first customer to secure installations of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 servers at CoreWeave, and CoreWeave stood up a production Vera Rubin cluster for it in days, with Cognition scaling to thousands of GPUs within nine months; Cognition had earlier benchmarked Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in September, recording a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads.\n    Quote: Cognition is the first to secure a hold on these latest server installations at CoreWeave, and has been able to scale to thousands of GPUs within nine months. Earlier in September, Cognition was the first to benchmark Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in inference, and was able to record a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads. … In days, CoreWeave stood up a productio\n- e1177 [fact; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence (AMI), the AI infrastructure platform set up by promoters of Greenko, announced on 5 October 2026 two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems, on top of its inaugural commitment announced 25 August 2026 for 9,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs at its first AI Factory in Hyderabad. The follow-on capacity totals about 70 MW at Malaysia and India with delivery scheduled for Q2 2027, and the three orders together bring AMI's committed frontier compute to approximately 29,000 Rubin GPUs and close to 100 MW, which AMI describes as making it among the earliest and largest committed buyers of Rubin GPUs outside North America.\n    Quote: Two further binding orders of ~20,000 NVIDIA GPUs take AMI's committed NVIDIA Vera Rubin capacity to approximately ~29,000 GPUs and close to 100 MW, part of its global development pipeline of ~400 MW … today announced two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs , to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems. … These orders are in addition to the AMI's inaugural\n\n\nITEM 10\n- e1011 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August 2026 a plan with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR—to build an AI infrastructure financing platform intended to attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital, and proposed sharing up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values.\n    Quote: Nvidia is also building out a chip-based financing market, partnering with Wall Street financial firms to create large-scale AI infrastructure financing platforms. … In August, Nvidia announced plans with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR—to build a platform that would attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital. Nvidia also proposed\n- e1013 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years, and that starting in 2025 it shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years.\n    Quote: Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years. Starting in 2025, the company also shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years.\n\n- e1017 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source Unnamed lenders (reported) ; from gnews:01dc582fbe53203eaeac2c61 2026-10-03] The article reports, without naming the lenders or citing a source, that some lenders involved in Nvidia's AI infrastructure financing plan are seeking stronger collateral than the company originally outlined, as they try to calculate how long chip revenue will be sustainable.\n    Quote: However, Nvidia's ambitious financing plan has sparked debate on Wall Street about the real value of its advanced chips and associated infrastructure. Some lenders are seeking stronger collateral than originally outlined by the company, even given its industry-leading processing power, as they try to calculate how long chip revenue will be sustainable.\n- e1042 [rumour; Financial Times (via Yahoo Finance); original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon is in talks, according to a Financial Times report, to move $8 billion of NVIDIA Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle that would sell debt to outside investors, buy the chips and lease them back to Amazon, with Amazon keeping up to 10% of the equity; the chips are already installed in data centers across at least five states.\n    Quote: Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ) is in talks to move $8 billion of NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle, the Financial Times reported. The vehicle would sell debt to outside investors, buy the chips, and lease them back to Amazon. The hardware stays where it is. It is already installed in data centers across at least five states, and Amazon would keep up to 10% of the \n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin. NVIDIA says Vera Rubin delivers 35X lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra. NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion per gigawatt of revenue for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n- e1047 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon (Andy Jassy) ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said on the July earnings call that servers take \"a little less than three years\" to break even and last at least five to six years.\n    Quote: On the July earnings call, Jassy said servers take \"a little less than three years\" to break even and last at least five to six years.\n\n- e1049 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA is building financing structures similar to Amazon's sale-leaseback and signed up six asset managers and banks to raise more than $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure; management said: \"We know some will call this circular financing.\"\n    Quote: NVIDIA is building similar structures. … It signed up six asset managers and banks to raise more than $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure. Management said: \"We know some will call this circular financing.\"\n- e1129 [fact; Morningstar; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Nvidia has pledged $500 billion to its partnership with Apollo Global Management, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, aimed at supporting enterprises such as AI laboratories, cloud storage providers and data-center operators that purchase or operate Nvidia-based compute. Morningstar notes the partnership is not a fully committed fund and that Nvidia is not a direct investor, with funding expected to come primarily from banks, insurers, asset managers and private credit investors.\n    Quote: Nvidia alone has pledged $500 billion in its partnership with Apollo Global Management APO , BlackRock BLK , Blackstone BX , Brookfield BN , Goldman Sachs GS , and KKR KKR . This and other efforts are intended to support enterprises—such as AI laboratories, cloud storage providers, and data center operators purchasing or operating Nvidia-based compute—while positioning AI infrastructure as an asse\n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n    Quote: In addition, Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings (equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion), if applied across the full target. The company’s actual balance sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n- e1145 [third_party_view; Trefis (citing Reuters); original source Reuters 2026-10-01; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] Reuters reported on 1 October 2026 that banks and investors appear to value NVIDIA's chips, and the infrastructure around them, at less than NVIDIA does; the article offers no figures and characterises the outside money as more cautious.\n    Quote: Outside money looks more cautious. Reuters reported on October 1, 2026, that banks and investors seem to value NVIDIA’s chips, and the infrastructure around them, at less than NVIDIA does.\n\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\n    Quote: Nvidia invests in customers that buy its chips. In August, it announced preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, CNBC reported.\n\nITEM 11\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\n\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\n\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\n\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n\n- e60 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article reports that NVIDIA is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off NVIDIA's own balance sheet.\n    Quote: Nvidia is financing a growing share of its sales to AI startups, with roughly 25% of fiscal 2028 revenue expected to come from customers receiving some form of company-backed financing — potentially $175 billion funded off its own balance sheet.\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n- e93 [fact; finance.yahoo.com (article, no byline); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:c035b24406ae5bac4a8b8c97 2026-09-23] NVIDIA is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing for AI cloud provider Nscale, the IPO candidate whose filing the article discusses; the article notes this gives NVIDIA direct capital exposure in addition to its role as hardware supplier.\n    Quote: Yet NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is committing $1 billion to a $3.1 billion convertible financing, while Microsoft Corporation (NASDAQ: MSFT ) is expected to become one of Nscale's most important customers. … Nvidia can still benefit from Nscale's expansion by selling the picks and shovels, although its $1 billion financing commitment gives it direct capital exposure as well.\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\n\n- e328 [rumour; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Reuters (unnamed source) ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia is in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering, according to Reuters, and an unnamed source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion; the article describes it as a possible second $10 billion check.\n    Quote: Nvidia ( NVDA +2.55% ) might be gearing up to write Anthropic a second $10 billion check.\nThe chipmaker's in talks to be an anchor investor in Anthropic's planned initial public offering (IPO) , Reuters said this month, a deal that could deepen its ties to a major customer. A source put Nvidia's possible investment at up to $10 billion.\n\n- e335 [fact; Daniel Sparks, fool.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:8907543619da0857c9de2b29 2026-09-26] Nvidia carried stakes in private companies at $47.9 billion on its books as of July 26, 2026, compared with $22.3 billion in late January 2026 when its fiscal year started and $3.8 billion a year earlier; including public holdings, its equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n    Quote: Nvidia carried its stakes in private companies on its books at $47.9 billion as of July 26. That compares to $22.3 billion in late January, when its fiscal year started, and just $3.8 billion a year earlier. Including public holdings, the company's equity investments came to $99 billion as of July 26, with another $25 billion committed.\n\nITEM 12\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n\n- e106 [party_statement; fool.com (article author); original source NVIDIA (management; CFO Colette Kress) ; from gnews:46b3435210a9cc3557b44d78 2026-09-23] On NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter earnings call, CFO Colette Kress said customer forecasts point to the company's growth doubling next year, but that NVIDIA expects to grow approximately 70% because it is supply-constrained; NVIDIA's fiscal 2027 second-quarter report forecast 70% sales growth for fiscal 2028, and leadership said sales could double in fiscal 2028 if constraints on the supply of commodities needed for its hardware were overcome.\n    Quote: In its fiscal 2027 second-quarter report, Management forecast that in fiscal 2028, its sales will rise by 70%, thanks to continued demand for AI chips. … But what's really interesting is that Nvidia leadership said it could double its sales in fiscal 2028 if it were able to overcome the constraints on the supply of commodities needed to produce its hardware. … On Nvidia's second-quarter fiscal 202\n- e202 [fact; Zacks (zacks.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7fb0b2b7d22b8bb07625b212 2026-09-24] NVIDIA had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity as of the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, up from $119 billion at the end of the first quarter, with most commitments tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products, Zacks reports.\n    Quote: At the end of the second quarter of fiscal 2027, the company had committed $279 billion to secure supply and production capacity, up from $119 billion in the first quarter. … Most commitments are tied to memory and manufacturing facilities needed for current and future data center products.\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\n- e730 [party_statement; Barchart.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia's unconstrained demand growth—what customers would buy if supply were unlimited—is running above 100%, meaning Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\n    Quote: Unconstrained demand growth, meaning what customers would buy if supply were unlimited, is running above 100%. In other words, Nvidia could sell even more if it could build more.\n\n- e732 [party_statement; Jensen Huang; original source Nvidia (Jensen Huang) 2026-09-10; from gnews:87a7d1b498492ae728c74167 2026-09-30] Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said the supply chain is the bottleneck, listing packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators and wafers as tight spots, and said land, power and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n    Quote: The bottleneck is the supply chain. Huang listed packaging, memory, connectors, voltage regulators, and wafers as tight spots. Land, power, and building space for data centers are also scarce.\n\n- e953 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d38374c4abf4bd13d2cd2ccd 2026-10-02] Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, of which $279 billion is tied mostly to memory procurement.\n    Quote: Its own books carry weight too. Nvidia disclosed $366 billion in multiyear AI infrastructure commitments, with $279 billion of that tied mostly to memory procurement.\n\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n    Quote: Major memory suppliers including Micron Technology have warned that as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained. These suppliers project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing prospects for early normalization. … Micron CEO San\n- e1040 [fact; Geoffrey Seiler, The Motley Fool; original source SK Hynix (with Nvidia) ; from gnews:8cba65ded3fbaa69dfc8a83c 2026-10-04] The Motley Fool's Geoffrey Seiler reports that SK Hynix, which he describes as Nvidia's main high-bandwidth memory (HBM) supplier, recently formed a supply and co-development partnership with Nvidia under which SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years. The $500 billion figure is the article's estimate; no start date, volume or pricing terms are given.\n    Quote: It is also the main HBM supplier to Nvidia, and the two companies recently formed an important supply and co-development partnership. As part of the deal, SK Hynix will supply Nvidia with an estimated $500 billion worth of memory over the next several years.\n- e1062 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA's second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. The filing scheduled $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward, and said NVIDIA may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orders, though changes can carry costs.\n    Quote: Its second-quarter filing disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments, up from $119 billion at the previous quarter-end. … The filing allocated $92 billion of these commitments to the remainder of fiscal 2027, $87 billion to fiscal 2028 and $88 billion to fiscal 2029, with smaller amounts afterward. NVIDIA also said it may cancel, reschedule or adjust some agreements before firm orde\n\nITEM 13\n- e18 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA management expects roughly a quarter (about 25%) of the company's fiscal 2028 business to come from AI labs that NVIDIA will back with its own balance sheet.\n    Quote: If you hold NVIDIA (NVDA) stock, one number deserves your attention: roughly a quarter. That is the share of fiscal 2028 business management expects from AI labs it will back with its own balance sheet. … Roughly a quarter of expected fiscal 2028 business comes from AI labs NVIDIA plans to back.\n- e22 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA plans to provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity for one additional AI lab.\n    Quote: For one more AI lab, it will provide credit support for nearly 2 gigawatts of computing capacity.\n\n- e23 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:5594f685fcb554db910e45f8 2026-09-22] NVIDIA guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility's capacity with some cloud partners.\n    Quote: With some cloud partners, it guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a facility’s capacity.\n- e41 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA is the exclusive AI-compute provider for SB Energy's PORTS-Pike campus in Ohio and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts of capacity there, with an option covering another 3.75 gigawatts.\n    Quote: Nvidia is the exclusive AI-compute provider for that site and is providing credit support for an initial 4.25 gigawatts, with an option covering another 3.75.\n\n- e42 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:1b632a31e83a56abe9f3c5fa 2026-09-22] NVIDIA has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, tying its economics more closely to OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n    Quote: Nvidia has disclosed maximum guarantee exposure that can reach $105 billion under defined conditions, so the economics become more intertwined with OpenAI's ability to meet its lease obligations.\n- e44 [third_party_view; Zacks Research Daily; original source Zacks analyst (Zacks Investment Research) 2026-09-22; from gnews:529d51ca192a19aae5f1c7eb 2026-09-22] A Zacks analyst research report on NVIDIA, published in the 22 September 2026 Zacks Research Daily, stated that NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees, while noting that China remains largely closed to data center compute. No amounts, counterparties or dates were given for the increased commitments.\n    Quote: China remains largely closed to data center compute, and NVIDIA is taking on larger supply commitments, customer credit support and long-dated guarantees.\n\n- e61 [third_party_view; barchart.com; original source barchart.com (article analysis) 2026-09-22; from gnews:355e074116b277efe4dcaaab 2026-09-22] The article states that NVIDIA has off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects such as the SB Energy campus for OpenAI, which it says introduces credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n    Quote: Additionally, off-balance-sheet guarantees exceeding $100 billion tied to projects like the SB Energy campus for OpenAI introduce credit risk that traditional valuation metrics do not capture.\n- e114 [fact; 24/7 Wall St. (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dac89e12f005c43e4ec94571 2026-09-23] The article lists guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion among NVIDIA's real risks.\n    Quote: Real risks include supply commitments of $279 billion, guarantee obligations capped at $108.5 billion, and DSO extending to 60 days.\n- e136 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3e5fcbff91ec0ab7c56e3c7e 2026-09-23] The article reports that NVIDIA's supply commitments have surged to $279 billion and its guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion, describing both as real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n    Quote: Bear: the sale is large, outside a prearranged plan, and arrives as the market questions whether the spending cycle is self-funded. Supply commitments surged to $279 billion, and guarantee obligations are capped at $108.5 billion. Those are real balance-sheet exposures if demand slips.\n- e143 [fact; fool.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:7f14bde8195bae63bcb71249 2026-09-23] NVIDIA agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n    Quote: For example, Nvidia agreed to guarantee up to $105 billion of data center lease obligations for OpenAI.\n- e196 [fact; Beatrice Nolan (Fortune), citing Nscale's U.K. company accounts filed October 2025; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:0a4dc52800a886079fb749e0 2026-09-24] Nscale's own U.K. company accounts, filed in October 2025, showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million and, in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale's lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n    Quote: Previous filings from Nscale’s own U.K. company accounts in October 2025 showed that Nvidia bought warrants for over 157,945 Nscale shares for $60 million—and in exchange, agreed to guarantee up to $860.3 million of Nscale’s lease obligations at a facility in Ward County, Texas.\n\n- e220 [fact; techrepublic.com; original source Aolani 2026-09-21; from gnews:fd2ffd8b19aaab49b7359ce8 2026-09-24] Aolani said its arrangement with Nvidia includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand; the financial terms of that revenue-sharing and credit-support model were not disclosed.\n    Quote: Aolani’s arrangement with Nvidia also includes a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to align infrastructure deployment with customer demand. Financial terms were not disclosed.\n\n- e500 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:62d89ab530bbd287d3772f1a 2026-09-28] NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo, according to the Financial Times; the backstop offer is reported as preceding the insurer talks.\n    Quote: The insurance push comes after Nvidia offered to backstop part of financing deals designed to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\n- e582 [fact; Financial Times (reported by Yahoo Finance); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:791d301ec1a1c19ef4c6fbbe 2026-09-29] According to the FT-sourced material cited in the article, NVIDIA has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo. The item does not state the size of NVIDIA's backstop portion or the timing of the offer.\n    Quote: According to the supplied material, Nvidia has offered to backstop part of financing deals intended to unlock $500 billion of capital from Wall Street firms including Goldman Sachs and Apollo.\n- e778 [fact; finance.yahoo.com article (contributor piece); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:890bfe7d206f0531a54beb16 2026-10-01] NVIDIA's guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion, according to the article.\n    Quote: Supply obligations reached $279.0 billion, mostly memory for Vera Rubin, and guarantee obligations for partners are capped at $108.5 billion.\n- e796 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a0b58e332b9b0027cd82bb5a 2026-10-01] NVIDIA outlined more than US$500 billion of compute financing agreements and plans with partners including Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, intended to mobilize more than US$500 billion of third-party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\n    Quote: Nvidia outlined more than US$500b of compute financing agreements and plans with partners such as Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR to mobilize over US$500b of third party capital for AI infrastructure over time.\n- e828 [party_statement; gurufocus.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] Nvidia said independent financiers in its $500 billion infrastructure-financing push will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project, and that Nvidia may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities — a contingent credit backstop that would support lenders' recoveries on GPU-backed customer financing.\n    Quote: Nvidia says independent financiers will assess customer quality, utilization, cash flow and equipment values project by project. It may provide residual-value support for up to 25% of selected opportunities.\n- e830 [third_party_view; gurufocus.com; original source gurufocus.com ; from gnews:ff3c940a31d608112807883e 2026-10-01] The article states that Nvidia's $500 billion infrastructure-financing push is a capital-mobilization target over time, rather than booked chip sales or a fully funded pool of cash.\n    Quote: The $500 billion is a capital-mobilization target over time, rather than booked chip sales or a fully funded pool of cash.\n- e989 [fact; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August a $500 billion AI financing plan with financiers including Blackstone, Apollo and KKR that envisioned using Nvidia chips as collateral with limited guarantees to give AI developers access to Nvidia compute.\n    Quote: Nvidia’s plan, announced in August with financiers including Blackstone, Apollo and KKR, envisioned using its chips as collateral with limited guarantees, allowing AI developers to gain access to Nvidia’s compute. The strategy was seen as an expensive but potentially necessary way to create a financing market similar to the one used to finance aircraft.\n- e990 [party_statement; Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia has said some deals under its $500 billion financing initiative could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\n    Quote: Nvidia has said some deals could carry a residual-value guarantee of no more than 25%, arguing that the initiative is designed to address concerns over circular financing.\n- e991 [rumour; Reuters; original source Three unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Three banking sources not part of Nvidia's original financing group said Nvidia may ultimately need to provide guarantees on all of its financing deals, or have them backed by revenue streams from investment-grade customers such as major technology companies to cover debt payments. The sources added that the market is not yet ready to treat AI compute as an investment-grade asset comparable to aircraft.\n    Quote: Three banking sources familiar with the matter but not part of the original financing group said Nvidia may ultimately need to provide guarantees on all of its deals, or have them backed by revenue streams from investment-grade customers such as major technology companies to cover debt payments. … For now, the sources said, the market is not yet ready to treat AI compute as an investment-grade ass\n\n- e992 [rumour; Reuters; original source Unnamed banking sources ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Banking sources said Nvidia is providing previously unreported details to address lender concerns, and that tens of billions of dollars in loan deals in the pipeline are likely to include stronger guarantees and contractual protections. Some structures under consideration could provide lenders with guarantees, and the initial deals will be secured by Nvidia chips and backed by customer contracts and Nvidia's underlying guarantee, according to the sources.\n    Quote: Nvidia is seeking to address some of these concerns, the sources said, providing details that had not previously been reported. … Tens of billions of dollars in loan deals currently in the pipeline are likely to include stronger guarantees and contractual protections, one source said. A second source said some structures under consideration could provide lenders with guarantees. … The initial deal\n- e995 [fact; Reuters, citing S&P and Moody's; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d2db7f7e14e0a30d3e7a23de 2026-10-03] Nvidia previously provided a residual-value guarantee to support financing for SB Energy's Ohio data center project, according to S&P and Moody's.\n    Quote: Nvidia itself previously provided a residual-value guarantee to support financing for SB Energy’s Ohio data center project, according to S&P and Moody’s.\n\n- e1048 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n    Quote: NVIDIA reported $279 billion in supply commitments and guarantee obligations limited at $108.5 billion for AI cloud and data center partners.\n- e1063 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:b0e3eb6b8404ada08d7535e1 2026-10-04] NVIDIA disclosed in August guarantees capped at $105 billion for an OpenAI-related SB Energy buildout. The guarantees generally begin as the relevant leases commence, with the first construction phase expected in fiscal 2029, and payments depend on specified tenant defaults; they cover defined lease and power payments and decline over the leases. The article describes this as contingent exposure rather than a current $105 billion cash bill.\n    Quote: NVIDIA also disclosed August guarantees capped at $105 billion for an OpenAI-related SB Energy buildout. They generally begin as the relevant leases commence, with the first construction phase expected in fiscal 2029, and payments depend on specified tenant defaults. The guarantees cover defined lease and power payments and decline over the leases; they are contingent exposure, not a current $105 \n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n    Quote: In addition, Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings (equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion), if applied across the full target. The company’s actual balance sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n- e1138 [fact; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] NVIDIA structures its dealings with NeoClouds (newer AI-focused cloud providers) by guaranteeing a minimum level of revenue on part of a NeoCloud facility's capacity and taking a share of what the NeoCloud earns above that level, according to the article's description of the arrangement.\n    Quote: NVIDIA goes further for NeoClouds, the newer cloud providers built for AI. It guarantees a minimum level of revenue on part of a NeoCloud facility’s capacity, and it takes a share of what the NeoCloud earns above that level.\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\n    Quote: Nvidia invests in customers that buy its chips. In August, it announced preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, CNBC reported.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\"));\nfor i in (x[2],x[3],x[8],x[9]):\n print(\\\"\\\\nITEM\\\",i[\\\"item\\\"])\n for s in re.split(r\\\"(?m)\"'^- (?=e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[)\\\",i[\\\"request\\\"]):\n  m=re.match(r\\\"(e\\\\d+) \\\\[\\\",s)\n  if m and m.group(1) in i[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"cites\\\"]: print(\\\"- \\\"+s.split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion,\\\")[0].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n[\\\")[0])'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json,re; x=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/carried-run/packet-answers.json\\\"));\nfor i in (x[2],x[3],x[8],x[9]):\n print(\\\"\\\\nITEM\\\",i[\\\"item\\\"])\n for s in re.split(r\\\"(?m)\"'^- (?=e'\"\\\\d+ \\\\[)\\\",i[\\\"request\\\"]):\n  m=re.match(r\\\"(e\\\\d+) \\\\[\\\",s)\n  if m and m.group(1) in i[\\\"answer\\\"][\\\"cites\\\"]: print(\\\"- \\\"+s.split(\\\"\\\\n    Opinion,\\\")[0].split(\\\"\\\\n\\\\n[\\\")[0])'\"","aggregated_output":"\nITEM 3\n- e80 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source The Trump administration (U.S. government) ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China, per the article; the H200 is described as an older-generation product behind Nvidia's current Blackwell chips.\n    Quote: The Trump administration has allowed Nvidia to ship its H200 AI chips to approved customers in China.\n- e81 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source Chinese authorities ; from gnews:bc186eb544a1d76e41aba487 2026-09-23] Chinese authorities have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips into China, according to the article, despite U.S. permission for those shipments to approved customers.\n    Quote: Chinese authorities, however, have so far restricted shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips.\n- e88 [party_statement; barchart.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:a5837f6a8cb2c7893ec87974 2026-09-23] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%, and GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins to approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points. The forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China.\n    Quote: Furthermore, Nvidia issued an even stronger outlook. For Q3 fiscal 2027, the company expects revenue of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. Importantly, the forecast assumes no Data Center compute revenue from China. Nvidia expects GAAP and non-GAAP gross margins of approximately 74%, plus or minus 50 basis points.\n- e148 [fact; CNBC; original source U.S. government (Washington) ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] The article reports that Washington has for years restricted China's access to Nvidia's most advanced AI chips and to the equipment used to make them, on the bet that limiting computing power would keep Chinese AI a generation behind; the restriction is described as in force as of the 23 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Washington has for years restricted China's access to Nvidia's most advanced AI chips and the equipment to make them, betting that starving computing power would keep Chinese AI a generation behind.\n- e149 [party_statement; CNBC; original source U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer ; from gnews:d0f75c07bd80b3050c40b17c 2026-09-23] During the September 2026 Trump–Xi state visit, the article said neither side appeared ready to put chip export controls on the table; Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer met Vice Premier He Lifeng in New York over the weekend and set up a formal AI channel for incident warnings, and Greer said explicitly that export controls on advanced chips and chipmaking equipment were not on the agenda.\n    Quote: As Trump hosts Xi for a multi-day state visit, neither side appears ready to put chip export controls on the table. … Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent and U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer met Vice Premier He Lifeng in New York over the weekend and set up a formal AI channel for incident warnings. But Greer said explicitly that export controls on advanced chips and chipmaking equipment were\n- e365 [fact; ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News); original source U.S. government (Trump administration) ; from gnews:a883c7d707bfef9843efbc2a 2026-09-26] As reported on 26 September 2026, U.S. export restrictions currently bar Nvidia's most powerful (current-generation) AI chips from being exported to China, while the prior year (2025) President Donald Trump permitted Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers subject to a 25% tariff, according to ABC News Australia (relayed by UA.News).\n    Quote: Nvidia’s most powerful chips are currently prohibited from export to China due to Washington’s restrictions. At the same time, last year Trump allowed Nvidia to sell previous-generation H200 chips to approved Chinese buyers with a 25% tariff.\n- e383 [party_statement; Nvidia spokesperson, reported by Reuters; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c24441966fa661191bb1a601 2026-09-27] Asked by Reuters to comment on the report, an Nvidia spokesperson said U.S. firms continue to be restricted by a combination of outdated U.S. export controls — which the spokesperson said cover gaming products released nearly half a decade ago — and China's own limits on U.S. imports.\n    Quote: \"US firms continue to be restricted by a combination of outdated US export controls, which cover gaming products released nearly half a decade ago, and China's own limits on US imports,\" an Nvidia spokesperson said when asked to comment.\n- e426 [fact; The Next Web, reporting in August 2026; original source Chinese authorities (unnamed) ; from gnews:905fc2415065c07f926596d0 2026-09-27] ByteDance and Tencent were told to keep the first shipments of Nvidia's H200 chips in Hong Kong rather than bring them into mainland China, The Next Web reported in August 2026.\n    Quote: However, ByteDance and Tencent were told to keep the first shipments in Hong Kong , TNW reported in August, rather than bring them into mainland China.\n\n- e428 [fact; sg.finance.yahoo.com; original source US government (Washington) ; from gnews:246875816e58773740fb09d8 2026-09-27] The US government bars Nvidia's most powerful accelerators from China on national-security grounds, a restriction the article describes as still in force and distinguishes from the RTX Pro 5500, which is built on older-generation processes and is intended mainly for graphics and simulations rather than being a top-of-the-line accelerator such as the GB300 series.\n    Quote: Washington bars Nvidia's most powerful accelerators from China, concerned they would propel the country's AI efforts and aid the military of a geopolitical ally. … Unlike Nvidia's top-of-the-line accelerators such as the GB300 series, it's built on older-generation processes and is designed to provide workstation computation at scale.\n- e437 [fact; finance.biggo.com; original source Chinese regulators ; from gnews:8a227e26dadd5fb06a8adb66 2026-09-27] Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line, according to the article.\n    Quote: Chinese regulators have pushed buyers toward domestic silicon, restricting H200 use to model training and steering routine inference to Huawei's Ascend line.\n\n- e715 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:06cfbef2e042b99ce960fcd8 2026-09-30] Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market, and warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n    Quote: Nvidia said in its latest quarterly filing that U.S. export restrictions and Chinese rules have effectively shut it out of competing broadly in China's data-center compute market. … The company warned that its absence has helped rivals build \"larger developer and customer ecosystems to challenge us worldwide.\"\n\n- e745 [fact; theregister.com; original source US Commerce Department / Trump administration ; from gnews:e4d51e6fa8c39d866c4d7289 2026-09-30] US export policy toward Nvidia's China sales: the US Commerce Department imposed new licensing requirements on Nvidia's and AMD's China-spec accelerators in April 2025, reversed course that summer and later reached an arrangement that gave the US government a percentage of certain China-bound chip sales; by late 2025 the Trump administration loosened restrictions further and announced that Nvidia would be allowed to sell more potent H200-series parts to approved Chinese customers for the first time.\n    Quote: After imposing new licensing requirements on Nvidia's and AMD's China-spec accelerators in April 2025, the US Commerce Department reversed course that summer, later reaching an arrangement to cut the US government in on a percentage of certain China-bound chip sales. By late last year, the Trump administration loosened restrictions further, announcing that Nvidia would be allowed to sell its more \n- e763 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA guided fiscal third-quarter 2027 revenue to $108 billion, plus or minus 2%; the outlook excludes Data Center compute revenue from China, which Zacks said suggests current expectations do not depend on a China recovery.\n    Quote: The near-term outlook also remains strong. NVIDIA expects fiscal third-quarter revenues of $108 billion, plus or minus 2%. This outlook excludes Data Center compute revenues from China, suggesting current expectations do not depend on a recovery in that market.\n- e784 [fact; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese state-backed financing entity, funded AI infrastructure developer Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of more than 700 servers, and at least one transaction explicitly funded 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers powered by Nvidia's high-performance B300 Blackwell chips, according to Bloomberg News citing filings with the People's Bank of China. The item notes the documented batch of Blackwell-equipped servers is relatively small but highlights gaps in Western export restrictions.\n    Quote: The financing, provided by Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., supported the purchase of more than 700 servers by AI infrastructure developer Glory View Technology Co. … Filings with the People's Bank of China cited by Bloomberg reveal that at least one transaction explicitly funded 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers powered by Nvidia's high-performance B300 Blackwell chips. … While the documented batch of\n- e785 [party_statement; Bloomberg News (cited by Investing.com); original source NVIDIA Corporation ; from gnews:39a92312eb5f3f0a29db0c8d 2026-10-01] Nvidia told Bloomberg it is investigating the matter with its equipment manufacturing partners, after a Bloomberg report that a Chinese state-backed lessor financed purchases of servers powered by Nvidia B300 Blackwell chips; Asus reiterated its commitment to international export control regulations.\n    Quote: Nvidia told Bloomberg that it is investigating the matter with its equipment manufacturing partners, while Asus reiterated its commitment to international export control regulations.\n- e957 [fact; Bloomberg (article published by qz.com); original source Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co. and Glory View Technology Co. (regulatory filings) ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese financing company controlled by local government entities, provided capital for Glory View Technology Co.'s purchase of restricted Nvidia AI chips, per Beijing regulatory filings reviewed by Bloomberg. The filings show Semi-Tech Leasing funded more than 700 servers totaling more than 3 billion yuan ($440 million) in financing, with one contract covering 32 Asustek Computer Inc. servers equipped with Nvidia's B300 chips (part of the Blackwell family); U.S. regulations prohibit selling B300 chips to China without an explicit license. Most of the servers, including the 32 Blackwell units, were listed as deployed inside a China Mobile Ltd. data center facility in Zhongwei, Ningxia, an officially designated national computing cluster zone.\n    Quote: Semi-Tech Leasing Group Co., a Chinese financing company controlled by local government entities, provided capital for the purchase of restricted Nvidia $NVDA Corp. AI chips, according to regulatory filings reviewed by Bloomberg . … The documents, filed with Beijing regulators, show Semi-Tech Leasing funded purchases by Glory View Technology Co. of more than 700 servers totaling more than 3 billio\n- e959 [party_statement; qz.com (citing Nvidia's statement); original source Nvidia ; from gnews:d2cade2fa87c4a5c234ce480 2026-10-02] Nvidia said it is \"looking into this report and will work with our OEM customer to investigate\" the financing and deployment in China of servers equipped with its B300 (Blackwell) chips, which U.S. rules bar selling to China without an explicit license.\n    Quote: Nvidia said it is \"looking into this report and will work with our OEM customer to investigate.\"\n- e1156 [fact; Pamela Kock, The Motley Fool; original source U.S. government (export restrictions); characterization by The Motley Fool ; from gnews:c457f426c0c1dd3ca206841b 2026-10-05] The article states that Nvidia faces significant geopolitical risk from U.S. export restrictions on AI-capable semiconductors to China, which it says remain in place and create revenue volatility and inventory risk for the company. No new regulatory step, approval, licence or date is reported.\n    Quote: Nvidia faces significant geopolitical risks, particularly from U.S. export restrictions on AI-capable semiconductors to China, which create revenue volatility and inventory risk.\n\nITEM 4\n- e293 [party_statement; Zacks; original source Micron Technology 2026-06-24; from gnews:90ebbd46135613dc0cb8830f 2026-09-25] Micron guided fiscal fourth-quarter 2026 revenue to around $50 billion and a gross margin of approximately 86%, citing strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\n    Quote: Looking ahead, Micron expects revenues to rise to around $50 billion in the fiscal fourth quarter of 2026. The company also projects a healthy gross margin of approximately 86%, supported by strong demand for AI-related memory products and favorable pricing.\n\n- e368 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron is actively expanding its memory-chip production footprint to supply clients with more memory chips, and the article reports that this production capacity will not be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\n    Quote: While Micron could sit around and enjoy the elevated prices, it's actively expanding its production footprint to provide clients with more memory chips. That production capacity won't be online until mid-2027 and into 2028.\n\n- e369 [party_statement; Keithen Drury (The Motley Fool); original source Micron ; from gnews:7a1c5168f62af67f4e1fbe93 2026-09-26] Micron does not expect the \"tightness\" in the memory-chip market to alleviate until 2028, per The Motley Fool's Keithen Drury; memory chips are described as a component NVIDIA's and Broadcom's computing units cannot run without.\n    Quote: Micron also doesn't expect the \"tightness\" in the memory chip space to alleviate until 2028\n- e933 [party_statement; Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology (management) ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron management said the company has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements covering more than 35% of its expected revenue through 2030, and that customer financial commitments rose to $32 billion from $22 billion in the prior quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n    Quote: According to management, Micron has 26 multi-year take-or-pay agreements, covering more than 35% of expected revenue through 2030. Customer financial commitments have soared to $32 billion from $22 billion last quarter, with the vast majority in cash deposits.\n\n- e934 [party_statement; Micron Technology, reported by Zacks Investment Ideas; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:23fdf5ee8cbf0b27ad04e66c 2026-10-02] Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains and said it does not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance, in its earnings release the night before the Zacks article of 2 October 2026.\n    Quote: Last night, Micron confirmed that the DRAM supply/demand imbalance remains, saying: … \"We do not have line-of-sight to when supply and demand will return to balance.\"\n- e1027 [party_statement; Sanjay Mehrotra, Micron CEO, on an earnings call (reported by finance.biggo.com); original source Micron Technology (CEO Sanjay Mehrotra) ; from gnews:d5aa9ac26fd22e8535ac3698 2026-10-03] Micron Technology and other memory suppliers have warned that, as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained, and they project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing early normalization; Micron CEO Sanjay Mehrotra said on an earnings call that \"demand will exceed supply in 2027 and 2028\" and that the company cannot predict when supply and demand will return to balance.\n    Quote: Major memory suppliers including Micron Technology have warned that as production lines shift toward High Bandwidth Memory (HBM) for AI data centers, supply of general-purpose high-performance memory such as LPDDR5X has become extremely constrained. These suppliers project the supply-demand imbalance will persist through 2027 and 2028, dismissing prospects for early normalization. … Micron CEO San\n- e1074 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source Nvidia (CFO) ; from gnews:4f6390171510e65b5e369925 2026-10-04] Nvidia's CFO flagged 'extreme pricing conditions in memory' as a margin headwind.\n    Quote: Nvidia also disclosed $279 billion of supply and capacity commitments as of July 26, primarily related to memory procurement, while its CFO flagged \"extreme pricing conditions in memory\" as a margin headwind.\n\n- e1107 [third_party_view; Benzinga; original source Morgan Stanley ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Morgan Stanley said that delayed data-center deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\n    Quote: Morgan Stanley said delayed deployments could force customers to push out or cancel deliveries of memory, optical, power-management and analog components.\n\n- e1108 [party_statement; Benzinga; original source Micron Technology ; from gnews:3785635fc4c5f95c340a6f52 2026-10-05] Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed.\n    Quote: Micron says more than 75% of its 2027 output is already committed\n\n- e1173 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA 2026-08-26; from gnews:6bfaa6b41767a6c301bdec6d 2026-10-05] NVIDIA guided gross margin down from 75% in fiscal Q2 2027 to 74% expected in fiscal Q3 2027, and said it expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in fiscal Q4 2027, blaming memory prices that rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\n    Quote: NVIDIA does not expect its gross margin to hold. It was 75% in fiscal Q2 2027, and management expects 74% for Q3. Management then expects margins to bottom at 71% to 72% in Q4, and it blamed the price of memory, which rose more than NVIDIA had expected.\n\nITEM 9\n- e181 [fact; GuruFocus (gurufocus.com); original source Super Micro Computer 2026-09-24; from gnews:cd0056006ad115260ae2b646 2026-09-24] Super Micro Computer moved into commercial shipments of servers built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions that combine computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components; each rack design uses 72 Rubin GPUs and 36 Vera CPUs, according to a GuruFocus report published 24 September 2026.\n    Quote: the server maker moved into commercial shipments of systems built around NVIDIA's next-generation Vera Rubin platform, adding another data-center product to its AI infrastructure lineup. … The company is shipping Vera Rubin NVL72 racks through its Data Center Building Block Solutions, combining computing hardware with liquid cooling, networking, storage and power components. The rack design uses 7\n- e236 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA (CFO) ; from gnews:06218d9421731e5e59c1dbef 2026-09-24] NVIDIA's CFO expects supply to remain a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme; the article reports memory prices have risen more than management expected.\n    Quote: The CFO expects supply to stay a bottleneck at least through the end of fiscal 2028 and calls memory pricing extreme. … Memory prices have risen more than management expected, and the guide has that margin settling in the 72% to 73% range in fiscal 2028, helped by price increases NVIDIA has already executed.\n- e279 [third_party_view; qz.com, citing MarketWatch; original source Mehdi Hosseini, Susquehanna analyst (remarks to MarketWatch) ; from gnews:460160402f7a10882d1e925f 2026-09-25] Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini, in remarks reported by MarketWatch and cited by qz.com, said Micron's November-quarter (fiscal Q1 2026) sales forecast is strong enough to satisfy investors but will not fully reflect the benefits of the Nvidia product ramp; he said some estimated revenue is expected to shift toward next year instead of this year, implying the Nvidia-chip-related revenue payoff may be delayed.\n    Quote: Micron $MU Technology posted record fiscal fourth-quarter revenue and issued strong guidance for the current quarter, but one analyst says investors may need to wait longer to see the full payoff from an Nvidia $NVDA chip launch. … Susquehanna analyst Mehdi Hosseini told MarketWatch that Micron 's November-quarter sales forecast looks strong enough to satisfy investors, though it won't fully refle\n\n- e410 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to bring in about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n- e416 [party_statement; NVIDIA management; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:d4f3bddf04a4d029a097541c 2026-09-27] NVIDIA management expects sales to its largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027 as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n    Quote: Management expects sales to the largest cloud companies to speed up again in fiscal Q4 2027, as Vera Rubin supply grows.\n\n- e555 [party_statement; Trefis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4334522792e44c6fd9d18ccd 2026-09-28] NVIDIA management expects the newly launched Vera Rubin platform to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027, according to Trefis.\n    Quote: The newly launched Vera Rubin platform is one source of that revenue. Management expects it to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027.\n- e659 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave announced at its CoreWeave Fully Connected event (held the week of the 30 September 2026 NVIDIA blog post) that NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking are available on CoreWeave Cloud, making CoreWeave one of the first cloud providers to deliver the platform in customers' hands; per the announcement, Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: At CoreWeave Fully Connected, running this week in San Francisco, CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 systems with Spectrum-X 102.4T Ethernet networking. Cognition, the applied AI lab behind the Devin AI software engineer, is the first customer running production workloads on Vera Rubin. … CoreWeave announced availability of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 on CoreWeave Cloud, makin\n- e660 [fact; NVIDIA Corporation (NVIDIA Blog); original source CoreWeave ; from nvblog:coreweave-agentic-ai-vera-rubin 2026-09-30] CoreWeave received its first NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks earlier in September 2026, before the 30 September 2026 article.\n    Quote: Earlier this month, CoreWeave received its first Vera Rubin NVL72 production racks.\n\n- e764 [party_statement; Zacks; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:95eab8d6d71dd0f94a8f763e 2026-10-01] NVIDIA said it started shipping its Vera Rubin platform in August 2026 and expects Vera Rubin to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in fiscal Q3 2027.\n    Quote: NVIDIA started shipping Vera Rubin in August and expects the platform to account for about 20% of Data Center revenues in the third quarter.\n- e857 [fact; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 earnings call that the company began production shipments of its Vera Rubin AI data-center platform in August 2026.\n    Quote: NVIDIA began production shipments in August 2026, management said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call.\n- e858 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management forecast on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that Vera Rubin would make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027; the article notes this is a forecast, not a result.\n    Quote: Management expects Vera Rubin to make up about 20% of data center revenue in fiscal Q3 2027. That is a forecast, not a result.\n- e864 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance (finance.yahoo.com); original source NVIDIA management ; from gnews:8867ec84bb48b27c3ae694f5 2026-10-02] NVIDIA management says the company cannot build enough to meet demand, called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year, and said on the fiscal Q2 2027 call that its entire supply chain is running flat out.\n    Quote: NVIDIA cannot build enough to meet demand, management says. Management called its fiscal 2028 forecast supply-constrained, and expects supply to stay a bottleneck through at least the end of that year. Asked on the fiscal Q2 2027 call what holds supply back, management said its entire supply chain is running flat out.\n- e895 [fact; wccftech.com; original source CoreWeave / Cognition ; from gnews:4875d3af5072a2d9c8532a7f 2026-10-02] Cognition is the first customer to secure installations of NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 servers at CoreWeave, and CoreWeave stood up a production Vera Rubin cluster for it in days, with Cognition scaling to thousands of GPUs within nine months; Cognition had earlier benchmarked Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in September, recording a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads.\n    Quote: Cognition is the first to secure a hold on these latest server installations at CoreWeave, and has been able to scale to thousands of GPUs within nine months. Earlier in September, Cognition was the first to benchmark Vera Rubin against Grace Blackwell in inference, and was able to record a 4.8x gain in total token throughput for SWE-2 inference workloads. … In days, CoreWeave stood up a productio\n- e1177 [fact; Business Wire (AM Intelligence press release, via Yahoo Finance UK); original source AM Intelligence (AMI) 2026-10-05; from gnews:8ec8d22f933c8556d24c6c9d 2026-10-05] AM Intelligence (AMI), the AI infrastructure platform set up by promoters of Greenko, announced on 5 October 2026 two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems, on top of its inaugural commitment announced 25 August 2026 for 9,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs at its first AI Factory in Hyderabad. The follow-on capacity totals about 70 MW at Malaysia and India with delivery scheduled for Q2 2027, and the three orders together bring AMI's committed frontier compute to approximately 29,000 Rubin GPUs and close to 100 MW, which AMI describes as making it among the earliest and largest committed buyers of Rubin GPUs outside North America.\n    Quote: Two further binding orders of ~20,000 NVIDIA GPUs take AMI's committed NVIDIA Vera Rubin capacity to approximately ~29,000 GPUs and close to 100 MW, part of its global development pipeline of ~400 MW … today announced two further firm and binding orders for 20,000 NVIDIA Rubin GPUs , to be deployed as NVIDIA Vera Rubin NVL72 rack-scale systems. … These orders are in addition to the AMI's inaugural\n\n\nITEM 10\n- e1011 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Nvidia announced in August 2026 a plan with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR—to build an AI infrastructure financing platform intended to attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital, and proposed sharing up to $125 billion in risk by guaranteeing hardware residual values.\n    Quote: Nvidia is also building out a chip-based financing market, partnering with Wall Street financial firms to create large-scale AI infrastructure financing platforms. … In August, Nvidia announced plans with six financial partners—Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs, and KKR—to build a platform that would attract more than $500 billion in third-party capital. Nvidia also proposed\n- e1013 [party_statement; finance.biggo.com; original source Amazon ; from gnews:bca40137e0cfa49b30e5b0f7 2026-10-03] Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years, and that starting in 2025 it shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years.\n    Quote: Amazon tells investors that each generation of semiconductors has a useful life of at least five years. Starting in 2025, the company also shortened the useful life of certain server and networking equipment from six years to five years.\n\n- e1017 [rumour; finance.biggo.com; original source Unnamed lenders (reported) ; from gnews:01dc582fbe53203eaeac2c61 2026-10-03] The article reports, without naming the lenders or citing a source, that some lenders involved in Nvidia's AI infrastructure financing plan are seeking stronger collateral than the company originally outlined, as they try to calculate how long chip revenue will be sustainable.\n    Quote: However, Nvidia's ambitious financing plan has sparked debate on Wall Street about the real value of its advanced chips and associated infrastructure. Some lenders are seeking stronger collateral than originally outlined by the company, even given its industry-leading processing power, as they try to calculate how long chip revenue will be sustainable.\n- e1042 [rumour; Financial Times (via Yahoo Finance); original source Amazon ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon is in talks, according to a Financial Times report, to move $8 billion of NVIDIA Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle that would sell debt to outside investors, buy the chips and lease them back to Amazon, with Amazon keeping up to 10% of the equity; the chips are already installed in data centers across at least five states.\n    Quote: Amazon ( NASDAQ:AMZN ) is in talks to move $8 billion of NVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA ) Grace Blackwell chips into a special purpose vehicle, the Financial Times reported. The vehicle would sell debt to outside investors, buy the chips, and lease them back to Amazon. The hardware stays where it is. It is already installed in data centers across at least five states, and Amazon would keep up to 10% of the \n- e1046 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA says Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin and that Vera Rubin delivers 35 times lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra; NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion of revenue per gigawatt for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion per gigawatt for Vera Rubin.\n    Quote: Grace Blackwell is already one generation behind Vera Rubin. NVIDIA says Vera Rubin delivers 35X lower token costs than Grace Blackwell Ultra. NVIDIA estimates about $25 billion per gigawatt of revenue for Grace Blackwell and roughly $40 billion for Vera Rubin.\n- e1047 [party_statement; Yahoo Finance; original source Amazon (Andy Jassy) ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] Amazon CEO Andy Jassy said on the July earnings call that servers take \"a little less than three years\" to break even and last at least five to six years.\n    Quote: On the July earnings call, Jassy said servers take \"a little less than three years\" to break even and last at least five to six years.\n\n- e1049 [fact; Yahoo Finance; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ed14a9d1371ea43f3c5dcd3d 2026-10-04] NVIDIA is building financing structures similar to Amazon's sale-leaseback and signed up six asset managers and banks to raise more than $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure; management said: \"We know some will call this circular financing.\"\n    Quote: NVIDIA is building similar structures. … It signed up six asset managers and banks to raise more than $500 billion of outside capital for AI infrastructure. Management said: \"We know some will call this circular financing.\"\n- e1129 [fact; Morningstar; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Nvidia has pledged $500 billion to its partnership with Apollo Global Management, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, aimed at supporting enterprises such as AI laboratories, cloud storage providers and data-center operators that purchase or operate Nvidia-based compute. Morningstar notes the partnership is not a fully committed fund and that Nvidia is not a direct investor, with funding expected to come primarily from banks, insurers, asset managers and private credit investors.\n    Quote: Nvidia alone has pledged $500 billion in its partnership with Apollo Global Management APO , BlackRock BLK , Blackstone BX , Brookfield BN , Goldman Sachs GS , and KKR KKR . This and other efforts are intended to support enterprises—such as AI laboratories, cloud storage providers, and data center operators purchasing or operating Nvidia-based compute—while positioning AI infrastructure as an asse\n- e1130 [third_party_view; Morningstar; original source Morningstar 2026-10-05; from gnews:62b41f9768d1b65460f326ca 2026-10-05] Morningstar estimates that Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings under its partnership with Apollo, BlackRock, Blackstone, Brookfield, Goldman Sachs and KKR, equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion if applied across the full target. It adds that Nvidia's actual balance-sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n    Quote: In addition, Nvidia may backstop up to 25% of qualifying financings (equivalent to a maximum potential exposure of $125 billion), if applied across the full target. The company’s actual balance sheet exposure will depend on its collateral, the transaction volume and structure, and the final allocation of credit and residual-value risk.\n- e1145 [third_party_view; Trefis (citing Reuters); original source Reuters 2026-10-01; from gnews:02fb6007185a3496e48b4046 2026-10-05] Reuters reported on 1 October 2026 that banks and investors appear to value NVIDIA's chips, and the infrastructure around them, at less than NVIDIA does; the article offers no figures and characterises the outside money as more cautious.\n    Quote: Outside money looks more cautious. Reuters reported on October 1, 2026, that banks and investors seem to value NVIDIA’s chips, and the infrastructure around them, at less than NVIDIA does.\n\n- e1154 [fact; Darryn Pollock, beincrypto.com (republished by Yahoo Finance), citing CNBC; original source Nvidia (announcement), as reported by CNBC ; from gnews:76c7aa5a044eaaa4dbb5521c 2026-10-05] Nvidia announced in August [2026] preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, according to CNBC, aimed at funding customers that buy its chips. The agreements were described as preliminary; the item gives no signing or closing date and does not state how much of the financing, if any, Nvidia itself guarantees or backstops.\n    Quote: Nvidia invests in customers that buy its chips. In August, it announced preliminary agreements with six financial firms for about $500 billion in customer financing, CNBC reported.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"item\":1,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"The 100,000-unit figure is a possible forecast, not a disclosed shipment total. A below-threshold outcome with no formal abandonment falls in rung 3.\"}\n{\"item\":2,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"The forecast of 12–14 GW falls in rung 2. The rough annual estimate and reported labor, power, and construction constraints support a cautious forecast, though they do not precisely determine the range.\"}\n{\"item\":3,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"Existing restrictions support the forecast that both approvals will not be obtained. The shown material does not establish a qualifying new prohibition through the deadline, so rung 3 fits.\"}\n{\"item\":4,\"verdict\":\"INSUFFICIENT\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"Tight supply and extreme pricing support upward pressure, but the material does not establish whether the full-year average contract-price increase reaches 30% rather than a lower band.\"}\n{\"item\":5,\"verdict\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"The answer forecasts more than $50 billion, which belongs in rung 5, but selects rung 1 below $5 billion. The claimed contrary filing is not shown.\"}\n{\"item\":6,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"The reported intention and likelihood of approval support a forecast of approval by the deadline. The answer acknowledges that approval has not yet been shown and weighs the restrictions.\"}\n{\"item\":7,\"verdict\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[{\"id\":\"e590\",\"why\":\"The passage's author says Delangue sold the company; it does not show Delangue saying this or establish closing.\"}],\"why\":\"The answer forecasts completion and disclosure, which is rung 1, but selects rung 2. Its reasons explicitly say there is no evidence of a qualifying termination or prohibition.\"}\n{\"item\":8,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[{\"id\":\"e590\",\"why\":\"The passage's author says Delangue sold the company; it does not show Delangue saying this or establish closing.\"}],\"why\":\"The reported agreement and expected closing before the deadline support rung 1 as a forecast. The answer recognizes that a qualifying filing is not yet shown.\"}\n{\"item\":9,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"Management's forecast of Q3 revenue and reported production shipments support a forecast of revenue recognition and disclosure before the deadline; the answer distinguishes that forecast from an established result.\"}\n{\"item\":10,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"The proposal, financing partners, and reported lender demand support a forecast of a definitive guarantee by the deadline, while the answer acknowledges preliminary terms and countervailing risks.\"}\n{\"item\":11,\"verdict\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"The answer forecasts guarantee exposure above $50 billion, which falls in rung 5, but selects rung 1 below $5 billion.\"}\n{\"item\":12,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"The disclosed $279 billion balance less $92 billion scheduled for the rest of the year gives a $187 billion benchmark in rung 5. Future commitments and cancellations remain uncertain.\"}\n{\"item\":13,\"verdict\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"The $108.5 billion figure is a cap, not a demonstrated minimum. The answer treats it as a floor for the future disclosed amount without showing why that floor follows.\"}\n{\"item\":14,\"verdict\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"The $800 billion reference falls in rung 3, but the $820 billion estimate adds Amazon's increase without establishing that Nvidia's reference excluded it or that all of it is data-center capex.\"}\n{\"item\":15,\"verdict\":\"UNSUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"The answer forecasts about 14 GW and explicitly rules out 15 GW or more. That is rung 2, while the selected rung 5 requires at least 25 GW.\"}\n{\"item\":16,\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"bad_citations\":[],\"why\":\"Nvidia's $1.3 trillion projection falls in rung 3. The answer reasonably treats Barclays' differently scoped figure and the qualitative demand signals as insufficient to move it across a boundary.\"}"}}
